window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("system-design", {"topics":[{"key":"system-design/ai-solution-architecture","sources":["Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems","Zaharia et al., 2024 — The Shift from Models to Compound AI Systems (BAIR blog)"],"why":"The first version of almost every AI feature is one function: build a prompt string, call the API,\nshow the answer. It demos well and it ships. Then the failures arrive, and none of them is in the\nmodel. Answers vary between runs, so nobody can tell a regression from noise. Cost scales with every\nuser and every retry. The prompt swells to 3,000 tokens of rules because there is no retrieval and no\ntool access. A user pastes a contract and gets a confidently invented clause. Security asks where the\ndata goes and who can see whose documents. The provider deprecates the model version and the carefully\ntuned prompt stops working. Each failure lives in the system the model was supposed to be surrounded by.\n\nClassical solution architecture already exists, so why treat this as its own discipline? Because a\nmodel breaks three assumptions that classical architecture leans on. Components are deterministic:\nsame input, same output. Correctness can be unit-tested. Cost per call is negligible. A model's\nbehaviour is instead specified by data (prompts, examples, retrieved context) rather than by code, its\nquality can only be measured statistically over a dataset, and its latency and per-call cost are\nfirst-order constraints that shape the design. Sculley et al. described the classical version of this\nas hidden technical debt: the model is a small box and the plumbing is the rest of the diagram. With\nrented, general-purpose LLMs the ratio is even more extreme, because the model is the same one your\ncompetitor rents. Differentiation lives entirely in how you assemble context, constrain outputs, and\nclose the evaluation loop.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"better prompts will fix it\", fails because a prompt is a configuration\nsurface, not an architecture. It cannot give you access control, retrieval freshness, caching,\nretries, fallbacks, schema guarantees, evaluation, or cost accounting.","how":"### The layers and the reason each one is separate\n1. **Experience and integration layer.** UI, public API, chat channels, IDE plugins. Owns identity,\n   sessions and streaming to the client. It never holds a prompt or a model key, *because* otherwise\n   every surface becomes coupled to a specific model's behaviour and cannot be changed centrally.\n2. **Orchestration layer.** Turns a request into one or more model calls: context assembly (retrieval,\n   memory, tool results), prompt rendering, routing to a model, the tool-execution loop, output\n   parsing. Most application code lives here *because* this is the deterministic part you can test.\n3. **Model layer.** A portfolio of models behind a gateway: pinned versions, fallbacks, rate limits,\n   cost metering, provider credentials. Separate *because* models change on the provider's schedule,\n   not yours, and because the gateway is the one place to enforce budgets and policy.\n4. **Knowledge and data layer.** Ingestion, chunking, indexes, memory stores, attribute lookups, each\n   carrying access-control metadata. Separate *because* freshness, permissions and provenance are data\n   engineering problems; a prompt cannot know that a document was revoked an hour ago.\n5. **Control layer.** Input and output guardrails, schema validation, policy checks, approval gates\n   before consequential actions. Exists *because* model output is untrusted input to the rest of your\n   system: a retrieved document can carry an injected instruction, and the model will sometimes follow it.\n6. **Evaluation and observability.** Traces of every call (prompt, context, output, tokens, latency,\n   cost), golden datasets, offline eval gates in CI, online metrics and feedback capture. Exists\n   *because* quality is statistical: a test case tells you nothing, a dataset tells you a distribution.\n\n### The design moves specific to AI systems\n- **Push determinism out of the model.** Anything that can be code (lookups, arithmetic, routing on\n  known fields, formatting, validation) should be code. Code is cheaper, faster, testable and does not\n  hallucinate; the model is reserved for language understanding and generation.\n- **Treat context assembly as the primary data flow.** Answer quality is bounded by what is in the\n  context window. The architecture decides what is retrieved, in what order, under what token budget,\n  and with which permissions; this is where most quality bugs originate.\n- **Budgets as non-functional requirements.** A latency budget (say p95 ≤ 3 s, split across retrieval,\n  prefill and decode), a token budget per request, and a cost per successful task. These numbers drive\n  model choice, whether to stream, and whether to cache.\n- **Typed output contracts.** Structured outputs validated against a schema so downstream code has a\n  real boundary and a parse failure is an error you can count, not a silent corruption.\n- **Deliberate placement of humans.** Where a wrong output is expensive, add review or approval. The\n  architecture decides where that risk boundary sits, not the prompt.\n- **A cheap feedback loop.** Traces → labelled failures → golden set → eval → change in prompt, model\n  or retrieval → release gate. If this loop takes a week, the system will not improve.\n\n### The two budgets as formulas\nLatency and cost are the two NFRs that the model call dominates, and both decompose into terms the\narchitecture controls:\n\n```\np95_latency  ≈ t_auth + t_retrieval + t_prefill(input_tokens) + output_tokens / decode_rate\n               + t_validation + P(retry) × t_retry\ncost_per_successful_task = Σ over calls (in_tokens × price_in + out_tokens × price_out)\n                           ÷ fraction of tasks that succeed\n```\n\nThe latency formula shows why output length, not model choice, is usually the biggest lever: decode\ntime is linear in tokens generated, so a cap on answer length or streaming (which moves the perceived\ncost to time-to-first-token) beats shaving milliseconds off retrieval. The cost formula has two traps\nit makes visible: input tokens are multiplied by every call in an agent loop or retry, and the\ndenominator means a cheaper model with a lower success rate can cost *more* per useful outcome once\nretries, escalations and human rework are counted.\n\n### Solution architecture versus AI architecture\nThe *solution* half is enterprise integration: single sign-on, the systems of record the context comes\nfrom, deployment constraints such as VPC or data residency, compliance evidence, operations and support\nboundaries. The *AI* half is the model-facing decisions: retrieval versus fine-tuning, model tiers,\nagent versus workflow, guardrails. A working architecture needs both, and the FDE usually lives exactly\nat their seam.\n\n### Artefacts a reviewer expects\nC4 diagrams for structure; ADRs for the decisions that are hard to reverse (model family, RAG versus\nfine-tune, agentic versus workflow); an NFR table with the budgets; a data-flow diagram showing every\npath by which data reaches a model and where it is stored; a threat model covering prompt injection and\ncross-tenant leakage; and a cost model tied to expected traffic.","example":"A claims-handling assistant for an insurer (all figures illustrative). Requirements: answer adjusters'\nquestions over policy documents and the claim file, draft customer letters, never expose another\ncustomer's data, p95 under 4 s, 2,000 adjusters at about 20 requests each per day, so roughly 40k\nrequests per day.\n\nRequest path: SSO token → orchestration service → ACL-aware retrieval scoped to the claim and the\nadjuster's entitlements, top 8 chunks ≈ 2,500 tokens → prompt rendered from a 600-token system prompt\nplus context plus a 100-token question ≈ 3,200 input tokens → gateway routes Q&A to a mid-tier model and\nletter drafting to a frontier model → output parsed into `{answer, citations[]}` → citation checker\nverifies every citation points at a retrieved chunk → output guardrail scans for identifiers belonging\nto other claims → streamed to the UI → trace written with tokens, latency and cost.\n\nLatency budget: retrieval 300 ms, prefill ≈ 500 ms, decode of a 300-token answer at ≈ 50 tokens/s is\n6 s, which blows the 4 s target. Two architectural responses: stream, so the user perceives time to\nfirst token (≈ 1 s), and cap Q&A answers at 200 tokens with a \"show more\" follow-up. Cost: 40k × 3.2k\n≈ 128M input tokens per day, plus 40k × 200 ≈ 8M output tokens. That volume is what justifies the\nmid-tier model for Q&A: at an illustrative 10× price ratio between tiers, sending Q&A to the frontier\nmodel multiplies the daily bill by ten for answers the eval shows are no better. The breakdown of the\n3,200 input tokens also tells you where to optimise. Caching the fixed 600-token prefix touches only\nabout 19% of input; the retrieved context is 2,500 tokens, about 78%, so cutting top-k from 8 to 5\nchunks (≈ 940 fewer tokens, about 29% of input) is the larger saving, *if* the eval shows citation\nrecall does not drop. Evaluation: 150 real adjuster questions with reviewed answers,\nfaithfulness measured by citation match, run on every prompt or model change and weekly in production.","tradeoffs":["Layering gives testability and swappable models, but each hop adds latency and another service to operate; a single internal tool with one model is better served by a modular monolith that keeps the same boundaries as modules rather than services.","Logic in code instead of prompts gives determinism and tests, but iterates more slowly; while the problem is still being discovered a prompt-heavy prototype is the right call, and the architecture hardens as behaviour stabilises.","Guardrails and approval gates reduce risk but add latency and user friction; the flip point is the cost of a wrong action, so low-stakes drafting needs far fewer gates than money movement.","Full tracing of prompts and outputs is what makes evaluation possible, but it is also a store of sensitive data; retention, redaction and access policies have to be designed with it, not after. The flip is data class: for regulated data, keep redacted traces by default and a small sampled full-fidelity set under tighter access, accepting slower debugging for a smaller breach surface."],"pitfalls":["Prompt-as-architecture: a 4,000-token system prompt holding business rules, data and formatting. Symptom: every edit breaks something else and nobody can say what the system does.","No evaluation set before launch. Symptom: whether a new model or prompt is better is argued from anecdotes, and regressions are discovered by users.","Trusting model output: feeding it to SQL, a shell, or an email-send without validation. Symptom: a retrieved document containing an instruction causes an unintended action.","Designing for average latency. Symptom: the demo is snappy; production p95 is 15 s because of long contexts, retries and queueing on the provider.","Forgetting the model is rented: no version pinning, no fallback. Symptom: a provider deprecation or regional outage becomes a product incident with no runbook.","No cost model. Symptom: usage grows, the bill grows faster, and the feature is throttled or quietly removed."],"when":"This is less a pattern you choose than the way to think about any system with a model in it, applied\nat the right weight. For a prototype, sketch the layers and implement them as modules in one process.\nWhen moving to production, add in this order: tracing, an evaluation set and gate, a gateway with\npinned versions and fallback, retrieval with access control, output contracts, then guardrails sized to\nthe risk. Measure p95 latency per layer, cost per successful task, eval score, and failure rate by\ncategory. The first architecture question is always whether a model is needed at all; if a rule or a\nlookup solves it, that is the better architecture. In interviews, \"design a document assistant for X\"\nexpects the layers above, explicit budgets, the eval loop and the trust boundary around model output.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the orchestration layer: context assembly, schema-validated outputs, tracing, and a single model interface so the model can be swapped without touching business logic.","architect":"Owns the layer boundaries, the NFR budgets for latency, cost and quality, the RAG-versus-fine-tune and agent-versus-workflow decisions as ADRs, and the evaluation gate.","fde":"Maps the reference shape onto the customer's identity provider, data sources and deployment constraints, and explains to stakeholders why the model call is the smallest box on the diagram."},"check":{"q":"A team has a working prototype: a web form sends the user's question plus a pasted document to\na frontier model and shows the answer. They want to ship it to 5,000 enterprise users next quarter.\nWhat do you change in the architecture, and in what order, and why that order?","a":"First move the model call behind an orchestration service and a gateway so the UI holds no\nprompts or keys, pin the model version and add a fallback; that is the cheapest change and removes the\nsingle biggest operational risk. Second, build the evaluation loop before touching quality: capture\ntraces, collect one to two hundred real questions with reviewed answers, define the bar. Without it\nevery later change is a guess. Third, replace pasted documents with ingestion and ACL-aware retrieval,\nwhich bounds context size, enforces permissions and enables caching, fixing cost and the data-leakage\nexposure at once. Fourth, add typed outputs with citations and input and output guardrails, with human\nreview wherever an answer drives a decision. Then set budgets, p95 with streaming and cost per task,\nand choose the cheapest model that clears the eval bar. Record each decision as an ADR."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does an AI solution architecture keep the orchestration layer separate from the model layer?","options":["Because models cannot execute code, so tools must run in a different process","Because models change on the provider's schedule and the deterministic logic must stay testable and model-independent","Because provider APIs do not allow direct calls from application servers","Because orchestration frameworks require their own deployment unit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The boundary exists so that pinning, fallback, routing and cost policy live in one place while the application logic can be unit-tested against a stable interface. Tool execution location and framework packaging are implementation details, not the reason for the boundary."},{"q":"A team keeps adding business rules to the system prompt because it is faster than writing code. What breaks first?","options":["Token cost rises, and that is the only consequence","The model stops reading instructions past a fixed length","Behaviour becomes untestable configuration: rule changes interact unpredictably and regressions cannot be caught","Provider rate limits are exceeded by the longer prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rules in a prompt are applied probabilistically and cannot be unit-tested, so each edit can silently change unrelated behaviour. Cost does rise, but the architectural failure is losing the ability to reason about and verify what the system does."},{"q":"Which component most determines whether an AI system improves after launch?","options":["A larger model behind the same prompt","A vector database with more documents","Tracing plus a golden evaluation set wired into the release process","A streaming interface that lowers perceived latency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Improvement requires seeing failures, turning them into labelled examples, and measuring each change against them before release. A bigger model or more data may raise quality once, but without the loop nobody can tell whether the next change helped or hurt."}]},{"key":"system-design/reference-architectures","sources":["Lewis et al., 2020 — Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents"],"why":"Every team building its first document assistant draws roughly the same diagram, and leaves out roughly\nthe same boxes: evaluation, tracing, access control on retrieval, a gateway with version pinning, output\nvalidation. They find out which boxes were missing in production, one incident at a time. The second\nteam in the same company does it again, with different names for the same components, so the two\nsystems cannot share a review checklist, a monitoring dashboard or an engineer.\n\nA reference architecture solves three problems at once. It is a **memory**: the accumulated knowledge of\nwhich components a given shape needs and why, including the ones that are invisible in a demo. It is a\n**vocabulary**: when a reviewer says \"where is your control layer\" everyone knows what is meant and can\npoint at the diagram. And it is a **baseline for deviation**: a team that omits the reranker or the\napproval gate has to say why, which is exactly the conversation an architecture review should be.\n\nThe naive alternative is to let each team design from first principles. It is slower, and worse, it\nis inconsistent in the places that matter most for risk, because the omitted parts are precisely the\nones that only hurt later (evals, guardrails, cost metering). The other naive alternative, a single\nmandated architecture for everything, fails because a batch extraction job and an interactive agent\nhave different latency, state and risk profiles; forcing one shape onto both produces a bad version\nof each. Reference architectures sit between these: a small catalogue, each matched to a problem shape.","how":"### What a reference architecture contains\nA useful one is more than a diagram. It states the **problem shape** it fits (interaction pattern,\nlatency class, risk class), the **components** with one-line responsibilities, the **data flows**\nincluding what reaches the model and what is stored, **default NFRs** (typical latency budget, cost\ndriver, availability target), the **variation points** where legitimate choices exist, and the\n**anti-patterns** seen in the wild. It should fit on a page and map directly onto a C4 container\ndiagram; if it needs twenty pages it is a design document, not a reference.\n\n### The recurring shapes in AI systems\n1. **Retrieval-augmented assistant.** Ingestion pipeline → chunk and embed → index with metadata and\n   ACLs → query path: rewrite, retrieve, rerank, assemble context → model → cite and validate → UI.\n   Latency class interactive, risk is wrong or leaked information. Variation points: hybrid versus\n   dense retrieval, reranker or not, long context versus retrieval. The deep treatment is\n   `rag-architecture`; the point here is that every one of these boxes exists for a reason that\n   surfaces only when it is missing.\n2. **Extraction and classification pipeline.** Queue or batch scheduler → document parsing → model with\n   a strict output schema → validation and confidence scoring → human review queue for low-confidence\n   items → system of record. Latency class batch, risk is silent data corruption. Variation points:\n   small fine-tuned model versus general model, sampling rate for review. This shape is where small\n   models and distillation pay off most.\n3. **Tool-using agent or copilot.** Orchestrator running a loop: plan, call tools through typed\n   contracts, observe, repeat, with step budgets, approval gates before consequential actions, and a\n   sandbox for execution. Latency class seconds to minutes, risk is an unintended action. Variation\n   points: single agent versus multi-agent, how much autonomy per action class. Deep treatment in\n   `agentic-architecture`; the Anthropic guidance on building effective agents is the standard\n   argument for starting with the simplest shape that works.\n4. **Multi-step workflow.** A fixed DAG where some nodes are model calls and others are code; each node\n   has a contract and a test. Latency class depends on the longest path, risk is low because the\n   control flow is deterministic. This is the default when the steps are known in advance;\n   `workflow-orchestration` covers the engine choices. The agent shape is justified only when the\n   steps cannot be enumerated up front.\n5. **Conversational assistant with memory.** The RAG assistant plus session state, memory retrieval\n   and update policies, context compaction for long sessions, and streaming. The new risk is memory\n   poisoning and stale personalisation; see `memory-architecture`.\n6. **Platform layer.** A gateway in front of every model: authentication, routing, version pinning,\n   rate limits, caching, cost attribution, tracing, central guardrails. It is the shape that makes the\n   other five governable at company scale; see `ai-gateway`.\n\n### Cross-cutting components every shape shares\nEvaluation harness and golden set, tracing with cost per call, a prompt and model registry, and a\ncontrol layer. They are drawn once in each reference so that no team can claim they were optional.\n\n### How to apply one\nMatch the problem to a shape by asking a short sequence of questions, in this order *because* each one\nremoves shapes the later ones would otherwise have to rule out:\n\n1. **Does the system act, or only inform?** Actions (writes to a system of record, money, messages\n   sent) put an approval gate and a validation contract on the path regardless of shape.\n2. **Can the steps be enumerated in advance?** Yes → workflow (shape 4), possibly with extraction or\n   retrieval nodes. No → agent (shape 3), with step budgets.\n3. **Is the input a stream of items or a person waiting?** Items → batch extraction (shape 2) with a\n   review queue. A person → interactive, so a latency budget and streaming.\n4. **Must answers be grounded in a corpus the model has not seen?** Yes → retrieval (shape 1).\n5. **Does state persist across sessions?** Yes → add memory (shape 5).\n6. **Is more than one team or product calling models?** Yes → the platform layer (shape 6) belongs\n   underneath all of them.\n\nReal systems are compositions: most production assistants are a workflow whose nodes include a\nretrieval step and an extraction step, behind the platform layer. Cloud providers and vendors also\npublish reference architectures; they are useful inputs, but they tend to put the vendor's product in\nevery box, so extract the shape and keep the component names abstract. Start from the blueprint, then record each deviation as an\nADR with the reason: \"no reranker because the corpus is 400 documents and recall is already above the\nbar\" is a fine decision; silence is not. Use the component list as the review checklist and the\nvariation points as the agenda for the design discussion. Revisit the catalogue when a system fails in\na way the blueprint did not anticipate, and fold the lesson back in; a reference architecture that is\nnever updated becomes folklore.","example":"A logistics company asks for \"AI to handle inbound supplier emails\". Discovery shows two distinct\njobs: (a) classify each email (invoice, delay notice, dispute, other) and extract fields such as PO\nnumber and new delivery date into the ERP; (b) let a coordinator ask questions like \"which suppliers\nhave slipped twice this quarter\" over the resulting data plus contracts.\n\nJob (a) is shape 2, the extraction pipeline. Components: mail connector → queue → parser for PDF\nattachments → model call with a JSON schema of about ten fields → validation (PO number must exist in\nthe ERP, dates must be in the future) → confidence from a second, independent check (for example the\nmodel's PO number must also match a regex pass over the raw text, and the supplier on the PO must\nmatch the sender's domain) → anything that fails a check or disagrees goes to a review queue; everything else writes to the ERP with provenance. Default NFRs:\nprocessing within 15 minutes, zero silent writes of invalid data, cost dominated by attachment pages.\nVariation point taken: start with a general model, route *every* item to review for the first weeks,\nand lower the review rate only as the measured precision on reviewed items stays above the bar; the\nreview queue is simultaneously the safety net and the labelling pipeline. Evaluate a small fine-tuned\nmodel once a few thousand reviewed examples exist.\n\nJob (b) is shape 1 plus a structured-data tool, not an agent: the questions are answerable by a\nretrieval step over contracts and a parameterised SQL query over the extracted table, so a two-node\nworkflow (route, then answer with citations) is enough. The deviation recorded in the ADR is \"no\nfree-form SQL generation; queries are templates with parameters\" because the risk class is read-only\ninformation, but the coordinators are not able to check generated SQL. Both jobs sit behind the same\ngateway, share the tracing stack, and each has its own golden set: 300 labelled emails for (a), 80\nquestion-answer pairs for (b).","tradeoffs":["A shared blueprint speeds design and review, but it biases teams toward the known shape; a genuinely new problem may need a new reference, and forcing it into an old one hides the novelty. The signal that the catalogue needs a new entry is the same unblessed deviation recurring across teams.","Rich references with many mandatory components raise the quality floor, but they also raise the cost of a prototype; keep a lighter \"exploration\" variant and insist on the full shape only at the production gate.","Standardising on the platform gateway shape centralises governance and cost control, but it creates a shared dependency whose outage or latency affects every product; it flips in favour of per-team gateways only when teams have truly independent risk and release cadences.","Catalogues that are owned centrally stay consistent but go stale; ones owned by whoever last had an incident stay current but drift apart. A small owning group with a lightweight change process is the usual compromise."],"pitfalls":["Picking the agent shape for a problem whose steps are known in advance. Symptom: unpredictable latency and cost, and debugging sessions that end in \"the model decided to\".","Copying the diagram and dropping the cross-cutting boxes because \"we will add evals later\". Symptom: the first model upgrade is a coin flip and nobody can say whether quality moved.","Treating the reference as mandatory in every detail. Symptom: a 400-document corpus gets a reranker, a query rewriter and a multi-hop planner, and the simplest question takes eight seconds.","Letting the reference describe a vendor's product rather than a shape. Symptom: switching a component requires rewriting the architecture document, and the exit cost is discovered at renewal.","Never recording deviations. Symptom: six months later nobody knows whether the missing approval gate was a decision or an oversight."],"when":"Reach for the catalogue at the start of any design, during an architecture review, and when onboarding\nan engineer to an existing system; the first question is always \"which shape is this, and where does\nit deviate\". Do not use a reference as a substitute for discovery: the shape follows from the\ninteraction pattern and risk class, which only requirements work can establish. Measure the catalogue\nby how often reviews find missing cross-cutting components (should trend to zero) and how long design\ntakes for a standard shape. In interviews, naming the shape, its components and its default risks in\nthe first two minutes of a system-design question is what separates practised candidates from those\nwho start by drawing a box labelled \"LLM\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses the blueprint as a build checklist and knows which components can be stubbed in a prototype and which must exist before anything is user-facing.","architect":"Owns the catalogue, decides which shape a new problem belongs to, and insists that every deviation from the blueprint is written down as an ADR with its reasoning.","fde":"Carries the blueprints into customer engagements to scope quickly, and adapts them to the customer's identity, data and deployment constraints without dropping the governance boxes."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a system that reads incoming support tickets, looks up the customer's account,\ndecides whether to issue a refund under policy, and replies. Which reference shape or shapes apply, and\nwhere would you place the human?","a":"This is a workflow, not an agent: the steps are known in advance, so a fixed DAG with contracts\nper node is the right shape. Classify the ticket and extract the claim (extraction shape with a strict\nschema and validation), look up the account through a typed tool, evaluate the policy in code rather\nthan in the model because refund rules are deterministic and auditable, draft the reply with a model\ngrounded on the policy text and the account facts, and send. The human belongs at the action boundary:\nthe refund itself is a consequential, irreversible action, so it goes through an approval gate at\nleast until the system's precision on a golden set justifies auto-approval below a monetary threshold.\nReplies that do not move money can be sent after output validation. Both halves share the gateway,\ntracing and an evaluation set built from historical tickets with their resolutions."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes a reference architecture from a design document for one system?","options":["A reference architecture names specific vendors for each component","A reference architecture describes a reusable shape with variation points and known failure modes, independent of any one system","A reference architecture is produced after the system ships, as documentation","A reference architecture only covers the model and prompt, not the infrastructure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A reference is a blueprint for a class of problems: components, responsibilities, defaults and anti-patterns, with explicit places where choices are allowed. A design document instantiates one for a particular system and records its deviations."},{"q":"A team proposes an autonomous agent for a process whose five steps are the same for every input. What is the architectural objection?","options":["Agents cannot call more than one tool per step","Agents are only appropriate for batch workloads","A fixed workflow gives the same result with deterministic control flow, predictable cost and testable nodes, so the agent's flexibility buys nothing and costs reliability","Agents require fine-tuned models, which the team does not have"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The agent shape is justified when the steps cannot be enumerated up front. When they can, a DAG of contracted nodes is cheaper to run, easier to test and easier to debug; choosing the agent adds variance without adding capability."},{"q":"Which components appear in every AI reference architecture regardless of shape?","options":["A vector database and a reranker","A planning loop and a sandbox","An evaluation harness with a golden set, tracing with cost attribution, and a control layer for validation and policy","A fine-tuning pipeline and a distillation step"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retrieval, planning and fine-tuning are shape-specific. Evaluation, tracing and output control are cross-cutting because every shape has probabilistic output whose quality must be measured and whose downstream effects must be bounded."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-selection","sources":["Chiang et al., 2024 — Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference","Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"At any moment there are dozens of plausible models, spanning one to two orders of magnitude in price\nper token and several-fold in latency, and whichever one you pick is multiplied by every request for\nthe life of the feature. The decision therefore deserves a method, and the obvious method is wrong.\n\n\"Pick the top of the leaderboard\" fails for four reasons. Public benchmarks measure broad ability on\npublic data; your task is a narrow distribution with its own format, domain vocabulary and failure\ncosts, and the benchmark questions may have leaked into training data. The quality gap between the top\ntwo or three models on a narrow task is frequently smaller than their cost gap, so the ranking is\nunstable exactly where the money is. Non-quality dimensions often decide feasibility outright: context\nlength at your p95 prompt size, reliable structured output, tool-calling behaviour, language coverage,\nrate limits at your peak, data residency, and the provider's deprecation cadence. And your prompt was\ntuned on the model you started with, so a naive swap systematically underrates every other candidate.\n\n\"Pick the cheapest and hope\" fails on the tail: the errors a weaker model makes concentrate in the\nhard, high-stakes cases, which are precisely the ones that generate support tickets or compliance\nfindings.\n\nThe reasoning that resolves this: quality has a threshold value. Below the bar the feature does not\nwork; above it, extra points rarely change the business outcome. Cost and latency, by contrast, are\nlinear in volume. So the optimum is not the best model but the cheapest model that clears the bar with\na margin, and the only way to know which one that is to measure your task.","how":"### Step 1: apply hard constraints first\nFilter before you evaluate quality, because no quality number rescues a model that cannot run where\nthe data is. The usual gates: modality (text, vision, audio); context window versus your *measured*\np95 prompt size, not the mean; native structured output or JSON-schema support; tool calling;\nlanguages; deployment constraints (a region, a VPC, on-prem or air-gapped, which narrows the field to\nopen-weight models or private endpoints); licence terms; quota and rate limits at launch peak; the\nprovider's version pinning and deprecation policy; data-use and retention terms.\n\n### Step 2: build the task evaluation before touching candidates\nFifty to a few hundred examples drawn from real or realistic traffic, with expected outputs or a\nscoring rubric, and a metric that is the task's own: exact or normalised match for extraction and\nclassification, citation faithfulness for retrieval-augmented answers, a calibrated LLM-as-judge rubric\nfor free-form generation, pass@k for code. Build it first *because* otherwise the prompt gets tuned to\nthe first model tried and every later comparison is biased toward the incumbent. The set should\nover-sample the hard and high-cost cases, since that is where candidates differ.\n\n### Step 3: run candidates fairly\nSame examples, same prompt structure, then a bounded, equal amount of prompt adaptation per model,\nbecause format sensitivity differs between families and zero adaptation punishes the newcomer. Fix\nsampling settings, run several seeds, and report a mean with a confidence interval: on 100 examples a\nproportion near 0.9 has a 95% half-width of roughly ±6 points (1.96 × √(0.9 × 0.1 / 100) ≈ 0.059), so a\n2-point gap is noise. Two consequences follow from that formula. Repeated runs only average out\nsampling randomness; they do not shrink the error from having drawn *these* 100 examples, which only\nmore examples can do, and halving the interval needs four times as many (about 860 examples for ±2\npoints at 0.9). And because every candidate runs on the same examples, compare them **paired**: count\nthe examples only model A gets right and only model B gets right (McNemar's test uses exactly these\ndiscordant counts). Pairing cancels the difficulty of each example, so it detects real differences\nthat two overlapping intervals hide, and it tells you *how* the models differ: few discordant examples\nmeans they behave alike; many in both directions means they fail on different inputs, which argues for\na cascade rather than a straight swap. Keep the set private; a model that has seen your examples is not\nbeing evaluated.\n\n### Step 4: measure cost and latency on real request shapes\nCost per request is input tokens × input price plus output tokens × output price, adjusted for cached\nprefixes. Measure the tokens from the eval runs rather than estimating, *because* tokenizers differ\nacross families (the same document can be 20–30% more tokens in one than another, and more for\nnon-English text) and because output verbosity differs. For reasoning models the hidden reasoning\ntokens are billed as output on most providers and can dominate, so price per token alone misleads. Measure time to\nfirst token and total latency at p50 and p95, under realistic concurrency, at the hours you will run.\nThe number to compare is not cost per call but\n\n```\ncost per successful task = (cost of all calls, including retries after format failures and escalations)\n                           ÷ (tasks that pass the eval)\n```\n\n*because* a model that is 40% cheaper per call but needs a retry on one call in five, or escalates\noften, can lose its advantage once the denominator and the extra calls are counted.\n\n### Step 5: find the frontier and apply the rule\nLay candidates out on quality versus cost versus latency and discard any model that another beats on\nall three. Among the rest, choose the cheapest whose *lower* confidence bound clears the bar and whose\np95 fits the latency budget. If the cheap model fails only on an identifiable tail, consider a cascade:\ncheap model by default with escalation to a stronger one on low confidence or a trigger condition,\nwhich is often cheaper than running the strong model everywhere.\n\n### Step 6: operational factors and re-evaluation\nPin an exact model version, never a floating alias, so quality cannot change under you. Check for a\nsecond source: a fallback model that also clears the bar, even at higher cost, turns an outage or a\ndeprecation notice into a configuration change. Then schedule re-selection: when a deprecation is\nannounced, when a new model could move the frontier, or on a fixed cadence. The eval set is what\nmakes each re-run cheap; this is the compounding payoff of having built it first.","example":"A small harness that applies the funnel's quantitative steps. Each candidate exposes a `call` that\nreturns the text plus the measured token counts; prices are placeholders you fill from the provider's\ncurrent sheet. The selection rule requires the lower confidence bound, not the mean, to clear the bar.\n\n```python\nimport statistics, time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Callable\n\n@dataclass\nclass Candidate:\n    name: str\n    price_in: float          # currency per 1M input tokens (fill from current price list)\n    price_out: float         # currency per 1M output tokens\n    call: Callable           # prompt -> (text, input_tokens, output_tokens)\n\ndef evaluate(c: Candidate, golden, score, runs=3):\n    per_example, lat, cost = [], [], []\n    for ex in golden:\n        s = []\n        for _ in range(runs):\n            t0 = time.perf_counter()\n            text, n_in, n_out = c.call(ex[\"prompt\"])\n            lat.append(time.perf_counter() - t0)\n            s.append(score(text, ex[\"expected\"]))\n            cost.append((n_in * c.price_in + n_out * c.price_out) / 1e6)\n        per_example.append(statistics.mean(s))   # repeats of one example are not independent samples\n    n = len(per_example)                          # the CI is over examples, not over example x run\n    mean = statistics.mean(per_example)\n    half_width = 1.96 * statistics.pstdev(per_example) / n ** 0.5   # rough 95% CI\n    p95 = sorted(lat)[int(0.95 * (len(lat) - 1))]\n    return {\"model\": c.name, \"quality\": mean, \"lower\": mean - half_width,\n            \"p95_s\": p95, \"cost_per_1k\": 1000 * statistics.mean(cost)}\n\ndef select(results, bar, max_p95):\n    eligible = [r for r in results if r[\"lower\"] >= bar and r[\"p95_s\"] <= max_p95]\n    return min(eligible, key=lambda r: r[\"cost_per_1k\"]) if eligible else None\n\n# results = [evaluate(c, golden_set, exact_match) for c in candidates]\n# print(select(results, bar=0.92, max_p95=2.5))\n```\n\nIf `select` returns `None`, no candidate clears the bar within budget; the next moves are prompt work,\nretrieval improvements, a cascade, or fine-tuning a smaller model, in roughly that order of cost.","tradeoffs":["\"Cheapest that clears the bar\" minimises spend but leaves little headroom when the traffic distribution drifts harder; if your task changes often, buy margin with a stronger model or a cascade.","A private eval set gives a trustworthy comparison but costs expert time and goes stale; a small set has wide confidence intervals, so differences of a few points cannot be acted on.","Adapting the prompt per candidate makes the comparison fair but multiplies the effort; skipping it is cheaper and systematically favours the incumbent model.","Standardising on one provider's best model simplifies operations but concentrates deprecation and outage risk; a second source doubles evaluation work and halves the blast radius.","Reasoning-heavy models raise quality on genuinely hard tasks but bring high output-token cost and latency variance; the choice flips as soon as the task is not reasoning-bound."],"pitfalls":["Choosing on public leaderboard rank. Symptom: the \"better\" model scores lower on your eval, or wins it and then fails on JSON formatting or tool calls in production.","Estimating cost from list price without measuring tokens. Symptom: the bill differs from the forecast by a large fraction because of tokenizer differences or verbose outputs.","Comparing single runs and treating a 2-point gap as real. Symptom: the ranking flips when someone re-runs the comparison the following week.","Deploying a floating \"latest\" alias. Symptom: quality or format changes overnight with no deploy and no entry in the change log.","Ignoring quotas and rate limits at peak. Symptom: 429 errors at launch, and the fallback path has never been exercised.","Evaluating in English only for a multilingual product. Symptom: context overflows and higher cost for languages whose tokenization is two to three times less efficient."],"when":"Run the funnel whenever a new task is introduced, when a deprecation notice arrives, when a model\nrelease plausibly moves the quality-cost frontier, or when the latency or cost target changes. Do not\nre-select on every release announcement; the eval harness makes a scheduled re-run cheap, so put it on\na cadence instead. Track eval score with its interval, cost per successful task, p95 latency, and\nfailure rates by category (format, refusal, timeout). Day to day this shows up as \"can we move this\nto the cheaper model\", and the honest answer is always \"the eval says\". In interviews, articulating\nthe threshold-versus-linear reasoning, the hard-constraint filter, and \"I would build the eval set\nfirst\" is the expected depth; naming a favourite model is not.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the eval harness, measures real token counts and latency percentiles, and keeps the model behind one interface so the next re-selection is a configuration change.","architect":"Sets the quality bar and the latency and cost budgets per task, decides on second sourcing and cascades, and records the selection and its evidence as an ADR.","fde":"Runs the funnel on the customer's own data under their deployment constraints, and shows stakeholders the quality-cost table rather than a vendor's benchmark slide."},"check":{"q":"You are asked to replace an expensive frontier model with one at a third of the price for a\nretrieval-augmented assistant. On your 100-example eval the cheaper model scores 2 points lower. How do\nyou decide, and what would make you say no?","a":"First, 2 points on 100 examples is inside the noise: the 95% interval around a score near 0.9 is\nroughly ±6 points, so the gap is not established, and re-running seeds will not fix that, only more\nexamples will. Expand the set toward several hundred and compare each model's lower bound against the\nbar, not against each other. Because both ran on the same examples, look at the paired view: if the\n2-point gap is 3 losses against 1 win, the models behave almost identically and I read those few\nexamples; if it is 14 losses against 12 wins, they fail on different inputs, which means the score is\nunstable and a cascade may beat either model alone. Second, read the failures by category: if the cheaper model's extra misses cluster in a high-cost class such as\nwrong citations on compliance questions, that is disqualifying even if the total is small; if they\nare stylistic, it is not. Third, check the dimensions the score ignores: format compliance, p95\nlatency, rate limits at peak, and the real cost ratio measured from tokens, since tokenizers differ.\nFourth, consider a cascade, cheap by default with escalation on low confidence, which may capture most\nof the saving while covering the tail. I would say no if the lower bound misses the bar or the misses\nland in the costly category."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"pick the model at the top of a public leaderboard\" a poor selection rule?","options":["Leaderboards are updated too infrequently to be useful","Benchmarks measure a different distribution from your traffic and may be contaminated, while your cost and latency are exact, so the rank says little about which model clears your bar most cheaply","Top-ranked models are always rate-limited more aggressively","Leaderboard models do not support structured output"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leaderboards estimate broad ability on public data. Your task is narrow, has its own failure costs, and must fit a budget; the question is which eligible model clears your measured bar at the lowest cost, which only a task-specific evaluation answers."},{"q":"Two candidate models differ by 3 points on a 60-example evaluation set. What is the right conclusion?","options":["The higher-scoring model is better and should be chosen","The cheaper model should be chosen because 3 points never matters","The difference is probably within noise; enlarge the set, inspect the paired per-example disagreements, and compare lower confidence bounds against the quality bar before deciding","Both models should be rejected for a larger model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With 60 examples the 95% interval is roughly ±7 to ±8 points near 0.9, so a 3-point gap (about two examples) cannot be distinguished from sampling noise. More examples tighten it; repeated runs do not. The decision should be made against the bar with margin, not by comparing two noisy means."},{"q":"Which of these must be applied as a hard filter before any quality comparison?","options":["Price per million output tokens","Position on a human-preference leaderboard","Whether the model can be run in the region or environment where the data is allowed to be","How recently the model was released"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deployment and residency constraints are binary: a model that cannot legally or technically process the data is not a candidate regardless of quality or price. Price and recency are inputs to the later optimisation step, not gates."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-portfolio","sources":["Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"Two failure modes bracket the problem. The first is **one model for everything**: the frontier model\nclassifies support intents at frontier prices and frontier latency, cannot be deployed in the region a\ncustomer requires, and when its provider has an outage or announces a deprecation, every product is\naffected at once. The second is **every team picks its own**: eighteen months later there are a dozen\nmodels across half a dozen providers, a dozen sets of prompt lore, no shared evaluation, a separate\nsecurity review and data-processing agreement per provider, incompatible data-use terms, no record of\nwhich model is allowed to see personal data, and when a provider retires a version nobody knows which\nsystems depend on it.\n\nThe reasoning that resolves this is a cost curve. Every model in the set carries a **fixed** cost\nregardless of how much traffic it serves: an eval baseline per task family, a security and privacy\nreview, prompt expertise, monitoring, quota management, and a deprecation watch. The **marginal**\nbenefit of adding a model diminishes fast once the main tiers are covered, because the tasks that\nremain are already served well enough by something on the list. So the optimum is small but greater\nthan one: enough variety to match task difficulty to price and to satisfy deployment constraints, few\nenough that the task-by-model evaluation matrix is actually run. Engineering organisations already do\nthis with approved languages and databases; the portfolio applies the same discipline to the most\nexpensive dependency in the system.","how":"### Roles, and why each exists\n- **Frontier or reasoning tier.** The hardest work: long-horizon agentic flows, complex code, low-volume\n  high-value analysis. Slow and expensive, so it is used by exception, typically behind a router or an\n  explicit task assignment.\n- **Workhorse tier.** The mid-size model that handles most retrieval-augmented answers, drafting and\n  summarisation. Chosen by the model-selection funnel as the cheapest model that clears the bar for\n  the typical task families.\n- **Small and fast tier.** Classification, routing, schema-bound extraction, PII detection and\n  guardrail checks. Often fine-tuned, sometimes self-hosted, expected to answer in well under a second.\n  This tier exists *because* most traffic is easy and the price gap between tiers is roughly an order\n  of magnitude per step (illustrative, provider-dependent).\n- **Embeddings and rerankers.** The retrieval stack. Versioned with extra care *because* changing an\n  embedding model invalidates every stored vector; vectors from two models are not comparable.\n- **Specialised models.** Vision and document understanding, speech-to-text, text-to-speech, code.\n- **Fine-tuned variants.** Derived from a base for a specific high-volume task; owned by the team that\n  trained them but registered centrally so their lifecycle is visible.\n\n### The registry entry\nEach model has a record: exact pinned version, provider and deployment location, data-use and\nretention terms, approved data classifications (may it see customer PII, source code, regulated\ndata?), eval baselines per task family that uses it, a cost card (list price plus measured average\ntokens per request type), quota and rate limits, owner, announced deprecation date, and its designated\nfallback. The record is what turns \"we use several models\" into something an auditor, a finance team\nand an on-call engineer can each read.\n\n### Sizing the set: when a tier earns its place\nThe fixed-versus-marginal argument becomes a rule you can compute. Moving a task family from model A\nto a cheaper model B is worth a new portfolio entry when\n\n```\nrequests per month × (cost per request on A − cost per request on B)\n  − added errors per month × cost per error   >   fixed monthly cost of running B as an entry\n```\n\nwhere the fixed cost is the eval runs, the security and contract review amortised, prompt maintenance,\nmonitoring and on-call attention. The rule explains both failure modes at once: a small, high-volume\ntask family clears it easily (the classification move in the example below saves about 2,300 units a\nday), while a ninth model added for one team's low-volume task does not, even if it is slightly better.\nIt also says *when* to re-run the question: when volume grows, when prices shift, or when an incumbent\ngets cheaper.\n\n### Assignment versus routing\nMost tasks are **assigned** statically: the configuration says this task family uses `workhorse`, and\nit is changed only after an eval. **Routing** chooses a tier per request at run time (by difficulty,\nconfidence or input length) and needs its own evaluation of the router. Start with assignment *because*\nit is cheap and auditable; add routing only for families where tracing shows a clear mix of easy and\nhard requests, which is exactly what the agentic slice in the example turns out to be.\n\n### Entry and exit rules\nA model enters only if it beats an incumbent on a shared eval for some task family, or unlocks a\nconstraint the set cannot meet (a new modality, a cheaper tier, a residency requirement). This rule\nis what keeps the set from regrowing. A model exits through a planned migration: deprecation notice →\nlist of consuming tasks from gateway logs → regression evals on each → cutover behind the gateway →\nremoval. Because provider deprecations are routine, the process has to be routine too.\n\n### Enforcement through indirection\nApplications reference **logical names** such as `workhorse`, `classifier-small` or `embed-v3`, and\nthe gateway maps those to the pinned vendor model. Only registered models are routable. This\nindirection is the mechanism that makes fallback, rotation and vendor exit possible *because* the\ndependency lives in one configuration table rather than in a dozen code bases. It does not make\nprompts magically portable; a rotation still runs regression evals on every consuming task.\n\n### Diversity and concentration\nCritical tiers should have a second source on a different provider, for outage resilience and for\nnegotiating leverage. Every additional provider, though, is another security review and contract, so\ndiversity is bought per tier, not blanket. Two concentration metrics tell you whether the portfolio is\nworking: share of spend per provider, and share of requests landing on the frontier tier. If the\nfrontier handles a large fraction of volume, routing or task assignment is wrong.","example":"A worked scenario with **illustrative price ratios** (frontier 10 units per million input tokens and 30\nper million output; workhorse 1 and 3; small 0.1 and 0.3; real prices vary by provider and date).\nDaily traffic is 1,000,000 requests: 70% intent classification (300 input, 10 output tokens), 25% RAG\nanswers (3,000 in, 250 out), 5% agentic tasks averaging 6 model calls each (20,000 in, 2,000 out per call).\n\n**Everything on the frontier model.** Classification: 700,000 × (300×10 + 10×30)/10⁶ = 2,310 units.\nRAG: 250,000 × (3,000×10 + 250×30)/10⁶ = 9,375. Agentic: 300,000 calls × (20,000×10 + 2,000×30)/10⁶ =\n78,000. Total ≈ 89,700 units per day.\n\n**Tiered portfolio.** Classification on the small tier: 700,000 × (300×0.1 + 10×0.3)/10⁶ ≈ 23 units.\nRAG on the workhorse: 250,000 × (3,000×1 + 250×3)/10⁶ ≈ 938. Agentic still on the frontier: 78,000.\nTotal ≈ 78,960, only a 12% saving, because the agentic 5% of requests is 87% of spend.\n\n**Portfolio plus routing inside the agentic task.** Tracing shows that 4 of the 6 calls per task are\ntool-result summarisation and formatting that the workhorse handles at the same eval score. Frontier:\n100,000 calls × 0.26 = 26,000. Workhorse: 200,000 × (20,000×1 + 2,000×3)/10⁶ = 5,200. Agentic total\n31,200; grand total ≈ 32,160 units, a 64% reduction against the single-model baseline. The lesson is\nthat the portfolio's first job is to make spend legible: the biggest lever was not the obvious 70% of\ntraffic but the step-level routing inside the smallest slice, and prefix caching of the shared 20k\ncontext would cut it further.","tradeoffs":["Tiering cuts spend, but every tier adds evaluation, prompt and monitoring overhead; once a tier's monthly saving falls below that fixed overhead (the sizing rule above), a two-model portfolio, one strong and one small, is the right size.","A central approved list gives governance and purchasing leverage but slows experimentation; the usual fix is a sandbox tier for unregistered models on non-sensitive data, with registration required before anything reaches production.","Multi-provider diversity buys resilience and negotiating power, but each provider is another review, contract and set of behavioural quirks; for low-stakes tasks a single provider with graceful degradation is enough.","Logical model names decouple applications from vendors, but prompts and tool-calling behaviour are not fully portable, so a rotation still costs a regression run per consuming task.","Fine-tuned variants give the best cost-quality on narrow tasks but multiply lifecycle work, since each must be retrained or re-evaluated when its base model is retired."],"pitfalls":["The portfolio exists on paper while applications call vendor model IDs directly. Symptom: a single deprecation notice turns into code changes and releases across a dozen repositories.","The frontier tier carries bulk traffic because \"it works\". Symptom: frontier share of spend far exceeds the share of tasks that need it, and cost grows linearly with adoption.","Rotating the embedding model like any other model. Symptom: retrieval quality collapses overnight because the index was not rebuilt with the new model's vectors.","No per-task eval baselines in the registry. Symptom: a rotation approved on one team's evaluation silently degrades another team's task.","Too many registered models. Symptom: the task-by-model evaluation matrix is never fully run, so the quality of most combinations is unknown.","Skipping data-classification approval per model. Symptom: an audit finds personal data flowing to a model whose terms never permitted it."],"when":"Start a portfolio as soon as more than one task family or more than one team uses models; even a\nsingle product benefits from a small-plus-strong pair with explicit assignment. A lone prototype does\nnot need one. Measure spend share per tier and provider, the fraction of requests on the frontier\ntier, the number of registered models (it should stay roughly flat), time to complete a migration\nafter a deprecation notice, and eval coverage of the task-by-model matrix. In daily work this is the\nquestion \"which model should this new feature use\", answered by the registry rather than by habit. In\ninterviews, \"how would you manage models across an organisation\" is looking for tiers, a registry,\nentry and exit rules, logical names behind a gateway, and the concentration metrics.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Calls models by logical name through the gateway, adds eval baselines for any task that joins the portfolio, and runs the regression suite when a rotation is proposed.","architect":"Owns the tier structure, the entry and exit rules, second-sourcing decisions per tier, and the concentration metrics that show whether routing matches intent.","fde":"Translates the portfolio into the customer's environment, substituting models that meet their residency and data-use constraints while keeping the same logical roles and baselines."},"check":{"q":"After eighteen months your organisation runs eleven models across six providers. Leadership\nwants this reduced without breaking products. How do you rationalise the portfolio, and how do you\nstop it regrowing?","a":"Start with an inventory from gateway or billing logs: which applications call which model, at\nwhat volume and spend. Cluster the calls into task families and assemble or collect an eval set per\nfamily. For each family, run the selection funnel across the models already in use, which reveals the\nminimal set that clears every family's bar, typically a frontier model, a workhorse, a small model,\nan embedding model and one or two specialised ones. Plan migrations family by family with regression\nevals, moving applications onto logical names behind the gateway as you go so the next change is\nconfiguration only. Keep a second provider for the tiers that matter. To prevent regrowth, enforce the\nentry rule, a new model must beat an incumbent on a shared eval or unlock a constraint, give every\nentry an owner and a retirement date, and publish spend share and frontier-tier share monthly."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main reason to keep a model portfolio small rather than registering every useful model?","options":["Providers charge more when an organisation uses many models","Each model carries fixed costs for evaluation, security review, monitoring and deprecation tracking, while the benefit of another model diminishes once the tiers are covered","Gateways can only route to a limited number of back ends","Small portfolios always produce higher quality outputs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cost of a model is not only its tokens; it is the task-by-model evaluations, reviews and lifecycle work it demands. Once a strong, a mid and a small tier exist, an extra model rarely clears a bar that nothing on the list already clears."},{"q":"Why should applications reference logical names such as `workhorse` instead of vendor model identifiers?","options":["Logical names are billed at a lower rate","Providers require logical names in their SDKs","So fallback, rotation and vendor exit become a configuration change in the gateway rather than code changes across every application","Logical names avoid provider rate limits"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The indirection puts the dependency in one table. A deprecation or outage is then handled centrally, with regression evals per consuming task, instead of being discovered repository by repository."},{"q":"Which portfolio rotation requires more than a prompt regression evaluation before cutover?","options":["Replacing the frontier model with a newer version from the same provider","Replacing the embedding model used by the retrieval index","Replacing the small classification model with a fine-tuned variant","Replacing the speech-to-text model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vectors produced by different embedding models live in different spaces, so queries embedded with the new model cannot be compared against documents embedded with the old one. The whole corpus must be re-indexed and retrieval quality re-measured before any switch."}]},{"key":"system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","sources":["Kaplan et al., 2020 — Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models","Hinton et al., 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network"],"why":"The gap between a 7B-class model and a frontier model is not ten percent. It is an order of magnitude\nor more in price per token, a similar factor in weight bytes read per generated token and therefore in\nlatency, and the difference between \"fits in the customer's VPC on one GPU, or on the device\" and\n\"requires an external API\". A choice with that much leverage needs a reasoned basis rather than a\ndefault.\n\nBoth defaults are wrong. \"Always use the biggest model\" overpays on the large fraction of traffic that\nis easy, blows latency budgets for interactive and on-device use, and is simply unavailable wherever\ndata cannot leave the premises. \"Small models are good enough now\" fails on the tail: the hard five\npercent of inputs where errors concentrate, which is also where incidents and compliance findings come\nfrom. Small models follow long, complex instruction sets less reliably, hallucinate more on knowledge\nquestions because fewer facts fit in fewer parameters, and degrade more sharply in long contexts.\n\nWhat makes the decision genuinely hard is that the gap is task-dependent. Pattern-following tasks\n(classify, extract, reformat, answer from supplied text) saturate early: beyond some size, a bigger\nmodel does not do them measurably better, and fine-tuning closes whatever gap remains. Broad reasoning\nand knowledge tasks keep improving with scale. So the question is never \"are SLMs good enough\" in the\nabstract; it is \"is this task the kind that saturates\", and that has to be measured.","how":"### What shrinks when a model shrinks\nScaling-law work established that loss falls smoothly with parameters and training tokens, and the\ncompute-optimal follow-up showed that smaller models trained on far more tokens recover much of the\ngap, which is why current small models are much stronger than their parameter count once suggested.\nBut capabilities do not shrink uniformly. The first to go are **stored world knowledge** (facts live in\nparameters; fewer parameters, fewer facts, more confident invention unless retrieval supplies the\nfacts), **multi-step reasoning and planning**, **robustness to long or intricate instruction sets**,\nand **long-context use**. What holds up is in-distribution pattern behaviour, because a narrow task\noccupies a small region of input space that fine-tuning can cover directly.\n\n### The physical arithmetic\nWeight memory is parameters × bytes per parameter: a 7B model is about 14 GB in fp16 and roughly 3.5\nto 4 GB at 4-bit, which fits a consumer GPU or a laptop; a 70B model is about 140 GB in fp16 and\nneeds several GPUs or aggressive quantization. Decode is memory-bandwidth bound: every generated token\nreads the model's active weights once, so single-stream tokens per second are approximately bandwidth\ndivided by weight bytes, and a model ten times smaller generates roughly ten times faster on the same\ncard (approximate; batching, KV-cache traffic and mixture-of-experts designs change the constant). The\nKV cache is smaller too, since there are fewer layers and heads. This is why SLMs win on latency and\non edge deployment, not only on price. Mixture-of-experts models blur the size label: decode speed\ntracks the *active* parameters read per token, but memory must hold *all* experts, so an MoE with a\nsmall active count decodes like a small model while needing the memory footprint of a large one.\n\n### Closing the gap: fine-tuning and distillation\nThe standard path is to use the large model as a teacher: run it over your task's inputs to produce\nlabels or target outputs, verify a sample by hand, and fine-tune the small model on the result,\ntypically with a parameter-efficient method. The student learns the **task behaviour**, not the\nteacher's general capability, so it matches in-distribution and falls off out of distribution. Narrow\ntasks commonly need hundreds to a few thousand examples (an order of magnitude, not a rule). The\nstudent also inherits the teacher's mistakes, which is why the test set must be human-labelled rather\nthan teacher-labelled.\n\n### The decision dimensions\n- **Task breadth:** closed schema and bounded inputs favour the SLM; open-ended generation favours the LLM.\n- **Tail cost:** if one error in the hard cases is expensive, the LLM or a cascade; if errors are cheap\n  and reviewable, the SLM.\n- **Latency budget:** sub-second interactive or on-device targets usually force the SLM.\n- **Volume:** self-hosted SLM cost is fixed per GPU-hour and amortises only at volume.\n- **Residency and connectivity:** on-prem, air-gapped or offline rules out external APIs.\n- **Knowledge need:** if answers depend on facts, pair the SLM with retrieval or choose the LLM.\n- **Change rate:** a task whose definition shifts monthly makes retraining a treadmill.\n- **Operational capacity:** a self-hosted model needs someone to own serving, monitoring and upgrades.\n\n### The hybrid\nA cascade runs the SLM first and escalates to the LLM on low confidence, failed validation, or a\ntrigger condition. It captures most of the saving while giving LLM quality on the tail, at the price of\na confidence signal that itself needs calibration. For classification with a self-hosted SLM the\nsignal is cheap: the probability the model assigns to its chosen label, read from the logits. Raw\nprobabilities from fine-tuned models are often overconfident, so the threshold is set empirically: on\na held-out human-labelled set, sort predictions by confidence and pick the cut-off at which accuracy on\nthe accepted items still meets the bar. Everything below it escalates, so the threshold directly sets\nthe trade between escalation rate (cost) and accepted-item accuracy (quality). For free-form output,\nthe signal comes from validators instead: schema checks, citation checks, or agreement between two\nsamples. Routing and cascading have their own lesson; the point here is that the choice is rarely\nbinary.","example":"A worked scenario with **illustrative figures**. A support platform classifies 5,000,000 inbound\nmessages a day into intents and PII categories; each call is about 300 input and 10 output tokens.\n\nEvaluation on 1,000 human-labelled messages: the frontier API model scores 97%; a 7B open-weight model\nzero-shot scores 90%; the same 7B model fine-tuned on 3,000 teacher-labelled examples (500 of them\nhuman-verified) scores 96%, with a 95% interval of about ±1 point.\n\nCost per day. API at an illustrative 1 unit per million input tokens and 3 per million output:\n5M × (300×1 + 10×3)/10⁶ = 1,650 units. Self-hosted SLM on a GPU node at an illustrative fixed 100 units\nper day, capacity-planned separately. Naive saving: 1,550 units, about 94%.\n\nCheck the break-even and the load before trusting that. An API request costs 330 / 10⁶ ≈ 0.00033\nunits, so the GPU's fixed 100 units a day only pays for itself above about 300,000 requests a day; at\n5M the SLM wins on cost, at 100k it would lose. And 5M a day is about 58 requests a second on average,\nroughly 17,000 input tokens a second of prefill, with peaks well above that. Whether one node carries\nthe peak must be load-tested; if it takes two, the fixed term doubles and the break-even moves with it.\n\nNow price the quality gap. One point of accuracy on 5M messages is 50,000 misrouted messages a day. If\neach costs 0.02 units of human rework, the SLM-only path's extra point of error costs 1,000 units a\nday, so the real saving is 550 units, and the confidence interval means it could be close to zero.\n\nCascade. The fine-tuned SLM is confident (calibrated probability above threshold) on about 85% of\ntraffic, where its accuracy is close to the LLM's; the remaining 15% escalates. API cost falls to\n0.15 × 1,650 ≈ 248 units, plus 100 for the GPU, total ≈ 348 units a day, with overall accuracy\nindistinguishable from 97% on the test set. The saving is about 79% with no measurable quality loss.\nThe lesson: the error-cost term, quality gap × volume × cost per error, must appear in the comparison,\nand once it does, the cascade usually dominates both pure options at this volume.","tradeoffs":["An SLM gives roughly an order of magnitude lower cost and latency but a narrower competence; it flips as soon as the task needs world knowledge or multi-step reasoning that retrieval cannot supply.","Fine-tuning an SLM closes the in-distribution gap but creates an artefact you must retrain when the input distribution shifts or the base model is retired; it flips when the task changes faster than you can label.","Self-hosting an SLM delivers residency, offline operation and fixed cost, but needs operations capacity and only pays at volume; at low volume a small model behind a hosted API is the better middle.","The LLM through an API gives breadth and zero infrastructure, at the price of variable cost, external data flow and exposure to the provider's deprecation schedule.","A cascade captures most of the saving with LLM-level quality, but adds a confidence estimator that must be calibrated and monitored, and a second code path to operate."],"pitfalls":["Judging the SLM on zero-shot results and discarding it. Symptom: the fine-tuned version would have matched the bar, but the comparison never got that far.","Using an SLM for knowledge-heavy question answering without retrieval. Symptom: fluent, confident, wrong answers about facts the model never held.","Handing an SLM a 3,000-token instruction set written for a frontier model. Symptom: rules late in the prompt are ignored and output format drifts.","Comparing price per token only. Symptom: the business case omits the cost of errors and escalations, and the GPU's fixed cost at low utilisation.","Assuming quantization is free. Symptom: the 4-bit artefact that looked fine on a general benchmark loses several points on your task; always evaluate the exact artefact you deploy.","Distilling without a human-labelled test set. Symptom: 99% agreement with the teacher, and the same systematic errors the teacher makes."],"when":"Reach for an SLM for narrow, high-volume, latency-sensitive work: classification, routing, schema-bound\nextraction, guardrail checks, on-device or on-prem features, and anywhere data cannot leave. Reach for\nthe LLM for open-ended generation, complex tool use, low-volume high-value analysis, and for any task\nstill being discovered: prototype with the large model, and distil once the task definition is stable.\nMeasure accuracy with a confidence interval on your own set, cost per successful task including the\ncost of errors, p95 latency, and the escalation rate if you cascade. In interviews, the expected depth\nis naming which capabilities degrade with size, the bandwidth arithmetic behind the latency gain,\ndistillation with a human-labelled test set, and the cascade as the usual answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval that compares the candidates on the real task, runs the distillation pipeline, and tests the quantized artefact rather than the fp16 original.","architect":"Decides which task families are SLM-eligible, sets the quality bar and the error-cost model, and chooses between pure SLM, pure LLM and a cascade per family.","fde":"Makes an SLM work inside a customer's VPC or air-gapped site when the API model cannot be used, and sets expectations about which capabilities will not carry over."},"check":{"q":"A customer requires that document extraction run entirely inside their VPC on a single GPU.\nYour current pipeline uses a frontier API model. How do you assess whether a small model can replace\nit, and what does the path look like if it can?","a":"Characterise the task first: fixed-schema extraction over a known document population is narrow\nand in-distribution, so it is a strong SLM candidate. Build a human-labelled golden set from the\ncustomer's real documents, measure the frontier model on it as the ceiling, then measure candidate\nopen-weight models zero-shot. If there is a gap, generate training data by running the frontier model\nover the customer's documents where terms permit, human-verify a sample, fine-tune with a\nparameter-efficient method, and re-evaluate the exact quantized artefact on the human-labelled set.\nCheck the memory arithmetic: a 7B model at 4-bit is a few gigabytes of weights, but long documents\nmean a large KV cache, so chunk pages and bound context. Plan operations: serving engine, monitoring,\na retraining trigger when templates change. Because there is no LLM to escalate to inside the VPC, add\na human review queue for low-confidence outputs, or budget a larger open-weight model for the tail."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which capabilities degrade first as a language model gets smaller?","options":["The ability to produce valid JSON for a fixed schema","Stored world knowledge and multi-step reasoning","Tokenization accuracy on common words","The ability to follow a single short instruction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Facts are stored in parameters and reasoning depth grows with scale, so both fall off first. Format compliance and simple instruction following are narrow pattern behaviours that small models retain, especially after fine-tuning."},{"q":"Why does a dense 7B model generate tokens roughly ten times faster than a dense 70B model on the same hardware at batch size 1?","options":["It uses a smaller vocabulary, so each token is shorter","It produces fewer output tokens for the same prompt","Decode is memory-bandwidth bound and each token reads all the weights once, so ten times fewer weight bytes means roughly ten times more tokens per second","Smaller models skip the attention layers during decoding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Per-token decode time is dominated by streaming the weights from GPU memory, not by arithmetic. Fewer bytes per token means proportionally more tokens per second; vocabulary and output length do not change the per-token cost."},{"q":"A team reports 99% agreement between a distilled small model and its large teacher on a held-out set labelled by the teacher. What is missing before shipping?","options":["A larger teacher model to generate more labels","A human-labelled test set, because the student inherits the teacher's systematic errors and agreement with the teacher cannot reveal them","A longer fine-tuning run to reach 100% agreement","A quantized version of the teacher for comparison"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agreement measures how well the student copies the teacher, including its mistakes. Only labels from an independent source show whether either model is actually correct on the task."}]},{"key":"system-design/build-vs-buy","sources":[],"why":"In mature engineering domains the question is settled: nobody writes their own database. The AI stack\nis young, so the boundaries are not settled. Is an AI gateway a product or three hundred lines? Is\nevaluation tooling a vendor or a script and a spreadsheet? Is a retrieval pipeline a managed service or\nyour core intellectual property? Because the answers are unclear, teams default to one of two\nmistakes.\n\nThe first is **build everything, because AI is strategic**. Six months later there is a home-grown\nvector store, gateway, tracing system and prompt manager, the actual product is behind schedule, and\nthe platform is now a maintenance burden competing with open-source projects that improved faster than\nthe team could. The error is conflating strategic importance with differentiation: the model is the\nmost strategic component in the system and almost nobody should train one.\n\nThe second is **buy an end-to-end product, because speed matters**. The start is fast. Then the\nlimits appear: the vendor's workflow is not your workflow, your most sensitive data source cannot be\nsent to them, per-seat pricing scales with adoption rather than with value, the quality loop is a\nblack box you cannot run your own evaluation against, and at renewal you discover that prompts,\nanalytics and the test set all live inside their platform. The error is treating the application layer\nas commodity when it is precisely where your domain knowledge and data live.\n\nNeither default is wrong everywhere; each is wrong at specific layers. That is why the decision has to\nbe decomposed before it can be made.","how":"### Decompose the stack into layers\n1. **Foundation model.** Rent through an API or run open weights; training from scratch is for a\n   handful of organisations. The \"build\" option that is actually open to most teams is *adaptation*:\n   fine-tuning or distilling a rented or open base for a narrow task. The other real choices here are\n   self-host versus API and open-weight versus proprietary, each with its own lesson.\n2. **Serving.** Buy (the API) or build on open-source engines if self-hosting; rarely write your own.\n3. **Gateway and routing.** Buy, adopt open source, or a thin build; commodity, but you must own the\n   configuration because it encodes your portfolio and budgets.\n4. **Orchestration and agent logic.** Usually built, in plain code or on an open-source framework,\n   because this is where your workflow lives and where most of the testing happens.\n5. **Retrieval and knowledge.** Buy the vector store or search engine; build ingestion, chunking,\n   access-control mapping and ranking tuned to your corpus, because that is where quality is decided.\n6. **Evaluation and observability.** Buy or adopt the tracing and eval platform; always build the\n   golden sets and task metrics. Nobody can sell you your own test set.\n7. **Guardrails.** Buy generic detectors (PII, toxicity, injection); build the domain policies and the\n   evaluations that prove they hold.\n8. **Application and experience.** Build if it is your product; buy if it is internal productivity\n   with no differentiation.\n\n### Four questions per layer\n- **Does it differentiate?** Would a customer choose you or pay more because of your version of this\n  layer? Differentiation usually lives in data, domain workflows, evaluation sets and integrations,\n  and rarely in plumbing. If the answer is no, buy.\n- **How fast is the layer moving?** A fast-moving commodity layer should be bought or adopted,\n  *because* a build will be obsolete before it is finished. A fast-moving core layer should be built\n  thin and kept switchable.\n- **What do data and compliance require?** A source that cannot leave your environment, retention\n  rules, or audit requirements narrow the buy options to vendors that deploy where you are, and\n  sometimes force a build.\n- **What is the total cost including exit?** Build cost is engineering time to a first version plus\n  ongoing maintenance, which is a material fraction of the build cost every year, plus the opportunity\n  cost of what those engineers did not ship. Buy cost is licence plus integration (routinely\n  underestimated) plus the lock-in premium at renewal plus the exit cost: data migration, prompt\n  re-tuning, user retraining. Compare over three years, not year one:\n\n```\nTCO_build = build effort + 3 × annual maintenance + infrastructure + usage + opportunity cost\nTCO_buy   = licence over 3 years (at projected seats or usage) + integration\n            + P(switch) × exit cost\n```\n\nWriting it out exposes the terms people drop: maintenance and opportunity cost on the build side,\nintegration and exit on the buy side, and the fact that the licence term grows with adoption while\nmost of the build side is fixed. That asymmetry is why the answer often flips with scale.\n\n### Patterns that come out of the questions\n- **Buy then build.** Adopt a vendor to learn the real requirements, then replace the differentiating\n  part once usage shows what it is. Justified when requirement uncertainty is high, *because* you\n  cannot know what differentiates before users use it.\n- **Build on open source.** The middle path for gateways, serving, eval frameworks: you own deployment\n  and configuration but not development. It trades licence fees for operational expertise.\n- **Buy the platform, build the product.** The common enterprise shape: a managed model platform with\n  governance, and in-house applications on top.\n- **Build the eval whatever else you buy.** The evaluation set is the one asset that is always yours,\n  *because* it is the only instrument that holds a vendor accountable and makes switching measurable.\n- **Own the seams.** Wrap every bought component in an interface you control. The test of a seam is\n  \"how long would it take to swap this\", and that number should be known, not guessed.\n\n### Procurement realities\nSecurity review, data-processing agreement, residency, subprocessor lists and certifications take\ncalendar time regardless of the technical merits. Pricing models matter as much as price: per-seat\npricing punishes successful adoption, usage pricing without caps punishes success differently. A\ncredible build alternative is the strongest negotiating position. For FDEs the roles reverse: the\ncustomer is making this decision about *your* product, and your job is to make \"buy\" credible by\nshowing the integration path, the exit path and a transparent quality loop.","example":"An internal knowledge assistant for 2,000 employees over wikis, tickets and documents, one of which is\nrestricted and cannot be sent to third parties. Three options over three years, all figures\n**illustrative** and in abstract units.\n\n**A. Buy a vertical SaaS product.** 20 units per seat per month × 2,000 seats × 36 months = 1,440,000\nunits, plus about two engineer-months of connector and SSO integration. No differentiation, the\nrestricted source is excluded, and the evaluation loop is the vendor's.\n\n**B. Build on managed components.** Rented model API, managed vector database, open-source gateway and\ntracing. Build: two engineers for four months, 8 engineer-months at 15,000 units each = 120,000.\nMaintenance at roughly 25% of build per year ≈ 90,000 over three years. Infrastructure ≈ 2,000 per\nmonth = 72,000. Usage: 2,000 users × 10 queries per day × 20 workdays × 36 months = 14.4M queries at\nabout 3,500 tokens each ≈ 50 billion tokens, at 1 unit per million ≈ 50,400. Total ≈ 332,000 units.\nThe team owns the eval set, the access-control filter that lets the restricted source in, and the\nmodel seam.\n\n**C. Fully self-host open weights.** Option B without the API usage, plus a 24/7 GPU footprint at\n8,000 per month = 288,000 and additional operations effort of perhaps 180,000 over three years:\n332,000 − 50,400 + 288,000 + 180,000 ≈ 750,000 units. At this volume the GPUs cost almost six times\nthe tokens they replace, so C is justified only if residency rules forbid the API, which they do not\nhere.\n\nDecision: B. The useful insight is the flip point. Option A costs 720 units per seat over three years\nplus about 30,000 of integration (two engineer-months); B is dominated by about 282,000 of fixed cost\nplus roughly 25 units per seat of usage. Setting 720s + 30,000 = 282,000 + 25s gives s ≈ 360 seats.\nBelow that, buy; above it, build on managed components. The exact number matters less than its order\nof magnitude, and it moves if B's infrastructure grows with seats or the vendor discounts at volume. The restricted data\nsource would have forced B regardless, which is the compliance question overriding the cost question.","tradeoffs":["Buying gives time-to-value and someone else's roadmap, but you pay lock-in and lose control of the quality loop; it flips the moment the capability is the product rather than a utility.","Building gives control and differentiation, but you pay the build, permanent maintenance and the opportunity cost, while open-source alternatives keep improving; it flips when the layer commoditises.","Open source avoids licence fees and vendor lock-in, but you own upgrades, security patches and the expertise to run it; with a small team that cost often exceeds a licence.","Buy-then-build de-risks requirements, but you pay twice plus a migration; it is worth it when uncertainty about what users need is high and the vendor's exit path is clean.","Seams and adapters bound switching cost, but they add abstraction and leak when a vendor-specific feature is the reason you bought; invest in proportion to switching probability × switching cost."],"pitfalls":["Reading \"AI is strategic\" as \"build every layer\". Symptom: a platform team six months in with an in-house vector database and no shipped product.","Underestimating integration for a bought product. Symptom: the licence was a third of the project cost; SSO, connectors and access-control mapping were the rest.","Buying a product with an opaque quality loop. Symptom: nobody can explain a wrong answer, run the internal eval set against it, or tell whether last month's update made it worse.","Mismatched pricing model. Symptom: per-seat pricing that doubled when adoption succeeded, or usage pricing with no cap that produced a surprise invoice.","No exit plan. Symptom: at renewal the price rises and the prompts, analytics and evaluation data all turn out to live inside the vendor's platform.","Building the evaluation set only inside the vendor's tooling. Symptom: switching vendors means starting the test set from zero."],"when":"Run the per-layer decision at the start of any AI initiative, again at each renewal, and whenever a\nlayer visibly commoditises (a capability that needed a vendor last year ships in an open-source\nproject this year). Buy models, serving, gateways, tracing, vector stores and generic guardrails by\ndefault. Build evaluation sets, domain workflows, context assembly, integrations with your systems of\nrecord, and the experience if it is your product. Measure time-to-swap per bought component, spend by\nlayer, the integration-to-licence cost ratio, and whether a new engineer can point to which parts are\nyours. In interviews, \"would you build or buy X\" is answered with the layer decomposition and the four\nquestions, never with one word.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the adapter seams, evaluates a vendor component and a home-built option against the same golden set, and measures how long a swap actually takes.","architect":"Owns the layer map and each layer's decision with a three-year cost that includes exit, and negotiates with a credible build alternative in hand.","fde":"Sits on the vendor side of the customer's decision, proving integration path, exit path and evaluation transparency, and recognising honestly when the customer should build instead."},"check":{"q":"A fintech wants a customer-facing assistant that answers questions about a user's own\ntransactions and explains fees. The CTO asks, \"build or buy?\" Walk through your answer.","a":"Decompose it. Model: rent, through an API or a private endpoint if the regulator requires it;\nnever train. Gateway, tracing and eval tooling: buy or adopt open source, they are commodity. Retrieval\nover fee documents: a managed vector store, but build the ingestion and, above all, the per-user\ntransaction tool, because that integration with the ledger and its access controls is both the most\nsensitive and the most differentiating piece. Guardrails: buy PII and toxicity detection, build the\n\"no financial advice\" policy and the evaluation that proves it holds. Evaluation set: always build,\nfrom real support conversations with compliance-reviewed answers. The experience: build, it is the\nproduct. A vertical banking-chatbot SaaS fails three tests at once: transaction data would flow to a\nvendor, fee explanations are the product, and compliance cannot review a black box. So buy the\nplatform layers, build the data, workflow and evaluation layers, keep seams at the model and the\nvector store, and revisit at renewal."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which layer should an organisation build regardless of what else it buys?","options":["The foundation model, to avoid vendor dependence","The vector database, because retrieval quality depends on it","The evaluation set and task metrics, because they are the only instrument that holds any vendor or model accountable","The AI gateway, because it handles credentials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Models, vector stores and gateways are commodity layers with mature options. The golden set encodes your task and your quality bar; without it you cannot compare vendors, detect regressions or switch with confidence."},{"q":"A vendor's end-to-end product costs less than building in year one. What most often reverses that over three years?","options":["Open-source alternatives become free, eliminating the build cost","Per-seat or usage pricing scales with adoption, integration costs more than the licence, and exit costs appear at renewal","Models become cheaper, so the vendor's margins shrink","Engineers become more productive, so builds get faster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Year-one comparisons ignore how licence cost grows with success, how much integration work the purchase still requires, and the lock-in premium a vendor can charge once your prompts, data and analytics live inside their platform."},{"q":"What is the purpose of an adapter seam around a purchased component?","options":["To reduce the component's latency by caching its responses","To satisfy the vendor's API contract requirements","To bound the cost of switching so the build-versus-buy decision stays reversible","To hide the component's cost from the finance team"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A seam you own turns a vendor swap into a known amount of work rather than a rewrite. Its value is proportional to how likely and how expensive a switch would otherwise be."}]},{"key":"system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","sources":["Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"At any given moment the proprietary frontier usually leads on raw capability, and an API is the\nlowest-effort way to use it. So the question is really why anyone runs open weights at all, and the\nanswer is that an API bundles conditions you may not be able to accept. Your data leaves your\nenvironment, mitigable by contract but not by architecture. The provider retires versions on its own\nschedule, and the behaviour your prompts and evals were tuned to disappears with them. You cannot see\nor change the weights, the tokenizer, the sampling internals, or more than a sliver of the logits.\nFine-tuning is limited to what the provider offers. The price is per token, forever, and set by\nsomeone else. And the core capability of your product is a dependency you cannot fork.\n\nOpen weights invert each point. The weights are a file you keep: the same bytes in five years. Data\nnever has to leave. You can fully fine-tune, quantize, distil, prune, inspect and constrain decoding.\nThe cost is hardware and people rather than tokens. In exchange you take on serving, safety tuning,\nupgrades and supply-chain checks, and you usually accept a capability gap to the frontier.\n\nBoth naive positions fail. \"Open means free\" ignores GPUs, utilisation, serving engineering and\noperations, and the fact that the strongest open models trail the frontier. \"Proprietary is best, so\nuse it\" breaks under residency rules, deprecation churn, scale economics and deep customisation\nneeds. A further trap is the word \"open\" itself: most open-weight releases are not open source in the\nsoftware sense. Training data and code are usually withheld, and licences range from permissive\n(Apache-2.0, MIT) to custom community licences with acceptable-use policies and clauses that trigger\nat scale or restrict using outputs to train competitors. The licence has to be read, not assumed.","how":"### The dimensions, and why each one matters\n1. **Control and lifecycle.** An open-weight model is an immutable artefact you pin by content hash. A\n   proprietary model is a versioned API with deprecations typically announced months ahead. This\n   matters *because* an LLM system's behaviour is tuned to a specific model; when the model changes,\n   the prompts, evals and downstream parsers were all written against something that no longer exists.\n2. **Data governance.** Open weights on hosts you control mean data physically never leaves.\n   Proprietary access relies on data-processing agreements, zero-retention options and regional\n   endpoints. The middle ground is a proprietary model offered as a managed service by the cloud\n   provider you already use, reached over private networking in your region: traffic never crosses\n   the public internet and the processor is one you have already contracted, but inference still runs\n   on the provider's infrastructure, not on hosts you operate. Which option suffices depends on whether\n   the constraint is legal (a contract and a network path can satisfy it) or architectural (only\n   hardware you control can).\n3. **Customisation depth.** Open weights allow full fine-tuning, parameter-efficient adapters,\n   quantization, distillation, raw logit access, custom constrained decoding, speculative decoding\n   with your own draft model, and tokenizer work. Proprietary models offer prompting, structured\n   output features and sometimes a fine-tuning API. The difference matters most for narrow,\n   high-volume tasks, which is where deep customisation pays.\n4. **Capability.** The proprietary frontier generally leads; the best open models trail by a margin\n   that varies by task and shifts with every release, so this is contested and must be measured on\n   your own evaluation rather than read off a leaderboard.\n5. **Cost structure.** Proprietary is per-token operating cost that scales with usage. Self-hosted\n   open weights are fixed cost per GPU-hour, so the economics hinge on utilisation; the self-host\n   versus API lesson works the break-even. Open weights served by a hosting provider turn back into\n   per-token cost, often lower than frontier proprietary pricing, with less control than self-hosting\n   and no operations.\n6. **Licensing.** Permissive licences allow redistribution and commercial use with few conditions.\n   Custom licences may add acceptable-use policies, attribution or naming rules, restrictions on\n   training other models with outputs, and thresholds for very large deployments. The licence decides\n   whether you can ship on-device, redistribute fine-tunes, or embed the model in a product you sell.\n7. **Ecosystem and supply chain.** Open weights come with mature serving engines, quantization\n   formats and fine-tuning tooling, which is a large part of their practical value. They also come\n   with risk: weights downloaded from a hub can be tampered with, pickle-format checkpoints can execute\n   code on load, and a popular fine-tune may carry behaviour nobody audited. Pin revisions, prefer\n   safetensors, verify hashes, and read the model card.\n8. **Safety and support.** A proprietary API includes the provider's safety tuning, moderation\n   layers, SLAs and someone to call. With open weights you own safety behaviour and guardrails, and\n   outages are your outages.\n\n### Decoupling the axis from deployment\nFour combinations exist and all are used. Open weights self-hosted: maximum control and isolation,\nmaximum operations. Open weights through a hosting API: portability and lower price without hardware,\nbut the host's lifecycle and shared infrastructure. Proprietary through the public API: frontier\ncapability, least effort, least control. Proprietary through your cloud provider's managed service\nwith a private endpoint: frontier-class capability with contractual and network isolation, often at a\npremium and with model availability that lags the provider's own API. A fifth, rarer arrangement is a\nvendor licensing proprietary weights for deployment inside the customer's environment under contract,\nwhich gives physical isolation without the right to modify or redistribute. Choosing \"open\" does not\nforce you to run GPUs, and choosing \"proprietary\" does not force data onto the public internet.\n\n### How the decision runs\nApply constraints first: isolation requirements, licence compatibility with how you ship, and the\ncustomisation the task needs. Then capability on your eval, then cost at your real utilisation, then\nlifecycle risk and exit cost. The common outcome is a portfolio: proprietary frontier for the hardest\nand lowest-volume work, open weights (hosted or self-hosted, often fine-tuned) for high-volume narrow\ntasks, both behind a gateway so the split can move as the models do.","example":"Three things an open-weight artefact lets you do that an API-only model does not: pin the exact bytes,\nread the full next-token distribution, and constrain the decision to an allowed set without generating\ntext. The restricted softmax below yields a per-class score that a cascade can route on.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\"                 # any small open-weight chat model\nrevision = \"<commit hash from the model page>\"      # pin exact weights, never a moving branch\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name, revision=revision)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    name, revision=revision, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16,\n    use_safetensors=True,                           # refuse pickle checkpoints: they can run code on load\n).eval()\n\nlabels = [\"refund\", \"shipping\", \"other\"]\n# First token of each label, with no leading space: the chat template ends the assistant header with a\n# newline, so the reply's first token is not space-prefixed. Check the ids are distinct for your tokenizer.\nlabel_ids = [tok(l, add_special_tokens=False).input_ids[0] for l in labels]\nassert len(set(label_ids)) == len(labels)\n\nmsgs = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Classify this message: 'My parcel never arrived'. \"\n                                    \"Reply with exactly one lowercase word: refund, shipping or other.\"}]\nenc = tok.apply_chat_template(msgs, add_generation_prompt=True, return_tensors=\"pt\", return_dict=True)\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    next_logits = model(**enc).logits[0, -1]        # full-vocabulary distribution for the next token\nprobs = torch.softmax(next_logits[label_ids], dim=-1)   # renormalise over the allowed labels only\nmass = torch.softmax(next_logits.float(), dim=-1)[label_ids].sum().item()\nfor label, p in zip(labels, probs.tolist()):\n    print(f\"{label:9s} {p:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"mass on allowed labels: {mass:.3f}\")   # low mass: the model wanted to say something else\n# No decoding loop and no parsing of free text. The renormalised probabilities are a confidence signal\n# to calibrate on held-out data (not a calibrated probability as-is); low mass flags odd inputs.\n```\n\nThe same classification through a proprietary API would need a generated answer parsed from text,\nand any confidence would have to be inferred from limited top-k log probabilities if the provider\nexposes them at all.","tradeoffs":["Open weights give lifecycle control and physical data isolation, but you take on serving, safety tuning, upgrades and supply-chain checks; it flips when the team lacks operations capacity or the volume cannot keep a GPU busy.","Proprietary access gives frontier capability with no infrastructure, but the behaviour you tuned to can be retired on the provider's schedule; it flips when behaviour must stay fixed for years, as in regulated workflows or embedded devices.","Deep customisation buys quality on narrow tasks but creates artefacts you must retrain and re-certify when the base changes; it flips when the task is broad and prompting suffices.","Hosted open weights are the middle path, portable and cheaper per token than frontier pricing with no operations, but you inherit the host's lifecycle and share its infrastructure; it fits when you want exit optionality without hardware.","The most capable open model may carry the most restrictive licence; permissiveness versus capability flips the moment you redistribute, embed on-device, or sell the model as part of a product."],"pitfalls":["Reading \"open weights\" as \"open source\" and skipping legal review. Symptom: an acceptable-use or scale clause surfaces during a customer's due diligence or an acquisition.","Loading weights from an unverified source or in pickle format. Symptom: code execution on load, or a fine-tune that behaves differently from its model card.","Assuming open weights are cheaper without a utilisation model. Symptom: a GPU running at a small fraction of capacity costs more per task than the API would have.","Pointing a proprietary integration at a floating \"latest\" alias. Symptom: outputs change overnight, evals fail, and there is nothing in the change log.","Migrating from an API to open weights and expecting the provider's safety behaviour to come along. Symptom: jailbreak success and policy violations rise because the guardrails were implicit in the API model.","Choosing by public benchmark instead of your own eval. Symptom: a migration that was \"equivalent on benchmarks\" loses several points on the task that matters."],"when":"Choose open weights when data isolation must be architectural rather than contractual, when behaviour\nmust be frozen for years, for high-volume narrow tasks where fine-tuning pays, for on-device or edge\ndeployment, when you need logits or custom decoding, or when vendor exit is a board-level requirement.\nChoose proprietary when you need frontier capability now, volume is low or spiky, the team is small,\nand the provider's terms satisfy your governance. Most organisations run both through a portfolio.\nMeasure the eval delta between candidates, GPU utilisation if self-hosting, cost per successful task,\nand time to migrate after a deprecation notice. In interviews, the expected depth is separating this\naxis from deployment, naming the licence question, and making the lifecycle argument concretely.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pins weights by revision, verifies safetensors and hashes, runs the same eval against the open and proprietary candidates, and keeps both behind one interface.","architect":"Owns the decision per task family, the licence review with legal, the isolation requirement classification (legal versus architectural), and the exit plan.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"no data leaves\" demand into the cheapest option that truly meets it, and sets expectations about capability, safety and operations when open weights are the answer."},"check":{"q":"A healthcare customer insists on open-weight models \"so that no data leaves\". Their platform\nteam is three engineers and expected volume is low. What do you advise, and how do you reason about it?","a":"Separate the two axes first. The actual requirement is data isolation, and depending on what\n\"leaves\" means it can be met by self-hosted open weights, or by a proprietary model through their\nexisting cloud provider's managed service over a private endpoint in-region, with contractual zero\nretention. Which one depends on whether their constraint is legal, where a contract with an existing\nprocessor and a private network path satisfy the regulator, or physical, where only hosts they operate\nwill do. I would get that definition in writing before choosing. With\nthree engineers and low volume, a self-hosted GPU fleet is an operations burden with poor utilisation\neconomics, so if the constraint is legal, the private endpoint or a hosted open model in-region is\nlikely cheaper and safer. If the environment is genuinely air-gapped, open weights are the only\noption: pick a permissively licensed model, pin by hash, verify safetensors, serve with a standard\nengine, size the GPU from the memory arithmetic, and add explicit guardrails because the API's implicit\nsafety tuning is gone. Either way, build the eval on their data first and keep the model behind a\ngateway so the decision stays reversible."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does \"open-weight\" actually guarantee about a model?","options":["Its training data and training code are published","It may be used for any purpose without restriction","Its parameters are published for download and use under a licence; training data, code and unrestricted use are not implied","It is always cheaper to run than a proprietary model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Open-weight releases publish the parameters, usually with a licence that may carry acceptable-use, attribution or scale conditions. Data and code are typically withheld, and cost depends entirely on how the weights are served and how busy the hardware is."},{"q":"Which capability is available only with open weights, not through a proprietary API?","options":["Structured JSON output conforming to a schema","Pinning the exact model bytes by hash and keeping that behaviour indefinitely","A service-level agreement on availability","Lower latency on every request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A provider can retire any API version; a downloaded artefact cannot be retired. Structured outputs and SLAs are things proprietary APIs commonly offer, and latency depends on serving, not on whether the weights are open."},{"q":"A customer's rule is that data must stay in-region, never cross the public internet, and go to no processor other than their existing cloud provider. Which option is not automatically excluded?","options":["A proprietary model through its vendor's public API with a zero-retention agreement","An open-weight model served by a third-party hosting provider's multi-tenant API","A proprietary model offered as a managed service by their existing cloud provider, reached through a private endpoint in-region","None of these; only self-hosted open weights can satisfy the requirement"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The public API and the third-party host both add a new processor and a public network path, so they fail the stated rule. The cloud provider's managed service over private networking satisfies it, which shows the isolation requirement is separate from the open-versus-proprietary choice. A rule demanding hosts the customer operates would exclude it too."}]},{"key":"system-design/self-host-vs-api","sources":["Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"Every AI system has to answer where inference runs, and the two options have opposite cost structures.\nAn API is pure variable cost: you pay per token, it scales to zero, and someone else does capacity\nplanning. A self-hosted cluster is mostly fixed cost: a GPU bills by the hour whether it is serving 2,000\ntokens per second or idling at 3 a.m., and it comes with an engineer on call.\n\nThe naive comparisons both fail. \"The API charges X per million tokens and a GPU costs Y per hour, so\nthe GPU is cheaper\" ignores utilisation: a GPU that averages 20% of its peak throughput delivers tokens\nat five times its nominal price. \"Our data is sensitive, so we must self-host\" ignores that enterprise\nAPI terms with zero retention and in-region endpoints often satisfy legal, while some regulated\nenvironments genuinely cannot send data to any third party, which makes the API option unavailable at\nany price. \"Open models are good enough now\" is a per-task claim that only your eval set can settle.\n\nHidden costs sit on the self-host side: upgrades of engines and drivers, security patching, capacity for\npeaks, cold starts measured in minutes while a model loads, and re-running evals after every change.\nHidden costs sit on the API side too: deprecation schedules you do not control, rate limits during\nincidents, and coupling to provider-specific features. The decision exists because neither default is\nright, and because the right answer changes over a product's life.","how":"### The cost model\nAPI cost is the sum over calls of tokens × price, with separate prices for input, output and (often)\ncached input tokens. Self-host cost is fixed spend divided by useful work:\n\n```\neffective_$ per 1M tokens = gpu_hourly_cost × 1e6 / (tokens_per_second × 3600 × utilisation)\n```\n\nThree terms matter. `tokens_per_second` is the aggregate throughput of one GPU at *your* batch size and\nprompt/output mix, not a vendor headline; decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so it rises with batching\nuntil the KV cache fills memory. `utilisation` is average load divided by provisioned capacity; because\ninteractive traffic has daily peaks several times its average, you provision for the peak and run the\nrest of the day partly idle, unless batch work fills the valleys. The third term is people: a fraction of\nan engineer plus on-call, which is a fixed cost that does not shrink with traffic.\n\n### Break-even is a staircase, not a point\nThe API bill is linear in volume: `api = V × p_blended`, where `p_blended` weights the input, output and\ncached-input prices by your real token mix (prompt-heavy traffic has a blended price close to the input\nprice; the example below works out to about $0.23 per million tokens). The self-host bill is\n`ceil(peak_load / per_gpu_throughput) × gpu_hourly × 730 + people`, which is flat until peak load needs\nanother GPU and then jumps. Its first step is whatever the model needs to run at all: one GPU for a\nquantised 8B model, a multi-GPU node for a 70B model at 16-bit precision. Two consequences follow. Below\nthe first step the API almost always wins, because you pay for a whole node to serve a fraction of its\ncapacity. Above it, anything that raises per-GPU throughput or lowers the peak pulls the staircase down:\na smaller or quantised model, larger batches, prefix caching for shared system prompts, and batch jobs\nthat fill the night-time valleys so the peak-to-average ratio falls. The comparison is really about\nmodel size and utilisation, which is why two teams quoting the same GPU price reach opposite answers.\n\n### The constraints that decide before cost does\n- **Data.** Residency, retention, customer contracts, or an air gap. Check what the API vendor's\n  enterprise terms and in-cloud platforms (a model served inside your cloud account's boundary) actually\n  provide before assuming hosting is required.\n- **Control.** Pinning a model version for a year, deterministic behaviour across time, custom\n  fine-tunes or LoRA adapters, logit access, engine-level constrained decoding. APIs offer some of these;\n  hosting offers all of them.\n- **Latency.** Hosting inside your VPC removes a WAN hop and lets you tune time-to-first-token; but a\n  small, poorly utilised cluster has worse tail latency than a provider's large fleet.\n- **Capability.** Frontier proprietary models are usually ahead on open-ended tasks; the gap on a narrow\n  task (classification, extraction) is often zero once a small model is fine-tuned. Only your eval knows.\n- **Elasticity.** APIs absorb spikes up to your quota; hosting absorbs spikes up to your headroom, and\n  adding a replica means minutes of model loading.\n\n### The middle options\nManaged open-weight hosting (a vendor serves open models per token), provisioned-throughput plans from\nAPI vendors (fixed cost, no ops), cloud model platforms inside your account boundary, serverless GPUs for\nspiky loads, and the hybrid most teams end up with: the API for low-volume frontier tasks and self-hosted\nsmall models for one or two high-volume narrow tasks that dominate spend.\n\n### How the decision evolves\nPrototype on the API because it is the fastest way to learn whether the product works. Instrument\ntokens per month per task, prompt/output ratio, peak-to-average ratio and quality on a golden set. When a\nsingle narrow task dominates spend and an open model clears the eval bar, move that task and keep the\nlong tail on the API. Revisit quarterly: API prices and open-model quality both move, and the break-even\npoint moves with them.","example":"A break-even calculator with illustrative inputs. Every figure is an assumption to replace with your\nown measurements; the point is the shape of the result, not the numbers.\n\n```python\n# All numbers illustrative. Replace with your traces and a throughput benchmark on your prompt mix.\nmonthly_in, monthly_out = 40e9, 8e9           # tokens/month: 40B prompt, 8B completion\napi_in_price, api_out_price = 0.15, 0.60      # $ per 1M tokens, small-model tier (illustrative)\napi_cost = monthly_in / 1e6 * api_in_price + monthly_out / 1e6 * api_out_price\n\ngpu_hourly = 2.50                             # $ per GPU-hour on demand (illustrative)\nagg_out_tps = 1500                            # decode tokens/s per GPU at your batch size (measure it)\npeak_to_avg = 3.0                             # interactive traffic: provision for the daily peak\neng_cost = 12000                              # $/month: part of an engineer plus on-call\n\navg_tps = monthly_out / (30 * 24 * 3600)      # ~3,086 output tokens/s averaged over the month\ngpus = -(-avg_tps * peak_to_avg // agg_out_tps)          # ceil: GPUs needed at peak\ngpu_cost = gpus * gpu_hourly * 730\nutilisation = avg_tps / (gpus * agg_out_tps)\nprint(f\"API ${api_cost:,.0f}/mo vs self-host ${gpu_cost + eng_cost:,.0f}/mo \"\n      f\"({int(gpus)} GPUs at {utilisation:.0%} utilisation)\")\n```\n\nWith these inputs the API wins: about $10,800 per month against $24,775 for 7 GPUs running at 29%\nutilisation. Change two parameters, five times the traffic, reserved GPUs at $1.50 per hour and batch\njobs that flatten the peak ratio to 1.5, and the result flips: $54,000 on the API against about $29,520\nfor 16 GPUs at 64% utilisation. The model ignores prefill compute, which matters when prompts are five\ntimes longer than outputs, so benchmark aggregate throughput on your real traffic before trusting it.","tradeoffs":["Lower marginal cost per token when self-hosted, but only above a utilisation threshold; low or spiky traffic flips the advantage back to the API, and every provider price cut moves the break-even point.","Full control over model version and behaviour, in exchange for owning every upgrade; providers deprecate on their schedule, but you must re-evaluate each engine and model change yourself.","Data stays inside your boundary when hosted, yet zero-retention terms and in-cloud model platforms often meet the same requirement without operations; it flips only when no third-party processor is permitted at all.","Frontier APIs usually lead on open-ended quality; the gap narrows to nothing on narrow tasks where a fine-tuned small model beats a general model on your eval, which is when hosting that task pays.","APIs absorb traffic spikes up to the quota you negotiated; self-hosting needs idle headroom or a queue, and it flips when provider rate limits themselves become the bottleneck during peaks."],"pitfalls":["Comparing list prices without utilisation; the symptom is projected savings that never appear in the bill while dashboards show GPUs idling at 20%.","Treating \"sensitive data\" as automatically meaning self-hosting; the symptom is months of platform work for a requirement a contract addendum met, or the reverse, learning at procurement review that data could never leave and the API prototype must be rebuilt.","Migrating on cost alone without a golden-set eval; the symptom is spend falling while quality complaints and escalations rise within weeks.","Omitting people and on-call from the model; the symptom is a single engineer who is the cluster's real single point of failure and cannot take holiday.","Hard-coding provider-specific features (tool-call formats, cache controls, response schemas) deep in application code; the symptom is \"we can't switch\" during a price negotiation.","Sizing for average load instead of peak; the symptom is queueing and time-to-first-token collapsing at the daily peak, exactly when the most users are present."],"when":"Default to the API for new products and for any task whose volume is small or whose quality bar needs a\nfrontier model. Reach for self-hosting when a hard constraint applies (air gap, strict residency with no\nacceptable in-cloud option, logit or adapter access, version pinning the vendor will not give you) or\nwhen one narrow task has high sustained volume, an open model passes your eval, and your utilisation\nmodel shows a clear margin after people cost. Measure tokens per task per month, prompt/output ratio,\npeak-to-average ratio, p95 latency and golden-set quality before deciding. In interviews, the expected\nanswer is the utilisation formula plus the constraint checklist, ending in a hybrid portfolio with a\nrevisit cadence, not a one-word preference.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds behind a provider-agnostic interface from day one, instruments tokens and latency per task, and feeds real trace numbers into the break-even model rather than vendor estimates.","architect":"Owns the decision record: constraints first, cost second, a hybrid split of tasks across API and hosted models, and a scheduled review as prices and model quality move.","fde":"Maps a customer's residency, procurement and network rules to the deployment options, and often lands on an in-cloud managed model as the compromise between the API and a GPU cluster."},"check":{"q":"A customer insists on self-hosting a 70B-parameter model for a support assistant handling about\n2,000 chats a day, because \"APIs are too expensive and our data is sensitive\". How do you evaluate the\nrequest?","a":"Quantify first. 2,000 chats at roughly 4,000 tokens each is about 8 million tokens a day, around\n240 million a month, which is a modest API bill at any current price tier. A 70B model in 16-bit\nprecision has about 140 GB of weights, so it needs a multi-GPU node running around the clock; 240 million\ntokens a month averages under 100 tokens per second against a node that can serve many times that, so\nutilisation would be in the single-digit percent range and the per-token cost many times the API's. Then\ntest the data claim: what exactly may not leave, and would zero-retention enterprise terms or an\nin-cloud model platform in their region satisfy legal? If a genuine air-gap requirement survives that\nconversation, propose a smaller model on one GPU after an eval shows it meets the quality bar, budget the\non-call, and record the decision and its assumptions in an ADR so it can be revisited when volume grows."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which parameter most often decides whether self-hosting beats the API on cost?","options":["The GPU's list price per hour","Average utilisation: served load divided by the capacity you had to provision for peak","The number of parameters in the model","The length of the system prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-host cost is fixed spend divided by useful tokens, so idle capacity multiplies the effective price per token. GPU price and model size matter, but a cheap GPU at 15% utilisation still loses to the API, while an expensive one at 70% can win."},{"q":"A prototype runs on a frontier API and one high-volume classification task is 70% of spend. What is the best next step?","options":["Move all traffic to a self-hosted frontier-class model to eliminate API spend","Negotiate a discount and keep the architecture unchanged","Evaluate an open small model on that task's golden set and, if it passes, self-host only that task","Switch the classification task to a larger API model to raise accuracy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The hybrid split captures most of the saving with the least risk: one narrow task, measurable quality, predictable volume. Moving everything multiplies operational burden and puts the open-ended tasks on a weaker model."},{"q":"Which requirement cannot be met by a hosted API regardless of contract terms?","options":["Zero retention of prompts and outputs by the provider","Processing inside a specific cloud region","Inference inside an air-gapped network with no outbound connectivity","Sub-second time to first token for short prompts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retention and regional processing are routinely covered by enterprise agreements and in-cloud platforms, and APIs can deliver fast first tokens. An air gap removes the network path the API needs, so hosting is the only option."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-routing-cascading","sources":["Chen, Zaharia, Zou, 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance","Ong et al., 2024 — RouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data"],"why":"The price gap between a small model and a frontier model is commonly one to two orders of magnitude per\ntoken (treat that as an illustrative range; it changes with every price sheet), and the latency gap is\nseveral-fold. Yet in most products a large share of requests are easy: a lookup, a reformulation, a\nyes/no classification, a short summary. Paying frontier prices for those is waste, and it is waste that\nscales linearly with success.\n\nThe naive alternatives all fail in a specific way. One big model for everything makes cost and latency\nthe product's ceiling. One small model for everything fails on the hard tail, and the hard tail is\nexactly the set of requests users remember and escalate. Letting a product manager choose a model per\nfeature is better but coarse: within a single feature, difficulty still varies by an order of magnitude,\nso the per-feature choice is always too expensive for some requests and too weak for others.\n\nWhat forced the pattern into existence is that the decision has to be made per request, and it has to\nbe made cheaply. FrugalGPT showed that a cascade of models with a learned \"is this answer good enough\"\nscorer can match the strongest model's accuracy on several benchmarks at a fraction of its cost;\nRouteLLM showed that a router trained on human preference data can send most traffic to a cheaper model\nwith little quality loss. The engineering question is where the quality signal comes from.","how":"### Routing: decide before the call\nThe router sees the request and its metadata (feature, tenant tier, language, length, conversation\nstate) and returns a model. In rough order of sophistication:\n\n1. **Static rules.** Feature or tenant maps to a model. Zero latency, no learning; the right first step.\n2. **Heuristics.** Token count, presence of code, number of constraints in the instruction, whether\n   tools are needed. Cheap and surprisingly effective for coarse splits.\n3. **Learned classifier.** An embedding plus a small classifier trained on past traffic labelled with\n   \"which model was needed\". Labels come from offline evals: run both models, judge the small model's\n   answer, and record whether it passed.\n4. **LLM-as-router.** Ask a small model to grade difficulty. Useful when you have no labels yet, but it\n   adds a model call to every request.\n\nRouters make two kinds of error. Routing a hard request down costs quality; routing an easy request up\ncosts money. The threshold is set by the cost ratio and the quality penalty you are willing to accept,\ntuned on an eval set, and then monitored per route in production because the error rates drift as prompts\nand models change. The router's own cost must be small next to the saving *because* it runs on every\nrequest: a 30 ms classifier is free, a 2 s LLM call usually is not.\n\nThe threshold has a simple form worth deriving. Let the router estimate `p`, the probability that the\nsmall model's answer is adequate, and let `L` be the cost of an inadequate answer in the same units as\ntokens (a retry, a human escalation, a lost customer). Sending the request small is worth it when\n`c_small + (1 − p) × L < c_large`, so route small when `p > 1 − (c_large − c_small) / L`. With\nillustrative costs c_small = 1 and c_large = 15, a bad answer costing 50 gives a threshold of p > 0.72;\na bad answer costing 500 (a billing dispute) pushes it to p > 0.972, and only requests the router is\nalmost certain about go small. The threshold therefore encodes the business cost of a miss, not the\nrouter's accuracy, which is why one router can need a different threshold per feature.\n\n### Cascading: decide after a cheap attempt\nCall the small model; run a check; return if it passes, escalate if it fails. The check is the whole\ndesign. Options, from cheapest to most expensive: a deterministic gate (JSON parses, required fields are\npresent, the cited document id exists, the generated SQL executes); agreement across *n* sampled answers\n(disagreement is a usable proxy for uncertainty); a verifier model or LLM judge; a grounding check against\nretrieved passages. The small model's self-reported confidence is the tempting option and the least\nreliable one, because models are poorly calibrated about their own correctness.\n\nExpected cost per request is `c_small + c_check + p_escalate × c_large`. With illustrative units of\n1 for the small model, 0.5 for a judge and 15 for the large model, and a 25% escalation rate, a cascade\ncosts 1 + 0.5 + 3.75 = 5.25 units against 15 for always-large, a 65% saving. The price is latency: the\n25% of requests that escalate pay the small call, the check and the large call *in series*, so p95\nlatency gets worse even as mean cost falls.\n\n### Routing versus cascading\nRouting makes one call, so latency is bounded, but its risk is prediction error and it needs labelled\ndata to train. Cascading needs no training data and has a quality floor set by the check, but it pays\nserial latency and the check's cost on escalations. In practice they combine: route the obvious cases,\ncascade the ambiguous band in the middle.\n\n### Operating it\nLog the route taken and why. Evaluate quality *per route*, not only in aggregate, since a healthy\naverage can hide a failing hard tail. Keep a small random slice (a few per cent) always served by the\nstrong model as a continuous baseline, so you can see the quality gap move when a provider silently\nupdates the small model. Put a budget and an alert on the escalation rate, because a degraded small model\nturns into 100% escalation and a cost spike on the large one. Decide the consistency policy: the same\nquestion may get different answers depending on the route, which is acceptable for chat and not for an\naudit trail.","example":"A two-rung cascade with a deterministic gate followed by a small judge, using LiteLLM so the same code\ntalks to several providers. The number to watch in production is `escalated`; its rate is what you tune.\n\n```python\nimport json, litellm\n\n# Model ids are illustrative and date quickly; use your providers' current ids. The judge should be a\n# different model from the generator it grades: models rate their own outputs favourably.\nSMALL, LARGE, JUDGE = \"openai/gpt-4o-mini\", \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\", \"anthropic/claude-3-5-haiku-20241022\"\n\ndef ask(model, messages, **kw):\n    r = litellm.completion(model=model, messages=messages, temperature=0, **kw)\n    return r.choices[0].message.content, r.usage\n\ndef passes_check(question, answer):\n    try:                                              # 1) deterministic gate: valid JSON, required fields\n        obj = json.loads(answer)\n        if not isinstance(obj, dict) or not {\"answer\", \"confidence\"} <= obj.keys():\n            return False\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        return False\n    verdict, _ = ask(JUDGE, [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":   # 2) cheap judge, only after the gate passes\n        f\"Question: {question}\\nAnswer: {obj['answer']}\\n\"\n        \"Is the answer complete and correct? Reply PASS or FAIL only.\"}], max_tokens=2)\n    return verdict.strip().upper().startswith(\"PASS\")\n\ndef cascade(question):\n    msgs = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": 'Reply as JSON: {\"answer\": str, \"confidence\": number 0-1}'},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    answer, usage = ask(SMALL, msgs)\n    if passes_check(question, answer):\n        return {\"model\": SMALL, \"answer\": answer, \"escalated\": False, \"usage\": usage}\n    answer, usage = ask(LARGE, msgs)                  # escalate: same prompt, stronger model\n    return {\"model\": LARGE, \"answer\": answer, \"escalated\": True, \"usage\": usage}\n\nprint(cascade(\"Our invoice says net-45 but the signed contract says net-30. Which applies, and why?\"))\n# Emit `escalated`, both usages and the judge verdict as metrics; alert when the escalation rate jumps.\n```\n\nNote that the deterministic gate runs first *because* it is free and catches the most common failure\n(unparseable output); the judge only spends tokens on answers that are at least well-formed. The judge is\ndeliberately a different model from the small generator: LLMs show self-preference bias when grading their\nown outputs, so a same-model judge under-escalates, and the judge itself needs a calibration check against\nhuman labels before its verdicts drive production routing.","tradeoffs":["Mean cost falls substantially, but tail latency rises on escalated requests; this flips when the product has a strict p95 (voice, autocomplete), where routing or hedging beats cascading.","Routing needs labelled data and a classifier you must retrain; cascading needs only a reliable cheap check; which is cheaper depends on whether you already have an eval set with judgments.","Per-request model choice means the same question can get different answers; fine for chat, a problem for reproducible or audited workflows where one pinned model is required.","Savings scale with the share of easy traffic; a workload that is uniformly hard yields little and adds a moving part, so measure the difficulty distribution before building.","The check bounds the cascade's quality: a judge that favours verbose answers or misses subtle errors lets bad small-model answers through, so judge calibration is part of the system's eval."],"pitfalls":["Using the small model's self-reported confidence as the escalation signal; the symptom is an escalation rate near zero while judged quality on the small route is poor.","Measuring only aggregate quality; the symptom is a healthy dashboard metric while complaints concentrate in the hard tail that was misrouted or wrongly passed.","No continuous baseline; the symptom is quality drift that nobody notices for weeks after a provider silently updates the small model, because nothing compares it with the strong one.","Escalation storms: a small-model outage or regression sends everything to the large model; the symptom is 429s and a cost spike on the large model during an incident that was not about it.","Unbounded cascades (retry small with a new prompt, then medium, then large); the symptom is p99 latency several times the single-call latency and users abandoning before the answer arrives.","A router trained on last quarter's prompts; the symptom is a misroute rate that creeps up after a prompt-template change with no code change in the router."],"when":"Reach for routing or cascading when traffic has a visible easy/hard split and cost or latency is a\nproduct KPI; skip it for low-volume features or uniformly hard tasks where a single pinned model is\nsimpler and more consistent. Before building, sample traffic, run both models, and judge the results: that\nsingle experiment tells you the easy share, gives you router labels and sets expectations for the saving.\nIn production measure escalation rate, quality per route, cost per successful task and latency per route.\nIn interviews, derive the expected-cost formula, name the latency penalty, and explain where the quality\nsignal comes from and why self-reported confidence is not it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the router or the check, logs the route and reason on every request, builds the per-route eval, and tunes thresholds against cost and judge scores.","architect":"Decides where routing lives (gateway versus application), the quality floor, budget guardrails on escalation, and the consistency policy for audited flows.","fde":"Tunes thresholds to a customer's actual traffic mix, explains why two similar questions used different models, and shows cost per resolved task before and after."},"check":{"q":"You are asked to cut LLM spend on a support assistant by half without hurting customer\nsatisfaction. Design a routing or cascading approach and explain how you would prove it did no harm.","a":"Start with data: sample a few thousand recent requests, answer each with both the small and the\nlarge model, and have a judge plus a human sample label whether the small answer was adequate. That gives\nthe easy share, which bounds the achievable saving, and labelled data for a router. Then do the\narithmetic: with unit costs of 1 for the small model, 0.5 for the check and 15 for the large one, halving\nspend means 1.5 + 15 × p_escalate ≤ 7.5, so at most 40% may escalate, which needs an easy share of at\nleast 60% even with a perfect check. If the measured share clears that with margin, a cascade with a\ndeterministic gate plus a cheap judge (or a trained router if p95 latency is tight) can reach the\ntarget; if it does not, no router will, and the conversation moves to prompt length or caching. To prove no harm: offline eval per route on the labelled set; an online A/B against the\nall-large baseline on resolution rate and satisfaction; a permanent few-per-cent shadow slice served by\nthe large model to track the gap continuously; and alerts on escalation rate and judge disagreement. Also\ncheck tail latency per route and that answer consistency is acceptable for the product."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the essential difference between routing and cascading?","options":["Routing uses a classifier, cascading uses rules","Routing chooses the model before any call; cascading calls a cheap model first and escalates on a failed quality check","Routing saves cost, cascading saves latency","Routing requires a gateway, cascading runs inside the application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both can live anywhere and use any decision mechanism. The structural difference is timing: a router predicts difficulty up front and pays prediction error; a cascade observes a real answer and pays serial latency and the cost of the check."},{"q":"Small model costs 1 unit, the check 0.5 and the large model 12 units; 30% of requests escalate. Expected cost per request?","options":["4.1 units","5.1 units","13.5 units","3.6 units"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expected cost is small + check + escalation share × large = 1 + 0.5 + 0.3 × 12 = 5.1. Forgetting the check gives 4.1; adding every component as if all ran every time gives 13.5; 3.6 is only the escalated portion."},{"q":"Which escalation signal is least reliable for a cascade?","options":["The answer fails JSON schema validation","Several sampled answers disagree with each other","The small model's own stated confidence is below a threshold","Generated SQL fails to execute against the schema"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Models are poorly calibrated about their own correctness, so stated confidence is often high on wrong answers. Schema checks, execution and sample agreement are external signals that do not depend on the model knowing when it is wrong."}]},{"key":"system-design/multi-model-architecture","sources":[],"why":"Early LLM applications were one prompt and one model. Production systems are not: a request passes\nthrough a safety classifier, PII redaction, query rewriting, embedding, retrieval, reranking, generation,\nstructured extraction, perhaps a judge, and sometimes speech or vision on the way in or out. Pushing all\nof that through one frontier generator fails three ways. Cost: embedding a hundred thousand document\nchunks through a generative model is nonsense when an embedding model does it for a tiny fraction of the\nprice. Capability: a chat model does not emit vectors, and a prompted generalist ranks query–document\npairs worse and far more expensively than a cross-encoder trained for exactly that. Latency: every extra\njob on the critical path is another multi-second call.\n\nThe opposite naive design, \"best model for every job from whichever vendor, wired up ad hoc\", fails on\noperability. Each vendor brings its own authentication, limits, failure modes and tokeniser; nobody can\nsay which versions are in production; and when quality drops there is no way to tell which stage\nregressed. The architecture exists to get the benefits of specialisation while keeping the system one\nthing you can reason about: a governed portfolio of models, stage contracts, shared telemetry, and an\nevaluation that runs per stage *and* end to end.","how":"### Decompose by job, not by vendor\nList the jobs a request needs. For each, record the output type (vector, label, score, free text,\nstructured object), quality sensitivity, volume, latency budget and data sensitivity. The mapping then\nmostly writes itself: high-volume narrow outputs go to small specialised models, often self-hosted;\nlow-volume open-ended generation goes to a frontier model; vectors go to an embedding model; pairwise\nrelevance goes to a cross-encoder reranker; safety screening goes to a fast classifier that runs on every\nrequest, with a larger model consulted only for ambiguous cases. Routing within a job (see model\nrouting / cascading) is orthogonal to decomposition across jobs.\n\n### Patterns of composition\n1. **Pipeline.** Guard → rewrite → embed → retrieve → rerank → generate → verify. Stages that do not\n   depend on each other (guard and embed) run concurrently. A gate that almost always passes can also\n   run *speculatively*: start retrieval, or even generation, while the guard decides, and discard the\n   work if it blocks. You pay wasted calls on the few blocked requests to remove the guard's latency\n   from every other request; it is unsafe only if the speculative stage has side effects or streams to\n   the user before the gate has passed.\n2. **Generator plus verifier.** A second model checks grounding, schema or policy. Inline when it gates\n   the response; sampled and asynchronous when it feeds monitoring.\n3. **Planner plus executors.** A strong model decomposes a task and cheap models carry out subtasks; the\n   backbone of most agent designs.\n4. **Ensemble.** Several models answer and a selector or vote picks; expensive, reserved for high-stakes\n   decisions where the extra calls are cheaper than a mistake.\n\n### Contracts between stages\nEach stage has a typed, versioned input and output schema (a Pydantic or Zod model, not free text), and\neach stage records the model id and version it used in the trace. This is not bureaucracy: the next stage\nwas tuned against *this* stage's output distribution. Swap the query rewriter and the embedder sees\ndifferent text; swap the embedding model and every stored vector is in a different space; swap the\ngenerator and the citation format the verifier parses may change. Typed contracts make the drift\ndetectable at the boundary instead of three stages downstream, and versions make it attributable.\n\n### Budgets compose, tails do not average\nLatency adds along the serial path, so parallelise what you can and budget with tail numbers: the p99 of a\nsum of four stages is at least the largest single-stage p99 and can exceed the sum of all four p99s,\nbecause tail events in different stages combine (the sum of the p99s is only guaranteed to be at least\nthe p96 of the total, by a union bound over the four stages). In practice, when stages are slow for\nindependent reasons, the sum of the p99s is a pessimistic budget, because tails rarely coincide; it is\ncorrelated slowness (two stages on one GPU pool or one provider, a retry policy at every stage, a\nshared overloaded network path) that drives the real p99 towards or past that sum. Budget with the sum,\nthen measure the end-to-end distribution. Cost adds per request; find the dominant stage, which is\nusually generation but is sometimes a reranker scoring 50 candidates with a large cross-encoder. Failure\nis per stage: each needs its own timeout, fallback and degraded mode (drop the reranker, skip the judge).\n\n### Evaluate per stage and end to end\nComponent metrics (recall@k for retrieval, precision for the guard, schema pass rate for extraction) tell\nyou *which* stage moved; the end-task metric tells you whether it mattered. You need both *because* a\nstage can improve on its own metric and hurt the task: a reranker that prefers long passages raises\nnDCG and pushes the generator past its useful context. Any model change ships behind a flag and a rerun of\nboth evaluations.\n\n### Run it as a portfolio\nA registry of approved models with versions, owners and deprecation dates; a gateway as the single egress\nso cost and usage are attributable per stage; and a rule that a stage upgrade is a release, with a\ncanary, not a config edit.","example":"A typed pipeline skeleton: each stage names its model, records timing and model version into a trace,\nindependent stages run concurrently, and the judge runs off the hot path on a sample. `classify`,\n`embed`, `rerank`, `generate`, `judge` and `vector_search` are thin adapters around whatever providers\nor servers you use.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time, random\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nMODELS = {\"guard\": \"safety-classifier-v3\", \"embed\": \"text-embed-v2\",\n          \"rerank\": \"cross-encoder-v1\", \"generate\": \"frontier-chat-2026-06\", \"judge\": \"small-chat\"}\nBACKGROUND = set()                 # references to fire-and-forget tasks (asyncio only keeps weak ones)\n\n@dataclass\nclass Trace:                       # one record per request: which model served which stage, how long\n    stages: list = field(default_factory=list)\n    def add(self, stage, model, t0):\n        self.stages.append({\"stage\": stage, \"model\": model, \"ms\": round((time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000)})\n\nasync def stage(name, fn, trace, **kw):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    out = await fn(MODELS[name], **kw)              # every adapter takes the model id as its first argument\n    trace.add(name, MODELS[name], t0)\n    return out\n\nasync def answer(question):\n    trace = Trace()\n    verdict, qvec = await asyncio.gather(          # guard and embedding are independent: run concurrently\n        stage(\"guard\", classify, trace, text=question),\n        stage(\"embed\", embed, trace, text=question))\n    if verdict != \"allow\":\n        return {\"answer\": \"I can't help with that request.\", \"trace\": trace.stages}\n    candidates = await vector_search(qvec, k=50)                                 # a database call, not a model\n    top = await stage(\"rerank\", rerank, trace, query=question, docs=candidates, k=5)\n    draft = await stage(\"generate\", generate, trace, question=question, context=top)\n    if random.random() < 0.05:                                                   # sampled judge, off the hot path\n        t = asyncio.create_task(stage(\"judge\", judge, trace, question=question, answer=draft, context=top))\n        BACKGROUND.add(t); t.add_done_callback(BACKGROUND.discard)              # keep a reference or it may be GC'd\n    return {\"answer\": draft, \"sources\": [d.id for d in top], \"trace\": trace.stages}\n```\n\nThe trace is what makes the architecture debuggable: when quality drops you can group judge scores by the\n`generate` model version, or latency by stage, instead of guessing. Note that the sampled judge appends\nto the trace *after* the response has returned, so the trace must be emitted when background work\nfinishes, not at return.\n\nRead the latency budget off the structure. With illustrative p95s of guard 80 ms in parallel with embed\n40 ms, vector search 30 ms, rerank 150 ms and generation 2.5 s, the serial budget is about\n80 + 30 + 150 + 2,500 ≈ 2.8 s. Running guard and embed together saved the 40 ms of the shorter one, and\ngeneration is nine-tenths of the total, so streaming its output (which moves the user-visible number to\ntime-to-first-token) does more for perceived latency than any change to the other stages. Cost usually\nhas the same shape, until someone raises the rerank candidate count from 50 to 500 and the reranker\nbecomes the dominant line.","tradeoffs":["Each stage gets a right-sized model for quality and cost, but there are more moving parts, vendors and versions to manage; for low-volume products one strong model's simplicity can win outright.","Stages can be upgraded independently, yet hidden coupling means a swap is safe only with an end-to-end eval; the independence evaporates when stages have been tuned to each other's quirks.","Running independent stages concurrently cuts latency, but raises peak concurrency and cost during bursts, and complicates per-stage rate-limit budgeting.","Vendor diversity gives best-of-breed quality and resilience, but every new processor needs a security review and a data-processing agreement; in regulated settings fewer vendors is the better trade.","An inline verifier raises the quality floor on every request at a latency and cost premium; a sampled asynchronous judge is nearly free but only catches problems after users have seen them."],"pitfalls":["Upgrading the embedding model without re-indexing; the symptom is retrieval recall collapsing because query vectors and stored document vectors now live in different spaces.","Budgeting latency with averages; the symptom is an end-to-end p99 far above the sum of stage p50s while every stage looks healthy on its own dashboard.","No per-stage telemetry; the symptom is \"the answers got worse\" with no way to tell whether the rewriter, retriever, reranker or generator regressed.","Passing free text between stages instead of typed objects; the symptom is parse failures after a model update and prompt-injection text riding from one stage into the next.","Running an LLM judge inline on all traffic; the symptom is cost roughly doubling and latency rising with no gain the product can show, when a 5% sample would have caught the same regressions.","Treating two vendors' \"same\" capability as interchangeable; the symptom is dimension mismatches, silent truncation at a different token limit, or changed tool-call formats after a swap."],"when":"Any system beyond a prototype is already multi-model; the question is whether the decomposition is\nexplicit. Make it explicit as soon as you have more than one model class in the path, or more than one\nteam touching the pipeline. Measure cost and latency per stage (p50, p95, p99), each stage's component\nmetric and the end-task metric, and alert on divergence between them. Resist adding a stage without a\nmeasured gain; each one is a dependency with its own failure mode. In interviews, draw the pipeline, name\nthe model class and budget for each stage, and explain how you would localise a regression after an\nupgrade. That coupling-and-eval story is what distinguishes architecture from a diagram.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements typed stage adapters, records model versions in traces, writes component evals and makes sure independent stages actually run concurrently.","architect":"Owns the portfolio: which model classes are approved for which jobs, the version and deprecation policy, and how the latency and cost budget is split across stages.","fde":"Assembles the pipeline from models the customer has already approved, often under procurement limits, and explains why the reranker and the generator come from different vendors."},"check":{"q":"A RAG product swapped its generator for a newer model that benchmarks higher, and answer quality\ndropped. How does thinking in stages explain this, and how would you diagnose it?","a":"The generator is one stage whose prompt and context handling were tuned against the old model's\nbehaviour: citation format, response length, how it treated conflicting passages, how literally it\nfollowed the system prompt. First confirm the other stages did not move: retrieval recall@5 and reranker\nscores on the golden set should be unchanged, which isolates the regression to generation. Then compare\njudge scores old versus new on the same fixed set, check structured-output and citation parse failure\nrates, check for context truncation from a different tokeniser or limit, and read a sample of\ndisagreements. The fix is usually prompt re-tuning plus an end-to-end eval before a canary rollout. The\nlesson is procedural: a stage upgrade is a release that needs per-stage telemetry, end-to-end evaluation\nand a feature flag, regardless of what the public benchmark says."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do multi-model systems need typed, versioned contracts between stages?","options":["Because providers require JSON between calls","Because each stage's output is the next stage's input distribution, so drift must be detectable at the boundary and attributable to a version","Because typed contracts reduce token usage","Because it allows all stages to share one model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The coupling between stages is the central risk of the pattern. Schemas catch shape changes immediately and versions tell you which upgrade caused a downstream regression; neither has anything to do with provider requirements or token counts."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to rerank with a cross-encoder rather than asking the generator to rank candidates?","options":["Generators cannot read more than five documents","A cross-encoder scores query–document pairs jointly, cheaply and well, and leaves the generator's context for answering","Rerankers remove the need for embeddings entirely","Rerankers are always more accurate than generators at every task"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Specialisation is the point: the cross-encoder is trained for pairwise relevance and costs a fraction per pair, so the expensive model sees only the best few passages. Embeddings are still needed for the first-stage retrieval."},{"q":"Four serial model stages each have p50 = 200 ms and p99 = 1 s. What can you say about the end-to-end p99?","options":["It is 800 ms, the sum of the p50s","It is 1 s, since the slowest stage dominates","It is exactly 4 s, the sum of the p99s","It is at least 1 s, may exceed 4 s, and cannot be derived from the p50s; measure it end to end"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Latencies add pointwise, so the total is at least as slow as any stage. Tail events in different stages combine: the total can only exceed the sum of the p99s when at least one stage exceeds its own p99, which by a union bound happens on at most about 4% of requests, so the sum of the p99s is guaranteed only to be at least the p96 of the total, and the true p99 can be higher still."}]},{"key":"system-design/ai-gateway","sources":["Official docs: LiteLLM","Official docs: Portkey"],"why":"Without a gateway, the second team to ship an LLM feature copies the first team's retry loop, pastes\nthe same provider key into its own secret store, and starts spending against the same organisation\nquota with no coordination. By the fifth team nobody can answer basic questions: how much does feature X\ncost per month, which models are in production, what data is sent to which vendor, and how do we switch\neverything off if a key leaks or a provider has an incident. Rate limits hit unpredictably because five\nservices share one quota. A provider migration touches every codebase.\n\nThe naive fix is a shared client library. It helps with code reuse but cannot *enforce* anything: teams\npin old versions, polyglot stacks need one library per language, keys still live in every service, and\norg-wide rate-limit and budget coordination needs shared state a library does not have. The same\nproblem in microservices produced the API gateway; the AI gateway is that idea with model-specific\nconcerns added: token accounting, model aliasing, provider translation, streaming passthrough, prompt\nlogging and guardrails. It moves cross-cutting policy to a network boundary where it applies to every\ncaller whether or not they remembered to.","how":"### The request path\nA client sends an OpenAI-compatible request (`/v1/chat/completions`, embeddings and so on) to the gateway\nwith a *virtual* key. The gateway authenticates the key, resolves the requested model alias to one or\nmore deployments, applies policy (budget remaining, requests and tokens per minute, guardrails), picks a\ndeployment by its routing strategy, translates to the provider's native API, streams the response back,\nand records usage (tokens, computed cost, latency, key, tags) for logging and export. The API is\nOpenAI-shaped *because* most SDKs already speak it, so adoption is changing a base URL and a key.\n\n### LiteLLM proxy\nAn open-source Python server (`litellm --config config.yaml`) and a matching in-process `Router` class.\nThe YAML config has a `model_list` of deployments (an alias name plus `litellm_params` with the provider\nand model, keys referenced as `os.environ/VAR`), `router_settings` (routing strategy, retries, fallbacks,\ncooldowns for failing deployments), `litellm_settings` (caching, logging callbacks to tracing backends)\nand `general_settings` (the master key, the database). Virtual keys with budgets and rate limits per key,\nteam or user are stored in Postgres; Redis holds shared state such as rate-limit counters, cache entries\nand cooldowns across replicas. The same alias can point at several deployments, which is how\nload-balancing and same-model failover work.\n\n### Portkey\nA hosted gateway (the core gateway is also open source, written in TypeScript) where behaviour is\ndeclared in a config: a `strategy` (fallback or load-balance) over `targets`, each with its own virtual key\nand optional retry, cache (exact or semantic) and timeout settings. Clients pass a config id or inline\nconfig in request headers; provider credentials live in Portkey's vault and clients reference them by\nan id rather than holding them (virtual keys, in Portkey's terms); traces, cost and\nmetadata tags appear in its dashboards; guardrails attach to configs. Hosted removes the operational\nburden but adds a third-party processor to your data path, which compliance must review, unless you\ndeploy the gateway inside your own environment.\n\n### Enforcing budgets and rate limits\nBudgets and limits are counters in shared state, and LLM traffic makes them awkward in one specific way:\na request's cost is not known until it finishes, because the model decides the output length. A gateway\ntherefore checks *before* the call (requests per minute, and tokens per minute from the prompt's token\ncount plus `max_tokens` or an estimate) and charges actual usage *after* the response completes. Two\nconsequences follow. A budget can be overshot by whatever is in flight when it runs out, so a hard cap\nneeds headroom or a per-request ceiling enforced through `max_tokens`. And with several replicas the\ncounters must live in Redis or similar, not in each process, or every replica enforces the full limit\non its own and the effective limit is multiplied by the replica count.\n\nChoosing among deployments of one alias is a routing strategy (LiteLLM offers simple shuffle,\nleast-busy, latency-based and usage-based routing, among others), and a deployment that fails\nrepeatedly is put into a cooldown and skipped for a period: a circuit breaker under another name. These\nstrategies only see what the gateway sees, latency and errors, never answer quality.\n\n### Alternatives\nOther products occupy the same slot. API-gateway vendors added AI plugins (Kong AI Gateway), CDNs offer\nhosted gateways (Cloudflare AI Gateway), and Envoy AI Gateway builds on Envoy for teams already running it\non Kubernetes. The choice follows the same axes as LiteLLM versus Portkey: where prompts flow, who\noperates the component, which providers and provider-specific features pass through intact, and whether\nit fits the traffic infrastructure you already run. At the light end, an in-process library such as the\nLiteLLM SDK's `Router` gives the same routing and fallbacks with no extra hop, but no central\nenforcement: every service holds keys again and budgets are per process.\n\n### What it centralises\nKey custody; budgets and quotas per team or customer; rate-limit coordination; retries and fallbacks;\ncaching; PII redaction and guardrails; logging and cost attribution via tags; and model aliases, so\napplications call `fast` or `strong` while the platform team decides what those resolve to, which makes\nmigrations and deprecations configuration changes rather than code changes.\n\n### Sharp edges\nIt is a lowest-common-denominator API: provider-specific features (prompt-cache controls, extended\nreasoning parameters, tool-call nuances, vision input formats) lag or are dropped unless passthrough is\nverified. It is a new single point of failure and a new hop: run several replicas per region, keep it\nclose to callers, and decide what happens when its Postgres or Redis is unavailable (fail open on budget\nchecks, or fail closed and take every AI feature down with the database). Streaming must be forwarded\nwithout buffering or time-to-first-token suffers. Token counts across tokenisers are approximations.\nGateway logs contain prompts, so they need a retention policy. And someone must own the config, or\naliases rot and fallbacks go untested.","example":"A LiteLLM configuration with two deployments behind one alias, a stronger fallback, caching and logging,\nfollowed by the application code, which only needs the OpenAI SDK pointed at the proxy. Replace the\nmodel ids with current ones for your providers.\n\n```yaml\n# config.yaml: applications call \"fast\" or \"strong\"; the platform team decides what those mean\nmodel_list:\n  - model_name: fast\n    litellm_params:\n      model: openai/gpt-4o-mini\n      api_key: os.environ/OPENAI_API_KEY\n  - model_name: fast                          # second deployment of the same alias shares the traffic\n    litellm_params:\n      model: azure/gpt-4o-mini-eu             # azure/<your-deployment-name>\n      api_base: os.environ/AZURE_EU_BASE\n      api_key: os.environ/AZURE_EU_KEY\n  - model_name: strong\n    litellm_params:\n      model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\n      api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\nrouter_settings:\n  routing_strategy: simple-shuffle\n  num_retries: 2\n  fallbacks: [{\"fast\": [\"strong\"]}]           # when every \"fast\" deployment fails, try \"strong\"\nlitellm_settings:\n  cache: true                                 # exact-match response cache (Redis when configured)\n  success_callback: [\"langfuse\"]              # usage and cost per request to your tracing backend\ngeneral_settings:\n  master_key: os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY\n  database_url: os.environ/DATABASE_URL       # virtual keys, teams and budgets live here\n```\n\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://litellm.internal:4000/v1\", api_key=\"sk-<support-team-virtual-key>\")\nr = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"fast\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise ticket #4821 in two lines.\"}],\n    extra_body={\"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"support-bot\", \"tenant:acme\"]}})   # tags drive cost attribution\nprint(r.choices[0].message.content)\n```\n\nThe application never sees a provider key, and moving `fast` to a different model is a config change\nand a canary, not a deploy of every service.","tradeoffs":["One place for keys, budgets and logs, against one more network hop and a shared failure point; the balance tips badly if you run a single replica or place it in a different region from callers.","Aliases make provider migrations cheap, but the unified API lags provider features; it flips when a provider-specific capability such as cache controls is core to your product and is not passed through.","A hosted gateway removes operations and gives dashboards on day one, while a self-hosted LiteLLM keeps prompts in your VPC but makes you run Postgres, Redis and upgrades; compliance usually decides.","Central routing policy lets a platform team move traffic without touching applications, at the cost of application teams losing fine control; a single app with unusual needs may be better served in-process.","Gateway-level caching is a one-line win but is context-blind; its safety depends entirely on keys and tags scoping entries per tenant."],"pitfalls":["A fail-closed dependency on the gateway's database; the symptom is every AI feature in the company going down when Postgres restarts for maintenance.","Buffering streamed responses in the proxy; the symptom is time-to-first-token jumping from hundreds of milliseconds to seconds right after adoption.","A shared cache without tenant scoping; the symptom is a cached answer containing one customer's data served to another, which is an incident, not a bug.","Assuming feature parity across providers; the symptom is provider-specific parameters silently dropped and cost or quality regressing after a migration that \"worked\".","One master key shared by all applications; the symptom is zero cost attribution and an unbounded blast radius when the key leaks.","Nobody owning the configuration; the symptom is dozens of aliases, half pointing at deprecated models, with fallbacks that have never been exercised.","Rate-limit counters kept per replica instead of in shared state; the symptom is provider 429s and budget overruns while the gateway's own limits never trip, worsening each time a replica is added."],"when":"Adopt a gateway once more than one team or more than one provider is involved, or as soon as finance or\ncompliance asks for spend by team and a list of what is sent where; for a single application an\nin-process router (LiteLLM's SDK or a thin client) is lighter. Choose the LiteLLM proxy when you want an\nopen-source component inside your own network and can operate a small Python service with Postgres and\nRedis; choose Portkey when hosted observability and declarative configs matter more and your data rules\nallow a processor. Measure added latency at p50 and p99, gateway availability, cache hit rate and spend\nper key. In interviews, be able to say what a gateway centralises and what it cannot do, which is make\noutputs correct.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Points the SDK at the gateway, calls aliases rather than model ids, tags requests for attribution, and debugs with gateway logs instead of five provider dashboards.","architect":"Decides placement per region, fail-open versus fail-closed policy, config ownership, and the boundary between what belongs in the gateway (routing, keys) and in the application (prompt logic).","fde":"Deploys a gateway inside the customer's cloud to satisfy key-custody and egress policies, and uses its per-key reports to show each business unit its own spend."},"check":{"q":"A platform team proposes routing all LLM traffic through a single LiteLLM proxy in one region,\nbacked by one Postgres instance, and failing requests whenever the budget check cannot run \"so budgets\nare never exceeded\". Critique the design.","a":"Three problems. A single replica in one region is a company-wide single point of failure and adds\ncross-region latency for every service elsewhere; run at least two replicas per region behind a load\nbalancer, with Redis for shared counters. Failing closed on the budget check couples every AI feature to a\ndatabase's uptime for a soft financial control; budgets should fail open with asynchronous\nreconciliation and alerts, reserving fail-closed for hard compliance limits on untrusted keys. Finally,\nthe proposal says nothing about operations: verify streaming passthrough, test the fallback chains, set a\nretention policy for logged prompts, assign an owner for aliases, and document an emergency bypass. The\ngateway is the right idea; the single-instance, fail-closed shape is what needs to change."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which concern is an AI gateway least able to solve?","options":["Attributing spend to teams and features","Failing over to another provider during an outage","Making a feature's outputs correct for its task","Keeping provider keys out of application code"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A gateway controls where requests go and what is recorded about them. Output quality depends on prompts, retrieval, models and evaluation, which live in the application and its eval harness, not at the network boundary."},{"q":"What most distinguishes the LiteLLM proxy from Portkey?","options":["Portkey only supports OpenAI models","LiteLLM is an open-source Python proxy you run with a YAML config; Portkey is primarily a hosted gateway driven by declarative configs, with an open-source core","LiteLLM cannot do fallbacks or load balancing","Portkey requires you to operate your own Postgres"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both offer routing, fallbacks, caching and observability across many providers. The real difference is operating model and where your data flows: self-hosted Python with your own database versus a hosted service with dashboards."},{"q":"Why do applications call model aliases such as \"fast\" and \"strong\" instead of concrete model ids?","options":["Aliases reduce the number of tokens billed","Aliases make model outputs deterministic","Aliases let the platform team remap, load-balance and migrate models by configuration without changing application code","The OpenAI API specification requires aliases"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Indirection is what makes deprecations and migrations cheap: the mapping from alias to deployments changes in one place and can be canaried, while the application code stays untouched."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-fallback-strategies","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Dean and Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale"],"why":"A model API is a remote dependency with several distinct ways of failing: hard errors (5xx, connection\nresets), throttling (429, sometimes with a `Retry-After`), slow responses that blow the deadline when the\nprovider is overloaded, capability changes (a model id retired, behaviour shifted by a silent update),\nand exhausted quota in one region while another has capacity. Each surfaces to the user as an error or a\nspinner that never resolves. If your availability target is higher than the provider's, arithmetic alone\nsays you need a second, independent path: two paths that each succeed 99.5% of the time give\n1 − 0.005² = 99.9975% *if their failures are independent*. They rarely are. Two resellers of the same\nupstream model, two deployments in one cloud region, or two keys drawing on one organisation quota fail\ntogether, so the real gain is set by how independent the rungs are, and independence is the property to\ndesign for.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"retry with backoff\", only covers transient blips on the same endpoint. During a\nreal incident it makes things worse: every client retries, load on the failing provider multiplies, and\neach user waits through several timeouts before seeing an error. The other naive alternative, \"add a\nsecond provider\", fails more quietly: the backup returns malformed JSON, ignores the tool schema, or\ntruncates at a smaller context window, so the error rate looks fine while the product silently breaks.\n\nFallback is a specific thing, distinct from its neighbours. A *retry* repeats the call on the same\ntarget. A *fallback* keeps the function but changes the target: another region, another provider,\nanother model. *Degradation* changes the function itself. Getting the three confused is how teams end up\nwith a fallback chain that is slower than the outage it was meant to hide.","how":"### Classify the failure first\nEvery error maps to one of three actions, and the mapping is the heart of the design.\n- **Retry the same target** for 429 (honour `Retry-After`), 5xx, connection errors and timeouts on\n  idempotent calls; once or twice, with jittered backoff, never unbounded.\n- **Fail over** on repeated 5xx or timeouts, an open circuit, a 404 for a retired model id, or regional\n  capacity errors.\n- **Neither** for 400 invalid request, 401, content-policy refusals and context-too-long: these recur\n  identically on every target, so failing over just multiplies latency and cost. They also say nothing\n  about the target's health, so they must not count towards its circuit breaker.\n- **Escalate on invalid output.** A response that arrives but fails schema validation after one repair\n  attempt is a legitimate reason to try the next rung, usually a stronger model. This is the cascade\n  idea applied to reliability; count it separately from error-triggered fallback, because it signals a\n  prompt or model-fit problem rather than an outage.\n\n### The ladder, ordered by behavioural distance\n1. Same model, another deployment or region: identical behaviour, different capacity.\n2. Same model through another provider (a cloud platform serving the same weights): identical weights,\n   possibly different limits and features.\n3. Same family, adjacent version: small drift in style and formatting.\n4. Comparable model from another vendor: prompts, tool-call formats, structured output and tokeniser\n   limits must all be re-validated.\n5. A smaller model: a quality drop, but still the same function.\n6. Hand off to graceful degradation: cache, non-AI path, honest unavailability.\nOrder *because* each rung down changes behaviour more; you want the first rung that works to be the one\nusers cannot tell from normal.\n\n### Deadlines, not timeouts\nEach attempt needs its own timeout, and the attempts must fit inside one user-facing deadline. Without\nthat, a 10 s primary timeout plus a 10 s fallback is a 20 s worst case nobody signed off. Pass the\nremaining budget down the ladder and skip rungs that cannot complete in time.\n\n### Circuit breakers\nPer target: after N failures in a window the breaker opens, the target is skipped for a cooldown, then a\nsingle probe decides whether to close it. Without a breaker, every request during an outage waits the\nfull primary timeout *before* falling back, so latency equals timeout plus fallback for the whole\nincident, and the failing provider keeps receiving your traffic.\n\n### Hedging for latency\nDean and Barroso's hedged request sends the same call to a second target after the p95 delay has elapsed\nand takes whichever answers first. It trims the tail at the cost of roughly 5% extra calls, but only for\nidempotent calls, never once a stream has been committed to the user, and it must switch off when the\nbreaker shows the primary is overloaded, or it doubles the load that caused the problem.\n\n### Equivalence is a test, not an assumption\nValidate outputs against a schema regardless of which model served them; keep per-model prompt variants\nwhen formats differ; send a small continuous slice (around 1%) through each fallback so it is exercised\nin daily traffic; run game days where the primary is blocked on purpose. Tag every response with the\ntarget that served it, and alert when the fallback share rises, otherwise the primary can be down for a\nweek and the only signal is the bill.\n\n### Side effects and streams\nA retry or failover after a tool call executed (an email sent, a record written) duplicates the side\neffect; tool calls need idempotency keys and agent state needs checkpoints so the fallback resumes after\nthe action rather than replaying it. A stream that fails midway restarts on the fallback with the partial\noutput discarded; models do not reliably continue each other's text.\n\n### Capacity and cost\nFailing all traffic over to a secondary hits the secondary's rate limits unless quota was provisioned in\nadvance; you cannot buy it during the incident. Failing over to a larger model is a cost spike. Both\nbelong in the runbook before the first outage.","example":"A fallback ladder with per-target circuit breakers (closed, open, half-open with a single probe), error\nclassification and a shared deadline. `call_model`, `ModelError` (with an HTTP `status`) and\n`ServiceDegraded` are the adapter types your client layer provides; the point is the control flow.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, random, time\n\nclass Breaker:                       # closed -> open (after N failures in window) -> half-open (one probe)\n    def __init__(self, fails=5, window=30, cooldown=60):\n        self.fails, self.window, self.cooldown = fails, window, cooldown\n        self.errors, self.open_until, self.probing = [], 0.0, False\n    def allow(self):\n        now = time.time()\n        if now < self.open_until: return False          # open: skip this target without waiting\n        if self.open_until and self.probing: return False   # half-open: one probe is already in flight\n        if self.open_until: self.probing = True         # cooldown over: let exactly one probe through\n        return True\n    def record(self, ok):\n        now = time.time()\n        if self.probing:                                # half-open: the probe alone decides\n            self.probing = False\n            self.open_until, self.errors = (0.0, []) if ok else (now + self.cooldown, [])\n            return\n        self.errors = [t for t in self.errors if now - t < self.window] + ([] if ok else [now])\n        if len(self.errors) >= self.fails:\n            self.open_until, self.errors = now + self.cooldown, []\n\nFAILOVER_ON = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504}   # target-side failures; same-target retries live in call_model\nLADDER = [(\"primary-us\", 3.0), (\"primary-eu\", 3.0), (\"vendor-b-equivalent\", 6.0)]   # (target, timeout s)\nbreakers = {t: Breaker() for t, _ in LADDER}\n\nasync def complete(messages, deadline=10.0):\n    start, last_err = time.time(), None\n    for depth, (target, timeout) in enumerate(LADDER):\n        remaining = deadline - (time.time() - start)\n        if remaining <= 0 or not breakers[target].allow():\n            continue                                    # out of budget, or breaker open: skip this rung\n        try:\n            resp = await asyncio.wait_for(call_model(target, messages), timeout=min(timeout, remaining))\n            breakers[target].record(ok=True)\n            return {\"served_by\": target, \"fallback_depth\": depth, **resp}\n        except ModelError as e:\n            if e.status not in FAILOVER_ON:             # 400/401/policy: the request is bad, not the target,\n                breakers[target].record(ok=True)        # so it must not trip the breaker...\n                raise                                   # ...and it would fail identically on every rung\n            breakers[target].record(ok=False); last_err = e\n            await asyncio.sleep(random.uniform(0, 0.2))  # jitter before the next rung\n        except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n            breakers[target].record(ok=False); last_err = \"timeout\"\n    raise ServiceDegraded(last_err)                     # hand off to the graceful-degradation layer\n```\n\n`served_by` and `fallback_depth` go into metrics and traces; the alert on fallback depth greater than zero\nis what tells you the primary is unhealthy before customers do. Two details are deliberate. Client errors\nare recorded as healthy, because a burst of malformed requests from one buggy caller must not open the\nbreaker and push everyone else's traffic to a worse rung. And the per-rung timeouts (3 s, 3 s, 6 s) sum\nto more than the 10 s deadline, so the third rung only gets what is left: if both primaries time out,\nit has just under 4 s of its 6 s, and a rung that cannot finish in that time is better skipped\n(`remaining <= 0` is the crude version of that check). A production breaker also has to release the half-open\nprobe slot on every exit path, including cancellation when the client disconnects, or a target can stay\nskipped indefinitely.","tradeoffs":["Availability rises, but output consistency falls as the ladder descends to other vendors; for audited or regulated outputs, restrict the ladder to same-model rungs and degrade honestly after that.","Hedged requests cut tail latency for a few per cent extra calls, yet they double load exactly when the provider is struggling; disable hedging whenever the breaker indicates overload.","A longer ladder raises the chance of success but stretches the worst-case latency; with a tight deadline a short ladder with a breaker beats a long one.","Cross-vendor fallback sends data to a second processor; it flips from asset to liability when that vendor is not on the approved list for the data class involved.","Pre-provisioned secondary quota costs money while idle, but without it the fallback fails with 429s at the exact moment it is needed."],"pitfalls":["A fallback never exercised in normal traffic; the symptom is the first real outage revealing expired credentials, a wrong model id or a schema mismatch on the secondary.","Retrying or failing over after a side effect; the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or payments appearing during provider incidents.","No circuit breaker; the symptom is every request during an outage taking primary timeout plus fallback time, and the failing provider being hammered by your retries.","Silent fallback with no metric; the symptom is discovering the primary has been down for days from the invoice or from a slow drift in answer quality.","Failing over on non-retryable errors; the symptom is a single bad request producing identical 400s from three providers with triple the latency and cost.","Ignoring context-window and limit differences; the symptom is context-length errors appearing only on long requests and only when the fallback is active."],"when":"Every production call should have at least the first two rungs and a documented ladder; how deep it goes\ndepends on the SLO, the audit constraints on outputs and the cost you can absorb during an incident.\nDecide the error classification, the deadline and the breaker thresholds per use case, and provision\nsecondary quota before launch. Measure fallback rate by rung, success rate after fallback, latency by\nrung, the cost delta during failover and the quality of fallback-served responses on your eval. In\ninterviews, distinguish retry, fallback and degradation cleanly, then talk about error classification,\nbreakers, deadline propagation and equivalence testing; that sequence is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements error classification, breakers and deadline propagation, tags responses with the serving target, and writes the game-day test that blocks the primary on purpose.","architect":"Defines the ladder per use case under governance constraints, provisions secondary quota and hedging policy, and sets the SLO the fallback path itself must meet.","fde":"Builds the ladder from models and regions the customer has approved, often inside their cloud platform, and runs a failover drill with them before go-live."},"check":{"q":"Your primary provider had a 40-minute outage. The fallback to a second vendor \"worked\" and the\nerror rate stayed flat, but the next morning support reports a spike in malformed outputs and several\ncustomers received duplicate emails from the agent. What went wrong, and how do you fix it?","a":"Two independent failures. First, the fallback model was never validated for these prompts: its\ntool-call format or JSON adherence differs, so outputs that passed through unvalidated were malformed.\nFix with schema validation that runs regardless of model, per-model prompt variants where formats\ndiffer, a permanent small slice of traffic through the fallback, and a quality metric split by serving\ntarget. Second, the agent retried or failed over after a tool had already executed, replaying the\nsend-email step. Fix with idempotency keys on tool calls, a rule that non-idempotent steps are never\nretried blindly, and checkpointed agent state so the fallback resumes after the completed action. Add an\nalert on fallback share so someone is watching during the outage, not the morning after."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which error should not trigger failover to another provider?","options":["A 503 from the primary endpoint","A timeout after the per-attempt budget expired","A 400 invalid-request error or a content-policy refusal","A 429 after the Retry-After period was honoured once"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Invalid requests and policy refusals are properties of the request, not the provider, so every rung returns the same error; failing over only multiplies latency and cost. The other three are capacity or transient failures that a different target can resolve."},{"q":"What does a per-target circuit breaker add to a fallback ladder?","options":["It lowers the token cost of fallback calls","It skips a failing target immediately instead of waiting out its timeout on every request, and stops hammering it","It guarantees identical outputs across targets","It removes the need for retries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a breaker, each request during an outage pays the primary's full timeout before falling back, and the retries add load to a provider that is already struggling. The breaker turns a slow failure into a fast one."},{"q":"Which statement about hedged requests is correct?","options":["They halve the cost of the request","They guarantee the p99 equals the p95","They trim tail latency for a few per cent extra calls and must be disabled when the primary is overloaded","They are safe for calls with non-idempotent side effects"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sending a second request after the p95 delay helps only the slow tail and costs roughly the fraction of requests that exceed that delay. During provider overload the extra load makes things worse, and duplicated side effects make hedging unsafe for non-idempotent calls."}]},{"key":"system-design/graceful-degradation","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Nygard, 2007 — Release It!"],"why":"Most AI features are enhancements on a product that worked before them: search, support, editors,\ndashboards. A model incident, an exhausted rate limit, a runaway cost day or a traffic spike will happen.\nIf the feature's failure also breaks the workflow around it, you have turned an enhancement into a new\nfailure point and lowered the availability of the whole product. Users who could have found the document\nthemselves now see a spinner.\n\nThe naive responses each miss something. \"Retry and show an error\" makes the user wait and then gives\nthem nothing. \"Fall back to another model\" handles a provider outage but not overload, budget exhaustion\nor a latency SLO that the fallback also misses. \"Turn the feature off\" is a single binary switch, so the\nsmallest incident costs the whole feature. There is also an AI-specific failure the others ignore: a\nresponse that was produced but should not be trusted, because the grounding check failed or the judge\nrejected it. The right move there is to show less and say why, not to fail.\n\nLoad shedding and priority classes came from site reliability engineering; AI systems add a new axis,\nquality levels, because the same request can be answered well, adequately, or not at all, at different\ncosts. Degradation is the discipline of deciding those levels before the incident.","how":"### Define the ladder per feature\n- **Level 0, full:** the strong model, tools, reranking, a verifier.\n- **Level 1, reduced:** a smaller model, fewer retrieved passages, no reranker, a lower `max_tokens`,\n  agent loops capped at one step. Visibly still an answer; cheaper and faster.\n- **Level 2, cached or precomputed:** a cached answer to a similar question, a precomputed summary, an\n  FAQ entry, scoped to the same tenant.\n- **Level 3, non-AI:** keyword search results, a template, the raw documents, a form. The product as it\n  was before the feature.\n- **Level 4, honest unavailability:** a clear message, the rest of the product working, and a way to\n  proceed (a human handoff, a link, try later).\nFor each level write down what the user sees, what it costs, and who gets it.\n\n### Triggers\nDependency health (breaker state, error rate, p95 latency), load (queue depth, concurrency), budget burn\n(spend today against the cap), rate-limit headroom, quality signals (a failed grounding check demotes\n*this* response to sources-only), and a human-operated kill switch. Evaluate triggers where the decision\nis made and with hysteresis: step down quickly on a bad signal, step back up only after the signal has\nbeen healthy for a sustained period, *because* flapping between levels is worse for users than staying\none level down. Concretely that means separate enter and exit thresholds plus a minimum dwell time: for\nexample (illustrative numbers), enter Reduced when p95 exceeds 4 s, and leave it only after p95 has\nstayed under 2.5 s for ten minutes. The gap between 4 s and 2.5 s is what stops a signal hovering near\none threshold from toggling the level on every sample.\n\nBudget triggers deserve the same care. Trigger on projected burn, not on spend so far:\n`spent + current_rate × hours_left > cap` fires mid-morning on a heavy day and lets you step down one\nlevel gently, whereas `spent ≥ cap` fires late in the day and drops every user straight to the cached\nlevel for the rest of it.\n\n### Shed by priority, isolate by class\nClassify requests: interactive versus batch, paying versus free, critical versus nice-to-have. Shed\nbatch and low tiers first and keep a reserved share of capacity for critical interactive traffic in its\nown bulkhead, so a batch backfill can never starve the chat box. Shed at admission, before any tokens\nare spent: a request abandoned after generation has started has already paid for the work it throws\naway, and under overload that waste is exactly the capacity you were short of.\n\n### Deadlines and partial results\nGive each request a deadline and return what you have when it expires: retrieved passages without the\nsynthesis, a streamed partial answer with a visible marker. Streaming makes partial output useful rather\nthan confusing, but partial answers are dangerous where a half-statement misleads; there, sources-only is\nthe right degraded form.\n\n### Honesty in the interface\nLabel degraded output (\"Showing matching documents; the AI summary is unavailable\"). Never present a\nLevel 1 answer as Level 0 when the quality difference matters, since users act on outputs and silent\ndegradation transfers the risk to them.\n\n### Keep the degraded paths alive\nA non-AI path that nobody runs rots: its index goes stale, its credentials expire, its flag is removed.\nSend a small share of traffic through each level routinely, run synthetic checks against them, and hold\ngame days. Every level sits behind a flag that flips without a deploy, and the runbook names the flags.\n\n### Measure\nTime spent at each level, requests served per level, user outcomes per level (completion rate, handoff\nrate), and time to recover to full. \"Percentage of sessions served at Level 0\" is a good SLO.","example":"A support-answer endpoint with a degradation ladder. A background task refreshes `h`, a health snapshot,\nevery few seconds; the handler picks a level, enforces a deadline, and steps down rather than raising\nwhen a stage fails. `keyword_or_vector_search`, `generate`, `semantic_cache_lookup` and `ModelError` are\nyour adapters.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nfrom enum import IntEnum\n\nclass Level(IntEnum):\n    FULL = 0; REDUCED = 1; CACHED = 2; NON_AI = 3; UNAVAILABLE = 4\n\ndef current_level(h):                            # h: health snapshot refreshed by a background task\n    if h.kill_switch:                                           return Level.NON_AI\n    if h.budget_used >= 1.0 or h.llm_breaker_open:              return Level.CACHED\n    if h.budget_used >= 0.8 or h.llm_p95_ms > 4000 or h.queue_depth > 200:\n        return Level.REDUCED\n    return Level.FULL\n\nasync def answer(q, user, h, deadline_s=6.0):\n    level = current_level(h)\n    if user.tier == \"free\" and h.queue_depth > 50:              # shed low priority first\n        level = max(level, Level.REDUCED)\n    t0 = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        docs = await keyword_or_vector_search(q, k=20 if level == Level.FULL else 5)\n    except Exception:                                           # even the non-AI path is down\n        docs, level = [], Level.UNAVAILABLE\n    if level <= Level.REDUCED:\n        full = level == Level.FULL\n        try:\n            remaining = deadline_s - (time.monotonic() - t0)\n            text = await asyncio.wait_for(\n                generate(\"strong\" if full else \"small\", q, docs if full else docs[:3],\n                         max_tokens=600 if full else 200), remaining)\n            return {\"level\": int(level), \"answer\": text, \"sources\": docs[:5],\n                    \"label\": None if full else \"Reduced-quality answer\"}\n        except (asyncio.TimeoutError, ModelError):\n            level = Level.CACHED                                # step down, do not raise\n    if level == Level.CACHED:\n        hit = await semantic_cache_lookup(q, tenant=user.tenant)\n        if hit:\n            return {\"level\": 2, \"answer\": hit.answer, \"sources\": hit.sources, \"label\": \"Cached answer\"}\n        level = Level.NON_AI\n    if level == Level.NON_AI:\n        return {\"level\": 3, \"answer\": None, \"sources\": docs[:5],\n                \"label\": \"AI assistant unavailable; showing matching documents\"}\n    return {\"level\": 4, \"answer\": None, \"sources\": [], \"label\": \"Search is temporarily unavailable\"}\n```\n\nThe `level` field in every response is the metric: a histogram of it over time is the dashboard that\nshows whether the product has been quietly running degraded. `current_level` is stateless for brevity;\nthe hysteresis belongs in the background task that builds `h`, which should hold a level until its exit\ncondition has persisted for the dwell time, so every request in a window sees the same level. Note also\nthe order of the ladder: each step down is cheaper than the one above it, and a failure inside a level\nmoves the request down rather than retrying, so an incident lowers spend instead of raising it.","tradeoffs":["The product survives incidents, but you maintain several code paths and the eval for each; when the AI feature *is* the product, degrade within AI levels and then be honest, since no non-AI path exists.","Honest labelling preserves trust but makes degradation visible to users and competitors; where outputs are acted on, visibility is the correct default and the trade is rarely worth reversing.","Priority shedding protects paying and critical users at the expense of the free tier, which may be the acquisition funnel; if growth is the KPI, shed batch work before shedding free users.","Partial results at the deadline beat nothing, but a half answer can mislead; in medical, legal or financial contexts return sources only rather than a truncated synthesis.","Eager triggers save cost and latency, but set too low they keep the product one level down most of the day without anyone noticing; hysteresis thresholds need the same review as the SLO."],"pitfalls":["Degraded paths never exercised; the symptom is the non-AI path failing with an expired credential or stale index at the exact moment of the incident.","Silent degradation with no time-in-level metric; the symptom is weeks of reduced answers discovered from quality complaints or a bill that dropped for no known reason.","Flapping between levels; the symptom is a user getting a fast reduced answer, then a slow full one, then a cached one within a single session, which reads as randomness.","Degrading to something more expensive, such as retries or a larger fallback model; the symptom is cost rising during the incident instead of falling.","A kill switch that requires a deploy; the symptom is a 40-minute mitigation for a 2-minute decision.","Triggers measured globally when load is per tenant; the symptom is one noisy tenant degrading everyone, or nobody being degraded while that tenant's queue grows unbounded."],"when":"Design the ladder for any user-facing AI feature with an SLO, at the same time as the non-functional\nrequirements, because the levels shape the architecture (what stays deterministic, where caches live,\nwhich flags exist). Offline batch work usually needs only retry-later. Agree the degraded behaviour with\nproduct and, in customer deployments, with the customer, before go-live. Measure time per level,\noutcomes per level and recovery time, and set an SLO on the share of sessions served at full quality. In\ninterviews, distinguish degradation from fallback, describe the ladder, the triggers with hysteresis,\npriority shedding, honest labelling and the discipline of exercising the degraded paths.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements levels as flagged code paths with deadlines, emits the level on every response, and keeps a test per level in CI so the non-AI path cannot rot unnoticed.","architect":"Writes the ladder and priority classes into the NFRs, decides what \"acceptable degraded\" means per feature, and owns the kill-switch and hysteresis policy.","fde":"Agrees with the customer what their users will see when the model is down, configures the tenant-level triggers, and runs the drill before launch."},"check":{"q":"A customer says \"the assistant must never show an error\". Design the degradation for a support\nassistant and say what you would push back on.","a":"Propose a ladder: full RAG with the strong model; reduced with a small model and three passages;\ncached answers scoped to the tenant; search results with canned links; and a human handoff form.\nTriggers come from breaker state, p95 latency, queue depth and the daily budget, with hysteresis so the\nlevel steps down fast and recovers slowly; free-tier and batch traffic shed first; every level labelled\nin the interface and recorded as a metric. Push back on \"never\": at the bottom of the ladder the product\nmust show a clear message and a path to a human, because an unlabelled, ungrounded answer is worse than\nan honest unavailability and the customer will own the consequences of it. Replace the absolute with an\nSLO, for example the share of sessions served at full quality, and agree the drill that proves each level\nworks."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between a fallback and graceful degradation?","options":["Fallbacks are automatic, degradation is manual","A fallback keeps the same function on a different target; degradation changes the function to a reduced, honest form","Fallbacks apply to models, degradation applies only to databases","They are the same thing with different names"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Switching provider or region preserves what the user gets. Degradation steps down to a smaller model, a cache, a non-AI path or a clear message. Both can be automatic and both apply to any dependency."},{"q":"Why do degradation triggers need hysteresis?","options":["To reduce the cost of the health checks","To make the full level recover as fast as possible","To stop the system flapping between levels, stepping down quickly but stepping up only after sustained health","To ensure free-tier users are always degraded first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A trigger that flips on every noisy sample produces alternating fast and slow, reduced and full answers inside one session. Asymmetric thresholds keep the system stable one level down until the signal has genuinely recovered."},{"q":"Which practice most determines whether degradation actually works during an incident?","options":["Choosing the largest available fallback model","Increasing timeouts so more requests complete","Routinely exercising every degraded path and controlling levels with flags that need no deploy","Adding more retries before stepping down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unused paths rot and deploy-gated switches are slow. Routine traffic through each level, synthetic checks and runtime flags are what make the ladder real; bigger models, longer timeouts and more retries typically make the incident worse."}]},{"key":"system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic prompt caching","Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: GPTCache"],"why":"A cache hit returns in milliseconds and costs almost nothing; a miss takes seconds and bills every token.\nPrompts are dominated by content that repeats across requests: a system prompt, tool definitions,\nfew-shot examples and retrieved documents can be thousands of tokens that are identical for every user\nof a feature. That makes caching the single biggest lever on cost and latency after model choice.\n\nThe naive approach, \"hash the prompt and cache the response\", fails in ways specific to AI systems.\nOutputs are sampled, so caching only makes sense when you want the same answer and the key must include\nthe sampling parameters. Prompts embed context, so an identical question from two tenants, or from the\nsame tenant a week apart, can require different correct answers. Semantic near-duplicates look like hits\nand differ in one decisive detail: a negation, a date, an account number. And the things that make a\ncached answer wrong are numerous and silent: the provider updates the model, someone edits the prompt\ntemplate, a document is re-ingested, the embedding model changes, an external tool's data moves.\n\nWithout a deliberate strategy teams land in one of two bad places: caching nothing and paying for every\nrepeated token, or caching eagerly and serving stale or wrong answers that cost more trust than the\ntokens saved. Both of the famous hard problems, naming things and invalidating caches, turn out to be the\nsame problem here: the name of the cache entry is the list of everything the answer depends on.","how":"### Layer 1: prefix and prompt caching (caching computation)\nA provider or a self-hosted engine reuses the KV cache for a byte-identical token prefix across requests,\nso the prefill of the shared part is not recomputed. The key is the exact prefix; any change *before or\nwithin* the cached span invalidates everything after it, because each cached key and value depends on all\nearlier positions. Design consequence: put static content first (system prompt, tools, few-shot, long\ndocuments) and dynamic content last, keep the ordering stable, and never inject a timestamp or the user's\nname near the top. Providers bill cached input tokens at a lower rate with short time-to-live windows,\nsome charge a premium for the cache *write*, and most enforce a minimum cacheable prefix length of\nroughly a thousand tokens or more, so a short system prompt alone may never cache (all figures are\nvendor- and model-specific and change); engines such as vLLM offer automatic prefix caching across\nrequests that share a prefix. The metric is cached-token share in usage reports, checked after every\nchange to prompt assembly, because a regression there is silent: requests keep succeeding and only the\nbill moves.\n\n### Layer 2: exact response cache\nKey = hash of model id and version, sampling parameters, the full normalised message list, tool schemas,\nresponse format, and the tenant or user scope whenever the prompt contains private context. Value =\nthe response plus its usage. Only sensible for deterministic intent (temperature 0, or a product that\naccepts identical answers to identical requests). Hit rates in open-ended chat are low; in form-like,\nclassification and extraction features, where identical inputs recur, they are high.\n\n### Layer 3: semantic cache\nEmbed the query, search cached queries within the same scope (tenant, prompt version, index version),\nand return the cached answer when similarity exceeds a threshold. The gain is a hit rate exact matching\ncannot reach; the risk is a paraphrase that is actually a different question. Mitigations: a high\nthreshold; scope keys; excluding or entity-keying queries that contain numbers, dates, names or\nnegations; a cheap verifier on borderline hits (\"does this cached answer address this query?\"); and a\nsampled false-hit audit. The economics: savings = hit_rate × (cost_miss − cost_hit) − false_hit_rate ×\ncost_of_a_wrong_answer − embedding cost per lookup. The last two terms are what people forget.\n\n### Layer 4: embedding cache\nKey = hash of embedding model id plus normalised text. Cheap and high-value in ingestion (unchanged\nchunks are not re-embedded on re-index) and for repeated queries. A new embedding model invalidates\neverything, which is fine *because* you must re-embed the whole index anyway; version the index.\n\n### Layer 5: retrieval and tool caches\nQuery → document ids, keyed by index version; external tool results with a TTL set by data volatility\n(exchange rates seconds, a product catalogue hours); memoised agent steps keyed by tool name and\narguments.\n\n### Read and write patterns\nMost AI response caches are **cache-aside**: the application looks up the key, calls the model on a\nmiss and writes the result. That keeps the cache optional (a cache outage means slower, not broken), at\nthe price of the stampede problem handled below. A gateway cache is effectively **read-through**:\ncallers do not know it exists, which is convenient and is also why it cannot see tenants or document\nversions nobody put in the request. **Precomputation** (answers for the few hundred most frequent\nquestions generated by a batch job, summaries written at ingestion) moves model cost off the request\npath entirely and suits features whose question set is predictable. **Stale-while-revalidate** serves\nan expired entry while one background call refreshes it, trading a bounded extra window of staleness for\nnever making a user wait on a hot key; it is wrong for content where stale means incorrect, such as\nprices or permissions. Eviction under a memory cap is usually LRU or LFU; both ignore that entries\ndiffer greatly in what they cost to recompute, so a long-context answer is worth more cache space than a\nshort classification.\n\n### Invalidation strategies\n1. **Versioned keys.** Include the model id, prompt template version, index version and embedding model\n   id in every key. Changing any of them yields a cold cache with no purge logic at all. Simplest and\n   most robust; the cost is cold misses after each deploy, which a replay of recent queries can pre-warm.\n2. **TTL.** Bounds staleness per data class; refresh ahead of expiry for hot keys; jitter expiries so\n   they do not align.\n3. **Event-driven purge.** When a document changes, purge the answers that used it. Needs a reverse\n   index from document id to cache keys, which the generation step must write. Precise, more complex,\n   and required when a deleted document must stop appearing in answers (erasure requests). It has a\n   race: a generation that read the old document can finish *after* the purge and write a stale answer\n   back. Close it by storing the version (a content hash) of each document an answer used alongside the\n   answer, and treating a hit as a miss when any of those versions is no longer current.\n4. **Scope isolation.** Tenant and user in the key wherever the prompt carries private context; a shared\n   cache across trust boundaries is a leak, not an optimisation.\n5. **Do not cache failures.** Errors, refusals, truncated streams and low-judge-score answers are not\n   cached, or an outage gets remembered after it ends.\n6. **Stampede control.** Single-flight locking or request coalescing on popular keys so a thousand\n   simultaneous misses produce one model call.\n\n### Where each layer lives\nPrefix caching lives in the provider or engine. Exact and semantic caches can live in a gateway, which\nis convenient but context-blind, or in the application, which knows tenants and document versions and is\nusually the better place. Observe hit rate, cost saved and latency saved per layer, sampled false-hit\nrate, and the age of served entries.","example":"An exact-match response cache in Redis with versioned, tenant-scoped keys, single-flight on misses, a\nrule against caching partial or refused answers, and a reverse index that lets the ingestion pipeline\npurge every cached answer that used a document when that document changes.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time, redis\nr = redis.Redis()\nVERSIONS = {\"prompt\": \"support-v14\", \"index\": \"kb-2026-10-19\", \"embed\": \"text-embed-v2\"}\n\ndef exact_key(tenant, model, params, messages):\n    body = json.dumps({\"v\": VERSIONS, \"t\": tenant, \"m\": model, \"p\": params, \"msgs\": messages},\n                      sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"))      # canonical form: same input, same hash\n    return \"resp:\" + hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()\n\ndef cached_complete(tenant, model, messages, params={\"temperature\": 0}, ttl=6 * 3600, tries=10):\n    k = exact_key(tenant, model, messages=messages, params=params)\n    if (hit := r.get(k)):\n        return json.loads(hit) | {\"cache\": \"exact\"}\n    if not r.set(k + \":lock\", \"1\", nx=True, ex=30):              # single-flight: one caller fills the key\n        if tries == 0:\n            return call_model(model, messages, **params) | {\"cache\": \"bypass\"}\n        time.sleep(0.2)\n        return cached_complete(tenant, model, messages, params, ttl, tries - 1)\n    try:\n        out = call_model(model, messages, **params)\n        if out[\"finish_reason\"] == \"stop\" and not out.get(\"refusal\"):   # never cache partial or refused\n            r.set(k, json.dumps(out), ex=ttl)\n            for doc_id in out.get(\"source_doc_ids\", []):             # reverse index for event-driven purge\n                r.sadd(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\", k)\n                r.expire(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\", ttl)\n        return out | {\"cache\": \"miss\"}\n    finally:\n        r.delete(k + \":lock\")\n\ndef on_document_updated(tenant, doc_id):                         # called by the ingestion pipeline\n    keys = r.smembers(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\")\n    if keys:\n        r.delete(*keys)\n    r.delete(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\")\n```\n\nBumping `VERSIONS[\"prompt\"]` at deploy time cold-starts the cache without any purge code; the reverse\nindex handles the case versioning cannot, a single document changing under an unchanged prompt. Two\nsimplifications to remove before production. The lock is released unconditionally, so a model call that\noutlives the lock's 30 s expiry deletes a lock another caller now holds; store a random token in the\nlock and delete only if it still matches (a small Lua compare-and-delete). And the purge has the\nin-flight race described above, which needs the document versions stored with each answer and checked\non read.","tradeoffs":["Looser keys and lower similarity thresholds raise the hit rate and the false-hit rate together; the right point depends on what a wrong answer costs, which differs between an FAQ bot and a billing assistant.","Longer TTLs save more and serve staler data; acceptable when you also have event-driven purge for the content that matters, dangerous when TTL is the only invalidation you have.","Versioned keys are simple and safe but every deploy cold-starts the cache; when the cache is what meets the latency SLO, add a warm-up replay or a brief dual-version window.","Prefix caching needs a static-first prompt layout, which constrains where personalisation can go; it flips only in the rare design where per-user content must precede the shared instructions.","A global cache maximises hits and is a leak surface; a per-tenant cache is safer with a lower hit rate; the trust boundary, not the hit rate, decides.","Event-driven purge is precise but requires dependency tracking storage and hooks in ingestion; TTL is cheap but can only bound staleness, never eliminate it."],"pitfalls":["Caching across tenants or users; the symptom is a user seeing another customer's data inside an answer, which is a security incident rather than a bug.","Dynamic content at the top of the prompt; the symptom is a cached-token share near zero while prompt caching is \"enabled\" and the bill is unchanged.","Semantic false hits; the symptom is confident wrong answers whose logs show a cache hit with high similarity to a question that differed by a negation or a number.","Leaving the model or prompt version out of the key; the symptom is half of users seeing the old behaviour for hours after a prompt change, and contaminated A/B results.","Caching errors, refusals or partial streams; the symptom is users still receiving an error message long after the provider recovered.","Stampedes on expiry; the symptom is periodic latency and cost spikes aligned with TTL multiples.","Purging without guarding against in-flight generations; the symptom is a stale answer reappearing minutes after a purge that logged success, typically when a document is edited during busy hours.","A deleted document still answered from cache; the symptom is a compliance finding after an erasure request that the index honoured and the cache did not."],"when":"Prefix caching is always worth having and is mostly a matter of prompt layout. An exact response cache\npays off for form-like, classification and extraction traffic with deterministic settings. A semantic\ncache suits high-volume FAQ-like traffic where a measured false-hit rate is tolerable, and should be\navoided for personalised or transactional queries. An embedding cache belongs in every ingestion\npipeline. Measure hit rate per layer, cost and latency saved, sampled false-hit rate and the age of served\nentries, and treat erasure requests as a cache concern. In interviews, lead with the key: list what the\nanswer depends on, show how versions make invalidation trivial, and explain when you still need\nevent-driven purge.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the versioned key function, scoping, single-flight and metrics, and lays prompts out static-first so provider prefix caching actually hits.","architect":"Decides which layers exist and where they live, the staleness budget per data class, the cross-tenant policy and the purge obligations that compliance imposes.","fde":"Tunes thresholds and TTLs to the customer's data volatility, demonstrates the cost saving with before-and-after usage, and verifies that erasure requests purge cached answers."},"check":{"q":"You added a semantic cache and cost fell by 30%. Two weeks later a customer reports that the\nassistant stated a policy that changed last week. Diagnose and redesign.","a":"The cache served a stale answer, and there are several candidate causes to check in the logs: a\nTTL longer than the policy document's change cycle, no event-driven purge when the document was\nre-ingested, an index version missing from the cache scope so the old answer survived the re-index, or a\nsemantic hit on a paraphrase of a question about the updated section. The redesign: put the index\nversion in the scope key so re-indexing cold-starts the relevant entries; have generation record the\ndocument ids it used and purge dependents on document update; set TTLs per content class, short for\npolicy; verify borderline semantic hits with a cheap check or exclude queries mentioning dates and policy\nnames; sample hits for a false-hit rate; and show the source freshness on cached answers so users can\nsee what the answer was based on."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the simplest robust way to invalidate every cached response when the prompt template changes?","options":["Flush the whole cache by hand after each deploy","Reduce the TTL to one minute","Include the prompt template version in every cache key","Rely on semantic similarity to notice that the prompt changed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Versioned keys make the old entries unreachable the moment the version changes, with no purge code and no risk of forgetting. A short TTL still serves stale answers for a minute and destroys the hit rate; manual flushes get skipped."},{"q":"Why does placing a timestamp at the top of the system prompt hurt?","options":["It increases the token count significantly","It changes the prefix on every request, so provider or engine prefix caching never matches","Models ignore timestamps and it is wasted space","Providers reject prompts that contain dates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefix caching requires a byte-identical prefix from the first token. A changing value near the top invalidates the entire shared span after it, which shows up as a cached-token share near zero."},{"q":"Which query pair is the biggest false-hit risk for a semantic cache?","options":["\"How do I cancel my subscription?\" and \"how do i cancel my subcription\" (typo)","\"How do I cancel my subscription?\" and \"How do I stop my subscription being cancelled?\"","\"What is your refund policy?\" and \"Tell me about refunds\"","\"Reset my password\" and \"I forgot my password\""],"answer":1,"explanation":"The second pair is nearly identical in surface form and embedding space but opposite in intent, so a cached answer to one is harmful for the other. The other pairs are genuine paraphrases, which is exactly what a semantic cache is meant to match."}]},{"key":"system-design/rag-architecture","sources":["Lewis et al., 2020 — Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","Cormack et al., 2009 — Reciprocal Rank Fusion outperforms Condorcet and individual Rank Learning Methods","Liu et al., 2023 — Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts"],"why":"A language model answers from two places: its weights and its context window. The weights are a\ncompressed snapshot of public text at training time. They do not contain your pricing table, last week's\nincident report or the customer's contract, and they cannot be told that user A may see document X and\nuser B may not. Fine-tuning does not fix this: it teaches style and format reliably and facts\nunreliably, it is stale the moment a document changes, and it has no notion of provenance or per-user\naccess, so you can neither cite a source nor revoke one.\n\nThe context window is the other place, and the naive design is to put everything there. For a handful\nof pages that is the right call, and the long-context-vs-RAG lesson covers when. It stops working for\nthree reasons. Cost and latency scale with input tokens on every request, so a 500-page knowledge base\nread on every question is unaffordable even when it fits. Attention quality degrades when relevant\nmaterial is buried in the middle of a very long prompt (the lost-in-the-middle effect), so more context\ncan mean worse answers. And access control still has to happen somewhere before the prompt is built.\n\nRetrieval solves the selection problem: pick the few passages that matter for this query, for this\nuser, right now, and let the model do what it is good at, which is reading and synthesizing. The\narchitecture exists to make that selection fast, fresh, permission-aware and measurable.","how":"### The offline pipeline: documents to index\n1. **Ingest.** Connectors pull documents from sources (wikis, tickets, object storage, databases)\n   together with metadata: owner, ACL, timestamps, source URL. Ingestion is event- or schedule-driven\n   *because* freshness is a property of this path and nothing downstream can repair a stale index.\n2. **Parse and normalize.** PDFs, HTML and slides become clean text with structure preserved (headings,\n   tables). This is where most silent quality loss happens; garbage in here is unrecoverable at query\n   time.\n3. **Chunk.** Text is split into retrieval units, typically a few hundred tokens, with overlap or\n   parent–child linking. Chunks are the unit of retrieval *because* an embedding summarizes one idea well\n   and a whole document badly, and because you want to pay for a paragraph of context, not a chapter.\n4. **Embed and index.** Each chunk gets a dense vector from an embedding model and usually a sparse\n   (BM25) representation too. Both are stored with the chunk text and metadata in a store that supports\n   approximate nearest-neighbour search plus filtering. Record the embedding model version with every\n   vector *because* vectors from different models are not comparable, so a model upgrade means a\n   re-index.\n\n### The online pipeline: query to answer\n1. **Query understanding.** Optionally rewrite the query: expand acronyms, resolve \"it\" from the\n   conversation, split multi-part questions. The raw user message is often a poor search query.\n2. **Retrieve.** Run dense and sparse search with metadata and ACL filters applied *inside* the search,\n   not after, *because* filtering after top-k can leave a user with zero permitted results and leaks the\n   existence of forbidden ones. Fuse the ranked lists; reciprocal rank fusion is the common default:\n   `score(d) = Σ_lists 1 / (k + rank_list(d))` with k ≈ 60. It fuses *ranks*, not raw scores,\n   *because* a BM25 score and a cosine similarity live on different scales and cannot be added. With\n   k = 60, a chunk ranked 1st by dense search only scores 1/61 ≈ 0.0164, while a chunk ranked 3rd in\n   both lists scores 2/63 ≈ 0.0317: agreement between two independent retrievers beats a single\n   strong vote, which is exactly the behaviour you want from fusion. The large k flattens the curve so\n   that rank 1 versus rank 5 matters little and presence in a list matters a lot.\n3. **Rerank.** A cross-encoder scores (query, chunk) pairs jointly and reorders the top 50–100\n   candidates. First-stage retrieval optimizes recall cheaply; the reranker buys precision for the\n   handful of chunks you will actually pay to put in the prompt.\n4. **Assemble context.** Select the top chunks under a token budget, drop near-duplicates, order them\n   deliberately (most relevant first or last, never buried), and attach citation handles.\n5. **Generate.** The prompt instructs the model to answer only from the provided passages and to cite\n   them; structured output can enforce the citation format. A grounding check (does each claim map to\n   a passage?) can run as a post-step.\n6. **Observe.** Log the query, retrieved ids, scores, the final prompt and the answer under one trace\n   id. Without this you cannot distinguish \"retrieval missed\" from \"model ignored the passage\", and\n   those are fixed in different places.\n\n### Single-shot versus iterative retrieval\nThe pipeline above retrieves once per question, which is right for most lookups and keeps latency and\ncost predictable. It fails on multi-hop questions (\"which of our customers on the legacy plan were\naffected by last week's incident?\") because the second hop's query depends on the first hop's answer,\nand no single embedding of the original question finds both. The architectural answer is to expose\n`search` as a tool and let a model call it in a loop (agentic RAG), or to run a fixed decompose →\nretrieve-per-sub-question → merge chain. Both multiply retrieval calls and latency per question, so\nroute to them only when a classifier or the first pass signals that one retrieval was not enough.\n\n### Where the boundaries go\nTreat retrieval as its own service with a typed interface, roughly `search(query, user, filters, k)\n-> [chunk]`, *because* it is shared by chat, agents and batch jobs, it has its own scaling profile\n(CPU- and IO-bound, cacheable), and it is the component you evaluate independently with recall@k. Keep\nthe index derived, never authoritative: the source systems stay the source of truth and the index is a\nrebuildable cache, which is what makes re-chunking and re-embedding a routine job rather than a\nmigration.\n\n### Sizing intuition (illustrative)\n50,000 documents at 20 chunks each is 1,000,000 chunks. A 1024-dimension fp32 vector is 4 KB, so raw\nvectors alone are about 4 GB before the ANN index and the chunk text; this is why pgvector is\ncomfortable at this scale and a dedicated vector database earns its keep one or two orders of magnitude\nlater. Retrieving 50 candidates and keeping 8 chunks of roughly 300 tokens puts about 2,400 tokens of\nevidence in the prompt, a fixed cost per question regardless of corpus size, which is the whole\neconomic argument for retrieval over stuffing.","example":"A compact version of the online path: hybrid retrieval with reciprocal rank fusion, a cross-encoder\nreranker, and token-budgeted context assembly with the ACL filter inside the search. The `store`,\n`reranker`, `embed` and `log_trace` calls stand in for your vector database, BM25 index, embedding model\nand tracing; the shape of the pipeline is what matters.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef rrf(ranked_lists, k=60):\n    \"\"\"Reciprocal rank fusion: ids near the top of any list win.\"\"\"\n    score = defaultdict(float)\n    for lst in ranked_lists:\n        for rank, chunk_id in enumerate(lst, start=1):\n            score[chunk_id] += 1.0 / (k + rank)\n    return sorted(score, key=score.get, reverse=True)\n\ndef retrieve(query, user, store, reranker, k_dense=40, k_sparse=40, budget_tokens=2400):\n    acl = {\"tenant\": user.tenant, \"groups\": user.groups}          # filter inside the search\n    dense = store.vector_search(embed(query), k=k_dense, filters=acl)\n    sparse = store.bm25_search(query, k=k_sparse, filters=acl)\n    candidates = rrf([dense, sparse])[:60]\n    chunks = store.fetch(candidates)                                # text + metadata\n    scores = reranker.score(query, [c.text for c in chunks])       # joint (query, chunk) scoring\n    ordered = [c for _, c in sorted(zip(scores, chunks), key=lambda p: -p[0])]\n    context, used = [], 0\n    for c in ordered:                                               # dedupe step omitted for brevity\n        if used + c.tokens > budget_tokens:\n            break\n        context.append(c); used += c.tokens\n    return context\n\ndef answer(query, user, store, reranker, llm):\n    ctx = retrieve(query, user, store, reranker)\n    passages = \"\\n\\n\".join(f\"[{i+1}] ({c.source}) {c.text}\" for i, c in enumerate(ctx))\n    prompt = (\"Answer using only the passages below and cite passage numbers. \"\n              \"If the passages do not contain the answer, say so.\\n\\n\"\n              f\"{passages}\\n\\nQuestion: {query}\")\n    out = llm(prompt)\n    log_trace(query=query, chunk_ids=[c.id for c in ctx], answer=out)   # one trace id per request\n    return out\n```","tradeoffs":["Fresh, citable, permission-aware answers versus an extra retrieval hop on every request; when the corpus is small and static enough to fit the window cheaply, long context wins and the index is overhead.","Hybrid search plus reranking raises answer quality measurably, but adds two more models and services to operate; if queries are dominated by exact identifiers (SKUs, error codes), sparse retrieval alone may be enough.","Small chunks improve retrieval precision while losing surrounding context; parent–document retrieval buys both at the cost of a second lookup and more prompt tokens.","A derived, rebuildable index makes re-embedding routine, but consistency with sources is eventual; for data where staleness is a correctness bug (prices, permissions) you need synchronous invalidation or a live lookup tool instead.","Running retrieval as a separate service gives independent scaling and evaluation but introduces a network boundary and a contract that must be versioned."],"pitfalls":["Filtering by ACL after retrieval instead of inside it; the symptom is users getting \"no results\" for questions the system clearly has documents about, or worse, a forbidden title appearing in a citation.","Treating parsing as solved; the symptom is retrieval that returns the right document whose chunk text is a mangled table, so the model answers confidently and wrongly.","Upgrading the embedding model without re-indexing everything; the symptom is a sudden recall collapse that only affects documents ingested after the upgrade, or only those before it.","Judging the system by end-to-end answer quality alone; without recall@k on a labeled query set you cannot tell retrieval failures from generation failures, and you end up tuning prompts to fix an index problem.","Raising the number of chunks in the prompt to paper over retrieval misses; the symptom is cost per question climbing while faithfulness falls, because the right passage is now buried among near-misses and the model blends them.","Letting the index become the system of record (hand-edited chunks, data that exists nowhere else); the symptom is a re-chunking project that nobody dares to run."],"when":"Reach for RAG when answers must come from content that is private, changing, large or access-controlled,\nand when users need citations they can check. Do not reach for it when the knowledge is small and stable\n(a long system prompt with prompt caching is simpler), when the task is precise querying of structured\ndata (give the model a SQL or API tool), or when the \"knowledge\" is really behaviour (prompt or\nfine-tune). Measure retrieval and generation separately: recall@k and reranker precision on a golden\nquery set, plus faithfulness of answers to the retrieved passages. Day to day the architecture shows up\nas two deployables with different cadences, indexing jobs and a query service, sharing one contract. In\ninterviews, the expected depth is to draw both pipelines, place the ACL filter and the reranker\ncorrectly, and explain why the index is derived rather than authoritative.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the chunk-embed-index jobs and the query service, wires hybrid search and reranking, and owns the retrieval eval harness that catches regressions before users do.","architect":"Decides the service boundary, the vector store (pgvector versus dedicated), the freshness and consistency model, and how tenancy and ACLs are enforced inside search.","fde":"Maps a customer's document sources, permissions and update frequency onto the pipeline, and explains why parsing quality and ACL propagation dominate the project timeline."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports that the assistant answers policy questions correctly for most employees but\ntells contractors \"I don't have information on that\", even for public policies. Where in the RAG\narchitecture would you look first, and why?","a":"The pattern, correct for one group and empty for another on the same public content, points at\naccess control interacting with retrieval, not at the model. The most likely cause is post-filtering:\nthe search returns a global top-k, then ACL filtering removes everything contractors cannot see, and\nbecause employees' private documents outrank the public policy, contractors are left with nothing. The\nfix is to push the ACL predicate into the search query so top-k is computed over permitted documents\nonly. I would verify by logging retrieved ids before and after filtering for a contractor query, and\ncheck that public policies carry the correct ACL metadata at ingestion, since a mis-tagged document in\nthe offline pipeline would produce the same symptom."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the index treated as derived data rather than the system of record?","options":["Vector databases cannot store the original text","It keeps re-chunking and re-embedding a safe, repeatable rebuild instead of a data migration","Regulations prohibit storing documents in more than one place","Derived indexes are faster to query than authoritative ones"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If sources remain authoritative, the index can be dropped and rebuilt whenever chunking or the embedding model changes. Making it authoritative turns every retrieval improvement into a risky migration."},{"q":"Where should per-user access control be applied in the query path?","options":["After generation, by redacting the answer","After top-k retrieval, by removing forbidden chunks","Inside the retrieval query, so top-k is computed over permitted documents only","In the embedding model, by training it on permitted documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Filtering after top-k can leave a user with zero permitted results and still reveals what was ranked. Pushing the predicate into search keeps recall for permitted documents and leaks nothing."},{"q":"What does a reranker add that first-stage hybrid retrieval does not?","options":["Higher recall across the whole corpus","Joint scoring of each (query, chunk) pair, buying precision for the few chunks that enter the prompt","Access-control filtering","Freshness, by re-fetching documents from their sources"],"answer":1,"explanation":"First-stage retrieval scores query and chunk independently for speed and recall. A cross-encoder reads them together and is more accurate, but is too slow to run over the whole corpus, so it is applied only to the shortlist."}]},{"key":"system-design/agentic-architecture","sources":["Yao et al., 2022 — ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models","Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents"],"why":"Most software encodes its control flow in advance: a request comes in, a known sequence of steps runs,\na response goes out. That works when the path through the task is known when you write the code. A\nlarge class of valuable tasks is not like that. \"Resolve this support ticket\", \"find out why this deploy\nfailed\", \"reconcile these two ledgers\" take a different number of steps each time, the next step depends\non what the previous one revealed, and the space of possible paths is too large to enumerate as\nbranches.\n\nThe first naive answer is a fixed chain of prompts: classify, then extract, then look up, then draft. It\nis predictable and testable, and for many tasks it is the right design (see workflows vs agents). It\nfails when the chain needs a branch you did not anticipate: the lookup returns two candidates, the\ndocument is in a language the extractor was not prompted for, the ticket is actually three tickets.\nEvery such case becomes another hard-coded branch and the chain ossifies.\n\nThe second naive answer is the opposite: hand the model every tool and a long system prompt, and let it\nrun. This produces demos quickly and incidents shortly after. Nothing bounds how many steps it takes or\nhow much it costs. A crash mid-task loses everything, because the state lived in a Python list. A tool\nwith write access to production is one bad tool call away from damage. And when it goes wrong, nobody\ncan reconstruct what happened.\n\nAgentic architecture is the discipline between those two: let the model decide *what* to do next, and\nbuild a system that decides what it is *allowed* to do, how long it may keep going, what happens when\nit fails, and how every step is recorded.","how":"### The core loop\nThe unit of execution is a loop: build the context (instructions, task, state, recent observations),\ncall the model with a set of tool definitions, and then branch. If the model returns a tool call,\nvalidate the arguments, execute the tool, append the result as an observation and repeat. If it returns\na final answer, stop. ReAct-style interleaving of reasoning and acting is the pattern underneath; every\nagent framework is a wrapper around this loop plus the boundaries below. A common variant,\nplan-and-execute, asks the model for an explicit plan first and then runs steps against it, re-planning\nwhen an observation contradicts the plan; it costs an extra call but gives the runtime something to\ncheckpoint, show a human and compare progress against, which a pure ReAct loop does not.\n\n### The cost shape of the loop\nEach step re-sends the accumulated context, so input tokens grow with the *sum* of context sizes, not the\nfinal size. Illustratively, a run that starts at 3k tokens and adds 1k per step sends\nΣ(3k + i·1k) for i = 0…11 ≈ 102k input tokens over 12 steps, though its final context is only 14k. That\nnear-quadratic growth is why the architecture caps steps, stores large tool outputs as references, and\nkeeps the stable prefix (instructions, tool definitions) byte-identical so provider prompt caching can\ndiscount it.\n\n### The layers around the loop\n1. **Interface layer.** Where tasks enter (chat UI, API, event, schedule) and where results and\n   intermediate events leave. For anything that may take more than a few seconds this is asynchronous:\n   accept the task, return a run id, stream events, deliver the result later. Agents are long-running by\n   nature *because* each step is a model call plus a tool call and the number of steps is unknown;\n   holding an HTTP request open across that is fragile.\n2. **Orchestration and runtime layer.** Owns the loop and its policies: step budget, token and cost\n   budget, wall-clock timeout, loop detection (same tool with the same arguments repeatedly), retries\n   with backoff for transient tool failures, cancellation. It persists state after every step *because*\n   a run that cannot resume after a worker restart will, at scale, fail constantly for reasons unrelated\n   to the task. Durable execution engines are one way to get this; a checkpoint store plus idempotent\n   steps is another.\n3. **Model gateway.** A single place that calls models: routing between models, rate limiting,\n   caching, cost accounting, fallbacks. Agents multiply model calls by the number of steps, so cost\n   control lives here, not in prompts.\n4. **Tool layer.** Each tool is a typed contract: schema-validated inputs, structured outputs, an\n   explicit side-effect classification (read-only versus consequential), and its own credentials scoped\n   to what that tool needs. The agent never holds credentials; the tool adapter does. Consequential tools\n   route through an approval gate, human or policy, *because* the model's confidence is not a\n   permission. Tools that execute code or browse run in a sandbox *because* their inputs may come from\n   untrusted content, which makes prompt injection a system-level threat rather than a prompt-level one.\n5. **State and memory.** Task state (plan, completed steps, artifacts) is separate from conversation\n   history and from long-term memory. Keep it external and addressable by run id. Large tool outputs are\n   stored as artifacts and referenced, not pasted into the context, *because* context is the agent's\n   scarcest resource and raw tool results are the fastest way to exhaust it.\n6. **Observability and audit.** Every step emits a structured event: model input hash, tool name,\n   arguments, result size, latency, cost, under a run id and tenant id. This is what makes trajectory\n   evaluation and incident review possible.\n\n### Where control flow should be code, and where model\nA useful rule: put the model in decisions that require judgment about content, and put code in\ndecisions that require guarantees. \"Which document is relevant\" is judgment. \"May this user issue\nrefunds above 500\" is a guarantee, enforced in the tool layer regardless of what the model asks. Mixed\nsystems, where a deterministic workflow calls an agent for one open-ended step, or an agent calls a\ndeterministic sub-workflow as a tool, are the norm in production, not the exception.\n\n### Stopping\nEvery agent needs explicit termination: a final-answer action, a maximum number of steps, a budget, and\na stuck detector. Without all four, the loop's failure mode is not a wrong answer but an unbounded one.\nEach terminal state should be distinct in the run record (`done`, `budget_exhausted`, `stuck`,\n`awaiting_approval`, `cancelled`) *because* they demand different follow-ups: a retry, a human, or a\nbug report.","example":"A minimal runtime that shows the boundaries rather than the cleverness: a budget, schema-validated\ntools with a side-effect flag, an approval gate for consequential actions that interrupts rather than\nblocks, and a checkpoint after every step. `call_model` is whatever model API you use and returns either\na tool call or a final answer; `validate` is a JSON Schema check.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nTOOLS = {   # name -> (input schema, needs_approval(args), implementation(args, idem_key))\n    \"search_tickets\": ({\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"q\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"q\"]},\n                       lambda a: False, lambda a, key: tickets.search(a[\"q\"])),\n    \"issue_refund\":   ({\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"ticket\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                        \"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"exclusiveMinimum\": 0, \"maximum\": 5000}},\n                        \"required\": [\"ticket\", \"amount\"]},\n                       lambda a: a[\"amount\"] > 500,          # policy threshold, enforced in code\n                       lambda a, key: payments.refund(a[\"ticket\"], a[\"amount\"], idempotency_key=key)),\n}\n\ndef execute(run_id, state, name, args):\n    if name not in TOOLS:\n        return {\"error\": f\"unknown tool {name}\"}               # an observation, not a crash\n    schema, needs_approval, fn = TOOLS[name]\n    validate(args, schema)                                     # rejects amount > 5000 outright\n    if needs_approval(args) and not approvals.granted(run_id, name, args):\n        return \"PAUSE\"\n    try:\n        return fn(args, f\"{run_id}:{state['step']}\")         # same key on retry -> no double refund\n    except TransientError as e:\n        return {\"error\": str(e), \"retryable\": True}           # the model decides: retry or give up\n\ndef run(run_id, task, store, max_steps=12, budget_usd=0.50):\n    state = store.load(run_id) or {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}], \"step\": 0, \"cost\": 0.0}\n    while state[\"step\"] < max_steps and state[\"cost\"] < budget_usd:\n        if state.get(\"pending\"):                              # resuming after a pause: no new model call\n            msg, name, args = state[\"pending\"]\n        else:\n            reply = call_model(state[\"messages\"], tools=[{\"name\": n, \"schema\": t[0]} for n, t in TOOLS.items()])\n            state[\"cost\"] += reply.cost_usd\n            if reply.final is not None:\n                store.save(run_id, {**state, \"status\": \"done\", \"answer\": reply.final}); return reply.final\n            msg, name, args = reply.as_message(), reply.tool_name, reply.tool_args\n        result = execute(run_id, state, name, args)\n        if result == \"PAUSE\":\n            store.save(run_id, {**state, \"status\": \"awaiting_approval\", \"pending\": [msg, name, args]})\n            return None                                        # interrupt; an approval event re-invokes run()\n        state.pop(\"pending\", None)\n        state[\"messages\"] += [msg, {\"role\": \"tool\", \"name\": name, \"content\": json.dumps(result)[:4000]}]\n        state[\"step\"] += 1\n        store.save(run_id, state)                              # checkpoint: a crash here loses nothing\n    store.save(run_id, {**state, \"status\": \"budget_exhausted\"})\n```\n\nThree boundaries are visible. The hard cap (`maximum: 5000`) lives in the schema, so a larger refund is\nrejected however persuasively the model argued for it. The policy threshold (above 500 needs approval) is\na predicate in code, and the gate *interrupts* the run, saving the exact pending call, rather than holding\na thread while a human decides; on resume the approved call runs without asking the model again, so the\nhuman approved precisely what executes. And the idempotency key is derived from run id and step, so a\nworker that crashes after the refund but before the checkpoint re-executes the step without paying twice.\nWalk one run: step 0 searches tickets (read-only, runs), step 1 proposes a 750 refund, the predicate\nfires, the run is saved as `awaiting_approval` with zero further cost; an approver accepts, `run()` is\nre-invoked, the refund executes with key `run_id:1`, and the model writes the final answer at step 2.","tradeoffs":["Flexibility on unknown task paths versus predictability and testability; when the path is known in advance, a deterministic workflow is cheaper, faster and far easier to evaluate.","Externalized, checkpointed state makes runs resumable and auditable but adds a store write per step; for sub-second, read-only agents an in-memory loop is acceptable.","Approval gates stop damage from bad tool calls but add latency and human load; the threshold for what counts as consequential is a product decision, not a technical one.","Giving an agent many tools broadens what it can do while lowering tool-selection accuracy and inflating context; narrow tool sets per task, or sub-agents, win once the tool list grows large (the exact threshold is model-dependent, so measure tool-selection accuracy rather than assume a number).","Asynchronous execution with streamed events fits long runs and survives restarts, but complicates clients, which must handle partial results, resumption and cancellation."],"pitfalls":["Keeping run state in process memory; the symptom is runs that vanish on deploys or autoscale events, and users who retry, doubling side effects.","No budget or step cap; the symptom is a single stuck run consuming a day's token budget while repeating the same failing tool call.","Trusting the model to enforce permissions (\"only refund if the policy allows\"); the symptom is a prompt-injected document that convinces the agent to call a write tool, which then succeeds.","Letting the model re-propose an action after approval instead of executing the stored pending call; the symptom is an approved 750 refund that executes as 800, because the second model call differed.","Pasting full tool outputs into context; the symptom is cost growing step over step and quality falling as the window fills with raw JSON.","Treating a demo loop as the architecture; the symptom is that the first production requirement (resume, audit, cancel, approve) requires a rewrite."],"when":"Build an agentic system when the number and order of steps genuinely depend on intermediate results,\nwhen tools exist to act on those results, and when you can define \"done\" well enough to evaluate it.\nPrefer a workflow when steps are known, when latency must be tight, or when the cost of a wrong action\nis high and you cannot gate it. Measure task completion rate, steps and cost per task, tool-call success\nrate, and the fraction of runs that hit budgets or require approval. Day to day this appears as decisions\nabout which tools are read-only, what the step budget is, and where state lives. In interviews, be ready\nto draw the layers, explain why state must be external, and argue where model judgment ends and\ncode-enforced guarantees begin.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the loop with budgets and checkpoints, writes tool adapters with schemas and scoped credentials, and instruments every step for tracing and cost accounting.","architect":"Defines the runtime boundary, the tool permission model, the sync-versus-async contract with clients, and which decisions are code-enforced rather than model-made.","fde":"Fits the agent into a customer's identity, approval and audit requirements, and negotiates which actions may run autonomously versus with a human in the loop."},"check":{"q":"A team's agent works well in demos, but in production about 5% of runs disappear without a\nresult and some customers report duplicate actions. What architectural gaps would you suspect, and how\nwould you fix them?","a":"Both symptoms point at in-process state and non-idempotent side effects. Runs that disappear are\nmost likely killed by deploys, autoscaling or worker crashes while the loop's state lived in memory; with\nno checkpoint there is nothing to resume, and no event stream shows where it stopped. Duplicates come\nfrom the obvious reaction: the client or a retry wrapper re-submits the task, and consequential tools\nexecute again. The fix is to externalize state by run id and checkpoint after every step so a worker can\nresume rather than restart, make tool execution idempotent with a key derived from run id and step,\nroute consequential tools through an approval or at least a dedupe layer, and emit structured events so\ndisappearing runs become visible as \"interrupted at step n\" instead of silence."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should agent run state be persisted outside the process after every step?","options":["Because model APIs require state to be stored before each call","So a worker crash, deploy or autoscale event interrupts a run instead of losing it, and the run can resume","To reduce the number of tokens sent to the model","Because tool results cannot be held in memory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agent runs span many model and tool calls over seconds to hours. Anything that lives only in process memory is lost on restart; a checkpoint per step turns that loss into a resumable interruption."},{"q":"Where should the rule \"refunds above 500 require approval\" be enforced?","options":["In the system prompt, so the model knows the policy","In the model's reasoning, by asking it to double-check amounts","In the tool layer, by schema validation and an approval gate that runs regardless of what the model requested","In the client, by warning the user after the refund"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model may be wrong or manipulated through its inputs. Guarantees belong in code the model cannot bypass; the prompt can mention the policy, but the tool layer must enforce it."},{"q":"When is a deterministic workflow a better choice than an agent?","options":["Whenever more than one tool is involved","When the sequence of steps is known in advance and predictability matters more than handling unanticipated paths","When the task involves reading documents","Never, because agents can emulate any workflow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agents trade predictability and cost for flexibility on unknown paths. If the path is known, that flexibility buys nothing and you pay for it in latency, cost and harder evaluation."}]},{"key":"system-design/multi-agent-systems","sources":["Wu et al., 2023 — AutoGen: Enabling Next-Gen LLM Applications via Multi-Agent Conversation","Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents","Anthropic, 2025 — How we built our multi-agent research system"],"why":"A single agent degrades predictably as a task grows. Its context fills with tool definitions,\ninstructions for every sub-case and the accumulated trace, and quality falls as the useful signal is\ndiluted. Tool selection gets worse as the tool list gets longer, since the model must pick among many\nsimilar schemas. Privileges accumulate: an agent that both reads untrusted web content and holds write\ncredentials to internal systems is a prompt-injection path by construction. And it is sequential: one\nloop, one step at a time, even when the task has ten independent branches.\n\nThe naive scale-up is a bigger prompt and a longer context window. That buys some headroom and then hits\nthe same walls, now more expensively per step. The opposite naive move, splitting everything into many\nagents because it looks modular, is just as common and more damaging: each agent re-reads context the\nothers already had, each hand-off loses information, each boundary is a place to disagree, and the\nsystem's success rate becomes the product of its parts. Five sequential stages at 95% reliability each\ngive roughly 0.95⁵ ≈ 77% end to end.\n\nMulti-agent architecture is the deliberate version: split where the split buys something specific.\nIsolation of context, so each worker sees only what its sub-task needs. Isolation of privilege, so the\nagent reading untrusted input has no write tools. Parallelism, so independent branches run concurrently.\nSpecialization, so each agent's instructions and evals stay small and sharp. If a proposed split buys\nnone of those, it is overhead.","how":"### Topologies and when each fits\n- **Supervisor (orchestrator–workers).** One agent decomposes the task, dispatches sub-tasks to workers\n  and synthesizes. Fits when sub-tasks are not known in advance and must be derived from the task. The\n  supervisor holds the plan; workers hold the detail.\n- **Pipeline.** Agents in sequence, each transforming the previous output (research, draft, review).\n  Fits when stages need distinct skills and outputs can be checked between them. Watch the\n  compounding-error arithmetic.\n- **Parallel fan-out with aggregation.** The same or different agents run on independent partitions\n  (one per document, region or hypothesis), then an aggregator merges. Fits breadth-first work; the\n  aggregator's context budget is the limit.\n- **Evaluator–optimizer (critic).** One agent produces, another critiques against explicit criteria,\n  looping until the critic passes or a budget ends. Fits when quality is checkable but hard to get right\n  first time.\n- **Peer hand-off.** Agents transfer control to one another based on the conversation (triage to a\n  billing specialist). Fits conversational routing where each specialist needs its own persona and tools.\n\n### What crosses the boundary\nDecide explicitly what a sub-agent receives and returns. Passing the full transcript is the default in\nmany frameworks and the main source of cost and confusion; passing a typed task (goal, constraints,\nrelevant artifacts, output schema) is what makes workers cheap and testable. Returns should be\nstructured and compact *because* the coordinator's context is the scarce resource and raw worker traces\nwill exhaust it. Large intermediate results go to a shared artifact store and are passed by reference,\n*because* copying them through model context costs tokens on both sides and degrades attention.\n\nThis compression is the main thing a sub-agent buys. Illustratively, a worker may read 40k tokens of\npages and tool output to answer its sub-question and return a 400-token structured finding; five such\nworkers let the supervisor reason over 2k tokens of distilled evidence instead of 200k of raw material\nthat would not fit, or would fit and drown the signal. The price is that the total token bill is the\nsum over all agents, which is why multi-agent runs cost several times a single agent's (Anthropic's\nwrite-up of its research system reports on the order of 15× the tokens of a chat interaction).\n\n### Reliability arithmetic: why topology matters\nCompounding is about *undetected* errors. In a pipeline, stage 3 receives stage 2's output and usually\ncannot tell a fluent wrong answer from a right one, so success is roughly the product p₁·p₂·…·pₙ. A\nfan-out whose join needs every worker is also a product (five workers at 0.9 each: 0.9⁵ ≈ 0.59), but\ntwo properties change the arithmetic. Workers are independent, so a failed one can be retried alone:\nwith one retry each, a worker succeeds with probability 1 − 0.1² = 0.99, and all five with\n0.99⁵ ≈ 0.95. And the join can be a quorum (synthesize if at least k of n succeeded). Both only work if\nfailure is *detectable*, which is why workers return a schema with an explicit \"not found\" or low\nconfidence rather than prose, and why pipelines need a validator between stages before any retry\nlogic helps. (Retries assume independent failures; a worker that fails because the sub-question is\nunanswerable will fail again, so cap them.)\n\n### Coordination state\nThere must be one place that knows which sub-tasks exist, their status and their results: a task table\nkeyed by run id. The coordinator can be a model (flexible, derives the plan) or code (predictable, fixed\nfan-out); mixed is common, where code owns the fan-out and join and a model owns what each worker does.\nTermination is decided at two levels *because* a worker can loop forever just as a single agent can,\nand the coordinator must also stop when the task is done, over budget, or when further delegation stops\nimproving the result.\n\n### Privilege and policy across agents\nEach agent gets its own tool set and credentials, scoped to its role. A policy layer enforces this\nindependently of prompts: the web researcher cannot call `send_email` even if a page it read tells it\nto. Delegation must not escalate: a worker is never granted more than the coordinator had for this\ntenant and user.\n\n### Observability across agents\nAll agents emit events under one run id with a parent–child span relationship, exactly like distributed\ntracing across services. Without this, debugging means reconstructing a conversation among five\nparticipants from five logs; with it, you can see which worker produced the wrong fact and what it was\ngiven.\n\n### Protocols\nWhen agents are built by different teams or vendors, an interoperability protocol (A2A is the\norientation point) standardizes discovery, task hand-off and status. Inside one codebase, a typed\nfunction call is simpler and sufficient.","example":"A supervisor that fans out independent research questions to workers with a narrow, read-only tool set,\nin parallel, with a per-worker budget, and aggregates compact structured results. `agent_run` is the\nsingle-agent loop from the agentic-architecture lesson, `call_model` is a stand-in for your model API,\nand `task_table` is the persisted coordination state.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\n\nREAD_ONLY = [\"search_docs\", \"fetch_page\"]            # workers never receive write tools\nFAILED = {\"answer\": None, \"sources\": [], \"confidence\": 0.0}\n\nasync def worker(run_id, subtask, budget_usd=0.10, retries=1):\n    for attempt in range(retries + 1):               # independent worker -> retry it alone\n        try:\n            result = await agent_run(\n                run_id=f\"{run_id}/{subtask['id']}/{attempt}\",   # child span under the parent run\n                system=\"Answer the sub-question using the tools. Return JSON: {answer, sources, confidence}. \"\n                       \"Use answer=null if the tools do not support an answer.\",\n                task=json.dumps(subtask), tools=READ_ONLY, max_steps=6, budget_usd=budget_usd)\n            out = json.loads(result)\n            if out.get(\"answer\"):\n                return {\"id\": subtask[\"id\"], **out}\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, TimeoutError):\n            pass                                     # malformed or slow: a detectable failure\n    return {\"id\": subtask[\"id\"], **FAILED}\n\nasync def supervise(run_id, question, max_workers=5):\n    plan = call_model(f\"Split into at most {max_workers} independent sub-questions as JSON \"\n                      f\"[{{id, question, why}}]. Question: {question}\").json[:max_workers]\n    task_table.create(run_id, [p[\"id\"] for p in plan])                  # coordination state, persisted\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(worker(run_id, p) for p in plan))  # parallel fan-out\n    for r in results:\n        task_table.mark(run_id, r[\"id\"], \"done\" if r[\"answer\"] else \"failed\")\n    evidence = [r for r in results if r[\"answer\"] and r[\"confidence\"] >= 0.5]\n    if len(evidence) < max(1, (len(plan) + 1) // 2):                    # quorum: a majority must succeed\n        return {\"status\": \"insufficient_evidence\", \"results\": results}\n    synthesis = call_model(\"Synthesize an answer with citations from these findings only. Name any \"\n                           \"sub-question that has no finding:\\n\" + json.dumps(evidence)\n                           + f\"\\n\\nQuestion: {question}\").text\n    return {\"status\": \"ok\", \"answer\": synthesis, \"sub_results\": results}\n```\n\nThe architectural choices that matter are visible: workers receive a typed sub-task rather than the\nsupervisor's transcript and return a schema rather than a trace; a worker's failure is detectable\n(null answer, bad JSON, timeout) so it can be retried in isolation; and the supervisor applies a\nquorum, declining to synthesize when most workers failed instead of producing a confident answer from\nthin evidence. For the data-residency question, a plan of three sub-questions (one per market) where one\nworker returns null twice yields two findings, which meets the quorum of two, and the synthesis is told\nto say that the third market is unanswered rather than fill it in.","tradeoffs":["Parallel workers cut wall-clock time for breadth-first tasks, but total tokens rise because each worker rebuilds context; for narrow sequential tasks one agent is cheaper.","Narrow per-agent tool sets improve selection accuracy and contain blast radius, at the cost of a coordinator that must route correctly; mis-routing becomes the new failure mode.","Pipelines make each stage independently testable, but errors compound multiplicatively across stages; add checks between stages or shorten the chain when end-to-end reliability matters more than modularity.","A model-driven supervisor adapts its plan to the task, while a code-driven fan-out is predictable and cheap; use the model only where the decomposition is genuinely unknown in advance.","Passing artifacts by reference keeps contexts small but requires a shared store with access control; for tiny results inline JSON is simpler."],"pitfalls":["Splitting into agents along an org chart or for \"cleanliness\" with no isolation, parallelism or specialization gain; the symptom is higher cost and latency than the single-agent baseline with no quality gain.","Forwarding full transcripts between agents; the symptom is worker contexts that are mostly the supervisor's history and costs that grow with every added agent.","Letting a worker that reads untrusted content hold write tools; the symptom is an action nobody asked for, traced back to instructions inside a fetched page.","Synthesizing over failed or low-confidence workers; the symptom is fluent final answers whose citations point at sub-results that said \"not found\".","No shared run id across agents; the symptom is an incident where five logs disagree about what happened and nobody can say which agent introduced the error."],"when":"Go multi-agent when you can name the gain: independent branches that can run in parallel, a privilege\nboundary that must separate reading untrusted input from acting, or a single agent whose tool list or\ninstruction set has grown past what one context handles well. Do not go multi-agent to look modular, to\nfix a weak single agent, or when the task is a short sequential chain. Measure against the single-agent\nbaseline: task success, total tokens, wall-clock, and per-stage failure attribution. Day to day it shows\nup as contract design between agents and as tracing work. In interviews, expect to be asked why not one\nagent, how you prevent compounding errors and privilege escalation, and how you would debug a wrong\nfinal answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements typed hand-offs, per-agent tool scopes and budgets, parallel fan-out with a join, and parent–child tracing so every worker's steps roll up under one run.","architect":"Decides whether a split is justified, chooses the topology, owns the privilege model across agents, and sets the aggregate budget and termination policy.","fde":"Maps customer workflows onto agents with the right data access each, and explains to stakeholders why the system refuses to answer when workers fail rather than guessing."},"check":{"q":"Your single research agent handles 20 tools and answers in about 90 seconds with 80% task\nsuccess. A colleague proposes five specialist agents. How would you decide whether that is an\nimprovement, and what would you watch for?","a":"First I would name what the split buys. If sub-questions are independent, parallel workers can\ncut wall-clock substantially, since latency becomes the slowest worker rather than the sum; if 20 tools are hurting tool selection, five agents with four tools each\nmay raise accuracy; if one tool reads untrusted web pages, separating it from write tools is a security\ngain. Then I would run both designs on the same eval set and compare task success, total tokens, latency\nand failure attribution per stage, because the multi-agent version will cost more tokens and introduce\nrouting and synthesis errors that did not exist before. I would watch for compounding: a pipeline of five\n95% stages is roughly 77% end to end, so the topology should be parallel with a join rather than\nsequential where possible, because independent workers can be retried alone and joined with a quorum,\nprovided their failures are detectable through a structured \"not found\". I would also check that the\nbaseline's failures are actually the kind the split fixes: if the single agent fails because it lacks\ndata, five agents will lack it too."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which gain does NOT by itself justify splitting one agent into several?","options":["Running independent sub-tasks in parallel","Separating an agent that reads untrusted content from one holding write credentials","Making the architecture diagram look more modular","Reducing a tool list that has grown past what one context selects from accurately"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Multi-agent systems add coordination cost, duplicated context and compounding errors. The split must buy parallelism, privilege isolation or specialization; modularity for its own sake is pure overhead."},{"q":"Why should a worker agent receive a typed sub-task instead of the supervisor's full transcript?","options":["Workers cannot parse long messages","It keeps worker context small and focused, makes workers testable in isolation, and stops cost growing with every added agent","Transcripts contain secrets that workers must not see","Typed tasks are required by agent protocols"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forwarding transcripts means every worker pays for context it does not need, and the supervisor's noise dilutes its instructions. A typed task defines a contract that can be evaluated independently."},{"q":"A five-stage agent pipeline has stages that each succeed about 95% of the time. What is the approximate end-to-end success rate, and what does it imply?","options":["About 95%, since the stages are independent","About 77%, so long sequential chains need intermediate checks or fewer stages","About 99%, because later stages correct earlier errors","About 50%, because errors always double at each hand-off"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sequential stages multiply: 0.95 to the fifth power is roughly 0.77, because a later stage cannot usually detect an earlier stage's fluent error. Compounding is why pipelines need validation between stages, and why independent parallel workers, retried alone and joined by quorum, fare better."}]},{"key":"system-design/agent-orchestration","sources":["Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents","Official docs: LangGraph"],"why":"A single prompt needs no orchestration. The moment you add a second call that depends on the first, a\ntool whose result feeds a third call, and a user who may need to approve something in the middle, you\nhave a program whose steps are spread across model outputs, and something must own it.\n\nThe naive implementation is a Python function that calls things in order, with results in local\nvariables. It works until the first production requirement. A tool times out at step four: do you redo\nsteps one to three, with their side effects? A user closes the tab while the agent waits for approval:\nwhere is the run? Traffic triples: can you run steps concurrently, and which ones? Someone asks what the\nagent did for a given ticket last Tuesday: which log line was that? Each answer bolts another ad-hoc\nmechanism onto the function until it is an orchestrator written by accident, untested and undocumented.\n\nA second naive answer is to let the model orchestrate everything: give it all the tools and let it\ndecide every transition. That is the right choice for genuinely open-ended steps and the wrong one for\nsteps that must happen (validation, logging, a compliance check), that must not happen twice\n(payments), or that must happen in a fixed order. Model-chosen control flow is also the hardest to\nevaluate, since every run may take a different path.\n\nOrchestration as a deliberate layer makes the control flow explicit: state is a typed object, steps are\nnodes, transitions are either fixed edges or router decisions (made by code or by the model), and a\nruntime executes it with persistence, events and policies attached. The reason is the same one behind\nevery explicit state machine: you can inspect, resume, test and reason about what you have written\ndown, and you cannot do any of that with control flow buried in prompts.","how":"### State, nodes, edges\nRepresent a run as a state object (the task, messages, intermediate results, counters) and the\napplication as a graph. A node is a function from state to updated state: call the model, call a tool,\nrun a sub-agent, ask a human. Edges are either static (A always leads to B) or conditional, decided by a\nrouter. Routers can be pure code (`if state.score < 0.7`), a classifier, or the main model's tool\nchoice. This representation is used *because* it makes the possible paths enumerable: you can draw it,\ntest each node in isolation, and know where loops can occur.\n\n### The orchestration patterns, from most to least deterministic\n1. **Prompt chaining.** A fixed sequence where each step checks the previous output. Use when the task\n   decomposes cleanly and predictably.\n2. **Routing.** Classify first, then dispatch to a specialized path (different prompt, model or tool\n   set). Use when inputs fall into distinct categories that want different handling.\n3. **Parallelization.** Run independent nodes concurrently and join (sectioning), or run the same node\n   several times and vote (ensembling). Use when latency matters and branches are independent.\n4. **Orchestrator–workers.** A model decides the sub-tasks at runtime; code runs the fan-out and join.\n   Use when sub-tasks cannot be known in advance.\n5. **Evaluator–optimizer.** Generate, evaluate against explicit criteria, revise, bounded by a budget.\n   Use when quality is checkable.\n6. **Autonomous agent loop.** The model picks the next node every step until it decides it is done. Use\n   when the path is unknown and the environment gives trustworthy feedback.\n\nEach step down this list trades predictability and cost for flexibility, which is why the right system\nusually mixes them: a routed, chained outer workflow with an autonomous loop for the one step that needs\nit.\n\n### Runtime responsibilities\nThe orchestrator executes the graph and owns the cross-cutting concerns, *because* putting them in nodes\nmeans every node reimplements them differently:\n- **Persistence.** Checkpoint state after each node so a run can resume at the node where it stopped.\n  This is also what makes human-in-the-loop possible: an interrupt is a checkpoint plus a status, and\n  resumption is loading it and continuing. Note the semantics this gives you: a crash *after* a node\n  finishes but *before* its checkpoint is written re-runs that node on resume, so node execution is\n  at-least-once, never exactly-once. That single fact is why side-effecting nodes need idempotency keys\n  and why model-calling nodes should be safe to repeat.\n- **Policies.** Step and cost budgets, per-node timeouts, retry with backoff for transient failures, and\n  idempotency keys for nodes with side effects so a retry does not repeat them.\n- **Concurrency.** Fan-out and join primitives with limits, so parallel branches do not exhaust rate\n  limits. When parallel branches write to the same state field, the runtime needs an explicit merge rule\n  (append to a list, take the max, last-writer-wins); LangGraph calls these reducers. Without one, the\n  branch that finishes last silently overwrites the others.\n- **Events.** A structured stream per run (node started, model called, tool result, waiting for\n  approval, done) that clients subscribe to, *because* long runs need progress and operators need traces.\n- **Cancellation.** A cooperative stop signal checked between nodes, with in-flight tool calls either\n  finished or compensated.\n\n### Request-driven versus event-driven orchestration\nFor short runs an orchestrator can execute inside a request. For runs that wait (approvals, slow tools,\nscheduled follow-ups) orchestration must be event-driven: a run advances when an event arrives, workers\nare stateless, and the durable state is in the store. This is where agent orchestration meets workflow\nengines such as Temporal; the graph is the same, the executor is a durable one.\n\n### Orchestration versus choreography\nEverything above is *orchestration*: one component holds the graph and tells each step when to run.\nThe alternative, *choreography*, has no central owner: each agent or service reacts to events on a bus\nand emits its own. Choreography couples components less and scales organisationally, but the end-to-end\nflow exists only implicitly in the sum of subscriptions, so \"where is run 42 and why did it stop?\" has no\nsingle answer. For LLM applications, where steps are non-deterministic and budgets and approvals must be\nenforced per run, a central orchestrator per run is the usual choice; choreography fits the boundaries\n*between* runs and systems (a finished run emits an event another team's pipeline consumes).\n\n### Frameworks\nGraph libraries such as LangGraph give you the state graph, checkpointing and interrupts; vendor agent\nSDKs give you the autonomous loop with hand-offs; durable execution engines give you the event-driven\nexecutor. The abstraction matters less than making state explicit and keeping runtime concerns out of\nthe nodes.","example":"A small graph executor that makes the ideas concrete: typed state, nodes as functions, a static edge, a\ncode router, a model-driven node, a checkpoint after every node, and an interrupt for human approval\nthat is resumed by calling `run` again with the same id. `call_model`, `mail` and `store` are stand-ins.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field, asdict\n\n@dataclass\nclass State:\n    ticket: str\n    draft: str = \"\"\n    score: float = 0.0\n    critique: str = \"\"\n    approved: bool | None = None\n    step: int = 0\n    status: str = \"running\"\n    history: list = field(default_factory=list)\n\ndef draft_reply(s):\n    s.draft = call_model(f\"Draft a reply to: {s.ticket}\\nFix these issues if any: {s.critique}\").text; return s\ndef grade(s):        # evaluator: structured output, so the router can branch on a number\n    g = call_model(f\"Policy compliance of this draft as JSON {{score: 0-1, critique}}:\\n{s.draft}\").json\n    s.score, s.critique = float(g[\"score\"]), g[\"critique\"]; return s\ndef approval(s):     # interrupt node: nothing to compute until a human acts\n    if s.approved is None: s.status = \"waiting_approval\"\n    elif not s.approved:   s.status = \"rejected\"\n    return s\ndef send(s):         mail.send_idempotent(key=f\"{s.ticket}:send\", body=s.draft); s.status = \"done\"; return s\n\nNODES = {\"draft_reply\": draft_reply, \"grade\": grade, \"approval\": approval, \"send\": send}\nEDGES = {\"draft_reply\": \"grade\",                                                          # static edge\n         \"grade\": lambda s: \"draft_reply\" if s.score < 0.7 and s.step < 6 else \"approval\",  # code router\n         \"approval\": lambda s: \"send\" if s.approved else None,\n         \"send\": None}\n\ndef run(run_id, store, max_steps=10):\n    s = store.load(run_id) or State(ticket=store.task(run_id))\n    node = store.cursor(run_id) or \"draft_reply\"\n    while node and s.step < max_steps:\n        s = NODES[node](s); s.step += 1; s.history.append(node)\n        if s.status != \"running\":                                   # interrupted, rejected or done\n            store.save(run_id, asdict(s), cursor=node); break\n        edge = EDGES[node]; node = edge(s) if callable(edge) else edge\n        store.save(run_id, asdict(s), cursor=node)                  # checkpoint after every node\n        emit(run_id, node=s.history[-1], score=s.score)\n    return s\n```\n\nTrace one ticket. `draft_reply` (step 1) → `grade` returns 0.55 with the critique \"promises a refund\ndate\" (step 2) → the router loops back → `draft_reply` uses the critique (step 3) → `grade` returns\n0.82 (step 4) → `approval` finds `approved is None`, sets `waiting_approval` and the run returns at step 5\nwith its cursor on `approval`; no worker is held. Hours later the approval service writes\n`approved=True, status=\"running\"` and calls `run` again: it loads the checkpoint, re-enters `approval`,\nroutes to `send`, and finishes. If the process dies after `send` sends but before its checkpoint, the\nresume re-runs `send`, and the idempotency key `ticket:send` turns the second send into a no-op. The\n`s.step < 6` guard in the router and `max_steps` in the loop are two independent bounds on the\nrevise cycle, one per level.","tradeoffs":["Explicit graphs make paths enumerable and testable, but every new behaviour is a code change; a model-driven loop adapts without redeploys at the price of unpredictable paths.","Checkpointing after each node enables resume and human interrupts, but adds a store round-trip per node; for sub-second, side-effect-free chains, in-memory execution is fine.","Parallel branches cut latency but multiply concurrent model calls, so rate limits and cost spikes arrive sooner; cap fan-out and reserve it for genuinely independent work.","Putting policies (budgets, retries, idempotency) in the runtime keeps nodes simple, but makes the runtime a critical dependency that must itself be reliable and well understood.","A framework accelerates the first version while shaping your state and persistence model; if you later need a durable, event-driven executor, migrating a framework-specific graph can be a rewrite."],"pitfalls":["Hiding control flow in prompts (\"after you search, always validate, then...\"); the symptom is runs that skip required steps and no way to assert they happened.","Retrying a node with side effects without an idempotency key; the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or payments after a transient failure.","Unbounded loops in evaluator–optimizer or self-correction edges; the symptom is a run that revises forever at increasing cost with no termination.","Treating human approval as a blocking call in a worker; the symptom is held connections, worker exhaustion and runs lost when the process restarts.","Parallel branches writing the same state key without a reducer; the symptom is a fan-out of three searches whose joined result contains only one branch's findings, varying run to run.","No per-run event stream; the symptom is users seeing a spinner for minutes and operators unable to say which node a stuck run is in."],"when":"Introduce an explicit orchestration layer as soon as a feature has more than one model call with\ndependencies, any tool with side effects, or any step that waits on a human or an external system.\nChoose the most deterministic pattern that handles the task, and confine model-driven control flow to the\nsteps that need judgment. Measure per-node latency and failure rates, run completion versus interruption\nversus budget exhaustion, and retries per run. Day to day this is where you decide what is a node, where\ncheckpoints go, and what is idempotent. In interviews, the expected depth is to describe the pattern\nladder from chaining to autonomous loop, say which runtime concerns the orchestrator owns, and explain how\nan approval interrupt is implemented without blocking a worker.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines nodes, routers and state types, adds checkpoints and idempotency keys, and emits the event stream that clients and traces consume.","architect":"Chooses where control flow is code versus model, whether execution is request- or event-driven, and which engine or framework owns persistence and policies.","fde":"Adapts the graph to a customer's approval chains and systems, and explains why a human step pauses the run rather than holding a connection open."},"check":{"q":"A team built an agent that handles refund requests end to end. Compliance now requires that every\nrefund above a threshold is approved by a person, sometimes hours later. How would you change the\norchestration, and what would break if you simply added a blocking approval call?","a":"A blocking call holds a worker and a connection for hours, exhausts the worker pool under load,\nand loses the run entirely if the process restarts, so it is not an option. I would make the run's state\nexplicit and persisted, add an approval node that checkpoints the state with status \"waiting_approval\"\nand returns, and make the run event-driven: the approval decision arrives as an event that loads the\ncheckpoint and resumes at the next node. The refund-executing node gets an idempotency key so a resumed\nor retried run cannot pay twice, the threshold check is code in the router rather than a prompt\ninstruction, and the event stream shows the run as waiting so support can see why it is pending. If runs\ncan wait days, a durable workflow engine is the natural executor."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main reason to represent an agent application as explicit state plus nodes and edges?","options":["Models produce better answers when the prompt describes a graph","It makes the possible paths enumerable, so you can test nodes, resume runs and know where loops can occur","Graphs execute faster than sequential function calls","It removes the need for a model to make any decisions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Control flow buried in prompts or local variables cannot be inspected, tested or resumed. An explicit graph can be drawn, checkpointed and reasoned about, while still leaving judgment calls to the model at router nodes."},{"q":"How should a human approval step be implemented in an orchestrated run?","options":["Block the worker thread until the human responds","Ask the model to wait and poll for the decision","Checkpoint the state with a waiting status, return, and resume from the checkpoint when the approval event arrives","Restart the run from the beginning once approved"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Approvals can take hours. Holding a worker wastes capacity and loses the run on restart; a checkpoint plus event-driven resumption costs nothing while waiting and survives deploys."},{"q":"Which pattern fits when sub-tasks cannot be known until the task is inspected, but you still want predictable fan-out and join behaviour?","options":["Prompt chaining","Orchestrator–workers, with a model deciding the sub-tasks and code running the fan-out and join","Routing to a fixed set of specialists","A single autonomous agent loop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Orchestrator–workers places model judgment exactly where it is needed, deciding the decomposition, while the concurrency, limits and aggregation stay deterministic in code."}]},{"key":"system-design/workflow-orchestration","sources":["Official docs: Temporal","Official docs: Apache Airflow","Official docs: Prefect"],"why":"Any process with several steps that take time, can fail independently and have side effects needs the\nsame machinery: remember which steps completed, retry the ones that failed without repeating the ones\nthat succeeded, wait (for a timer, a human, an external callback) without holding a process open, run on\na schedule or on an event, and expose state so a human can see what happened. Every team that starts\nwith a cron job and a script ends up reinventing this, badly, one incident at a time.\n\nThe naive design is a background job with a loop and a database row for status. It works for one step.\nWith five steps it needs per-step status, partial retries, idempotency for every side effect, a way to\nresume after a worker dies between steps, and a dashboard. The subtle failure is not a missing feature\nbut a wrong guarantee: a job retried after a crash re-executes the \"send email\" step because the crash\nhappened after the send and before the status write. Exactly-once is impossible across systems; what you\ncan have is a durable record of which steps were attempted plus idempotent steps, and that is precisely\nwhat orchestrators provide.\n\nAI systems make this acute. Ingestion pipelines are multi-stage, scheduled and expensive to redo\n(parsing and embedding a million chunks). Agent runs take minutes to hours, wait for approvals, call\nflaky external tools and must not repeat consequential actions. Both are workflow orchestration problems\nwith different shapes, which is why the three tools in this lesson coexist rather than compete.","how":"### Airflow: scheduled DAGs of tasks\nAirflow (started at Airbnb, now an Apache project) models work as a directed acyclic graph of tasks\ndefined in Python. A scheduler evaluates DAGs against their schedule, creates task instances per run and\nhands them to an executor (local, Celery, Kubernetes) that runs them on workers; a metadata database\nrecords every task state. Tasks are meant to be idempotent, coarse units (run this SQL, launch this\nSpark job, call this API) and exchange only small values through XCom, *because* the DAG is a scheduling\nplan, not a data-flow program. Each DAG run is bound to a *data interval* (its logical date), and\nwell-written tasks read only that interval's data and overwrite only that interval's output. That\nconvention is what makes a retry or a backfill safe: re-running \"2024-03-14\" replaces that day's\npartition instead of appending a duplicate. Retries, SLAs, backfills over past dates and a mature UI are\nits strengths. Its shape fits periodic batch: nightly re-embedding, daily data-quality checks, hourly\nconnector syncs. It fits poorly when the graph depends on runtime data or when a run must wait on a\nhuman for days.\n\n### Prefect: dynamic Python dataflows\nPrefect takes the opposite stance on structure: a flow is a Python function and tasks are decorated\nfunctions called from it, so loops, conditionals and fan-out driven by runtime data are ordinary code\nrather than a static graph. The orchestration layer (Prefect server or Cloud) tracks states, retries,\ncaching of task results, schedules and concurrency limits, while your own workers execute the code, a\nhybrid model chosen *because* data and credentials then never leave your infrastructure. It suits the\nmany AI pipelines where the number of documents, the branches and the parameters are discovered as the\nflow runs. It is still a pipeline tool: a flow run can be paused or suspended to wait for human input,\nbut there is no replayable event history. Recovery is by retrying tasks and by re-running the flow\nfunction while skipping tasks whose results were persisted under a cache key; a suspended run resumes by\nexecuting the flow from the top in this way. That works well when tasks are coarse and their results are\npersisted, and poorly for a process with many small stateful steps that must survive anything for\nweeks, which is not its design centre.\n\n### Temporal: durable execution\nTemporal (descended from Uber's Cadence) solves a different problem: making ordinary code durable. You\nwrite a *workflow* function that orchestrates calls to *activities*, which are the functions that do side\neffects: call an LLM, write a database row, send an email. The Temporal server records every event in the\nworkflow's life (started, activity scheduled, activity completed with its result, timer fired, signal\nreceived) in an append-only history. Workers poll task queues and execute workflow code; when a worker\ncrashes, another worker picks up the workflow and *replays* its code against the recorded history.\nBecause completed activity results come from history instead of re-execution, the code deterministically\nreaches the exact point where it stopped and continues. This is why workflow code must be deterministic:\nno wall-clock reads, random numbers or direct I/O inside it, only activities, timers and the SDK's\nside-effect APIs, *because* replay must reproduce the original decisions. Changing workflow code while\nruns are in flight therefore needs versioning (patch markers in the code).\n\nActivities get retry policies, timeouts (schedule-to-start, start-to-close) and heartbeats for long\ntasks. Workflows can sleep for days with no process alive, receive *signals* (an approval arrived),\nanswer *queries* (which step are you on), start child workflows, and run indefinitely by\ncontinuing-as-new to keep history bounded. The cost is the determinism discipline, a server to run or\nbuy, and keeping payloads small by passing large data by reference, because every activity input and\noutput lands in history.\n\n### The same crash, three recovery models\nPut a worker crash in the middle of step 3 of a five-step run, and the tools differ in what they know.\nAirflow knows task instances: tasks 1–2 are `success`, task 3 is marked failed (or found as a zombie and\nfailed) and retried as a whole unit, so correctness rests on task 3 being idempotent for its interval.\nPrefect knows task runs and persisted results: retrying the flow re-enters it from the top, steps 1–2\nreturn cached results, step 3 runs again. Temporal knows every decision the workflow made: a new worker\nreplays the code, steps 1–2 return their recorded results instantly, and step 3's activity is retried\nafter its timeout. The finer the recorded state, the smaller the unit of redo, and the more discipline\nthe code must obey (idempotent tasks; cacheable tasks; deterministic workflow code respectively).\n\n### Which fits an AI system where\n- Re-chunk and re-embed a corpus nightly, with backfill over past days: **Airflow**.\n- Parse, enrich and index whatever arrived in the last hour, branching per document type: **Prefect**\n  (or Airflow with dynamic task mapping).\n- An agent run that calls tools over an hour, pauses for a manager's approval until Monday, must never\n  issue a refund twice, and must be inspectable mid-run: **Temporal**.\n- A ten-second chain of three model calls inside a request: none of them; an in-process orchestrator is\n  enough.\n\n### What all three give you, and what none of them do\nAll three give persistence of step state, retries, scheduling, a UI and observability. None of them make\nyour steps idempotent for you, and none guarantee a side effect happens exactly once: a retry after a\ncrash between \"effect done\" and \"result recorded\" will re-run the step. Every activity or task that acts\non the outside world needs an idempotency key derived from the run and the step.","example":"A Temporal Python workflow for document extraction with an LLM call. Activities carry the side effects\nand retry policies, the workflow carries only deterministic orchestration, and the approval wait is a\ndurable signal rather than a blocked thread. `blob_store`, `db` and `llm_extract` are stand-ins for your\nown clients.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom temporalio import activity, workflow\nfrom temporalio.common import RetryPolicy\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def extract_fields(doc_id: str) -> dict:\n    text = await blob_store.read_text(doc_id)            # large payloads stay out of workflow history\n    return await llm_extract(text)                        # non-deterministic work belongs in an activity\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def write_record(doc_id: str, fields: dict) -> None:\n    await db.upsert(key=f\"extract:{doc_id}\", value=fields)   # idempotent by key: safe to retry\n\n@workflow.defn\nclass DocumentExtraction:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.approved: bool | None = None\n\n    @workflow.signal\n    def approve(self, decision: bool) -> None:            # delivered durably whenever it arrives\n        self.approved = decision\n\n    @workflow.run\n    async def run(self, doc_id: str) -> str:\n        fields = await workflow.execute_activity(\n            extract_fields, doc_id, start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(minutes=5),\n            retry_policy=RetryPolicy(maximum_attempts=4, initial_interval=timedelta(seconds=5)))\n        if fields.get(\"confidence\", 1.0) < 0.8:\n            try:   # durable wait: no worker is held; survives restarts and deploys\n                await workflow.wait_condition(lambda: self.approved is not None, timeout=timedelta(days=3))\n            except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n                return \"approval_timed_out\"\n            if not self.approved:\n                return \"rejected\"\n        await workflow.execute_activity(write_record, args=[doc_id, fields],\n                                        start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=30))\n        return \"written\"\n\n# Worker(client, task_queue=\"extraction\", workflows=[DocumentExtraction], activities=[extract_fields, write_record])\n```\n\nWalk one low-confidence document through it. The server's history for the run reads, simplified:\n(1) workflow started with `doc_id`; (2) activity `extract_fields` scheduled; (3) activity started,\nrecorded with attempt 2 because the server retried a provider 503 per the retry policy (intermediate\nattempts are not written as separate events); (4) activity completed with result\n`{..., \"confidence\": 0.62}`; (5) a 3-day timer started for the wait. No worker holds anything now. A deploy restarts every worker overnight. The next morning a\nreviewer sends the `approve(True)` signal, which is appended as event (6). A fresh worker replays `run`\nfrom the top: `execute_activity(extract_fields)` is matched to events 2–4 and returns the recorded\nfields without calling the LLM; `wait_condition` is matched to the timer; the signal handler applies\nevent (6), so the condition is now true; and the code reaches `write_record`, which is the first genuinely\nnew command. If someone had put `datetime.now()` in the workflow to stamp the record, the replay would\ncompute a different value than the original run, which is exactly the class of bug the determinism rule\nand the SDK's `workflow.now()` exist to prevent.","tradeoffs":["Airflow's static DAGs give predictable scheduling, backfills and a mature ecosystem, but once the graph depends on runtime data you are fighting the model; dynamic task mapping helps, Prefect removes the constraint.","Prefect's plain-Python flows are fast to write and natural for data-dependent branching, but flows are expected to complete; a run that must wait weeks for an external event is not its design centre.","Temporal's durable execution makes long-lived, stateful processes crash-proof and resumable, in exchange for the determinism discipline in workflow code, versioning for in-flight runs, and operating or buying a server.","Storing every activity input and output in history is what enables replay, but it caps payload sizes; passing blobs by reference keeps histories small at the cost of a second store to manage.","Any orchestrator adds an operational component and a learning curve; for a short chain inside a request, in-process orchestration with good logging wins on simplicity."],"pitfalls":["Doing I/O, reading the clock or generating random values inside Temporal workflow code; the symptom is non-determinism errors on replay after a worker restart or a code deploy.","Assuming the orchestrator makes side effects exactly-once; the symptom is duplicate emails or writes after a retry, fixed only by idempotency keys in the activity or task.","Using Airflow for per-request or event-driven work; the symptom is noticeable scheduler latency between tasks (it was built for batch, not milliseconds) and DAG files sprouting conditionals to fake dynamism.","Writing Airflow tasks that append rather than overwrite their interval's output; the symptom is doubled rows for exactly the days that were retried or backfilled.","Passing megabytes of document text or embeddings through task results or workflow history; the symptom is slow UIs, size limits being hit, and failed runs on large documents.","Deploying changed workflow code with runs in flight and no versioning; the symptom is those runs failing or taking a different path on replay than they did originally."],"when":"Reach for a workflow orchestrator when a process has multiple steps with independent failure, runs\nlonger than a request, needs scheduling or backfill, or waits on humans or external systems. Pick\nAirflow for scheduled batch with a fixed shape and a data-engineering team already running it; Prefect\nwhen the pipeline's shape is decided at runtime and you want plain Python; Temporal when runs are\nlong-lived, stateful and must survive anything, which describes production agent runs and\napproval-gated business processes. Measure run success rate, retries per step, time spent waiting versus\nexecuting, and queue latency. In interviews, be able to explain deterministic replay in one paragraph,\nsay why exactly-once is still your job, and place each tool against an AI pipeline and an agent run.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes idempotent tasks and activities, keeps workflow code deterministic, sets retry policies and timeouts, and keeps large payloads in object storage rather than history.","architect":"Chooses the engine per workload class, decides what state lives in history versus a database, and sets versioning policy and operational ownership of the orchestrator.","fde":"Fits customer approval flows and nightly data syncs onto the engine the customer can operate, and explains replay and retries when they ask why a step ran twice."},"check":{"q":"An agent run calls an LLM activity, then a payment activity. A worker crashes immediately after\nthe payment succeeded but before the activity completion was recorded. What happens on Temporal, and\nwhat must you have done to be safe?","a":"Temporal replays the workflow on another worker from the event history. The LLM activity's result\nis in history, so it is not called again. The payment activity has a scheduled event but no completion\nevent, so once its timeout elapses it is retried according to its retry policy, and the payment code\nruns a second time. Temporal guarantees the workflow's progress is durable; it cannot know whether the\nexternal side effect happened. Safety therefore depends on the payment activity being idempotent: it\nmust use an idempotency key derived from the workflow id and step so the payment provider deduplicates\nthe second call. This is the general rule for every orchestrator: durable progress plus idempotent steps\nis the achievable form of exactly-once."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must Temporal workflow code be deterministic?","options":["Because the server executes workflow code and cannot run arbitrary Python","Because a workflow is recovered by replaying its code against recorded history, which only works if the code makes the same decisions each time","Because activities cannot be retried otherwise","Because determinism is required for the UI to display progress"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Durable execution rebuilds in-memory state by re-running the workflow function and feeding it recorded results. Any divergence, from clocks, randomness or I/O, breaks the match between code and history."},{"q":"Which workload is the poorest fit for Apache Airflow?","options":["Nightly re-embedding of a document corpus with backfill","Hourly data-quality checks on ingested tables","An agent run that pauses for a human approval that may take days","A weekly report-generation pipeline"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Airflow is a scheduler for batch DAGs with a fixed shape. Long-lived, event-driven runs that wait on humans belong to durable execution engines such as Temporal."},{"q":"What does a workflow orchestrator NOT provide, regardless of which one you choose?","options":["Persisting which steps have completed","Retrying failed steps with backoff","Exactly-once execution of side effects against external systems","A view of where each run currently is"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Orchestrators make progress durable, but a crash between performing a side effect and recording its completion leads to a retry. Idempotent steps remain the application's responsibility."}]},{"key":"system-design/memory-architecture","sources":["Park et al., 2023 — Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior","Packer et al., 2023 — MemGPT: Towards LLMs as Operating Systems"],"why":"Every model call starts from zero. The only thing the model knows about this user, this task and the\nlast hour is what the application puts in the prompt. For a single question that is fine. For an\nassistant that runs over weeks it means that preferences, decisions, half-finished tasks and facts\nlearned in earlier sessions vanish unless something carries them forward.\n\nThe naive design is to carry everything forward: append the whole conversation on every call. Cost and\nlatency grow linearly with history, because every turn re-reads all previous turns; the window\neventually fills; and quality drops before that, because relevant facts get buried among hundreds of\nturns of chatter. It also does nothing across sessions or across the user's other devices and agents.\n\nThe second naive design is to embed every utterance into a vector store and retrieve by similarity. This\nbrings back stale and contradictory facts side by side (\"user lives in Berlin\" from last year, \"moved to\nLisbon\" from last week), has no notion of updating or deleting a fact, retrieves things the user told a\ndifferent agent in a different context, and cannot answer \"what did we decide\", because decisions are\nnot textually similar to the question that needs them.\n\nMemory architecture exists to replace both with explicit stores and policies. It borrows the\noperating-system picture: a small, expensive working set (the context window), a larger persistent\nstore, and a controller that decides what to page in and out. The \"what\" is the design problem.\nRemember too little and the assistant is amnesiac; remember too much and it is expensive, confused and\na privacy liability.","how":"### Memory types and where each lives\n1. **Working memory** is the context window: system prompt, the current task, recent turns, retrieved\n   items. It is rebuilt per call by code; the model never stores anything itself.\n2. **Session state** is the full transcript and task state of one conversation or run, kept in a\n   database keyed by conversation id. It is the source of truth for the session *because* the context is\n   a view of it, not the other way around; compaction summarizes it for the prompt without deleting it.\n3. **Episodic memory** records what happened: past sessions, outcomes, decisions, with timestamps. It\n   answers \"what did we do last time\".\n4. **Semantic memory** holds distilled facts and preferences: \"prefers concise answers\", \"account tier:\n   enterprise\", \"project X uses Postgres\". It is small, structured where possible, and updated in place.\n5. **Procedural memory** is learned how-to: instructions, tool-usage notes, corrections that should\n   change future behaviour. It lands in prompts, skills or configuration, usually after human review.\n6. **External knowledge** (documents, databases) is not memory; it is retrieval, covered by the RAG\n   architecture lesson. Keeping them separate matters *because* memory is per user and mutable while\n   knowledge is shared and versioned.\n\n### The four operations\n- **Write.** Decide what is worth keeping. Options: the model calls a `remember` tool explicitly; a\n  background extractor reads the session after each turn or at session end and proposes facts; or the\n  application records structured events (purchases, settings changes) directly. Extraction at write time\n  keeps reads cheap *because* a read happens on every call and a write only when something changes.\n- **Read.** Build the working set: the last *n* turns verbatim, a rolling summary of older turns,\n  semantic facts for this user that match the current task, and episodic items ranked by relevance,\n  recency and importance. Scope filters (tenant, user, agent, purpose) apply inside the query, not\n  after, for the same reason as ACLs in RAG.\n- **Update and consolidate.** New facts that contradict old ones must supersede them, not sit beside\n  them; duplicates must merge; many episodes collapse into one summary. Detecting a contradiction is\n  the hard part, and embedding similarity alone is a poor detector: \"lives in Berlin\" and \"moved to\n  Lisbon\" may not be close vectors, while \"likes Python\" and \"dislikes Python\" are. Two mechanisms work.\n  For facts that drive behaviour, use typed slots (`home_city`, `answer_style`) so an update is an\n  upsert by key and supersession is deterministic. For free-text memories, retrieve the few nearest\n  existing memories and ask a model to choose add, update, delete or no-op against them. Consolidation\n  runs as a background job *because* it needs a model call and should not sit in the request path.\n- **Forget.** Time-to-live for episodic items, explicit deletion on user request, purpose limits (memory\n  collected for support is not used for marketing), and tenant off-boarding. Deletion must reach every\n  derived copy, including embeddings and summaries, which is why memory rows need lineage back to their\n  source events.\n\n### A read policy, concretely\nA common ranking, from the generative-agents line of work, scores each candidate as a weighted sum of\nrelevance (embedding similarity to the current task), recency (exponential decay on age) and importance\n(a score assigned at write time), then takes the top *k* under a token budget:\n`score = w_rel·sim(q, m) + w_rec·exp(−age/τ) + w_imp·importance`, with each term normalized to [0, 1] so\nthe weights mean what they say. At scale, an ANN search fetches a few hundred candidates by relevance and\nthe composite score reranks only those, *because* computing recency and importance over every row is\nwasted work. The weights and τ are product decisions: a coding assistant weights recency (yesterday's\nbranch matters, last quarter's does not), a CRM assistant weights importance (the contract renewal date\nmatters however old it is).\n\n### Who controls paging\nThe MemGPT line of work makes the operating-system analogy literal: the model itself is given tools to\nread, write and evict memory, and decides when to page items in and out of its context. That is the\nsame set of operations as above with the controller moved from application code into the model. It buys\nflexibility on open-ended tasks and costs predictability, extra calls, and a model that sometimes forgets\nto remember, so production systems often combine a code-driven baseline read with model-callable memory\ntools for the rest.\n\n### Scoping and isolation\nEvery memory row carries tenant id, user id, the agent or purpose it was collected for, a source event\nid and timestamps. Cross-user leakage through memory is the most embarrassing failure in this area, and\nit happens when the key is the agent instead of the user, or when a shared summary is built across\nusers.\n\n### Budget arithmetic (illustrative)\nA 200-turn conversation at roughly 150 tokens per turn replays about 30,000 tokens per call if sent\nverbatim. Keeping the last 10 turns verbatim (about 1,500 tokens), a 400-token rolling summary and 800\ntokens of retrieved memories puts the working set under 3,000 tokens, a tenfold reduction that stays\nflat as the conversation grows. Over the whole conversation the gap is larger still, because verbatim\nreplay re-sends every earlier turn on every call: Σ 150·t for t = 1…200 ≈ 3.0M input tokens, against at\nmost about 0.6M for the bounded design. Linear-per-call growth is quadratic in total. That flat line is\nthe architectural goal.","example":"A memory layer with the tiers wired together: a session store as the source of truth, a write policy\nthat extracts facts after each turn and supersedes contradictions (by slot for typed facts, by model\nadjudication for free text), and a read policy that assembles a\nbounded working set using relevance, recency and scope. `llm`, `embed`, `cosine` and `store` are\nstand-ins for your model, embedding model and database.\n\n```python\nimport math, time\n\nRECENT_TURNS, SUMMARY_EVERY, MEM_BUDGET_TOKENS = 10, 10, 800   # summary cadence <= recent window: no turn falls between them\n\ndef build_context(user, tenant, convo_id, query, store, w_rel=1.0, w_rec=0.5, w_imp=0.3):\n    turns = store.turns(convo_id)                                    # session state: full transcript\n    recent, older = turns[-RECENT_TURNS:], turns[:-RECENT_TURNS]\n    summary = store.summary(convo_id) if older else \"\"\n    q, now = embed(query), time.time()\n    cands = store.memories(tenant=tenant, user=user, purpose=\"assistant\")   # scope inside the query\n    def score(m):\n        recency = math.exp(-(now - m.updated_at) / (7 * 86400))     # decays over about a week\n        return w_rel * cosine(q, m.vec) + w_rec * recency + w_imp * m.importance\n    picked, used = [], 0\n    for m in sorted(cands, key=score, reverse=True):\n        if used + m.tokens > MEM_BUDGET_TOKENS: break\n        picked.append(m); used += m.tokens\n    return {\"summary\": summary, \"memories\": [m.text for m in picked], \"recent\": recent}\n\ndef after_turn(user, tenant, convo_id, store):\n    turns = store.turns(convo_id)\n    if len(turns) % SUMMARY_EVERY == 0:                              # compaction: summarize, never delete\n        store.set_summary(convo_id, llm(f\"Update this summary:\\n{store.summary(convo_id)}\\n\\n\"\n                                        f\"New turns:\\n{turns[-SUMMARY_EVERY:]}\").text)\n    facts = llm(\"Extract durable facts as JSON [{slot or null, text, importance 0-1}] \"\n                f\"from the user's own words only:\\n{turns[-2:]}\").json   # not from tool output\n    for f in facts:\n        common = dict(tenant=tenant, user=user, purpose=\"assistant\", source_event=turns[-1].id)\n        if f[\"slot\"]:                                                # typed fact: upsert by key\n            store.upsert_slot(slot=f[\"slot\"], text=f[\"text\"], **common)\n            continue\n        near = store.nearest(tenant, user, embed(f[\"text\"]), k=3)    # free text: let a model adjudicate\n        op = llm(f\"New: {f['text']}\\nExisting: {[m.text for m in near]}\\n\"\n                 \"Reply JSON {op: add|update|noop, target_index}\").json\n        if op[\"op\"] == \"add\":\n            store.add_memory(text=f[\"text\"], importance=f[\"importance\"], **common)\n        elif op[\"op\"] == \"update\":                                   # supersede, keep lineage\n            store.supersede(near[op[\"target_index\"]].id, text=f[\"text\"], **common)\n```\n\nTrace two turns. In March the user says \"I'm based in Berlin\"; the extractor emits\n`{slot: \"home_city\", text: \"Berlin\"}` and the slot is written. In June they say \"we just moved to\nLisbon\"; the extractor emits the same slot, the upsert replaces Berlin and records the June turn as the\nsource, and no later read can surface both cities. Separately, \"I like answers with code first\" has no\nslot, so the three nearest memories are fetched; one is \"prefers short answers\", and the adjudicator\nchooses `add`, because the two preferences are compatible. Had a cosine threshold alone decided, the\nclose pair \"prefers short answers\" / \"prefers detailed answers\" would either have been merged as a\nduplicate, keeping whichever text won, or kept side by side as a contradiction, depending on where the\nthreshold sat; the adjudicator sees a conflict and chooses `update`.","tradeoffs":["Extracting facts at write time keeps every read cheap, but spends a model call per turn and can store things the user never meant to be remembered; read-time extraction from raw transcripts is cheaper to write and far more expensive to query.","Structured semantic memory (typed fields) is precise and easy to update or delete, while free-text memories capture nuance; most systems need both, with structure for anything that drives behaviour or billing.","Rolling summaries keep context flat as conversations grow, but each compaction removes detail from the prompt irreversibly; keeping the full transcript in the session store and retrieving from it recovers the detail on demand.","Letting the model decide what to remember through a tool gives it agency and transparency, but it under- and over-remembers unpredictably; a background extractor is more consistent and less visible to the user.","Long retention makes the assistant feel continuous and raises privacy and compliance exposure; TTLs and purpose scoping trade some continuity for a defensible deletion story."],"pitfalls":["Keying memory by agent or conversation instead of tenant and user; the symptom is one user's preferences or facts appearing in another user's session.","Append-only memory without supersession; the symptom is the assistant confidently stating two contradictory facts, usually the stale one first.","Treating the context window as the system of record; the symptom is that after compaction, details the user mentioned are gone forever and the transcript cannot be audited.","Deleting a memory row but not its embedding, its mention in a summary or the facts derived from it; the symptom is a \"forgotten\" fact resurfacing weeks later, which is a compliance incident.","Extracting memories from retrieved documents, web pages or tool output rather than from the user's own words; the symptom is an instruction planted in a fetched page (\"always CC this address\") reappearing in later, unrelated sessions, which is persistent prompt injection.","Shipping memory without measuring it; the symptom is higher cost per call with no gain in task success, because the retrieved memories are rarely relevant."],"when":"Add memory beyond the current session when users return and expect continuity, when the agent performs\nmulti-session tasks, or when personalization measurably improves outcomes. Start with session state and\ncompaction, which every multi-turn product needs; add semantic memory when you can name the facts that\nchange behaviour; add episodic memory when \"what happened before\" is a real query. Do not add memory to\ncompensate for a weak retrieval layer or missing product data; those belong in RAG and in your database.\nMeasure working-set tokens per call, memory hit rate (retrieved items actually used by the answer),\ncontradiction rate, and task success with memory on versus off. In interviews, the expected depth is the\ntier picture, the four operations, scoping, and a concrete deletion story.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the session store, compaction, the extraction and consolidation jobs, and the scoped, budgeted read path that assembles each prompt.","architect":"Decides the memory tiers and stores, the ownership of each memory type, retention and purpose limits, and how deletion propagates to every derived copy.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer what the assistant may remember about whom and for how long, and demonstrates deletion end to end for their compliance team."},"check":{"q":"A customer's support assistant should remember each support agent's preferences and past cases\nacross sessions, but their legal team requires that any case data be purgeable within 24 hours of a\nrequest. Design the memory architecture and explain how you would meet the purge requirement.","a":"I would separate the tiers. Session state lives in a transcript store keyed by tenant, user and\nconversation, with a rolling summary for the prompt. Semantic memory holds structured preferences per\nuser, updated in place with supersession. Episodic memory holds case summaries with a case id, tenant,\nuser, purpose, source event ids and timestamps, with a TTL. Every derived artifact, embeddings, summaries\nand extracted facts, records the source event ids it came from. A purge request for a case then becomes\na lineage query: find every row derived from events of that case, delete rows and vectors, and\nregenerate any summary that referenced them. Reads filter by tenant and user inside the query so nothing\ncrosses accounts. I would prove the purge with a test that plants a unique fact, purges, and shows that\nno retrieval, summary or extraction can surface it afterwards."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a session transcript store, rather than the context window, treated as the source of truth for a conversation?","options":["The context window cannot hold more than a few turns","Compaction removes detail from the prompt, so without a persistent transcript that detail is lost and cannot be retrieved or audited later","Models require transcripts to be stored before answering","Transcripts are cheaper to send than summaries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The prompt is a view built per call. Summarizing older turns keeps it small, but only works safely if the full record exists elsewhere to retrieve from and to audit."},{"q":"What problem does supersession in semantic memory solve?","options":["It reduces embedding dimensionality for faster search","It prevents the model from storing more than one fact per turn","It ensures a new fact that contradicts an old one replaces it, so the assistant does not retrieve both and state the stale one","It allows memories to be shared across tenants"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Append-only memory accumulates contradictions (\"lives in Berlin\", \"moved to Lisbon\"). Linking the new fact to the one it replaces keeps reads consistent and makes deletion traceable."},{"q":"Which design most directly prevents one user's memories from appearing in another user's session?","options":["Encrypting memory rows at rest","Using a separate embedding model per user","Keying every memory by tenant and user and applying those filters inside the retrieval query","Limiting memories to the last seven days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Leakage comes from wrong keys and post-filtering, not from encryption or recency. Scope must be part of the row and enforced where candidates are selected."}]},{"key":"system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","sources":["Evans, 2003 — Domain-Driven Design: Tackling Complexity in the Heart of Software","Vernon, 2013 — Implementing Domain-Driven Design","Conway, 1968 — How Do Committees Invent?"],"why":"Ask billing, support and sales what a \"customer\" is. Billing says a legal entity with a payment method\nand invoices. Support says a person with a login and a ticket history. Sales says an account with an\nowner, a pipeline stage and a renewal date. All three are right inside their own work and wrong inside\neach other's. A system that tries to define one Customer table with every field from every department\ngets a model nobody fully understands, where a change for billing breaks support, and where every team\nmust coordinate with every other team to ship anything.\n\nThe naive alternatives are both common. One is the enterprise canonical model: a committee defines the\nsingle true schema for every concept and everyone conforms. It fails because meaning is local; the\ncanonical model becomes a lowest common denominator with dozens of optional fields, and the committee\nbecomes the bottleneck. The other is splitting by technical layer or by table, a \"customer service\" that\nowns the Customer table for everyone, which recreates the same coupling behind an API.\n\nDomain-driven design starts from the observation that the inconsistency is not a defect to be fixed but\na fact about the business to be modelled. Each area has its own language, used by its own people, and\nthe software for that area should use exactly that language, consistently, inside a boundary. Between\nboundaries, translation is explicit. Conway's observation that systems mirror the communication\nstructures of the organizations that build them is why this works: a boundary that matches a team's\nresponsibility is one that team can own and change alone.\n\nAI systems raise the stakes. An agent is usually a consumer that reaches across many contexts at once,\nthrough tools, and retrieval indexes mix documents written in several vocabularies. If nobody has drawn\nthe boundaries, the agent inherits every ambiguity and resolves it by guessing.","how":"### Ubiquitous language\nWithin one area, developers and domain experts use the same terms, and the code uses them too: class\nnames, tool names, event names, prompts. The discipline is that if a term means two things, you have\nfound a boundary; if two terms mean one thing, you pick one. For an AI product this language is also\nwhat goes into system prompts, tool descriptions and evaluation rubrics, so inconsistency there shows up\ndirectly as model errors.\n\n### Bounded contexts\nA bounded context is the boundary inside which a model and its language are consistent. It owns its\ndata, its invariants and its storage; nothing outside writes to that storage directly. Draw contexts by\nlanguage and by responsibility, not by data volume or by technology. Billing, Support and Sales from the\nexample are three contexts, each with its own Customer, and that is correct. A context may be a service,\na module in a modular monolith, or a schema in one database; the boundary is about ownership and\nmeaning, and deployment is a separate decision.\n\n### Finding the boundaries\nBoundaries are discovered, not decreed, and the usual technique is to map the business process with\ndomain experts as a timeline of domain events (event storming) and then look for seams. Four signals\nmark a seam: a term that changes meaning (\"customer\", \"active\", \"account\"); a change in who decides\n(finance owns the dunning rule, support owns the escalation rule); a change in rate of change (pricing\nrules change weekly, ledger rules almost never); and a set of rules that must hold together at the same\ninstant, which belongs inside one context. When two candidate contexts must constantly ask each other\nquestions to make any decision, the seam is in the wrong place.\n\n### Subdomain classification\nNot every context deserves the same investment. The *core* subdomain is where the business\ndifferentiates, and it gets the most careful modelling and your best people. *Supporting* subdomains are\nnecessary but not differentiating, and *generic* subdomains (authentication, invoicing) should be bought\nor borrowed. For most AI products the core is the domain logic and the data, not the model call; this\nclassification is what tells you where custom work belongs and where a vendor is fine.\n\n### Context mapping: how boundaries relate\nThe relationships between contexts are named so teams can discuss them:\n- **Shared kernel**: two contexts share a small model and change it together. Cheap and dangerous; keep\n  it tiny.\n- **Customer–supplier**: the downstream team's needs feed the upstream team's roadmap.\n- **Conformist**: downstream adopts the upstream model wholesale because it has no leverage, typical with\n  a vendor API.\n- **Anticorruption layer (ACL)**: downstream translates the upstream model into its own at the boundary,\n  *because* letting a foreign model leak in corrupts your language one field at a time. This is the most\n  important pattern for AI integrations: the agent's context should see its own concepts, with adapters\n  translating each external system.\n- **Open host service with a published language**: upstream exposes a stable, documented interface in a\n  well-defined language for many consumers. A tool schema an agent calls is exactly a published language.\n- **Separate ways**: the contexts do not integrate at all. Sometimes correct.\n\n### Inside a context: aggregates, entities, value objects, domain events\nAn *aggregate* is a cluster of objects changed together under one transaction through one entry point\n(the root), with invariants that hold at the end of every transaction. Take an `Order` with its lines\nand the invariant \"total must not exceed the approved credit of 10,000\". Lines are changed only through\n`order.add_line(...)`, *because* if code could insert a line row directly, nothing would recheck the\ntotal. The design rules follow from that purpose. Keep aggregates as small as the true invariants\nallow, *because* the aggregate is the unit of locking and a large one serializes unrelated edits.\nReference other aggregates by id, not by object, so loading an order does not drag in the customer.\nChange one aggregate per transaction, and let the others catch up through *domain events*\n(\"OrderPlaced\", \"InvoicePaid\"); cross-context changes are always eventually consistent, *because* a\nsingle transaction spanning contexts would couple their storage and their uptime. This is where DDD\nhands over to event-driven architecture and sagas. *Entities* have identity over time (the order is the\nsame order after its lines change); *value objects* are defined by their attributes and are immutable\n(`Money(120, \"EUR\")`), which makes them safe to share and pass across boundaries. A *repository* loads\nand saves whole aggregates, one per aggregate type, which keeps the invariant check from being bypassed\nby partial writes.\n\n### Applying it to an AI system\n- One tool, or tool group, per bounded context, speaking that context's published language; the agent's\n  tool list then mirrors the context map.\n- Translation lives in tool adapters (the ACL), never in the prompt. Asking the model to reconcile three\n  meanings of \"customer\" at runtime is asking it to redo your architecture per request.\n- Retrieval indexes and memory are scoped per context when vocabularies conflict, or tagged with the\n  context so the prompt can say which \"account\" a passage refers to.\n- Consequential actions respect aggregate boundaries: an agent step that must update two contexts is a\n  saga with compensation, not one transaction.\n- Evals are per context, written in that context's language by that context's experts.","example":"Two bounded contexts, each with its own Customer, and a support agent that sees only its own concept\nthrough an anticorruption layer. The tool the agent calls is a published language; the translation from\nbilling's model happens in the adapter, not in the prompt. `billing_api`, `mapping_repo` and `tickets`\nare stand-ins for the real clients.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom datetime import date\n\n# Billing context: its own language and storage, owned by the billing team\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass BillingAccount:\n    account_id: str\n    legal_name: str\n    plan: str                      # billing's codes: \"ent\", \"pro\", \"free\"\n    past_due_since: date | None\n\n# Support context: a different \"customer\", shaped for support work\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass SupportCustomer:\n    customer_id: str\n    display_name: str\n    tier: str                      # support's words: \"enterprise\", \"pro\", \"free\"\n    payment_blocked: bool\n    open_tickets: int\n\nPLAN_TO_TIER = {\"ent\": \"enterprise\", \"pro\": \"pro\", \"free\": \"free\"}\n\nclass BillingAdapter:              # anticorruption layer: billing model -> support language\n    def __init__(self, billing_api, mapping_repo):\n        self.api, self.map = billing_api, mapping_repo\n    def payment_status(self, customer_id: str) -> dict:\n        acct: BillingAccount = self.api.get_account(self.map.billing_id_for(customer_id))\n        blocked = acct.past_due_since is not None and (date.today() - acct.past_due_since).days > 14\n        if acct.plan not in PLAN_TO_TIER:            # fail loudly: a silent default corrupts meaning\n            raise UnmappedUpstreamValue(f\"billing plan {acct.plan!r}\")\n        return {\"payment_blocked\": blocked, \"tier\": PLAN_TO_TIER[acct.plan]}\n\ndef lookup_customer(customer_id: str, tickets, billing: BillingAdapter) -> SupportCustomer:\n    \"\"\"Published language for the agent: no billing field names leak into its context.\"\"\"\n    b = billing.payment_status(customer_id)\n    return SupportCustomer(customer_id=customer_id, display_name=tickets.name(customer_id),\n                           tier=b[\"tier\"], payment_blocked=b[\"payment_blocked\"],\n                           open_tickets=tickets.count_open(customer_id))\n\nTOOLS = [{\"name\": \"lookup_customer\", \"description\": \"Fetch the support view of a customer.\",\n          \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"customer_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                           \"required\": [\"customer_id\"]}}]\n```\n\nRun one lookup. Billing returns `BillingAccount(\"B-881\", \"Acme GmbH\", \"ent\", 2026-09-30)`; on\n2026-10-20 the account is 20 days past due, more than 14, so the agent sees\n`SupportCustomer(tier=\"enterprise\", payment_blocked=True, open_tickets=3)` and never the words \"ent\" or\n`past_due_since`. The rule \"payment is blocked after 14 days past due\" is a support-context rule, so it\nlives in the support side's adapter, versioned and tested there, and neither billing nor the prompt needs\nto know it. Now billing launches a \"team\" plan. With a `.get(plan, \"free\")` default, every team-plan\ncustomer would silently be treated as free tier by the agent, a wrong answer with no error anywhere. The\nexplicit map turns the upstream change into a failing call and an alert on day one, which is the point of\nan anticorruption layer: translation is a contract, and an unmapped value is a contract break.","tradeoffs":["Separate models per context remove cross-team coupling and keep each model small, but the same real-world thing now exists in several places and must be reconciled through events or adapters.","An anticorruption layer protects your language from upstream churn, at the cost of maintaining a translation that changes whenever either side changes; for a tiny, stable upstream, conforming is cheaper.","Eventual consistency across aggregates and contexts gives independent deployability and uptime, but introduces windows where contexts disagree; when the business cannot tolerate that window, the boundary is probably drawn in the wrong place.","A rich core-domain model repays deep investment, while applying the same rigor to generic subdomains wastes it; misclassifying the core is the expensive mistake.","Giving an agent one tool per context keeps meanings clean and privileges scoped, but multiplies tools; when contexts are many, a facade context that speaks the agent's language may be justified."],"pitfalls":["One shared database schema behind several \"services\"; the symptom is a migration in one team's context breaking another team's deployment.","Drawing contexts by technical layer or by noun (\"the customer service\"); the symptom is every feature touching every service and release trains that must be coordinated.","Letting an upstream model leak through without an ACL; the symptom is vendor field names and enums appearing in your prompts, your UI and your tests.","Asking the model to reconcile conflicting vocabularies at runtime; the symptom is answers that mix the billing and support meanings of a term, and evals that cannot agree on correctness.","Translation tables in adapters with catch-all defaults; the symptom is a new upstream enum value (a new plan, a new status) quietly mapped to the wrong meaning and discovered weeks later through customer complaints rather than errors.","Transactions that span contexts; the symptom is distributed locks, timeouts under load, and partial updates nobody can compensate."],"when":"Reach for explicit bounded contexts when more than one team or more than one vocabulary touches the\nsystem, when an agent must integrate several enterprise systems, or when a shared schema has become the\nbottleneck for change. For a small product with one team and one language, one context is correct; do\nnot fragment prematurely. Measure coupling: how many contexts a typical change touches, how often\ncross-context releases must be coordinated, and how many tool adapters contain translation versus\npass-through. Day to day this shows up as naming decisions, where a rule lives, and whether a new field\nbelongs to you. In interviews, expect to be asked to draw a context map for a given business, to place an\nagent and its tools on it, and to explain why an anticorruption layer belongs in the adapter rather than\nthe prompt.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps each context's language consistent in code, tools and prompts, writes adapters that translate at the boundary, and publishes events instead of writing to another context's tables.","architect":"Draws the context map, classifies core versus supporting versus generic subdomains, chooses the relationship pattern per boundary, and decides where the agent sits on the map.","fde":"Discovers the customer's real vocabularies and system owners, maps each to a context and an adapter, and resolves \"which system is the source of truth\" before the agent is built."},"check":{"q":"You are building an agent that answers \"can this customer upgrade?\" using a CRM, a billing system\nand a support desk, each with its own notion of customer and status. Where do you put the logic that\nreconciles them, and why not in the prompt?","a":"I would treat the agent's own domain as a bounded context with its own small model, say an\nEligibility view with fields like tier, payment standing and open escalations, and build one adapter per\nupstream system acting as an anticorruption layer: the CRM adapter maps account stages to the agent's\ntier, the billing adapter maps past-due rules to payment standing, the support adapter counts\nescalations. The agent gets one tool in its own published language. Putting the reconciliation in the\nprompt would make the model re-derive the business rules on every request, from three vocabularies it\nonly partly understands, with no tests and no owner; a mapping change would be a prompt edit with\nunpredictable effects. In adapters the rules are code, owned by the team that owns each boundary,\nunit-tested, and versioned independently of the model."},"quiz":[{"q":"Billing, Support and Sales each define \"customer\" differently. What does domain-driven design recommend?","options":["Define one canonical Customer schema with every field and make all three conform","Keep a separate model per bounded context and translate explicitly at the boundaries","Store all customer data in one service that the other three call","Let the agent reconcile the differences at runtime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Meaning is local to each area of the business. A single model becomes a lowest common denominator owned by a committee; separate models with explicit translation let each team change independently."},{"q":"What is the role of an anticorruption layer when an agent integrates a vendor system?","options":["To scan vendor responses for malicious content","To translate the vendor's model into the agent's own language so foreign fields and semantics do not leak into prompts and code","To cache vendor responses for performance","To enforce rate limits on vendor calls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ACL protects a context's language. Without it, upstream names and enums spread through prompts, tests and UI, and every upstream change becomes your change."},{"q":"Why are changes that span two bounded contexts usually made eventually consistent via events rather than one transaction?","options":["Databases cannot run transactions across more than one table","Events are faster than transactions","A single transaction would couple the contexts' storage and availability, defeating the point of separate ownership","Domain-driven design forbids transactions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aggregates are the unit of transactional consistency; across contexts, independent deployability and uptime matter more than instantaneous agreement, so domain events and sagas carry the change."}]},{"key":"system-design/event-driven-architecture","sources":["Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns","Fowler, 2017 — What do you mean by \"Event-Driven\"?","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Apache Kafka"],"why":"Start with the naive design. The ingestion endpoint receives a PDF and then, in the same request,\ncalls the parser, the chunker, the embedder, the index writer, the notification service and the\nanalytics API. Three things go wrong as the system grows.\n\nFirst, **temporal coupling**: the upload succeeds only if all six downstream services are up at that\ninstant. If each is available 99.9% of the time, the chain is available roughly 0.999⁶ ≈ 99.4%, and\nits latency is the sum of six latencies. The slowest or flakiest consumer sets the user's experience.\n\nSecond, **knowledge coupling**: every new consumer (a compliance team wants a copy of every upload) means\na code change and a redeploy of the producer, plus a new failure mode inside the producer's request path.\nThe producer accumulates reasons to change that have nothing to do with uploading.\n\nThird, **lost history**: once the request returns, the fact that an upload happened exists only as a row\nthe producer chose to keep. You cannot replay last Tuesday's uploads through a fixed chunker, because\nthe calls were ephemeral.\n\nEvents invert the dependency. The producer's only job is to durably record the fact. Consumers depend on\nthe event contract, not on the producer; the producer depends on nothing. Availability becomes \"can I\nwrite to the broker\", and history becomes the log itself. That inversion, not the broker technology, is\nthe idea.","how":"### Events versus commands\nAn **event** is a past-tense, immutable statement of fact owned by the producer: `OrderPlaced`,\n`EmbeddingGenerated`. It has no addressee and no expected reply. A **command** (`ChargeCard`) is\nimperative, has exactly one handler and implies an outcome the sender cares about. The distinction\nmatters because an event lets the producer stay ignorant of consumers, whereas a command re-introduces\nthe coupling you were escaping. If you find yourself publishing `SendWelcomeEmail` as an event, you have\nwritten a remote procedure call with extra steps.\n\n### The envelope\nEvery event carries metadata separate from its payload: a globally unique `event_id` (so consumers can\ndeduplicate), `type` and `schema_version` (so consumers can evolve independently), `occurred_at`, the\n`aggregate_id` it belongs to (the partition key, see below), and a `trace_id` for observability. The\npayload is either a thin notification (\"document 42 changed, fetch it\") or a self-contained snapshot\n(\"document 42 now has these fields\"). Fowler calls these *event notification* and *event-carried state\ntransfer*; the second removes a callback to the producer at the cost of larger messages and consumers\nholding copies of data that can go stale.\n\n### The broker and its guarantees\nA log-style broker (Kafka, Pulsar, Redpanda) appends events to **partitions** and lets each consumer\ngroup keep its own **offset**. Ordering is guaranteed only within a partition, so you choose the\npartition key to be the thing whose order matters, usually the aggregate id: all events for document 42\nland in one partition and are seen in order, while documents 42 and 43 may interleave arbitrarily.\nBecause the log is retained, a new consumer can start from offset 0 and rebuild its state, which is the\nreplay property the naive design lacked. Queue-style brokers (RabbitMQ, SQS) instead delete a message\nonce a consumer acknowledges it and hand each message to one competing consumer; they are simpler for\nwork distribution but lose replay.\n\nBoth styles deliver **at least once**: a consumer that crashes after processing but before acknowledging\nwill see the message again. Consumers must therefore be idempotent, by recording processed `event_id`s or\nby making the effect naturally repeatable. Exactly-once is a property you build at the consumer, not one\nthe broker gives you end to end: Kafka's transactional producers cover only Kafka-to-Kafka read-process-write\nloops, and any effect outside the log (a database write, an HTTP call) still needs idempotence.\n\nPartitions are also the unit of parallelism: within one consumer group each partition is read by at most\none consumer at a time, so a topic with 12 partitions can keep at most 12 consumers of a group busy.\nChoosing the partition count is therefore a capacity decision you make up front, because adding\npartitions later changes which partition a key hashes to and breaks per-key ordering across the change.\n\n### When a consumer cannot process an event\nA message that fails every time (a malformed payload, a PDF that crashes the parser) is a **poison\nmessage**, and the way you retry it decides what you lose. Retrying in place preserves order but blocks\nthe whole partition behind it: every later event on that partition, for every document that hashes\nthere, waits. Moving it to a retry topic or dead-letter queue (DLQ) unblocks the partition, but if a\nlater event for the *same* key is processed while the failed one sits in the DLQ, that key's order is\nbroken (an `Updated` overtakes its `Created`). The careful middle path is to park the key: record it as\nblocked, divert its subsequent events to the side as well, and release them in order once the head\nevent is fixed or discarded. Which of the three is right depends on whether per-key order or partition\nthroughput matters more for that event type, and it should be decided per topic, not discovered.\n\n### Producing reliably\nThe producer writes its own database row and publishes the event. Doing those as two separate operations\nis the **dual-write problem**: a crash between them loses the event or emits one for a rolled-back\ntransaction. The transactional outbox pattern fixes it by writing the event into the same database\ntransaction and relaying it afterwards.\n\n### Choreography versus orchestration\nWith **choreography** each service reacts to events and emits new ones; the workflow is emergent. With\n**orchestration** a dedicated component listens for events and issues commands to drive the steps. For\ntwo or three steps choreography is lighter; beyond that nobody can answer \"where is order 42 right now\"\nwithout an orchestrator or a very good trace. Multi-step agent workflows with compensation needs usually\nend up orchestrated.\n\n### Flavours worth naming\n*Event sourcing* goes further and makes the event log the system of record, rebuilding current state by\nfolding the events. *CQRS* separates the write model from read models that are built from events. Both\nare separate commitments with their own costs; \"event-driven\" does not require either.","example":"A document-ingestion flow expressed as events. `publish` and `consume` stand in for any broker client;\nthe parts that matter are the envelope, the partition key and the idempotent consumer.\n\n```python\nimport uuid, json, datetime as dt\n\ndef make_event(type_, aggregate_id, payload, trace_id, version=1):\n    return {\n        \"event_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()),      # dedupe key for consumers\n        \"type\": type_, \"schema_version\": version,\n        \"aggregate_id\": aggregate_id,       # partition key: per-document ordering\n        \"occurred_at\": dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).isoformat(),\n        \"trace_id\": trace_id, \"payload\": payload,\n    }\n\n# producer: the upload service knows nothing about who listens\ndef on_upload(doc_id, storage_uri, tenant_id, trace_id, publish):\n    ev = make_event(\"DocumentUploaded\", doc_id,\n                    {\"uri\": storage_uri, \"tenant_id\": tenant_id}, trace_id)\n    publish(topic=\"documents\", key=doc_id, value=json.dumps(ev))\n\n# consumer: embedder reacts, and tolerates redelivery. The vector store is not in the same\n# transaction as any dedupe table, so idempotence comes from the effect itself: chunk ids are\n# deterministic (doc_id:chunk_no), and re-indexing the same document upserts the same rows.\ndef on_document_uploaded(raw, chunk_and_embed, vector_upsert, publish):\n    ev = json.loads(raw)\n    doc_id = ev[\"aggregate_id\"]\n    chunks = chunk_and_embed(ev[\"payload\"][\"uri\"])          # list of (text, vector)\n    vector_upsert([{\"id\": f\"{doc_id}:{i}\", \"vector\": v, \"text\": t,\n                    \"tenant_id\": ev[\"payload\"][\"tenant_id\"]} for i, (t, v) in enumerate(chunks)])\n    chunk_count = len(chunks)\n    # crash here => redelivery re-embeds and overwrites identical ids (wasted work, no duplicates)\n    publish(topic=\"documents\", key=doc_id, value=json.dumps(\n        make_event(\"DocumentIndexed\", ev[\"aggregate_id\"],\n                   {\"chunks\": chunk_count}, ev[\"trace_id\"])))\n```\n\nAdding a billing meter later means one more consumer of `DocumentIndexed`; the upload service and the\nembedder do not change. If the embedder is down for an hour, the events wait on the topic and are\nprocessed when it returns, in per-document order. Two details carry the design. A random chunk id would\nmake every redelivery add a second copy of the document to the index; the deterministic id turns the\nredelivery into an overwrite. And the downstream `publish` after the upsert can itself be lost or\nduplicated, which is acceptable only because the next consumer is idempotent too; if it were not, the\nembedder would need its own outbox. Note one gap the sketch leaves: if a re-upload produces *fewer*\nchunks, the old high-numbered chunks survive, so a real indexer deletes `doc_id:*` ids beyond the new\ncount or versions chunks by document revision.","tradeoffs":["Producers gain independence from consumers, but you lose the synchronous answer: the uploader cannot tell the user \"indexed, ask away\" unless something waits for `DocumentIndexed`; this flips back toward a synchronous call when the user needs the result in the same interaction.","Replayable logs let you rebuild indexes and backfill new consumers, but every event schema becomes a long-lived public contract; once events are retained for months, a breaking payload change needs versioning and dual-reading consumers.","Choreography keeps services small and decoupled, yet end-to-end visibility degrades with each hop; past roughly four or five steps, or whenever compensation is needed, orchestration pays for itself.","Event-carried state transfer removes callbacks and lets consumers survive producer outages, at the cost of duplicated data that drifts; thin notifications stay fresh but create a thundering herd of reads on the producer when a hot event fans out.","At-least-once delivery with idempotent consumers is robust and cheap, but it pushes deduplication state into every consumer; exactly-once brokers trade throughput and operational complexity for it."],"pitfalls":["Commands disguised as events (`SendEmailRequested`) with exactly one intended handler; the symptom is producers that break when \"their\" consumer changes, which means the coupling never left.","Dual writes without an outbox: rows exist with no corresponding event, usually noticed as \"the index is missing documents that are in the database\" after a deploy or a crash.","Assuming global ordering; a consumer that processes `DocumentDeleted` before `DocumentUploaded` because the two were keyed differently will resurrect deleted content in the index.","Non-idempotent consumers; duplicate embeddings, double-counted billing and repeated notifications appear after any consumer restart or broker rebalance.","Event soup: dozens of topics with no owner, no schema registry and no documented consumers; the symptom is that nobody dares delete or change anything, and the system's behaviour is discovered by reading consumer code.","Treating the broker as infinitely fast: a slow consumer accumulates lag silently until a dashboard shows events processed hours after they occurred; consumer lag must be a first-class alert.","Retrying a poison message in place forever; the symptom is lag climbing on one partition while every other partition of the group sits at zero, and the same offset appearing in the error log on a loop."],"when":"Reach for events when more than one party cares about a fact, when consumers may be down while\nproducers are up, when you need to replay history, or when the producer's latency budget cannot absorb\nthe consumers' work. Typical AI examples: ingestion pipelines (upload, parse, chunk, embed, index),\nagent run telemetry fanned out to billing, evaluation sampling and audit, and model-output moderation\nthat must not block the response. Do not use events when the caller needs the result now, when there is\nexactly one consumer and the workflow is short, or when the team has no way to trace a flow across\nservices yet. Measure consumer lag, redelivery rate and dead-letter volume; those three numbers tell you\nwhether the architecture is healthy. In interviews, the expected depth is to state the dual-write\nproblem unprompted, explain per-partition ordering, and say \"at-least-once plus idempotent consumers\"\nbefore anyone asks about exactly-once.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs event envelopes, chooses partition keys, writes idempotent consumers with an inbox table, and wires dead-letter queues and lag alerts for every consumer group.","architect":"Decides which facts become published contracts, who owns each event's schema, where choreography ends and orchestration begins, and which flows must remain synchronous.","fde":"Integrates with a customer's existing bus (often Kafka or SQS), maps their events onto the product's ingestion, and explains why the index trails the source system by seconds."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants uploaded contracts to be parsed, chunked, embedded and indexed, with an audit\nrecord per stage and a Slack notification when indexing completes. The current implementation does all\nof this inside the upload request and times out on large files. Redesign it with events and explain\nwhat guarantees each stage has.","a":"The upload handler stores the file, writes the document row and, in the same transaction, an\noutbox row for `DocumentUploaded` keyed by document id; a relay publishes it, so the user gets a fast\n\"received\" response and the event cannot be lost. Parser, chunker, embedder and indexer are consumer\ngroups on the same topic chain, each emitting its own completion event; keying every event by document\nid keeps per-document order. Audit and Slack are additional consumers of those events, so they add no\nlatency and no failure modes to the pipeline. Every consumer is idempotent because the broker delivers\nat least once: stages whose effect is a database write record the event id in an inbox table in the same\ntransaction, and the indexer, whose effect lands in a vector store outside that transaction, upserts\nchunks under deterministic ids so a redelivery overwrites rather than duplicates. The\nnotification consumer in particular must be idempotent, or every restart re-pings Slack. Failures go to\na dead-letter topic with alerts; lag per consumer group is the health metric. If the customer also needs\n\"ask questions immediately after upload\", the UI waits for `DocumentIndexed` via a push channel rather\nthan making indexing synchronous."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most clearly distinguishes an event from a command in a message-driven system?","options":["Events are JSON while commands use a binary encoding","An event states an immutable fact with no addressee; a command instructs one handler and implies an expected outcome","Events are always delivered exactly once and commands at least once","Commands can be replayed from the log but events cannot"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The difference is semantic, not technical. A fact (\"OrderPlaced\") lets the producer ignore consumers; an instruction (\"ChargeCard\") binds sender and handler and reintroduces coupling. Encoding and delivery guarantees are independent of which one you publish."},{"q":"You need every event about the same document processed in order. What is the correct mechanism on a partitioned log?","options":["Enable \"global ordering\" on the topic","Use a single consumer for the whole topic","Partition by document id so all events for one document share a partition","Add a sequence number and have consumers sort incoming events by it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Partitioned logs guarantee order only within a partition, so the partition key must be the entity whose order matters. A single consumer sacrifices throughput and still breaks if the producer spread the events across partitions; client-side sorting cannot know when the sequence is complete."},{"q":"After a deploy, some rows exist in the orders table with no corresponding event on the bus. What is the likely cause?","options":["The broker's retention period expired","Consumers were not idempotent and dropped duplicates","The service wrote the row and published the event as two separate operations and crashed in between","The partition key was wrong, so events landed on an unread partition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This is the dual-write problem: a database commit and a broker publish cannot be made atomic. A crash between them loses the event. The transactional outbox writes the event in the same database transaction and relays it afterwards, so an event exists if and only if the row does."}]},{"key":"system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)","Official docs: FastAPI (background tasks)"],"why":"A classic web request completes in tens of milliseconds, so holding the connection is free. An LLM\ncompletion takes seconds; a tool-using agent run takes minutes; a batch of a thousand document\nsummaries takes an hour. Holding a connection for that long collides with limits you do not control.\nEvery hop in the path has an idle timeout: load balancers and reverse proxies commonly default to around\na minute (the exact value varies by product), and mobile networks drop connections when the device\nchanges cells. When a hop times out, the client sees an error and retries, but the server is still\nworking on the original request. Now the same expensive run executes twice, you pay twice for tokens,\nand if the run has side effects (it posted a comment, it charged a card) they happen twice.\n\nCapacity is the second problem. By Little's law, concurrent in-flight requests ≈ arrival rate × time in\nsystem. At 10 requests per second averaging 30 seconds each, the fleet holds about 300 open connections\nat any moment, each with a goroutine, thread or coroutine, its buffers and its partial state; double the\nrun time and the count doubles even though the useful work per run is unchanged. Scale-out is driven by\nconnection count rather than by useful work, and a deploy that restarts an instance drops every run it\nwas holding.\n\nThe third problem is loss. If the server process restarts mid-request, a synchronous run vanishes; the\nclient learns only that the socket closed. There is no record that the work existed, so there is\nnothing to resume.\n\nThe naive fix, \"raise every timeout to ten minutes\", only hides the issue: long-lived connections pile\nup, one deploy drops them all, and the client still cannot distinguish \"slow\" from \"dead\". The real fix\nis to separate *accepting* the work from *doing* it, which is what asynchronous execution means.","how":"### Two meanings of \"asynchronous\"\nInside one process, *async I/O* (`asyncio`, Node's event loop) lets a thread wait on many sockets at once\ninstead of blocking per request. That improves how many connections a server can hold; it does not\nchange the interaction model, and it is covered in the language modules. This lesson is about the\n*interaction* model between a caller and a service: does the caller wait for the result or not?\n\n### The synchronous shape\nClient sends a request, the server does the work, the response carries the result. The caller's state is\nthe connection itself. This is the right shape when work completes well inside every timeout in the path\nand when the caller needs the answer to continue. Its failure semantics are simple: an error means the\nresult was not delivered, but it does *not* mean the work did not happen, which is why even synchronous\ncalls that have side effects need an idempotency key.\n\n### Streaming: synchronous with incremental delivery\nServer-sent events or chunked responses keep the connection synchronous but push partial results as they\nare produced (tokens, tool-call progress). Time-to-first-byte drops from the full generation time to the\nprefill time, which is what makes a 40-second answer tolerable to a person. The connection is still held,\nso timeouts and retries still apply; a reconnecting client needs a way to resume (\"give me events after\nsequence 812\") or it re-runs the generation.\n\n### The asynchronous shape\n1. **Submit.** The client sends the request with a client-generated idempotency key. The server validates\n   it, persists a job record (`status=queued`, inputs, key, tenant, created_at) and returns `202 Accepted`\n   with a job id and a status URL. The write to the job store is the only thing that must succeed inside\n   the request, so the request is fast and the work survives a process restart *because* it exists in\n   durable storage before anyone starts on it. A repeated key returns the existing job; a repeated key\n   with a *different* body is rejected (`409` or `422`), because silently returning the old job would\n   hand the client a result for a request it did not make.\n2. **Execute.** A worker claims the job from a queue (Redis, SQS, Postgres with `SKIP LOCKED`), marks it\n   `running` with a lease or heartbeat, performs the work, and writes the result and `succeeded` or\n   `failed` status. A lease matters because a worker that dies mid-job must have its job reclaimed by\n   another worker after the lease expires; without it the job stays `running` forever.\n3. **Deliver.** The client learns the outcome by polling `GET /jobs/{id}` (simple, works through any\n   proxy, costs requests), by a webhook the server calls (efficient, but the server must retry and the\n   receiver must verify and deduplicate), or by a push channel such as SSE or WebSocket subscribed to the\n   job (best UX for interactive clients).\n\nThe status model is a small state machine: `queued → running → succeeded | failed | cancelled`, often\nwith a `progress` field and an `attempt` counter. Results need a retention policy; a job store that keeps\nevery result forever becomes the biggest table in the system.\n\n### Cancellation is a request, not a guarantee\nOnce work is detached from the connection, closing the browser tab no longer stops it, so the job API\nneeds an explicit `POST /jobs/{id}/cancel`. The server can only set `cancel_requested`; the worker checks\nthe flag between steps (between tool calls, between batches of documents) and stops at the next safe\npoint, *because* interrupting mid-step can leave a half-applied side effect. Side effects already\nperformed stay performed, so a cancelled agent run may still have created a ticket; cancellation of\nmulti-step work with effects is really a saga question (compensate or leave), and the final status\nshould say which steps completed.\n\n### The hybrid: respond if fast, otherwise hand back a job\nMany APIs accept a `wait` budget: the server runs the job and, if it finishes within, say, 20 seconds,\nreturns the result inline; otherwise it returns the job id. The client code handles both outcomes. This\nkeeps short requests simple without making long ones fragile.\n\n### Backpressure\nAn asynchronous front door accepts work faster than workers drain it, so the queue is where overload\nbecomes visible. Bound it: reject submissions with `429` or `503` when queue depth or estimated wait\nexceeds a threshold, and expose that wait estimate to clients. An unbounded queue turns an outage into a\nmulti-hour backlog that keeps failing after the cause is fixed.","example":"A minimal FastAPI service with the asynchronous shape: submit returns `202` and a job id, a worker drains\na queue, and a status endpoint reports progress. The in-memory queue and dict are for illustration; in\nproduction they are a durable queue and a database table so jobs survive restarts.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, uuid\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Response\n\njobs: dict[str, dict] = {}              # production: a table with status, result, attempt, lease\nqueue: asyncio.Queue[str] = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=1000)   # bounded => backpressure\n\nasync def run_analysis(text: str) -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(5)              # stands in for a multi-second LLM/agent run\n    return f\"{len(text.split())} words\"\n\nasync def worker():\n    while True:\n        job_id = await queue.get()\n        jobs[job_id][\"status\"] = \"running\"\n        try:\n            jobs[job_id][\"result\"] = await run_analysis(jobs[job_id][\"input\"])\n            jobs[job_id][\"status\"] = \"succeeded\"\n        except Exception as e:          # production: retry with backoff, then dead-letter\n            jobs[job_id].update(status=\"failed\", error=str(e))\n        queue.task_done()\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app):\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker()); yield; task.cancel()\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\n@app.post(\"/analyses\", status_code=202)\nasync def submit(body: dict, response: Response):\n    job_id = body.get(\"idempotency_key\") or str(uuid.uuid4())\n    if job_id in jobs and jobs[job_id][\"input\"] != body[\"text\"]:\n        raise HTTPException(409, \"idempotency key reused with a different body\")\n    if job_id not in jobs:              # a retried submit returns the same job\n        jobs[job_id] = {\"status\": \"queued\", \"input\": body[\"text\"]}\n        try:\n            queue.put_nowait(job_id)\n        except asyncio.QueueFull:\n            del jobs[job_id]; raise HTTPException(503, \"busy, retry later\")\n    response.headers[\"Location\"] = f\"/analyses/{job_id}\"\n    return {\"job_id\": job_id, \"status\": jobs[job_id][\"status\"]}\n\n@app.get(\"/analyses/{job_id}\")\nasync def status(job_id: str):\n    if job_id not in jobs:\n        raise HTTPException(404)\n    return jobs[job_id]\n```\n\nA client retrying the `POST` with the same `idempotency_key` gets the same job back instead of a second\nfive-second run; a full queue is refused immediately rather than silently growing. Trace one request:\n`POST {\"text\": \"...\", \"idempotency_key\": \"k1\"}` returns `202` with `Location: /analyses/k1` in a few\nmilliseconds; a `GET` one second later shows `running`; the client's network drops and it re-sends the\nsame `POST`, which returns the same job, still `running`, rather than enqueuing a second; a `GET` after\nfive seconds returns `succeeded` with the result. What the sketch omits is exactly what production\nneeds: a restart loses `jobs` and the queue (so both must be durable), a crashed worker leaves the job\n`running` forever (so claims need a lease), and a key held only in memory cannot deduplicate across\ninstances (so the key needs a unique constraint in the job table).","tradeoffs":["Synchronous calls are simple to build, test and reason about, but their reliability is bounded by the shortest timeout in the path; once p99 work time approaches that timeout, the shape must change.","Asynchronous jobs survive restarts and absorb bursts, but they add a job store, a worker fleet, a status API and client-side polling or callback logic; for sub-second work that machinery is pure overhead.","Streaming gives the best perceived latency for a human reader, yet it holds connections, complicates retries and resumption, and offers nothing to a machine caller that only wants the final answer.","Polling is proxy-friendly and stateless but wastes requests and adds up to one interval of latency; webhooks are efficient but make you responsible for retries, signatures and the receiver's uptime.","The hybrid \"wait up to N seconds\" keeps simple cases simple, but clients must handle both response shapes, and a poorly chosen N recreates the timeout problem it was meant to avoid."],"pitfalls":["Raising timeouts layer by layer instead of changing the shape; the symptom is duplicate runs and double token spend after clients retry requests the server was still processing.","Accepting jobs without an idempotency key; every client retry of the submit creates a new job, visible as bursts of identical jobs seconds apart.","Workers with no lease or heartbeat; a worker crash leaves jobs in `running` indefinitely and users see spinners that never resolve.","Unbounded queues; an upstream outage builds a backlog that takes hours to drain, during which fresh requests wait behind stale ones nobody still wants.","Polling with a fixed short interval from thousands of clients; status endpoints become the top traffic source and the database behind them the bottleneck, which is the cue to add push delivery or backoff.","Results kept forever with no TTL and no size limit; the jobs table becomes the largest and slowest table and its backups the longest.","Treating a disconnect or a cancel as \"the work stopped\"; the symptom is tickets or emails produced by runs the UI reports as cancelled, because the worker never checked the cancel flag or the effect had already happened."],"when":"Decide from the p99 of the work, not the median, against the smallest timeout in the path and against\nhow long a human will wait. Work that finishes within a few seconds at p99 stays synchronous. Work that\ntakes several seconds to roughly a minute with a person watching should stream. Anything longer, anything\nthat must survive a restart, anything with retries and compensation, and anything with no human waiting\n(batch summarisation, re-indexing, nightly evaluations) is an asynchronous job. Measure time-to-first-byte,\nend-to-end completion time, queue depth and the ratio of retried submits to unique jobs. In day-to-day\nwork this shows up as the decision of whether an endpoint returns `200` with a body or `202` with a\n`Location` header, and whether your agent framework exposes a `run` call or a `submit` plus `events`\npair. In interviews, name the three shapes, justify each with a timeout or a human-attention argument,\nand mention idempotency keys and leases without being prompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the `202` + status endpoint pair, adds idempotency keys to submits, implements worker leases and retries, and streams tokens over SSE for interactive paths.","architect":"Sets the latency budget per interaction, chooses queue technology and delivery channel, and defines the job lifecycle, retention and backpressure policy as platform standards.","fde":"Fits the product's job model to the customer's integration style, often replacing a synchronous call that times out through their gateway with polling or a signed webhook."},"check":{"q":"A customer's workflow calls your agent synchronously through their API gateway, which times out at\n30 seconds. Agent runs take 5 to 120 seconds. They report intermittent \"failures\" and duplicate tickets\nbeing created in their system. Diagnose the problem and propose a design.","a":"The gateway closes the connection at 30 seconds, so every run longer than that looks like a failure\neven though the agent keeps working and eventually creates the ticket; the customer's retry then starts a\nsecond run that creates a second ticket. Two changes are needed. First, change the shape: `POST /runs`\npersists the run and returns `202` with a run id inside the gateway's timeout, a worker executes it, and\nthe customer polls `GET /runs/{id}` or receives a signed webhook on completion. Second, make the ticket\ncreation idempotent by deriving its key from the run id and the step, so even a genuine retry cannot\ncreate two tickets. Workers need leases so a crash reclaims the run, the queue should be bounded with a\nclear `503` on overload, and run results need a retention window. If the customer wants progress, offer\nan SSE stream keyed by run id rather than lengthening the gateway timeout."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does raising the load balancer timeout from 60 s to 600 s not fix an endpoint whose agent runs take up to five minutes?","options":["Load balancers cannot be configured above 60 seconds","The work still vanishes on a restart, retries still duplicate it, and connection count grows with run time","Longer timeouts increase token cost per request","HTTP/1.1 does not permit responses slower than 60 seconds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A longer timeout hides the symptom but keeps the synchronous shape: in-flight runs have no durable record, a client or proxy retry re-executes them, and each instance holds connections proportional to run time. Separating submission from execution addresses all three."},{"q":"A client retries a job submission after a network error and the server creates a second identical job. What was missing?","options":["A longer polling interval","A streaming response instead of a job id","A client-supplied idempotency key that the server uses to return the existing job","A webhook so the client would not have retried"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The submit request itself can be duplicated by the network, so the server must recognise a repeat. An idempotency key stored with the job lets the retry return the original job id instead of enqueuing new work. Delivery channel and polling cadence do not affect submission duplicates."},{"q":"When is streaming the right choice over a background job?","options":["A person is watching and the work finishes within the connection's timeout budget","The work must survive a server restart","The caller is a batch process that only needs the final answer","The result must be delivered to several consumers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Streaming improves perceived latency for a human reader while keeping a synchronous connection, so it fits interactive work that still completes within timeouts. Durability, machine callers and fan-out all point to a persisted job with polling, push or events."}]},{"key":"system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","sources":["Garcia-Molina & Salem, 1987 — Sagas","Gray & Reuter, 1992 — Transaction Processing: Concepts and Techniques","Richardson, 2018 — Microservices Patterns","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"Consider \"place order\": reserve stock in the inventory service, charge the card through a payment\nprovider, create a shipment in logistics, and record the order. Each system has its own database and its\nown transaction. If the card charge succeeds and shipment creation fails, the customer has paid for\nnothing. Inside one database you would wrap all four in a transaction and roll back; across four systems\nthere is no shared transaction to roll back.\n\nThe textbook answer is **two-phase commit (2PC)**. A coordinator asks every participant to *prepare*\n(write the change durably and hold locks, promising it can commit), and only when all vote yes does it\ntell them to *commit*. 2PC does give atomicity, and it still lives inside many database clusters. It fails\nas a general tool for two reasons. First, it is **blocking**: if the coordinator crashes after participants\nhave prepared, they must hold their locks until it returns, because committing or aborting unilaterally\ncould disagree with the coordinator's decision. Rows stay locked for as long as the outage lasts.\nSecond, it requires every participant to implement the prepare protocol; Stripe, Slack, Jira, a vector\ndatabase and an LLM provider do not expose \"prepare\", so the step with the most consequential side\neffect is exactly the one that cannot participate. Availability also compounds: a 2PC commit needs every\nparticipant up at the same moment, which is the temporal coupling that event-driven designs try to\nremove.\n\n2PC is not dead, though; it has moved inside systems that can make its weak point safe. Spanner-style\ndatabases run 2PC across shards, but each participant and the coordinator is itself a replicated\nconsensus group, so \"the coordinator crashed\" means a new leader of that group takes over with the\ndecision already in its log and nobody blocks for long. The XA standard exposes 2PC across a database\nand a broker that both implement it, which works inside one organisation's infrastructure and still\nexcludes every SaaS API. Three-phase commit adds a pre-commit round to avoid blocking under crash\nfailures, but it can decide inconsistently under network partitions, so it is rarely deployed. The\nlesson: atomic commit across participants is available when you own all of them and replicate the\ncoordinator; a business process across vendors is not that case.\n\nThe saga, proposed in 1987 for long-lived database transactions, accepts that there is no global\nrollback and designs the undo explicitly instead. That design work, deciding what \"undo a charge\" means\nand when it is no longer possible, is the real content of the pattern.","how":"### Structure\nA saga is a sequence of local transactions T₁ … Tₙ. Each Tᵢ commits on its own, in its own service.\nFor every Tᵢ that may need undoing there is a compensating transaction Cᵢ. If Tₖ fails, the saga runs\nCₖ₋₁, Cₖ₋₂, … C₁ in reverse order. The end state is either \"all Tᵢ committed\" or \"every committed Tᵢ\ncompensated\", which is the saga's version of atomicity. Durability comes from each local commit plus a\npersisted saga log that records which step the saga has reached, *because* the coordinator process may\ncrash between steps and must resume rather than restart. The log records intent before each step\n(`started charge_card`) and the outcome after (`done charge_card`): on recovery, a step with `started`\nbut no `done` is exactly the unknown-outcome case below, and it is resolved by querying the participant\nwith the step's idempotency key, never by assuming either answer.\n\n### Compensation is semantic, not physical\nA rollback restores bytes; a compensation is a new business action that cancels the effect. \"Charge\ncard\" is compensated by \"refund\", not by deleting the charge, and the refund is visible on the\nstatement. \"Reserve stock\" is compensated by \"release reservation\". \"Send email\" has no compensation; at\nbest you send a correction. This is why step ordering matters more in sagas than in transactions.\n\nA step can be made cheaper to undo by splitting it into a reservation and a confirmation, the\n**Try-Confirm-Cancel (TCC)** shape: *try* places a hold that expires on its own (a seat hold, a card\nauthorisation), *confirm* turns it into the real effect at the end of the saga, and *cancel* releases it.\nHolding instead of acting means most compensations are \"let the hold lapse or release it\", which is\ninvisible to the customer, *because* nothing irreversible happened until confirm. The cost is that the\nparticipant must support holds, and a hold's expiry must outlast the slowest path to confirm.\n\n### Compensatable, pivot and retriable steps\nClassify each step. **Compensatable** steps can be undone. The **pivot** is the step after which the saga\ncan no longer be abandoned: the first action with no undo, such as a non-refundable booking or a message\nalready delivered to a customer. Many payment flows treat the successful charge as the pivot even though\nrefunds exist, because a refund is visible on the statement and costs fees. **Retriable** steps come after\nthe pivot and are guaranteed to eventually succeed if retried (record the order, send the confirmation). The rule that falls out: put every step that might fail for\nbusiness reasons (validation, stock check, fraud score) *before* the pivot, so compensation is rarely\nneeded, and make everything after the pivot idempotent and retried forever, because you have promised\nthe customer the outcome.\n\n### No isolation\nLocal transactions commit immediately, so other sagas and readers observe intermediate state. Garcia-Molina\nand Salem's original paper already states that other transactions can see a saga's partial results;\nRichardson summarises this as sagas being \"ACD\": atomicity, consistency and durability without\nisolation. Concrete anomalies: a **lost update** when\nanother saga modifies the same order between a step and its compensation; a **dirty read** when a\ndashboard shows an order as paid that is about to be refunded. Countermeasures named in Richardson's\ntreatment: a **semantic lock** (write `status = PENDING` so other logic treats the record as in flight),\n**commutative updates** (debit/credit rather than set-balance), the **pessimistic view** (reorder steps to\nshrink the window), **re-reading values** before acting, and a **version** check that rejects stale writes.\n\n### Coordination styles\nIn **choreography**, each service listens for the previous step's event and emits its own; compensation\nis triggered by failure events. It needs no central component but scatters the saga's logic across\nservices. In **orchestration**, a saga orchestrator holds the state machine, sends commands, waits for\nreplies and runs compensations. Beyond a handful of steps, or whenever someone must answer \"which step is\norder 42 stuck on\", orchestration wins. Durable-execution engines (Temporal and similar) are orchestrators\nwith the saga log built in: your code is replayed from the log after a crash and the compensation list is\nordinary code.\n\n### Unknown outcomes\nA step that times out has an unknown result: the charge may or may not have happened. Compensating\nblindly can refund a charge that never occurred. The correct move is to query the participant with the\nstep's idempotency key before deciding, which means every step must be issued with such a key. Timeouts\nturn into a reconciliation step, not an error branch.\n\n### Agents are sagas\nAn agent that creates a Jira ticket, posts to Slack and emails a customer is executing T₁, T₂, T₃ with\nno transaction. Tool adapters should expose compensations where they exist (close ticket, delete\nmessage), the orchestrator should place irreversible tools after a pivot, and approval gates belong\nexactly at the pivot.","example":"A small orchestrator: steps run forward, each with an idempotency key derived from the saga id and step\nname, and committed steps are compensated in reverse on failure. The persisted log is a list here; in\nproduction it is a table or a durable-execution engine's history.\n\n```python\nimport logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"saga\")\nNOOP = lambda ctx, idempotency_key: None      # step had no side effect worth undoing\nIRREVERSIBLE = None                            # step cannot be undone: this is the pivot\n\nclass Step:\n    def __init__(self, name, action, compensate):\n        self.name, self.action, self.compensate = name, action, compensate\n\ndef run_saga(saga_id, steps, ctx, saga_log):\n    done = []                                        # steps whose action committed\n    for step in steps:\n        key = f\"{saga_id}:{step.name}\"               # same key on every retry of this step\n        saga_log.append((\"started\", step.name))      # intent first: recovery sees in-flight steps\n        try:\n            ctx[step.name] = step.action(ctx, idempotency_key=key)\n            saga_log.append((\"done\", step.name)); done.append(step)\n        except Exception as e:   # production: on a timeout, query the participant by key first\n            if any(s.compensate is IRREVERSIBLE for s in done):   # pivot passed\n                raise RuntimeError(f\"cannot abandon saga; retry {step.name} forward\") from e\n            log.warning(\"step %s failed: %s; compensating\", step.name, e)\n            for prev in reversed(done):\n                prev.compensate(ctx, idempotency_key=f\"{saga_id}:{prev.name}:undo\")\n                saga_log.append((\"undone\", prev.name))\n            return \"compensated\"\n    return \"completed\"\n\ndef fraud_check(ctx, idempotency_key):\n    if not fraud.passes(ctx[\"customer\"]):            # flights, fraud, payments, hotel, mail: service clients\n        raise ValueError(\"declined\")\n\nsteps = [   # business failures first, the irreversible step as late as possible\n    Step(\"hold_seat\",   lambda c, idempotency_key: flights.hold(c[\"flight\"], key=idempotency_key),\n                        lambda c, idempotency_key: flights.release(c[\"hold_seat\"], key=idempotency_key)),\n    Step(\"fraud_check\", fraud_check, NOOP),\n    Step(\"charge_card\", lambda c, idempotency_key: payments.charge(c[\"amount\"], key=idempotency_key),\n                        lambda c, idempotency_key: payments.refund(c[\"charge_card\"], key=idempotency_key)),\n    Step(\"book_hotel\",  lambda c, idempotency_key: hotel.book(c[\"stay\"], key=idempotency_key), IRREVERSIBLE),\n    Step(\"send_itinerary\", lambda c, idempotency_key: mail.send(c[\"employee\"], key=idempotency_key), NOOP),\n]\n```\n\nIf `charge_card` is declined, the seat hold is released and the saga ends as `compensated`. If\n`send_itinerary` fails after the non-refundable hotel is booked, the orchestrator refuses to compensate\nand the caller re-runs the saga from the log; the idempotency keys make the hold, charge and booking\nno-ops on the second pass, so only the email is actually retried.","tradeoffs":["Sagas keep every service available independently and need no shared lock manager, but they expose intermediate state to other readers; when an invariant must never be observably violated, keep those writes inside one database and one aggregate instead.","2PC gives true atomicity with no application-level compensation code, yet it blocks on coordinator failure and needs every participant to speak the protocol, which excludes external APIs and most SaaS systems.","Orchestration centralises visibility and compensation logic at the cost of a component that every service depends on; choreography removes that dependency but spreads the state machine across teams, which flips in favour of orchestration as step count or compensation complexity grows.","Designing explicit compensations forces the business to decide what \"undo\" means, which is valuable clarity, but some actions have no undo, and for those the only lever is ordering and approval gates.","Forward-only retry after the pivot guarantees completion without refunds, provided post-pivot steps are idempotent and the retry budget is effectively unbounded, which requires a dead-letter path to a human when a participant stays down."],"pitfalls":["Compensations that are not idempotent; a retried refund refunds twice, discovered when finance reconciles payouts.","Compensating on timeout without querying the participant; refunds issued for charges that never happened, or shipments created twice, visible as unexplained negative balances.","An irreversible step placed early; when a later validation fails there is nothing to compensate it with, and the customer has an email or a ticket for an order that does not exist.","Ignoring isolation; a second saga (or a human in the admin panel) edits the record while the first is mid-flight, and the compensation overwrites their change, surfacing as \"the system reverted my edit\".","No saga log or heartbeat; the orchestrator crashes between steps, nobody resumes, and orders sit in `PENDING` until a customer complains.","Holds that expire before the saga confirms them; sagas that wait on a human approval or a slow partner fail at confirm with \"hold not found\", and the seat or authorisation is gone.","Treating the LLM as a step that can be redone freely; re-running a non-deterministic step on retry produces different tool arguments, so the compensation keyed to the first attempt no longer matches."],"when":"Use a saga whenever one business operation must update more than one service, database or external\nsystem and the operation can legitimately fail part-way. Do not use one when all the data can live in a\nsingle database and a local transaction suffices, which is the better design more often than microservice\nenthusiasm suggests. Before choosing, write down each step's compensation and its idempotency key; if a\nstep has neither, it is your pivot and belongs as late as possible, possibly behind a human approval. In\npractice the pattern appears as a workflow definition in an orchestrator, as a `compensate` method on a\ntool adapter, or as a `status` column with `PENDING` values that other code must respect. Measure saga\ncompletion rate, compensation rate, time stuck per step and the count of sagas needing manual repair.\nIn interviews, state why 2PC is unsuitable across heterogeneous services, define compensation versus\nrollback, and bring up the lack of isolation and the pivot step without prompting.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements steps and compensations with idempotency keys, persists saga state, and writes reconciliation queries for steps whose outcome is unknown after a timeout.","architect":"Decides aggregate boundaries so that most invariants stay inside one transaction, classifies steps as compensatable, pivot or retriable, and picks orchestration versus choreography.","fde":"Maps a customer's cross-system process (CRM, billing, ticketing) onto a saga, identifies which of their systems cannot be undone, and places approval gates before those actions."},"check":{"q":"An agent books travel for an employee: it holds a flight seat, charges the corporate card, books a\nnon-refundable hotel through a partner API, and files an expense record. Order the steps, identify the\npivot, and describe what happens if the hotel API times out.","a":"Everything that can fail for business reasons goes first: validate policy, hold the seat\n(compensation: release the hold). The hotel booking is non-refundable, so it is the pivot and must come as\nlate as possible; the card charge is refundable, so it can precede the hotel. Order: policy check, seat\nhold, card charge, hotel booking, then retriable steps: confirm the seat and file the expense. If the\nhotel call times out, the outcome is unknown; the orchestrator must query the partner with the booking's\nidempotency key before deciding. If the booking exists, the saga continues forward and retries the\nremaining steps until they succeed. If it does not, the agent may retry the booking with the same key,\nor compensate by refunding the charge and releasing the hold. Because the hotel is irreversible and\ncostly, an approval gate belongs immediately before it, and the saga log must persist each step so a\ncrashed worker resumes rather than rebooking."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is two-phase commit rarely used to coordinate a payment provider, a ticketing SaaS and an internal database?","options":["2PC cannot guarantee atomicity across more than two participants","External services do not expose a prepare phase, and 2PC blocks participants if the coordinator fails after prepare","2PC requires all participants to use the same database engine","2PC is only defined for read-only transactions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"2PC needs every participant to vote in a prepare phase and hold locks until the decision arrives; SaaS APIs offer no such phase, and a coordinator crash leaves prepared participants blocked. Atomicity across many participants is exactly what 2PC does provide where it applies."},{"q":"In a saga, what does \"no isolation\" mean in practice?","options":["Steps cannot run on different machines","Compensations must run in the same order as the original steps","Other transactions can observe and act on the intermediate state between a step and its compensation","Each local transaction must use READ UNCOMMITTED"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each local transaction commits immediately, so a saga's partial results are visible system-wide until it completes or compensates. Countermeasures such as semantic locks and version checks exist precisely because of this; compensations run in reverse order, not the same order."},{"q":"A step after the pivot fails repeatedly. What should the orchestrator do?","options":["Run compensations for all earlier steps, including the pivot","Abort the saga and leave the current state as is","Keep retrying the step forward, because post-pivot steps must be idempotent and eventually succeed","Restart the saga from the first step with a new saga id"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once the pivot has committed the saga has promised its outcome; earlier steps may be uncompensatable. Post-pivot steps are designed to be retriable and idempotent so the saga completes forward, with a dead-letter path to a human only when a participant stays unavailable."}]},{"key":"system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","sources":["Richardson, 2018 — Microservices Patterns","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Debezium (outbox event router)"],"why":"The producer side of every event-driven system has to do two things when an order is placed: commit the\norder row and tell the rest of the world. There are three naive orderings and each is wrong.\n\n**Commit, then publish.** The process crashes, is OOM-killed or loses its broker connection after the\ncommit and before the publish. The row exists; the event never happens. Downstream, the search index,\nthe billing meter and the audit log never hear about the order. This is a silent failure: nothing errors,\ndata is simply missing.\n\n**Publish, then commit.** The event is out; the commit fails on a constraint violation or a deadlock and\nrolls back. Consumers now act on an order that does not exist.\n\n**Publish inside the transaction, before commit.** The consumer receives the event, queries the producer\nfor details, and finds nothing, because the transaction has not committed yet. Or the transaction later\nrolls back and the event is again a phantom.\n\nThe frequency is not academic. Suppose the crash window between the two operations is a millisecond and\nthe service processes 1,000 transactions per second; at an illustrative one restart per day you lose on\naverage about one event per day, and at scale that becomes \"the index is missing a few hundred documents\nand nobody knows which\". Retrying the publish in application code does not help when the process itself\nis gone. Wrapping both in a distributed transaction would require the broker to participate in two-phase\ncommit, which most brokers do not support and which blocks under coordinator failure.\n\nThe way out is to use the one atomic primitive you already have, the local database transaction, and to\nmake publishing a separate, retryable, idempotent step that reads from committed state.","how":"### The outbox table\nAlongside the business tables lives an `outbox` table: `id` (monotonic), `aggregate_type`,\n`aggregate_id`, `event_type`, `payload`, `created_at`, `published_at`. When the application commits a\nbusiness change it inserts the corresponding event row *in the same transaction*. Atomicity is now\nguaranteed by the database: either both the order and its event row are durable or neither is. The event\nis modelled as data, not as a side effect, *because* data is the only thing the transaction can protect.\n\n### The relay\nA separate process moves rows from the outbox to the broker. Two implementations exist.\n\n**Polling publisher.** A loop runs `SELECT ... FROM outbox WHERE published_at IS NULL ORDER BY id LIMIT n\nFOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, publishes each row, then marks it published (or deletes it). `SKIP LOCKED` lets\nseveral relay instances run without double-claiming rows. It is simple and database-agnostic; its cost is\npolling latency and load on the table. Selecting by `published_at IS NULL` rather than \"rows with `id` above\nthe last one I published\" is deliberate: ids come from a sequence assigned at insert time, but\ntransactions commit in a different order, so row 101 can be visible while row 100's transaction is still\nopen. A relay that advances a high-water mark past 101 never returns for 100, and that event is lost\nwithout any error.\n\n**Log tailing (CDC).** A connector such as Debezium reads the database's write-ahead log, sees committed\ninserts into the outbox table in commit order, and forwards them to the broker. There is no polling, the\nlatency is milliseconds, and ordering follows commit order. The cost is operating a CDC pipeline and\ngranting it replication access.\n\nEither way the relay can crash after publishing and before marking the row, so it will publish that row\nagain on restart. The outbox therefore gives **at-least-once** publication, never exactly-once. That is\nacceptable precisely because the inbox exists on the other side.\n\n### Ordering\nConsumers typically need per-aggregate order. Publish with `aggregate_id` as the partition key, and have\nthe relay process rows in `id` order. With several polling relays, order across relays is not guaranteed\nunless rows are partitioned among relays by aggregate as well; CDC avoids the problem by reading a single\ncommit-ordered log.\n\n### Housekeeping\nThe outbox grows by one row per event forever unless pruned. Delete or archive rows after publication\nwith a lag that covers your debugging needs; a table with hundreds of millions of published rows makes the\n`WHERE published_at IS NULL` scan slow unless a partial index exists.\n\n### The inbox table\nOn the consumer, every message handler runs inside a database transaction that does two things: performs\nthe business effect and inserts the message's id into an `inbox` table with a unique constraint\n(`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`). If the insert affects zero rows the message was already handled,\nand the handler commits nothing. Doing the inbox insert *first*, before the effect, also handles two\ncopies arriving concurrently on different consumer instances: in Postgres the second insert waits on the\nunique index until the first transaction commits, then finds the conflict and does nothing, so the\nunique constraint, not application logic, serialises the duplicates. Because the dedupe record and the effect commit together, a crash\nbetween them cannot leave the effect applied but unrecorded, or recorded but unapplied. The dedupe key\nmust be the producer's `event_id`, not the broker's delivery id, *because* a redelivery carries the same\nevent with a new delivery id.\n\nA variant stores the full message in the inbox first, acknowledges to the broker, and processes from the\ninbox asynchronously. This decouples receipt from processing and gives the consumer its own durable\nqueue, at the cost of another relay-like loop.\n\n### What the pair does and does not guarantee\nOutbox plus inbox plus an at-least-once broker gives effectively-once effects from the producer's\ndatabase to the consumer's database. It does not make effects outside the consumer's database idempotent:\nif the handler calls an external API, sends an email or invokes a model, a duplicate that slips in\nbetween the effect and the commit still repeats that call. Those effects need their own idempotency key at\nthe external boundary.","example":"Tables and the three code paths: the producer writes order and outbox row in one transaction, a polling\nrelay publishes with `SKIP LOCKED`, and a consumer deduplicates through the inbox. Uses psycopg 3; the\n`broker.publish` call stands in for a Kafka or SQS client.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE outbox (\n  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, aggregate_id TEXT NOT NULL, event_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n  payload JSONB NOT NULL, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(), published_at TIMESTAMPTZ);\nCREATE INDEX outbox_unpublished ON outbox (id) WHERE published_at IS NULL;\nCREATE TABLE inbox (event_id UUID PRIMARY KEY, handled_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now());\n```\n\n```python\nimport json, uuid, psycopg\n\ndef place_order(conn, order):                       # producer: one transaction, two inserts\n    with conn.transaction():\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO orders (id, total) VALUES (%s, %s)\", (order[\"id\"], order[\"total\"]))\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_id, event_type, payload) VALUES (%s, %s, %s)\",\n                     (order[\"id\"], \"OrderPlaced\",\n                      json.dumps({\"event_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), **order})))\n\ndef relay_once(conn, broker, batch=100):            # N copies won't double-claim, but can reorder\n    with conn.transaction():\n        rows = conn.execute(\"\"\"SELECT id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload FROM outbox\n                               WHERE published_at IS NULL ORDER BY id LIMIT %s\n                               FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\"\"\", (batch,)).fetchall()\n        for id_, agg, etype, payload in rows:\n            broker.publish(topic=\"orders\", key=agg, value=json.dumps({\"type\": etype, **payload}))\n            conn.execute(\"UPDATE outbox SET published_at = now() WHERE id = %s\", (id_,))\n    return len(rows)   # crash after publish, before commit => republish: at-least-once\n\ndef handle_order_placed(conn, msg):                 # consumer: effect + inbox in one transaction\n    with conn.transaction():\n        cur = conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO inbox (event_id) VALUES (%s) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING\",\n                           (msg[\"event_id\"],))\n        if cur.rowcount == 0:\n            return \"duplicate\"                      # already handled: commit nothing new\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO revenue_ledger (order_id, amount) VALUES (%s, %s)\",\n                     (msg[\"id\"], msg[\"total\"]))\n    return \"handled\"\n```\n\nKill the relay between `broker.publish` and the `UPDATE` and the row is published twice; the consumer's\nsecond `INSERT INTO inbox` conflicts, `rowcount` is zero, and the ledger is written exactly once. Two\nproduction details the sketch glosses: the relay publishes while holding row locks, so a slow broker\nstretches the transaction, which is why real relays use small batches and a short publish timeout; and\nwith several relays running, two events for the same order can be claimed by different relays and\npublished in the wrong order, so either run one relay per partition of aggregates or use CDC.","tradeoffs":["You gain a hard guarantee that events match committed state, but every event now costs a database write and a relay hop, adding latency of tens of milliseconds with polling (less with CDC) compared with publishing directly.","A polling relay is trivial to run anywhere, whereas CDC gives lower latency and commit ordering but requires replication privileges and a connector to operate; polling flips to CDC once event volume or latency sensitivity makes table scans costly.","The inbox makes any handler idempotent regardless of its logic, yet it adds a write and a growing table per consumer; when the effect is naturally idempotent (an upsert keyed by aggregate id) the inbox is redundant and can be skipped.","Keeping outbox and inbox rows longer eases debugging and widens the dedupe window, but table size and index bloat grow linearly; the retention must at least exceed the broker's maximum redelivery delay.","The pattern couples publishing to the service's database schema and transaction, which is the point, but it means services without a relational store (or using a database per request) need a different mechanism, such as a broker with transactional producers."],"pitfalls":["Publishing from a database trigger or an ORM \"after commit\" hook that calls the broker directly; this is still a dual write and shows up as occasional missing events after deploys and failovers.","Running multiple polling relays without `SKIP LOCKED` or partitioning; the same event is published several times in a burst and per-aggregate order breaks, seen as out-of-order status transitions downstream.","Deduplicating on the broker's message or receipt id instead of the producer's `event_id`; redeliveries carry new ids and sail past the inbox, so duplicates reappear exactly when the broker retries.","A relay that tracks \"last published id\" instead of querying unpublished rows; events from transactions that committed out of sequence order are skipped forever, showing up as rare, permanent gaps that no retry ever fills.","Letting the outbox grow unbounded with no partial index; relay latency creeps from milliseconds to seconds as the unpublished scan degrades.","Treating the inbox as protection for external calls; the handler emails the customer, then crashes before commit, the redelivery emails again, and support hears about duplicate messages.","Writing a huge payload per event; outbox rows bloat the primary database and the WAL, slowing every unrelated transaction, which argues for thin events with an id to fetch."],"when":"Use the outbox whenever a service both persists state in a transactional database and must publish\nmessages about that state, which describes nearly every service in an event-driven system: order\nservices, document-ingestion services recording upload rows, agent runtimes persisting step results\nwhile emitting telemetry. Use the inbox in any consumer whose effect is not naturally idempotent (ledgers,\ncounters, \"send once\" notifications, calls that append rather than upsert). Skip both when there is no\ndatabase in the loop, when the broker itself is the system of record, or when a lost message is\ngenuinely harmless. Measure outbox lag (oldest unpublished row age), relay throughput, duplicate rate at\nthe inbox and table sizes. In interviews, the expected move is to raise the dual-write problem yourself,\nsketch the outbox with `SKIP LOCKED` or CDC, and state plainly that the result is at-least-once plus\nconsumer-side deduplication rather than exactly-once delivery.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds the outbox insert to the same transaction as the business write, runs the relay as a separate process, and guards every handler with an inbox insert keyed by event id.","architect":"Standardises the outbox and inbox schemas, event envelope and relay technology across services, and decides where CDC replaces polling.","fde":"Recognises dual writes in a customer's integration code (webhook handlers that update their CRM then call the product) and introduces an inbox so redelivered webhooks do not double-process."},"check":{"q":"Your ingestion service writes a document row and then publishes `DocumentUploaded` to Kafka. After\na Kubernetes node failure, 37 documents exist in Postgres with no chunks in the vector index. Explain the\ncause and redesign the write path so it cannot recur, including what happens on relay crashes.","a":"The service committed rows and was killed before the publish calls completed, a textbook dual\nwrite; nothing retried because the process was gone. Redesign: insert the `DocumentUploaded` event into\nan outbox table inside the same transaction as the document row, so the pair is atomic. A relay, either a\npolling loop using `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` or Debezium tailing the WAL, publishes unpublished rows to\nKafka keyed by document id and marks them published. If the relay crashes after publishing and before\nmarking, it republishes, so the indexer must be idempotent. If the chunks live in Postgres (pgvector),\nthe indexer inserts the event id into an inbox table in the same transaction as writing the chunks and\nskips the work on conflict. If the vector store is a separate system, no shared transaction exists, so\nidempotence has to come from the effect: deterministic chunk ids such as `doc_id:chunk_no` and upserts,\nso a republished event overwrites instead of duplicating. Backfilling the 37 missing\ndocuments is now a one-off insert of outbox rows for them. Add an alert on the age of the oldest\nunpublished outbox row and on inbox duplicate rate."},"quiz":[{"q":"What exactly does the transactional outbox guarantee?","options":["Each event is delivered to consumers exactly once","An event row is durable if and only if the business transaction that created it committed","Events are delivered to consumers in global order","Consumers never see duplicates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The outbox moves the event into the same local transaction as the state change, which is the only atomicity the database can give. Publication by the relay is still at-least-once, so duplicates and consumer-side deduplication remain part of the design."},{"q":"Why must the inbox deduplicate on the producer's event id rather than the broker's delivery id?","options":["Delivery ids are too long to index efficiently","Producers never generate ids, so the broker id is the only option","A redelivered message carries the same event id but may carry a new delivery id","The broker id changes when the consumer group rebalances, but the event id also changes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deduplication must identify \"the same event\", and only the producer-assigned id is stable across redeliveries, relay republishes and consumer rebalances. Keying on a per-attempt delivery id lets exactly the duplicates you wanted to catch through."},{"q":"The consumer handler sends an email and then commits the inbox row. A redelivery arrives after a crash between the two. What happens?","options":["The inbox prevents the second email because the first send was recorded","The email is sent twice, because the inbox only protects effects inside the consumer's transaction","The broker detects the duplicate and drops it","The first email is automatically recalled"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Outbox and inbox give effectively-once effects between databases; an external side effect that happens before the commit is outside the transaction and repeats on redelivery. External calls need their own idempotency key at the provider or must be moved after the commit with their own at-least-once loop."}]},{"key":"system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Gray, 1978 — Notes on Data Base Operating Systems","Official docs: Apache Kafka (idempotent producer and transactions)","Official docs: Apache Flink (checkpointing and exactly-once sinks)"],"why":"Every retry policy, every queue and every agent framework eventually meets the same question from a\nproduct owner: \"can you guarantee this runs exactly once?\" The honest answer needs the reasoning, because\nthe marketing answer (\"our broker supports exactly-once\") hides where the guarantee ends.\n\nPicture a consumer that reads a message, applies its effect and acknowledges. Three failure points exist:\nbefore the effect (safe to redeliver), after the effect but before the ack (redelivery duplicates the\neffect), and after the ack (fine). The middle case is unavoidable because \"apply effect\" and \"send ack\"\nare two operations on two systems. If the consumer acks *before* applying the effect, a crash in between\nloses the message instead. Those are the only two choices a protocol can make: **at-most-once** (ack\nfirst, may lose) or **at-least-once** (ack last, may duplicate). Jim Gray's framing of the two generals\nproblem makes it exact: two parties exchanging messages over a channel that can drop them can never reach\ncertainty that both know the outcome, no matter how many confirmations they send, because the last\nconfirmation could always be the one that was lost.\n\nThe naive escape is \"make the network reliable\" with TCP, retries and timeouts. TCP gives exactly-once\n*within a connection*; the moment a connection breaks and the client opens a new one and retries, the\nserver sees the request twice. Timeouts make it worse: a request that timed out may have been processed,\nand the client simply does not know. This is the state an LLM-calling service is in several times an\nhour.\n\nSo the problem is reframed. Nobody needs the *message* exactly once; they need the *effect* exactly once.\nThat is achievable, with conditions, and the conditions are the content of this lesson.","how":"### Effectively-once: the recipe\nTake at-least-once delivery and make processing idempotent. Two mechanisms do this:\n\n1. **Deduplication.** The receiver records each processed message id (an inbox table, a Redis set with\n   TTL; on the broker side, Kafka dedupes producer retries by per-partition sequence number) and skips\n   repeats. It works for any effect, but only if the\n   record and the effect commit atomically, which is why the inbox row lives in the same database\n   transaction as the business write.\n2. **Natural idempotency.** The effect is defined so that applying it twice equals applying it once:\n   `SET status='paid'`, an upsert keyed by order id, `PUT` rather than `POST`. No dedupe state is needed,\n   but the effect must be formulated as an absolute state rather than a delta (`balance += 10` is not\n   idempotent; `balance = 110 WHERE version = 7` is).\n3. **Store the input position with the effect.** A consumer that writes to a database can save the\n   source offset (topic, partition, offset) in the same transaction as its output, and on restart seek\n   to the stored offset instead of trusting the broker's committed one. The offset *is* the dedupe\n   record: a message is either reflected in both the output and the stored offset or in neither. This\n   works only when one consumer owns each partition's offset row, and it is the usual way to get\n   exactly-once from a log into a relational sink.\n\n### What Kafka's \"exactly-once semantics\" actually covers\nThe **idempotent producer** attaches a producer id and a per-partition sequence number to every batch;\nthe broker rejects a batch whose sequence it has already appended, so a producer retry after a lost ack\ndoes not create a duplicate record. **Transactions** extend this to read-process-write loops entirely\ninside Kafka: a consumer reads from topic A, produces to topic B, and commits its input offsets as part\nof the same transaction, so either both the output records and the offset advance or neither does.\nConsumers configured with `read_committed` never see records from aborted transactions. This is genuine\nexactly-once for stream processing whose input and output are both Kafka topics. The limitation is the\nboundary: if the processing step calls a payment API or writes to Postgres, those effects are outside the\ntransaction and the standard at-least-once reasoning returns. Flink's checkpoint-based exactly-once has\nthe same shape: operator state is restored consistently from the last checkpoint, and sinks are\nexactly-once only when they are idempotent or support a two-phase-commit protocol.\n\n### Where it breaks: the limitations\n- **External side effects.** Any effect in a system that does not share the transaction (HTTP APIs, email,\n  file storage, a model call) is at-least-once unless that system accepts an idempotency key and\n  deduplicates for you. Payment providers do; most internal services and most LLM endpoints do not.\n- **Finite dedupe windows.** Ids are kept for a window (a table retention, a TTL, Kafka's producer-session\n  state). A replay or redelivery that arrives after the window is treated as new. The window must exceed\n  the longest possible redelivery delay, including the dead-letter queue's replay time.\n- **Key stability.** Deduplication needs the same key on every attempt. A client that generates a fresh\n  UUID per retry defeats it. The key must be created once, before the first attempt, and persisted by the\n  caller; for agent steps it is derived from `(run_id, step_id)`, never from the attempt number or the\n  current time.\n- **Non-deterministic processing.** Replaying a step that calls a model yields a different output and\n  possibly different downstream tool arguments. Idempotency then means *memoisation*: store the output\n  under the key on first success and return the stored output on every later attempt. Durable-execution\n  engines do exactly this.\n- **Unknown outcomes.** When a call times out, dedupe alone does not tell you whether the effect happened.\n  The only resolution is to query the receiver by key (reconciliation) before retrying or compensating.\n- **Ordering across keys.** Dedupe is per key; two different messages that must be applied in order are a\n  separate problem, solved by partitioning, not by idempotency.\n- **Cost.** Transactional producers add coordinator round trips and reduce throughput; inbox tables add a\n  write and an index per message. Exactly-once is paid for per message, which argues for applying it only\n  where duplicates actually hurt.\n- **Human-visible effects.** A duplicate notification cannot be deduplicated after the fact; the dedupe has\n  to sit before the send, and the send itself must be the last thing before the commit, not the first.\n\n### A boundary test\nFor each effect a handler produces, ask: does the dedupe record commit atomically with this effect? If\nyes, the effect is exactly-once within the dedupe window. If no, it is at-least-once and needs either an\nidempotency key honoured by the receiver, a memoised result, or acceptance of duplicates.","example":"An agent step must email a customer a model-written draft at most once per `(run_id, step_id)`, even\nthough the worker can be killed at any instruction and the model is non-deterministic. The step is split\ninto phases, each idempotent on its own, with the state persisted between them.\n\n```python\nimport psycopg   # conn opened with autocommit=True: each statement commits on its own\n\n# CREATE TABLE step_results (step_key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, draft TEXT NOT NULL,\n#                            status TEXT NOT NULL);           -- 'drafted' | 'sent'\n\ndef load(conn, key):\n    return conn.execute(\"SELECT status, draft FROM step_results WHERE step_key = %s\",\n                        (key,)).fetchone()\n\ndef email_draft_once(conn, run_id, step_id, llm, mailer, prompt, to):\n    key = f\"{run_id}:{step_id}\"                    # stable across every retry, never per attempt\n    row = load(conn, key)\n    if row is None:                                # phase 1: generate once, persist before acting\n        draft = llm.complete(prompt)\n        conn.execute(\"\"\"INSERT INTO step_results (step_key, draft, status)\n                        VALUES (%s, %s, 'drafted') ON CONFLICT (step_key) DO NOTHING\"\"\",\n                     (key, draft))\n        row = load(conn, key)                      # if a racing worker inserted first, use ITS draft\n    status, draft = row\n    if status == \"sent\":\n        return draft                               # replay: no model call, no send\n    mailer.send(to=to, body=draft, idempotency_key=key)   # phase 2: provider dedupes on key\n    conn.execute(\"UPDATE step_results SET status = 'sent' WHERE step_key = %s\",\n                 (key,))                           # phase 3: mark sent\n    return draft\n```\n\nWalk the kill points. Killed during the model call or before the insert: the retry calls the model again,\nwhich wastes tokens but has no visible effect, because nothing was sent. Killed after the insert, before\nthe send: the retry finds `drafted`, skips the model and sends the *stored* draft, so what is sent is the\nsame text whichever attempt sends it. Killed after the send, before the update: the retry sends again\nwith the same key and the provider suppresses the duplicate, as long as its key retention outlasts the\nretry delay. Killed after the update: the retry returns immediately. Two workers racing on the same key\nboth may call the model, but `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` keeps the first draft and both send that one with\none key. The single residual duplicate is the third kill point with a provider that ignores idempotency\nkeys; no ordering of our code can remove it, which is the two-generals result showing up in practice.","tradeoffs":["At-least-once with idempotent consumers is cheap, broker-agnostic and robust, but it pushes dedupe state and key discipline into every consumer; broker-level transactions remove that burden only for broker-to-broker pipelines.","Kafka transactions give true exactly-once inside Kafka at the cost of throughput and latency from coordinator round trips; for pipelines whose sink is a database or an API they add cost without extending the guarantee.","Long dedupe windows catch late redeliveries but grow storage and slow the lookup; short windows are cheap but let a replayed dead-letter message through as a fresh event.","Memoising non-deterministic outputs makes agent replays reproducible and avoids repeated model spend, yet it freezes a possibly poor output; a deliberate \"regenerate\" must use a new key, which is a product decision, not a retry.","Accepting duplicates is sometimes the right trade: for an idempotent upsert into a search index, the cost of a duplicate is a wasted write, which is cheaper than any dedupe machinery."],"pitfalls":["Believing the broker's \"exactly-once\" flag covers the HTTP call inside the handler; the symptom is duplicate charges or double-posted messages that appear only during broker rebalances or deploys.","Generating the idempotency key per attempt (`uuid4()` inside the retry loop); duplicates persist despite \"having idempotency keys\", visible as several keys for one logical operation in the provider's logs.","A dedupe TTL shorter than the dead-letter replay delay; messages replayed from the DLQ a day later are processed again because their ids have expired.","Recording the processed id in a different store (Redis) from the effect (Postgres); a crash between the two writes either loses the effect or lets the duplicate through, and the inconsistency is rare enough to be blamed on \"flakiness\" for months.","Retrying a timed-out model or tool call without memoisation; each retry produces a different answer and the downstream saga compensations no longer match what was actually done.","Treating delta updates as idempotent; `credits -= 1` applied twice is wrong, discovered when customer balances drift from the ledger."],"when":"Decide per effect, not per system. For each side effect a handler or agent step produces, classify it:\nnaturally idempotent (upserts, absolute state), deduplicable in the same transaction (inbox or result\ntable), idempotent at the receiver (payment and messaging providers that honour keys), or\nduplicate-tolerant. Only effects in none of those classes need the heavyweight options: broker\ntransactions for Kafka-to-Kafka flows, or a reconciliation job that detects and repairs duplicates after\nthe fact. Measure duplicate rate at each dedupe point, the age distribution of redeliveries against your\nwindow, and the number of unknown-outcome timeouts needing reconciliation. In day-to-day work this is the\n`idempotency_key` parameter on every tool adapter and the `step_results` table in the agent runtime. In\ninterviews, the strong answer explains why delivery cannot be exactly-once, defines effectively-once via\natomic dedupe plus effect, and names the boundary where the guarantee ends.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Derives stable idempotency keys from run and step ids, memoises model outputs per step, and keeps dedupe records in the same transaction as the effect.","architect":"Classifies every external effect by its idempotency class, sets dedupe-window policy, and decides where broker transactions are worth their throughput cost.","fde":"Audits a customer's webhook and integration handlers for the \"exactly-once\" assumption, and negotiates idempotency-key support with their downstream systems before go-live."},"check":{"q":"A product manager asks you to guarantee that an agent's \"post summary to the customer's Slack\nchannel\" step happens exactly once per run, given that workers can be preempted at any moment. What can\nyou promise, and how would you build it?","a":"Promise exactly-once *effect* within a stated window, not exactly-once delivery. Build it in\nphases keyed by `(run_id, step_id)`: first generate the summary and persist it in a `step_results` row,\nso a preempted worker on retry reuses the same text instead of calling the model again; then call Slack\nwith that key as the idempotency token if their API honours one, and if it does not, record a \"sent\"\nmarker only after the call returns and accept that a preemption between the post and the marker can\nproduce a duplicate. Reduce that window by making the marker write the very next operation, and add a\nreconciliation that searches the channel for the summary's correlation id before any retry. State the\nresidual risk explicitly: with a provider that lacks idempotency keys, the duplicate probability equals\nthe probability of a crash between Slack accepting the post and our marker committing, a window of one\nresponse round trip plus one write, which is small but not zero."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can no protocol over a lossy network guarantee exactly-once delivery?","options":["Because TCP limits retransmissions to a fixed count","Because a sender without an acknowledgement cannot distinguish a lost message from a lost ack, so it must choose between risking loss and risking duplication","Because clocks on the two sides are never synchronised","Because brokers do not persist messages to disk by default"],"answer":1,"explanation":"This is the two generals argument: certainty about the other side's state would require a final confirmation that itself could be lost. Retries resolve toward at-least-once; acking first resolves toward at-most-once. Clock sync and persistence are separate concerns."},{"q":"A Kafka Streams job with transactions enabled reads orders, calls a payment API, and writes receipts to another topic. What is exactly-once?","options":["The payment API call, because the transaction wraps the whole handler","The offset commit and the receipt records, but not the payment API call","Nothing, because transactions only work with a single topic","Everything, provided consumers use read_committed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kafka transactions make input offsets and output records atomic inside Kafka. The payment call is an external effect outside the transaction, so it is at-least-once and needs its own idempotency key at the provider. read_committed affects what consumers see, not external calls."},{"q":"Which change makes a retried agent step produce the same downstream behaviour as the first attempt?","options":["Lower the model temperature to zero","Add a longer timeout before retrying","Persist the step's output under a stable (run_id, step_id) key and return it on replay","Generate a new idempotency key for each retry so the provider accepts it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memoising the first successful output under a stable key is what makes a non-deterministic step idempotent; the replay reads the stored result instead of recomputing. Temperature zero is not guaranteed deterministic across runs, and per-retry keys defeat deduplication entirely."}]},{"key":"system-design/distributed-systems","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Lamport, 1978 — Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events in a Distributed System","Fischer, Lynch & Paterson, 1985 — Impossibility of Distributed Consensus with One Faulty Process","Ongaro & Ousterhout, 2014 — In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm"],"why":"One machine is predictable. A function call either returns or the process is dead; a write either\nhappens or the program crashes, and the operating system gives you a consistent clock and shared memory.\nSystems leave that world for three reasons: capacity (one box cannot hold the model, the index and the\ntraffic), availability (one box is one failure domain), and geography (users and data live in many\nplaces, and the model provider is on someone else's network anyway).\n\nThe price is that the comforting binary \"it worked or it crashed\" disappears. Send a request to a model\nendpoint and get no reply within 30 seconds. Possibilities: the request was lost; it arrived and is\nqueued; it was processed and the response was lost; the server crashed mid-way; your own process paused\nfor garbage collection and the response is sitting in a buffer. Each possibility demands a different\nreaction, and the only evidence is the absence of a reply. Peter Deutsch's \"fallacies of distributed\ncomputing\" list the assumptions engineers bring from the single machine: the network is reliable,\nlatency is zero, bandwidth is infinite, topology does not change, there is one administrator. Each\nfallacy becomes an outage.\n\nThe naive strategy, \"add retries and bigger timeouts\", converts invisible failures into duplicate\neffects and cascading overload: when each of five layers in a call chain retries three times, one failed\ncall at the bottom can fan out into up to 4⁵ ≈ 1,000 attempts against the slow dependency. The alternative, \"make everything strongly consistent and\ncoordinated\", runs into mathematics: coordination costs round trips, and in an asynchronous network with\neven one crashed process, no deterministic protocol can guarantee that agreement terminates (the FLP\nresult). Working systems live between those poles, and knowing where is the skill this lesson orients.","how":"### System models: what you assume can go wrong\nEvery protocol is correct only under a stated model, so name it before trusting a design. On faults:\n**crash-stop** (a node halts and never returns), **crash-recovery** (it halts and comes back, possibly\nhaving lost in-memory state but keeping its disk, which is the realistic case for a restarted pod), and\n**Byzantine** (nodes may lie; relevant to blockchains and adversarial settings, almost never inside one\ncompany's backend). On timing: **synchronous** (known bounds on delay and pauses, which real networks do\nnot give you), **asynchronous** (no bounds at all, the setting of the FLP result) and **partially\nsynchronous** (bounds hold most of the time but can be violated for unknown periods). Practical systems\nassume crash-recovery and partial synchrony, and split their guarantees accordingly: **safety** (nothing\nbad ever happens, such as two leaders committing conflicting entries) must hold under any timing, while\n**liveness** (something good eventually happens, such as a leader being elected) is promised only while\nthe network behaves. Raft is built this way, which is how it coexists with FLP: timeouts drive elections\nand can stall progress, but they never make two nodes commit different values.\n\n### Failure detection is a timeout\nMachines do not announce their deaths. A heartbeat or request that gets no answer within *T* is the\nonly signal, and it conflates a dead node, a partitioned network, an overloaded node and a paused\nprocess. Choose *T* from measured latency distributions (p99 plus margin), propagate a **deadline** down\nthe call chain so a request that has 2 seconds left does not launch a 10-second sub-call, and treat a\ntimeout as \"outcome unknown\", never as \"did not happen\". Retries therefore need idempotency keys, jittered\nbackoff and a budget, and callers need circuit breakers so a dead dependency fails fast instead of tying\nup every thread.\n\n### Clocks lie\nWall clocks drift and NTP corrects them with jumps, so timestamps from two nodes cannot order events\nreliably at millisecond granularity. Use monotonic clocks for durations, and for ordering use causality:\nLamport's logical clocks (and vector clocks, version numbers, log offsets) order events by what they could\nhave influenced rather than by when a clock said they happened. Last-writer-wins by wall clock silently\ndiscards writes when the \"earlier\" writer had the faster clock.\n\n### Replication\nCopying data to several nodes buys availability and read throughput. **Leader-based** replication sends\nwrites to one node that ships them to followers; synchronous followers make writes slower but durable\nacross nodes, asynchronous ones create **replication lag**, the window in which a read from a follower\nreturns stale data. **Multi-leader** and **leaderless** designs accept writes in many places and must\nresolve conflicts. Leaderless (Dynamo-style) systems use quorums: with *N* replicas, writing to *W* and\nreading from *R* such that *W + R > N* guarantees at least one replica in every read saw the latest write,\nwhich is how availability is traded against the size of the quorum. With *N = 3, W = 2, R = 2* the system\ntolerates one replica down for both reads and writes. The overlap is necessary but not sufficient for\n\"always read the latest\": concurrent writes still need a rule to pick a winner, a write that reached\nfewer than *W* replicas is reported as failed yet is not rolled back on the replicas it did reach, and\n\"sloppy\" quorums that accept writes on fallback nodes during outages break the overlap. Quorum reads\ngive you probably-fresh data, not linearizability, unless the system adds more machinery.\n\n### Partitioning\nWhen data outgrows one node it is split by key. The partition key decides which operations stay local\n(cheap, atomic) and which span partitions (expensive, no atomicity without extra protocol). Hot keys (one\ntenant, one viral document) overload a single partition no matter how many exist, which is why tenant\nid is often a poor sole key for an AI platform.\n\n### Consensus and leases\nSome decisions need all nodes to agree: who is the leader, what is the next log entry, who holds a lock.\nRaft solves this with terms, leader election and majority-replicated logs. A cluster of *2f + 1* nodes\ntolerates *f* failures, *because* any two majorities overlap in at least one node: the majority that\nelects a new leader always contains a node holding every committed entry, and Raft's voting rule\nrefuses any candidate whose log is behind the voter's, so committed entries survive leader changes.\nThree nodes survive one failure, five survive two, and a fourth node adds cost without adding\ntolerance. Systems such as etcd and Consul (Raft) and ZooKeeper (its own\nZab protocol, same majority principle) package consensus so applications store small, critical state (configuration, leadership, locks) there and keep bulk data\nelsewhere, *because* each consensus write costs a majority round trip. Locks and leadership are granted\nas **leases** with an expiry, since a holder that dies must not hold the lock forever. The subtle part is\nthat a holder may *believe* it still has the lease after it expired (it paused, or its clock lagged). A\n**fencing token**, a number that increases with each grant and that the protected resource checks,\nrejects writes from the stale holder.\n\n### The CAP and PACELC framing\nDuring a network partition a replicated system must choose: refuse some requests to stay consistent, or\nserve them and diverge. Outside partitions the same system trades latency against consistency (PACELC):\nevery coordinated write costs a round trip to a quorum. The practical reading is that strong consistency\nis bought per operation, so systems make a few operations strongly consistent (allocating an id, taking a\nlock, debiting a quota) and the rest eventually consistent.\n\n### Seeing it\nWith no single stack trace, correlation ids and distributed tracing are not optional; they are how a\n\"request\" is reconstituted from the spans that ran on different machines.","example":"A lease-based lock with fencing tokens, the canonical illustration of why \"the lock holder\" is not a\nreliable concept. A worker takes the lease to re-index a document, pauses longer than the lease, and a\nsecond worker takes over; the storage layer rejects the first worker's late write because its token is\nstale.\n\n```python\nimport time, threading\n\nclass LeaseLock:                       # stand-in for etcd/ZooKeeper/Redis-with-care\n    def __init__(self, ttl): self.ttl, self.token, self.holder, self.expires = ttl, 0, None, 0.0\n    def acquire(self, who):\n        if self.holder is None or time.monotonic() > self.expires:\n            self.token += 1; self.holder, self.expires = who, time.monotonic() + self.ttl\n            return self.token         # strictly increasing fencing token\n        return None\n\nclass IndexStore:                      # the protected resource checks the token\n    def __init__(self): self.max_token, self.data = 0, {}\n    def write(self, token, doc_id, chunks):\n        if token < self.max_token:\n            raise PermissionError(f\"stale token {token} < {self.max_token}\")\n        self.max_token = token; self.data[doc_id] = chunks\n\nlock, store = LeaseLock(ttl=0.5), IndexStore()\n\ndef worker(name, pause):\n    tok = lock.acquire(name)\n    if tok is None: return\n    time.sleep(pause)                  # simulates a GC pause or a slow embedding call\n    try:\n        store.write(tok, \"doc-42\", f\"chunks by {name} (token {tok})\")\n        print(f\"{name}: wrote with token {tok}\")\n    except PermissionError as e:\n        print(f\"{name}: rejected ({e})\")\n\na = threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(\"A\", 1.0)); a.start()   # holds lease, then stalls\ntime.sleep(0.7)                                                   # lease has expired by now\nb = threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(\"B\", 0.0)); b.start()   # takes token 2, writes\na.join(); b.join()\nprint(store.data[\"doc-42\"])            # B's chunks survive; A's late write was fenced off\n```\n\nWithout the token check, worker A's write would land after B's and silently overwrite a newer index\nwith older chunks, and nothing in the logs would show a lock violation, because A really did hold the\nlock when it started.","tradeoffs":["Replicating data raises availability and read capacity, but every extra replica either slows writes (synchronous) or widens the stale-read window (asynchronous); the choice flips toward synchronous when a stale read has a business cost, such as permissions.","Consensus gives safe leadership and locks at the cost of a majority round trip per write and a minimum of three nodes; it is worth it for metadata and coordination, wasteful for bulk data.","Short timeouts detect failures fast but misclassify slow nodes as dead and trigger needless retries; long timeouts waste capacity holding connections; the right value comes from the latency distribution, not from a default.","Retries raise success probability for transient faults, yet without jitter, budgets and idempotency they amplify outages into retry storms and duplicate effects.","Partitioning by tenant keeps each tenant's operations local and simplifies isolation, but it concentrates a large tenant on one partition; compound keys spread load at the cost of cross-partition queries."],"pitfalls":["Treating a timeout as \"the request did not happen\"; the symptom is duplicate side effects (two tickets, two charges, two emails) that cluster around latency spikes.","Retrying at every layer (client, gateway, service, SDK) so one failure fans out multiplicatively; the symptom is a dependency that stays overloaded long after its original fault cleared.","Ordering events by wall-clock timestamps from different hosts; records appear to go backwards in time and last-writer-wins drops updates that users swear they made.","Holding a distributed lock without a fencing token; rare corruption after GC pauses or network blips, impossible to reproduce and blamed on \"a race\".","Reading from a replica immediately after writing to the leader; users report that their upload or setting \"disappeared\" and then reappears seconds later.","Treating a quorum store with *W + R > N* as linearizable; during a slow or partially failed write, one client reads the new value and a moment later another reads the old one, an anomaly that only appears under load or replica failure.","Running consensus-backed stores with an even number of nodes or across two data centres; a clean split leaves neither side with a majority and the whole cluster unavailable."],"when":"You do not choose to build a distributed system; any product that calls a model API, keeps state in a\ndatabase and runs more than one process already is one. What you choose is how much coordination each\noperation gets. Default to local, idempotent, eventually consistent operations and spend coordination on\nthe few invariants that must hold: quota debits, permission checks, idempotency keys, leader election\nfor singleton jobs. Measure timeout and retry rates per dependency, replication lag, lock hold times\nversus lease length, and the fraction of requests that hit a circuit breaker. Day to day this is the\ndeadline you pass to the model client, the idempotency key on every tool call, and the choice of\nPostgres primary versus replica for a given read. The reliability module covers the mitigation patterns\n(circuit breakers, bulkheads, multi-region) in depth; the consistency lesson covers what reads may\nreturn. In interviews, the expected depth is to reason from partial failure: what a timeout can and\ncannot tell you, why retries need idempotency, and why a lock needs a token.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets per-call deadlines, jittered retry budgets and idempotency keys on model and tool calls, and propagates trace context so a failed agent run can be reconstructed across services.","architect":"Decides which state needs consensus or strong consistency, how data is partitioned by tenant, and how many failure domains the system must survive, then budgets latency accordingly.","fde":"Diagnoses customer-side symptoms (duplicate records, stale reads, \"random\" timeouts) as partial-failure effects and adjusts timeouts, keys and read routing in their integration."},"check":{"q":"An agent platform runs one \"scheduler\" process that must be the only instance assigning jobs to\nworkers. The team uses a Redis key with a 30-second expiry as the lock and renews it every 10 seconds.\nTwice last month two schedulers ran at once for a few seconds. Explain how that happened and what to\nchange.","a":"The lock is a lease, and a lease holder cannot know it still holds the lease when it acts. If the\nscheduler paused for more than 30 seconds (a GC pause, a stalled Redis renewal, a VM migration, or a\nclock-related expiry on the Redis side), the key expired, a standby acquired it and started scheduling,\nand the original resumed believing it was still the leader until its next renewal failed. That window is\nexactly the overlap observed. Two changes close it. First, issue a strictly increasing fencing token with\nevery acquisition and make the job-assignment write (the protected resource) reject any token lower than\nthe highest it has seen, so the stale leader's assignments are refused rather than applied. Second, move\nleadership to a consensus-backed store (etcd, ZooKeeper) whose lease and token semantics are designed for\nthis, and alert on lock hold time approaching the TTL. Renewing more often helps only until the next pause\nlonger than the TTL."},"quiz":[{"q":"A call to the embedding service times out after 10 seconds. Which statement is correct?","options":["The embedding was not computed, so a retry is always safe","The embedding was computed, so the client should look it up instead of retrying","The outcome is unknown; a retry must carry an idempotency key or the client must query by key first","The service is down and the circuit breaker should open immediately"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A timeout only says that no reply arrived in time. The request may have been lost, queued, processed with a lost reply, or delayed by a pause on either side. Safe retries therefore need idempotency, and breakers open on a rate of failures, not on one timeout."},{"q":"With N = 5 replicas, which read/write quorum sizes guarantee a read sees the latest acknowledged write?","options":["W = 2, R = 2","W = 3, R = 3","W = 1, R = 4","W = 5, R = 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Overlap requires W + R > N. With 3 and 3 the sets must share at least one replica, so a read sees the newest write. 2 + 2 = 4 does not exceed 5, 1 + 4 = 5 does not either, and a read of zero replicas returns nothing."},{"q":"Why is a monotonically increasing fencing token needed in addition to a lease-based lock?","options":["To make lock acquisition faster under contention","To replace the need for a lease expiry","Because a holder may act after its lease expired, and the resource must be able to reject that stale action","To order events across data centres by wall-clock time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A paused or partitioned holder cannot know its lease lapsed. The token lets the protected resource compare the acting holder's grant against the newest grant it has seen and refuse the stale one; the lease still handles dead holders, and tokens have nothing to do with wall clocks."}]},{"key":"system-design/data-consistency","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Herlihy & Wing, 1990 — Linearizability: A Correctness Condition for Concurrent Objects","Bailis et al., 2014 — Highly Available Transactions: Virtues and Limitations","Vogels, 2009 — Eventually Consistent"],"why":"A single-node database with serializable transactions lets you forget this topic: every read sees the\nresult of every committed write, and constraints keep invariants true. The moment you add a second copy\nof any data, you have to say what a reader may see, because the copies cannot be updated at the same\ninstant. Copies are everywhere in an AI product: a read replica behind the API, a Redis cache in front of\nthe database, a vector index built from documents, an agent memory store summarised from conversations,\na permissions service mirrored into index metadata, a data warehouse fed by nightly jobs.\n\nThe naive position is \"it will catch up in a second, nobody will notice\". Then a user uploads a contract,\nimmediately asks a question about it, and the assistant answers \"I don't have that document\" because the\nindex refresh had not run; or an admin revokes a contractor's access to a folder and the contractor's\nnext query still retrieves chunks from it for thirty seconds; or an agent reads its own memory on a\nreplica, misses the note it wrote a moment ago, and repeats a tool call. Each is a consistency anomaly\nwith a precise name, and each has a precise fix, but only if you know which guarantee you need.\n\nThe opposite naive position, \"make everything strongly consistent\", fails on physics and economics.\nStrong consistency across copies requires coordination: a write must be acknowledged by a quorum, or\nreads must go to the leader, before anyone can proceed. Round trips within a region cost roughly a\nmillisecond; across continents tens to over a hundred milliseconds; and under a network partition the\nsystem must refuse requests to stay consistent. Paying that on every read of a product catalogue or a\nchunk embedding is waste. The skill is choosing per invariant, which requires knowing the ladder of\nguarantees and what each one rules out.","how":"### Consistency inside one database: isolation\nWithin a single database, concurrent transactions are isolated to a configurable degree. **Read\ncommitted** prevents dirty reads (seeing uncommitted data) but allows a row to change between two reads\nin the same transaction. **Snapshot isolation** (Postgres's `REPEATABLE READ`) gives each transaction a\nfrozen view of the database as of its start, which rules out non-repeatable reads and phantoms but\npermits **write skew**: two transactions each read a condition, both find it true, and both write, leaving\nthe invariant violated (two on-call doctors each see \"at least one other doctor on call\" and both go\noff duty). **Serializable** guarantees the outcome equals some serial order, at the cost of aborts under\ncontention. The decisive question is whether the invariant spans rows that one transaction reads and\nanother writes; if it does, you need serializable isolation or an explicit lock. This is covered in depth\nin the backend-fundamentals module.\n\n### Consistency across copies: the ladder\nOrdered from strongest to weakest, each level is defined by the anomaly it forbids.\n\n- **Linearizable.** The system behaves as if there is one copy and every operation takes effect at a\n  single instant between its start and end. Once any client sees a write, every later read sees it.\n  Needed for locks, leader election, unique-name registration, quota debits. Costs a quorum or leader\n  round trip per operation and is unavailable to the minority side of a partition.\n- **Sequential, then causal.** Sequential consistency: every client sees the same total order of writes,\n  consistent with each client's program order, but not necessarily aligned with real time. Causal\n  consistency relaxes that further: only causally related writes must be seen in order (a reply never\n  appears before the message it answers); concurrent writes may be seen in different orders by\n  different clients. Cheaper than linearizable, and causal is enough for most collaborative and\n  conversational data.\n- **Session guarantees.** Promises scoped to one client's session: **read-your-writes** (you see your own\n  updates), **monotonic reads** (you never see time go backwards), **monotonic writes** (your writes apply\n  in order), **writes-follow-reads**. These are what users actually notice, and they are cheap to provide\n  by routing a session's reads to the leader for a short period after a write, or by carrying a version\n  token the replica must have caught up to before serving.\n- **Eventual.** If writes stop, all copies converge. No bound on *when*, and no promise about what a read\n  returns in the meantime. Right for analytics, recommendations, derived indexes, caches with TTLs.\n\n### Converging needs a merge rule\n\"All copies converge\" hides a decision: when two copies accepted different writes to the same item\n(multi-leader replication, offline clients, two regions), something must decide the merged value.\n**Last-writer-wins** keeps the write with the highest timestamp; it always converges, *because* every\nreplica applies the same rule, but it silently discards the other write and, with wall-clock\ntimestamps, can discard the genuinely later one. **Version vectors** detect that two writes were\nconcurrent rather than ordered and keep both as siblings for the application to merge (two edits to a\nshared prompt template surface as a conflict to resolve). **CRDTs** are data types whose merge is\ndefined so that any order of applying updates yields the same result: a grow-only counter sums per-node\ncounts, an observed-remove set keeps an add unless the remove saw it, and collaborative text editors\nuse sequence CRDTs. They converge without coordination, but only for operations the type defines; an\ninvariant such as \"balance never below zero\" cannot be kept by merging after the fact and needs\ncoordination before the write.\n\n### Where lag comes from and how it is bounded\nReplication lag is the time between a commit on the leader and its visibility on a copy. Asynchronous\ndatabase replicas lag milliseconds to seconds, more under load. Search and vector indexes commonly make\nnew writes visible only after a periodic refresh (Elasticsearch's default refresh interval is one\nsecond). Caches lag until invalidation or TTL. Event-driven projections lag by queue depth. Bounding lag\nmeans measuring it (leader LSN minus replica LSN, event timestamp minus processing time) and routing\nsensitive reads around it.\n\n### Derived data and the permission problem\nA vector index is a *derived* copy of documents plus access metadata. Two consistency questions follow.\nFreshness: how long after a document changes or is deleted do stale chunks remain retrievable? Deletion\nmust propagate as an explicit tombstone event, and the retention of embeddings for deleted documents is a\ncompliance issue, not only a quality issue. Authorisation: if access control lists are copied into the\nindex as filter metadata, a revocation is eventually consistent, and the window is a leak. The robust\ndesign checks permissions at query time against the authoritative store (a strongly consistent read of a\nsmall set), or filters by document id against a fresh ACL lookup after retrieval, and uses index metadata\nonly as a pre-filter for efficiency.\n\n### Choosing per invariant\nList each piece of state, name the invariant, and ask what a stale read costs. Money, permissions,\nuniqueness, quotas and idempotency records: strong, in one place, read from the leader. User-visible\n\"did my action take\" state: session guarantees. Everything derived: eventual, with a measured and\ndisplayed lag (\"index updated 4 s ago\"). Aggregates in domain-driven design express the same rule: strong\nconsistency inside an aggregate, eventual between aggregates.","example":"Read-your-writes across a Postgres primary and an asynchronous replica. After a write commits, the API\nreturns a version token (the primary's write-ahead-log position at or after the commit record); later\nreads carry it, and the replica is used\nonly if it has replayed past that position, otherwise the read is routed to the primary.\n\n```python\nimport psycopg\n\ndef save_memory(primary, run_id, note):\n    with primary.transaction():\n        primary.execute(\"INSERT INTO agent_memory (run_id, note) VALUES (%s, %s)\", (run_id, note))\n    # read the LSN AFTER commit: the commit record is what makes the row visible on the replica,\n    # and it is written after any LSN observed inside the transaction\n    (token,) = primary.execute(\"SELECT pg_current_wal_lsn()::text\").fetchone()\n    return token                       # hand this back to the client as a 'consistency token'\n\ndef replica_is_fresh(replica, token):\n    (ok,) = replica.execute(\n        \"SELECT pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() >= %s::pg_lsn\", (token,)).fetchone()\n    return bool(ok)\n\ndef read_memory(primary, replica, run_id, token=None):\n    conn = replica if (token is None or replica_is_fresh(replica, token)) else primary\n    return conn.execute(\"SELECT note FROM agent_memory WHERE run_id = %s ORDER BY id\",\n                        (run_id,)).fetchall()\n\n# Usage inside an agent step:\n#   token = save_memory(primary, run_id, \"user prefers formal tone\")\n#   notes = read_memory(primary, replica, run_id, token)   # never misses its own write\n# Reads with no token (another user's dashboard) go to the replica and accept lag.\n```\n\nThe cost is one extra round trip to the replica when the token is present, and a primary read only when\nthe replica is behind, which is exactly the small fraction of reads that would otherwise have been\nwrong. A cheaper variant with the same guarantee is to route all reads of a session to the primary for a\nfixed window (a few seconds) after any write, at the price of some unnecessary primary load, and a\nguarantee that breaks if replica lag ever exceeds the window. The same token also buys **monotonic\nreads** when there are several replicas: if the client keeps the highest LSN it has *read* as well as\nwritten, and sends that, a request load-balanced to a more-lagged replica is redirected instead of\nshowing older data than the previous page did.","tradeoffs":["Linearizable reads make correctness trivial to reason about, but each costs a leader or quorum round trip and disappears on the minority side of a partition; once an operation's error cost is lower than its latency cost, drop to a weaker level.","Session guarantees give users almost everything they notice at a fraction of the cost of global strong consistency, yet they require sticky routing or version tokens, which complicates stateless load balancing.","Copying permissions into index metadata makes retrieval filters fast and self-contained, while turning every revocation into an eventually consistent leak window; query-time checks against the source are slower per request but close the window entirely.","Serializable isolation catches write skew automatically, but aborts rise with contention; for a hot counter or quota, an explicit row lock or an atomic single-statement update is both faster and simpler.","Displaying lag (\"updated 4 s ago\", \"indexing…\") costs UI work and admits imperfection, but it converts a bug report into an understood state and lets you keep the cheap eventual path."],"pitfalls":["Reading from a replica right after writing to the primary; users see their new document, setting or memory vanish and reappear, and the bug cannot be reproduced on a single-node dev setup.","Treating snapshot isolation as serializable; two concurrent requests both pass a \"seat is free\" or \"usage is under quota\" check, each inserts its own row, both commit, and the invariant is found broken days later (a conflicting update to the *same* row would have been caught; disjoint writes are not).","Enforcing authorisation only through copied index metadata; a revoked user still retrieves content for the lag window, which audit logs reveal as access after revocation.","Deleting source documents without emitting a tombstone to derived stores; embeddings of deleted or redacted content keep surfacing in answers, sometimes months later.","Assuming the cache is coherent with the database; after a write, one request path reads the fresh row and another the stale cache entry, producing responses that contradict each other within one page load.","Using wall-clock last-writer-wins to merge concurrent updates across replicas; an edit silently disappears and no error is ever logged."],"when":"You are always choosing a consistency level, whether you name it or not; the default of \"whatever the\nreplica returns\" is a choice. Make it explicit per invariant: write down which state is strong, which\nhas session guarantees, and which is eventual with what bound, and encode the routing (primary versus\nreplica, query-time ACL versus metadata filter, synchronous versus asynchronous indexing) accordingly.\nMeasure replication lag, index freshness lag and cache hit-on-stale rates, and alert when the lag exceeds\nwhat the product promises. In day-to-day work this is the `read_from_primary=True` flag on a repository\ncall, the tombstone event on document deletion, and the ACL check after retrieval. In interviews, be able\nto place linearizable, causal, read-your-writes and eventual on one ladder with the anomaly each forbids,\nexplain write skew, and apply the ladder to a RAG index that mirrors permissions.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Routes post-write reads to the primary or carries version tokens, emits tombstones for deletions, and chooses isolation levels or locks for invariants that span rows.","architect":"Classifies every data store as source of truth or derived copy, assigns a consistency level and lag budget to each, and decides where permissions are enforced.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a document is searchable seconds after upload rather than instantly, and verifies that their access revocations propagate to the index within the agreed window."},"check":{"q":"A customer's security team asks for a guarantee that when a user loses access to a SharePoint\nfolder, the assistant can never surface content from it. Your index stores ACLs as chunk metadata and\nsyncs them hourly. What do you tell them, and how do you redesign?","a":"The current design is eventually consistent with an hour-long leak window, which cannot meet\n\"never\". Redesign in layers. First, keep the metadata filter as a cheap pre-filter but add a query-time\nauthorisation check: after retrieval, look up the candidate documents' current permissions against the\nauthoritative source (SharePoint's API or a near-real-time mirror with a measured lag) and drop anything\nthe user cannot access now. That makes the guarantee as strong as the permission source, which is where\nthe customer already trusts it. Second, replace the hourly sync with change events or incremental\nsynchronisation so the pre-filter is seconds behind rather than an hour, and emit tombstones on document\ndeletion so chunks are removed, not merely hidden. Third, state the residual: the query-time check is\nonly as fresh as the permission source's own consistency, and a strongly consistent read there costs\nlatency per query, which you can bound by caching allow decisions for a few seconds at most. Measure\nrevocation-to-enforcement time and report it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which guarantee ensures a user always sees the memory note their agent wrote a moment ago, without requiring every read in the system to go to the leader?","options":["Linearizability for all reads","Read-your-writes, implemented by routing that session's reads to the primary or carrying a version token","Eventual consistency with a short TTL","Serializable isolation on the replica"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Read-your-writes is a session guarantee scoped to the writer, so only the writer's post-write reads pay for freshness. Global linearizability would solve it at far higher cost; eventual consistency gives no promise; isolation levels govern concurrent transactions, not replica staleness."},{"q":"Two concurrent requests each sum a tenant's usage rows, find the total below the quota, and each insert a new usage row, under snapshot isolation. What anomaly can occur?","options":["Dirty read","Non-repeatable read","Write skew: both see headroom in their snapshots and both insert, pushing the total over the quota","Lost update, because the second insert overwrites the first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each transaction reads a consistent snapshot in which the quota has headroom and then writes a *different* row; snapshot isolation only detects write-write conflicts on the same row, so both commit. The fix is serializable isolation, locking the tenant row (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) before the check, or keeping the usage as one counter updated by a single atomic conditional statement."},{"q":"Why is a query-time permission check more robust than relying on ACL metadata copied into a vector index?","options":["Metadata filters are too slow for vector search","Vector indexes cannot store string metadata","Copied ACLs are eventually consistent, so a revocation leaves a window in which content is still retrievable","Query-time checks make the index linearizable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The index is a derived copy, so its ACL metadata lags the source by the sync interval. Checking current permissions against the authoritative store at query time closes that window; the metadata filter stays useful as a pre-filter for efficiency, not as the enforcement point."}]},{"key":"system-design/modular-monolith-vs-microservices","sources":["Fowler, 2015 — MonolithFirst","Newman, 2021 — Building Microservices (2nd ed.)","Richardson, 2018 — Microservices Patterns","Conway, 1968 — How Do Committees Invent?"],"why":"Two failure modes forced this decision into existence. The first is the **big ball of mud**: a monolith\nwith no internal structure, where any table can be joined from anywhere and any function can call any\nother. It is easy to deploy and impossible to change; every release needs a full regression and nobody\ncan say which code owns which data. The second is the **distributed monolith**: a system split into\nservices too early or along the wrong lines, so that a single feature touches four repositories, releases\nmust be coordinated in lock-step, and a database schema change in one service breaks two others. You pay\nfor the network (latency, retries, timeouts, serialisation, tracing) and get none of the independence.\n\nMicroservices were a response to the first failure mode at organisations with many teams; the second\nfailure mode is what happens when smaller teams copy the result without the cause. The naive answers,\n\"monoliths are legacy\" and \"microservices are best practice\", both skip the question that actually\nmatters: *which boundaries in this system need to be independently deployable, scalable or owned, today?*\n\nAI systems make this sharper, because they genuinely contain components with different physics: a\nGPU-bound model server, a CPU-bound orchestration API, bursty batch ingestion and embedding, and\nlong-running agent workflows. That tempts teams to split everything on day one. The modular monolith is\nthe discipline that lets you keep the internal boundaries sharp without paying the network tax until a\nspecific boundary earns it.","how":"### What a module boundary is inside one deployable\nA module in a modular monolith has four properties, each chosen to make later extraction cheap:\n\n1. **A narrow public API.** Other modules import one facade (an `api.py`, an interface, a package's\n   exported surface) and nothing else. This exists *because* a service boundary is exactly a public API\n   over a network; if the in-process API is already narrow, extraction is a transport change.\n2. **Owned data.** Each module owns its tables (a schema per module, or at minimum a naming and ownership\n   rule) and no other module queries them directly. This matters *because* a shared database is the\n   coupling that makes services inseparable later; cross-module reporting goes through the owning\n   module's API or an event-fed read model.\n3. **Events for side effects.** When module A needs module B to react (\"document ingested\", \"chat\n   answered\"), it publishes an in-process event rather than calling B's internals. The bus is a function\n   call today and an outbox plus broker tomorrow; the contract does not change.\n4. **Enforcement in CI.** Boundaries that live only in a wiki erode in weeks. Tools such as\n   `import-linter` (Python), ArchUnit (JVM), Nx module-boundary lint rules (TypeScript) and Packwerk\n   (Ruby) fail the build when a module reaches into another's internals.\n\nIn exchange you keep what a single process gives you for free: in-process calls that cost microseconds\nrather than a network round trip of roughly a millisecond or more (illustrative order of magnitude),\none transaction boundary when you need atomicity, one build, one deploy, one log stream and one\ndebugger.\n\n### What changes when a boundary becomes a service\nTurning a module boundary into a service boundary adds a fixed set of problems, and the list is the\nsame every time:\n\n- **Network and partial failure.** Calls can time out, succeed without the caller knowing, or be\n  retried; every mutating call therefore needs idempotency, timeouts and circuit breakers.\n- **Data ownership becomes physical.** With no shared database, a business operation spanning two\n  services becomes a saga with compensating actions, or an outbox-driven eventually consistent flow.\n  Joins become data pipelines.\n- **Contracts become versioned.** The API must evolve backward-compatibly because callers deploy on\n  their own schedule; contract tests replace the compiler.\n- **Operational surface multiplies.** Per-service CI/CD, dashboards, alerts, on-call, secrets,\n  distributed tracing with propagated correlation IDs, and usually a platform team to make all of it\n  repeatable.\n\nNone of these are reasons never to split; they are the price, and the price is paid per boundary.\n\n### The arithmetic of a hop\nTwo pieces of arithmetic explain why that price compounds rather than adds up linearly.\n\n**Availability multiplies along a synchronous chain.** If a request must pass through *n* services in\nseries and each is available a fraction *a* of the time, the request succeeds roughly *aⁿ* of the time.\nFive services at 99.9% give 0.999⁵ ≈ 99.5%: the ~43 minutes of monthly downtime one service is allowed\nbecomes ~3.6 hours for the path. Real failures are correlated, so treat this as the shape rather than a\nforecast. Five modules in one process fail together but do not multiply. This is *why* service designs\nlean on asynchronous messaging, caches and fallbacks: each one cuts a link out of the synchronous chain.\n\n**Fan-out amplifies tail latency.** If a request calls *n* services in parallel and waits for all of\nthem, it is slow whenever any one of them is. With each call exceeding its own p99 1% of the time, the\nrequest exceeds that threshold with probability 1 − 0.99ⁿ, about 10% at *n* = 10, so the callees' p99\nbecomes the caller's p90 (Dean and Barroso's \"The Tail at Scale\" is the classic treatment). Timeouts with\npartial results and hedged requests are the standard mitigations, and both are extra code a modular\nmonolith never needs.\n\n### The reasons that justify paying it\nA boundary is worth a service when at least one of these holds and the modular monolith cannot deliver\nit:\n\n- **Different scaling or hardware profile.** A vLLM model server needs GPUs and scales on tokens per\n  second; the orchestration API scales on request concurrency on cheap CPU nodes. Co-deploying them\n  wastes the GPU.\n- **Different failure domain.** An ingestion worker that can OOM on a 400-page PDF must not take the\n  chat API down with it.\n- **Different team and release cadence.** Conway's law is descriptive: when two teams own two things,\n  their interface will become a service boundary whether or not you planned it. The \"inverse Conway\n  manoeuvre\" runs it the other way: shape the teams around the boundaries you want, then split.\n- **Different security or compliance envelope.** A component that handles raw PII or runs untrusted\n  agent code may need its own network segment and credentials.\n- **Different runtime.** Python for the ML path and TypeScript for the product surface is a common and\n  legitimate split.\n\nNote the middle option many teams miss: **one codebase, several deployables**. The same artifact runs\nas `api`, `worker` or `scheduler` depending on its entrypoint. Processes are isolated, scaling is\nindependent, but there is one build, one set of contracts and no network between modules that do not\nneed it.\n\n### Extraction when it is time\nBecause the module already has a facade, owned data and events, extraction follows a fixed path:\nput the facade behind an interface; implement a remote client with the same interface; route a\npercentage of traffic to the new service (strangler pattern); move the tables; delete the in-process\nimplementation. Contract tests written against the facade keep passing throughout.","example":"A Python layout in which `chat` depends only on the retrieval module's public contract, side effects go\nthrough an in-process event bus, and CI forbids reaching into retrieval internals. Extracting retrieval\ninto a service later means writing a second `Retriever` implementation that speaks HTTP; `ChatService`\ndoes not change.\n\n```python\n# app/retrieval/api.py — the only module other packages may import from retrieval\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Passage:\n    doc_id: str\n    text: str\n    score: float\n\nclass Retriever(Protocol):                      # the contract; unchanged by extraction\n    def search(self, tenant_id: str, query: str, k: int = 8) -> list[Passage]: ...\n\ndef get_retriever() -> Retriever:\n    from .service import PgVectorRetriever      # private: schema, SQL, embedding client\n    return PgVectorRetriever()\n\n# app/chat/service.py — depends on the contract, never on retrieval internals\nfrom app.retrieval.api import Retriever, get_retriever\nfrom app.events import publish                  # in-process today; outbox + broker later\n\nclass ChatService:\n    def __init__(self, retriever: Retriever | None = None):\n        self.retriever = retriever or get_retriever()\n\n    def answer(self, tenant_id: str, question: str) -> str:\n        passages = self.retriever.search(tenant_id, question)\n        reply = compose_answer(question, passages)       # LLM call elided\n        publish(\"chat.answered\", {\"tenant_id\": tenant_id, \"passages\": len(passages)})\n        return reply\n\n# .importlinter — the build fails if chat imports retrieval's internals\n# [importlinter:contract:retrieval-is-private]\n# name = chat may only use retrieval.api\n# type = forbidden\n# source_modules = app.chat\n# forbidden_modules = app.retrieval.service\n# allow_indirect_imports = True\n```","tradeoffs":["A modular monolith gives one deploy, one transaction and in-process latency, but every module shares one process's resource limits and release train; that flips once a module needs different hardware or a team needs to ship without waiting for others.","Microservices give independent scaling and failure isolation, but each boundary adds network failure handling, versioned contracts and eventual consistency; the price is worth it only for boundaries whose independence you will actually exercise.","Enforced module boundaries cost up-front design and occasional friction when a quick cross-module query is forbidden; that cost is lower than untangling a shared database later, unless the product is a short-lived prototype that will be thrown away.","\"One codebase, several deployables\" keeps contracts in one place while isolating processes, but it couples release versions across roles; that becomes a problem when roles need genuinely different dependency sets (for example CUDA libraries only in the serving role)."],"pitfalls":["Splitting along technical layers (an \"LLM service\", a \"database service\") instead of business capabilities; the symptom is that every user-facing change touches every service.","Keeping a shared database behind \"separate\" services; the symptom is a schema migration in one repository breaking another team's deploy with no compiler to warn anyone.","Declaring a monolith \"modular\" without CI enforcement; the symptom is a dependency graph that, six months later, is a single strongly connected component.","Splitting early because the components *feel* different, then discovering that most requests fan out across three services; the symptom is p99 latency dominated by hop count and retries, and incidents that need three on-call rotations to debug.","Treating the model server as \"just another module\" of the API process; the symptom is a GPU box running web handlers at 5% GPU utilisation, or an API deploy that evicts a loaded model."],"when":"Default to a modular monolith for a new AI product: one Python codebase with enforced modules for\ningestion, retrieval, orchestration, evaluation and tenancy, run as separate processes (`api`, `worker`)\nfrom the same artifact. Keep the model-serving tier separate from the start when you self-host, because\nits hardware and scaling profile are provably different. Add a service boundary when you can name the\nspecific independence you need (team, scaling, blast radius, compliance) and have measured that the\nmonolith cannot provide it. Measure before and after: deploy frequency per module, change failure rate,\np99 latency per request path, the number of synchronous hops on the critical path, and how many\nrepositories a typical feature touches; a rising count of coordinated multi-service deploys is the\nearliest sign of a distributed monolith. In interviews, the\nexpected answer is not \"monolith\" or \"microservices\" but a sequence: which boundaries, in what order,\njustified by which observed pressure, and how the extraction is kept reversible.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the module facades, keeps the import-linter contracts green, and extracts a module into a service by swapping an adapter rather than rewriting callers.","architect":"Owns the boundary map: which capabilities are modules, which are deployables, which are services, and the written criteria (scaling, team, blast radius) that promote one to the next.","fde":"Fits the product into a customer's deployment constraints, often collapsing several services into one deployable for an on-prem or VPC install where a platform team does not exist."},"check":{"q":"A six-engineer startup is building a RAG product: document ingestion, a chat API, a self-hosted\nembedding model and a hosted LLM. They propose seven microservices. How would you structure it and why?","a":"I would start with one modular monolith in Python with enforced modules (ingestion, retrieval,\nchat orchestration, tenancy, evaluation) and run it as two deployables from the same artifact: an `api`\nprocess and a `worker` process for ingestion, because ingestion is bursty and can crash on bad\ndocuments while chat must stay up. The embedding model gets its own serving process because it needs a\nGPU and scales on throughput, not request concurrency. The hosted LLM is already a service behind an\nHTTP boundary. That is three runtime units, not seven, with one build and one set of contracts. I would\nwrite down the triggers for further splits, for example a second team taking ownership of ingestion or\nretrieval needing a different data store, and keep module facades so extraction is an adapter swap."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most distinguishes a modular monolith from an ordinary monolith?","options":["It is written in several programming languages","Modules expose narrow public APIs, own their data, and the boundaries are enforced by tooling","It is deployed as several containers behind a load balancer","It uses an event broker for all internal communication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The defining property is enforced internal boundaries, including data ownership, inside one deployable. Multiple containers or a broker describe deployment and transport, which is exactly what the modular monolith avoids until a boundary needs it."},{"q":"Which observation is the strongest evidence that a service boundary was drawn in the wrong place?","options":["The service has its own database","The service is written in a different language from its callers","Most feature changes require coordinated deploys of this service and its callers","The service receives more traffic than any other"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lock-step deploys mean the boundary does not provide independence, which is the only thing a network boundary buys. A separate database and a different language are normal properties of a healthy service."},{"q":"A team self-hosts an embedding model and runs it inside the same process as their FastAPI chat API. What is the most likely consequence?","options":["Lower latency because no network hop exists, with no downside","Poor GPU utilisation and coupled scaling, because web concurrency and model throughput have different profiles","The model cannot be loaded because FastAPI is single-threaded","Requests must be made idempotent because of partial failure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPU serving scales on batched throughput while an API scales on connection concurrency; co-locating them wastes the expensive resource and makes every API deploy evict the model. Idempotency concerns arise from network boundaries, not from co-location."}]},{"key":"system-design/multi-tenancy","sources":["Official docs: AWS Well-Architected SaaS Lens","Official docs: PostgreSQL (row security policies)"],"why":"The alternative is a deployment per customer. For three customers it is fine. For three hundred it means\nthree hundred databases to migrate, three hundred model-version rollouts, three hundred sets of alerts,\nand GPUs sitting idle in most of them because no single customer's traffic fills a card. Cost scales with\ncustomer count rather than with usage, and every upgrade becomes a campaign. Single-tenant deployments\nremain the right answer for a few regulated or very large customers, but they cannot be the default\nshape of a product.\n\nThe naive multi-tenant answer, \"add a `customer_id` column and filter in the query\", fails in two ways.\nFirst, the filter is a convention, and one forgotten `WHERE` clause returns another customer's records;\nin an LLM product that record then lands in a prompt and is paraphrased back to the wrong user. Second,\na column solves data separation only. Tenants also need separate configuration (which model, which\nsystem prompt, which features), separate performance envelopes (one tenant's bulk ingestion must not\nstarve another's chat), separate cost accounting (who used which tokens) and sometimes separate\njurisdictions (EU data stays in the EU). Multi-tenancy is the discipline of answering all of those with\none identity and a small number of explicit sharing decisions, rather than rediscovering each one in an\nincident.","how":"### Tenant identity, established once and carried everywhere\nEvery request enters with a credential (a JWT from the identity provider, an API key, an SSO assertion).\nThe system resolves the tenant from that credential *at the edge*, never from a request body or a query\nparameter, because anything the caller can type is something the caller can change. From then on the\ntenant ID travels with the work: in a request-scoped context variable inside the process, as a header or\nmessage attribute when work crosses into a queue, and as a field on every log line, trace span and\nmetric. The rule is that no layer may need to re-derive it from data, because re-derivation is where\nmistakes hide.\n\n### Three sharing models, chosen per resource\nThe AWS SaaS Lens vocabulary is useful because it names the options precisely:\n\n- **Pool.** Tenants share the resource, separated by a tenant key: one PostgreSQL schema with a\n  `tenant_id` column on every table, one vector index with tenant metadata, one model-serving fleet.\n  Cheapest and simplest to operate; isolation depends entirely on correct filtering and quotas.\n- **Bridge.** Shared infrastructure, partitioned structure: a schema or namespace per tenant in a shared\n  database, a collection per tenant in the vector store. Stronger separation, per-tenant backup and\n  restore, and per-tenant migration timing become possible, at the cost of running every migration N\n  times, a catalog that grows with N × tables, and multiplied connection pools if each tenant gets its own\n  database credentials.\n- **Silo.** A dedicated resource per tenant: separate database, cluster or whole stack. Strongest\n  isolation, highest cost, and the operational model drifts back toward one-deployment-per-customer.\n\nMost real systems mix them *per resource and per tier*: pooled compute and a pooled vector index for\nself-serve customers, bridge databases for mid-market, silo for the enterprise tier that pays for it.\nThe architecture decision is not \"which model\" but \"which model for which resource, and what moves a\ntenant between tiers\".\n\n### The tenant directory: placement as data\n\"Which model for which resource\" only works if placement is data rather than code. A control-plane\n**tenant directory** maps each tenant to its tier, region, cell, database or schema, index namespace and\nencryption-key ID. The data plane looks the tenant up (and caches the answer) on every request, *because*\nthat indirection is what lets you move a tenant without touching callers. Moving a tenant from pool to\nsilo then has a fixed shape: provision the new placement, copy the data, dual-write or freeze writes for\nthe cut-over window, verify row and vector counts, flip the directory entry, and only then delete from\nthe old placement. Larger SaaS systems extend this into **cells**: several identical, independently\ndeployed pooled stacks, each holding a subset of tenants, so a bad deploy or a runaway tenant hurts one\ncell rather than everyone. Cells cap blast radius at the price of a routing layer and N fleets to deploy.\n\n### Tenant is a boundary, not an authorisation model\nThe tenant is the outer boundary of data ownership; inside it, users still have roles and documents\nstill have ACLs. Scoping retrieval by tenant is necessary but not sufficient: an intern and the CFO of\nthe same tenant must not retrieve the same board minutes. Decide early which level of the customer\nhierarchy (reseller, organisation, workspace) is the isolation boundary, because that is the key every\nstore is partitioned by, and changing it later means re-keying everything.\n\n### Lifecycle: onboarding and offboarding\nOnboarding creates the directory entry, configuration, keys and any dedicated resources in one\nidempotent workflow, so a half-failed signup can simply be retried. Offboarding is harder and is where\naudits find gaps: deleting a tenant must reach the primary database, the vector index, object storage,\ncaches, queues, logs and traces, evaluation samples, fine-tuned adapters and, on their retention\nschedule, backups. In a silo this is \"drop the stack\"; in a pool it is a delete keyed by tenant across\nevery store, which only works if every store carries the key. Per-tenant encryption keys turn the backup\nproblem into deleting one key (crypto-shredding).\n\n### Tenant-aware configuration\nTenants differ in more than data. A tenant record carries the model (or model tier) it is allowed to use,\nits system prompt and prompt version, enabled features and tools, rate and token quotas, retention\nperiod, data-residency region and allowed connectors. Loading this once per request and passing it\nexplicitly, rather than sprinkling `if tenant == \"acme\"` through the code, is what keeps a hundred\ntenants from becoming a hundred code paths.\n\n### Fairness and noisy neighbours\nPooled resources need fair-share mechanisms *because* the model-serving queue and the database do not\nknow tenants exist. Per-tenant rate limits and concurrency caps at the gateway, per-tenant queues or\nweighted scheduling for background work, and token budgets per billing period keep one tenant's batch\njob from turning into everyone's latency incident.\n\n### Metering and cost allocation\nEvery LLM call records input tokens, output tokens, model and tenant. This exists because token spend is\nthe dominant variable cost of an AI product and it is invisible without per-tenant attribution; it feeds\nbilling, quota enforcement and the margin analysis that decides which tier a tenant belongs in.\n\n### What is specific to AI systems\nTenant identity has to reach places a classic SaaS never had: the retrieval filter (the vector query\nmust carry the tenant key, and the index must enforce it rather than relying on a post-filter), the\nprompt assembly (tenant-specific instructions and no cross-tenant few-shot examples), the semantic and\nprompt caches (keys include the tenant, or a cache hit becomes a leak), agent memory and tool\ncredentials (a tenant's connector token is used only for that tenant's runs), fine-tuned adapters (a\nLoRA trained on one tenant's data is that tenant's asset), and the evaluation sets (per-tenant golden\nsets, because quality differs by corpus).","example":"A FastAPI slice that resolves the tenant from a verified token, stores it in a context variable so\ndeeper layers and `asyncio` tasks inherit it, scopes retrieval and configuration by it, and meters\ntoken usage against it. Nothing downstream takes `tenant_id` from user input. One detail matters: the\ndependency is `async def`. FastAPI runs a plain `def` dependency in a worker thread with a *copy* of\nthe context, so a `ContextVar.set` made there is silently discarded before the endpoint runs.\n\n```python\nfrom contextvars import ContextVar\nimport jwt                                    # PyJWT\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header\n\ntenant_ctx: ContextVar[str] = ContextVar(\"tenant_id\")\napp = FastAPI()\n\nasync def current_tenant(authorization: str = Header(...)) -> str:\n    token = authorization.removeprefix(\"Bearer \")\n    claims = jwt.decode(token, PUBLIC_KEY, algorithms=[\"RS256\"], audience=\"assistant-api\")\n    tenant_id = claims[\"tenant_id\"]           # minted by the IdP, never read from the body\n    tenant_ctx.set(tenant_id)                 # async dep: same context as the endpoint and its tasks\n    return tenant_id                          # (a sync `def` dep would set it in a thread's copy)\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(req: ChatRequest, tenant_id: str = Depends(current_tenant)):\n    cfg = await tenant_config.load(tenant_id)              # model, prompt version, quotas, region\n    await quotas.check(tenant_id, cfg)                     # fail fast before spending tokens\n    passages = await vector_store.search(\n        req.message, top_k=8, filter={\"tenant_id\": tenant_id}   # enforced in the index, not after\n    )\n    result = await llm.complete(\n        model=cfg.model, system=cfg.system_prompt, context=passages, user=req.message,\n        cache_key=f\"{tenant_id}:{cfg.prompt_version}\",          # cache never crosses tenants\n    )\n    await meter.record(tenant_id, model=cfg.model,\n                       input_tokens=result.usage.input_tokens,\n                       output_tokens=result.usage.output_tokens)\n    return {\"answer\": result.text}\n\n# Work handed to a queue must carry the tenant explicitly: context does not cross processes.\nasync def enqueue_ingestion(doc_id: str):\n    await queue.send({\"tenant_id\": tenant_ctx.get(), \"doc_id\": doc_id})\n```","tradeoffs":["Pooling maximises utilisation and minimises operations, but isolation rests on software correctness; that flips to bridge or silo when a tenant's contract or regulator demands physical separation.","Siloing gives the strongest isolation and simplest per-tenant customisation, but cost grows with tenant count and upgrades become fleets; it is sustainable only for a small, high-value tier.","Per-tenant configuration makes customisation cheap, but every knob multiplies the test matrix; it pays off when tenants differ in policy (model, prompt, tools) rather than in code.","Carrying tenant identity through every layer costs discipline and a little plumbing, but it is far cheaper than the forensic work of finding which code path lost it after a leak.","A shared model-serving fleet amortises GPUs across tenants, but one tenant's long-context traffic can dominate the KV cache; dedicated capacity becomes worth it when a tenant's load is both large and latency-sensitive."],"pitfalls":["Accepting `tenant_id` from a request body or query string; the symptom is a penetration test, or a curious customer, reading another tenant's conversations by editing one field.","Forgetting the tenant key on the vector search or on a semantic cache; the symptom is an answer that cites a document the user's organisation never uploaded.","Letting tenant context evaporate at a process or thread boundary (queues, cron jobs, thread pools, a sync FastAPI dependency that sets a `ContextVar` in a worker thread); the symptom is background jobs or handlers that run as \"no tenant\" and either fail or write into the wrong partition.","Hard-coding exceptions for a big customer in the code; the symptom is a release that breaks only for them, discovered in production because the test suite has no such tenant.","Offboarding by deleting database rows only; the symptom is a departed customer's document resurfacing from the vector index or a cached answer, or a deletion audit finding it in traces and eval samples.","Measuring cost per model but not per tenant; the symptom is a tenant on a flat plan quietly consuming most of the token budget with no alarm until the invoice."],"when":"Design for multi-tenancy from the first commit of any product that will serve more than a handful of\ncustomers, because retrofitting tenant identity into an existing schema, index and prompt pipeline is\none of the most expensive migrations there is. Choose the sharing model per resource, starting pooled\nfor compute and bridge or pooled for data, and write down what promotes a tenant to a dedicated tier.\nKeep single-tenant deployment as an explicit product option for regulated buyers rather than an\naccident. Measure per-tenant latency percentiles, error rates, token spend and quota hits; if you cannot\nbreak a dashboard down by tenant, you cannot run the product. In interviews, expect to be asked how the\ntenant ID reaches the vector store and the cache, and what happens when one tenant uploads a million\ndocuments at once.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Threads the tenant through request context, queue messages, retrieval filters, cache keys and meter calls, and writes tests that run as two tenants and assert they never see each other.","architect":"Decides the pool/bridge/silo model per resource and per tier, the quota and fairness policy, and the promotion path from shared to dedicated capacity.","fde":"Maps a customer's requirements (residency, SSO, dedicated GPUs, retention) onto the tenant configuration and tier model, and knows when the honest answer is a single-tenant install."},"check":{"q":"Your pooled RAG product has 400 tenants. One enterprise tenant uploads 2 million documents\novernight and complains the next morning that chat is slow for them, while three small tenants also open\nlatency tickets. Diagnose the mechanism and propose a fix at each layer.","a":"The bulk ingestion flooded shared resources with no tenant-aware fairness. The embedding queue was\nFIFO, so the enterprise tenant's two million chunks sat in front of everyone else's small jobs; the\nembedding model server and the database write path were saturated; and the vector index was being\nrebuilt or compacted under load, slowing searches for all tenants. Fixes: per-tenant queues with weighted\nfair scheduling and a per-tenant ingestion concurrency cap; separate worker pools or at least priority\nfor interactive traffic over batch; an ingestion quota per hour in the tenant configuration with a\nback-pressure response rather than silent queuing; and, for a tenant of this size, a bridge or silo tier\nfor the index so its compaction cannot affect others. The dashboard should show queue depth and p95\nlatency per tenant so this is seen before the tickets."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where should a multi-tenant API derive the tenant identity for a request?","options":["From a `tenant_id` field in the JSON body, validated against a list of known tenants","From the verified credential (token claims, API key record) at the edge, then propagated","From the first row returned by the user's query","From the subdomain, with the body value taking precedence if both exist"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The tenant must come from something the caller cannot forge. A body field is attacker-controlled even when validated against known tenants, because the attacker simply names another valid tenant."},{"q":"A team uses a shared semantic cache keyed only by the normalised question text. What is the risk?","options":["Cache hit rate will be too low to justify the cache","Embeddings will drift between tenants","One tenant can receive an answer generated from another tenant's documents","The cache will store duplicate entries per tenant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cached answer embeds the context it was generated from; without the tenant in the key, a similar question from a different tenant returns content grounded in someone else's data."},{"q":"Which statement about pool, bridge and silo is correct?","options":["Silo is always the right choice for data because it is most secure","Pool must be used for every resource or the system is not multi-tenant","The model is chosen per resource and per tier, and tenants may move between tiers","Bridge means one database per tenant on dedicated hardware"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The models describe degrees of sharing for a single resource. Real systems pool compute, bridge or pool data, and silo for the tier that pays for it; bridge is partitioned structure on shared infrastructure, not dedicated hardware."}]},{"key":"system-design/tenant-isolation","sources":["Official docs: PostgreSQL (row security policies)","Official docs: AWS Well-Architected SaaS Lens","OWASP, 2025 — Top 10 for LLM Applications"],"why":"Multi-tenancy establishes a tenant identity and tells every layer to respect it. Isolation is what you\nbuild because some layer, some day, will not. A developer adds an admin report that forgets the filter;\nan ORM relationship lazy-loads across the tenant column; a background job runs with no tenant in context\nand queries everything; a cache key omits the tenant; a retrieval call passes the filter as a soft\nre-ranking hint instead of a hard constraint. Each is an ordinary mistake, and in a classic SaaS it\nproduces a wrong row on a screen. In an LLM product the wrong row becomes part of a prompt, and the model\nhelpfully summarises another company's contract to the user.\n\nThe naive answer, \"we filter everything in the application layer and we have tests\", fails because it\nhas a single point of failure: the discipline of every engineer on every code path forever. Reviews\ncatch most omissions; the leak comes from the one they miss, and it is discovered by a customer. The\nsecond naive answer, \"give every tenant their own database\", is isolation by cost: correct but\nunaffordable at scale, and it still leaves compute, caches, model servers, logs and agent credentials\nshared.\n\nIsolation therefore means **defence in depth**: at least two independent mechanisms between any tenant\nand any other tenant's data, performance and secrets, such that the application being wrong is not\nsufficient to cause a leak. It also means being able to *prove* that to a security reviewer, which is why\nthe mechanisms must be enforceable and testable rather than conventions.","how":"### The dimensions you are isolating\nIsolation is not one property. A review should walk through each dimension and name the control:\n\n1. **Data at rest and in query.** Who can read which rows, documents, embeddings and files.\n2. **Performance.** One tenant's load must not become another's latency; noisy-neighbour control.\n3. **Secrets and credentials.** A tenant's connector tokens, API keys and encryption keys are usable only\n   in that tenant's work.\n4. **Execution.** Agent tool calls and generated code run in a sandbox scoped to the tenant.\n5. **Configuration and lifecycle.** Prompt versions, model choices and feature flags are per tenant, and\n   a tenant can be migrated, suspended or deleted on its own.\n6. **Telemetry.** Logs, traces and evaluation samples carry the tenant tag and are access-controlled by\n   it, because support staff reading traces is itself a cross-tenant read.\n7. **Residency.** Data and inference stay in the tenant's required region or environment.\n\n### Layer 1: the database enforces the filter\nWith a pooled schema, the strongest cheap control is **row-level security**: the database attaches a\npredicate to every query on a table, and the predicate reads the tenant from a per-transaction setting\nthat the application sets right after it verifies the caller. This exists *because* it moves the filter\nfrom \"every query\" to \"one policy\": a forgotten `WHERE` still returns only the current tenant's rows.\nTwo details make or break it. The application role must not be a superuser or hold `BYPASSRLS`, and the\npolicy must apply to the table owner (`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`), otherwise migrations and admin\nscripts silently bypass it. And the tenant setting must be transaction-local (`SET LOCAL`), because\nwith transaction-mode connection pooling a session-level setting leaks to whichever request gets the\nconnection next. Bridge and silo models get the same property structurally: a schema or database per\ntenant, with per-tenant database credentials so a connection can only ever reach one tenant.\n\nBe precise about what RLS defends against. It catches the *forgotten* filter, because the tenant is set\nonce, right after authentication, and every later query in the transaction inherits it. It does not stop\na *compromised* application: SQL injection, or any code path that can issue `SET LOCAL`, can name another\ntenant, because the setting is just a string the database trusts. That threat is what per-tenant\ncredentials (bridge or silo) address, and it is why RLS is one layer rather than the whole answer. Index\nfor it as well: the policy adds `tenant_id = …` to every query, so composite indexes should lead with\n`tenant_id`, or large tables degrade into scans that the policy then filters.\n\n### Layer 2: retrieval and caches are partitioned, not post-filtered\nVector search is where AI products most often leak. The tenant key must be a **hard pre-filter** or a\n**separate namespace**, never a re-ranking step applied after top-k, because a post-filter on a\ncross-tenant top-k can return fewer results than requested, which tempts someone to widen the search,\nand because the unfiltered candidates have already crossed the boundary in memory and logs. With\npgvector the row policy from Layer 1 runs inside the database, so no other tenant's row ever reaches the\napplication; with an approximate index (HNSW, IVFFlat) the engine still filters the index's candidate\nlist, so isolation holds but small tenants can get fewer than k rows. Iterative index scans (pgvector 0.8\nand later) mitigate that recall loss, and a partition or partial index per large tenant is the structural\nfix. With a dedicated vector database use a collection or namespace per tenant for larger tenants and mandatory metadata filtering for pooled ones,\nand make the filter non-optional in the retrieval client's signature. Semantic caches, prompt caches and\nagent memory stores must include the tenant in the key for the same reason: a cache hit is a read.\n\n### Layer 3: credentials are scoped per tenant and per run\nAgent tools that reach into a customer's systems (CRM, ticketing, email) use that tenant's stored\ncredentials, fetched from a secrets manager by tenant ID at run time, injected into the tool adapter for\nthat run only, and never placed in the prompt. Per-tenant encryption keys for stored documents mean\nthat even a storage-level leak yields ciphertext, and deleting a tenant becomes deleting a key.\nModel-provider keys can also be split per tenant or per tier so that a leaked key has a bounded blast\nradius and spend can be attributed.\n\n### Layer 4: performance isolation\nQuotas and fair scheduling at the gateway and in the job system cap what one tenant can consume:\nrequests per second, concurrent generations, tokens per period, ingestion jobs in flight. Dedicated\nworker pools or model-serving capacity for the top tier isolate the heaviest tenants physically. The\nindicator that this layer is missing is simple: a latency incident whose root cause is one tenant's\ntraffic.\n\n### Layer 5: verification\nIsolation that is not tested decays. Two tests earn their keep. A **cross-tenant canary**: each tenant's\ncorpus contains a unique marker document, and an automated probe runs representative questions as every\nother tenant and fails on any marker appearing in retrieved passages or generated answers. And\n**negative access tests** in CI: every data-access function is called with tenant A's context against\ntenant B's identifiers and must return nothing or raise. Combined with log review for queries executed\nwithout a tenant setting, this turns isolation into something you can show an auditor.","example":"PostgreSQL row-level security on a pooled `documents` table that also holds embeddings. The policy uses\na transaction-local setting. If the application forgets to set it, `current_setting(..., true)` returns\nNULL on a fresh connection but an empty string on a connection that has used the setting before (the\nplaceholder persists for the session), so the policy wraps it in `NULLIF`; either way the predicate is\nNULL and the query returns no rows. The failure mode is \"empty\", not \"leak\". The policy predicate is\napplied as a filter on the scan, so a forgotten filter in the vector query still cannot cross tenants;\nwith an approximate index (HNSW, IVFFlat) the filter may leave fewer than k rows, which is a recall\nproblem, not an isolation problem.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n    id         bigserial PRIMARY KEY,\n    tenant_id  uuid NOT NULL,\n    body       text NOT NULL,\n    embedding  vector(1536)          -- pgvector\n);\n\nALTER TABLE documents ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE documents FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;   -- owner is not exempt (migrations, admin scripts)\n\nCREATE POLICY tenant_rows ON documents\n    USING      (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid)   -- reads\n    WITH CHECK (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid);  -- inserts/updates\n\n-- The application connects as a role with no BYPASSRLS and no ownership of the table.\nCREATE ROLE app_rw LOGIN NOBYPASSRLS;\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON documents TO app_rw;\n\n-- Per request, inside one transaction (safe with transaction-mode pooling):\nBEGIN;\nSET LOCAL app.tenant_id = '2b6f1c1e-6c0a-4f7e-9d7e-1b8c3b2d9a10';\nSELECT id, body\nFROM   documents\nORDER  BY embedding <=> $1      -- cosine distance to the query embedding\nLIMIT  8;                        -- policy filters the scan, so the 8 rows are within-tenant\nCOMMIT;\n-- Same SELECT with no SET LOCAL: the predicate is NULL, so 0 rows come back (fails closed).\n-- INSERT naming another tenant_id: ERROR: new row violates row-level security policy for table \"documents\"\n```","tradeoffs":["Database-enforced policies catch application mistakes, but they add a per-query predicate and make \"run this query across all tenants\" an explicit privileged path; that is a feature until an analytics team needs cross-tenant reporting, which should then go through a separate replica and role.","Namespaces or collections per tenant give clean separation and per-tenant re-indexing, but many small collections waste memory and connections; pooled indexes with mandatory filters win for the long tail.","Per-tenant encryption keys make deletion and breach containment simple, but key management becomes part of every read path; the cost is justified when contracts require crypto-shredding or residency.","Dedicated capacity for a tenant removes noisy-neighbour risk, but it reintroduces idle cost; it is worth it only when a tenant's load is both heavy and latency-sensitive, or when they pay for it.","Canary and negative tests slow the suite slightly and need per-tenant fixtures, but they are the only evidence of isolation that survives a refactor."],"pitfalls":["Enabling RLS but connecting as the table owner or a superuser; the symptom is that tests pass, and a manual check with the production role shows every tenant's rows.","Using session-level `SET` behind a transaction-pooling proxy; the symptom is intermittent, unreproducible cases where a request sees a previous request's tenant.","Applying the tenant filter after top-k in vector search; the symptom is queries returning fewer than k results for small tenants, followed by someone raising k to compensate.","Putting customer connector tokens into the system prompt so the agent can \"use them\"; the symptom is a token appearing in a trace, an eval sample or a model response.","Trusting the vector database's metadata filter without making it mandatory in the client; the symptom is one new call site that omits the filter and passes review because the code \"looks like the others\".","Reading through views or `SECURITY DEFINER` functions owned by a superuser or a `BYPASSRLS` role; the symptom is a reporting view that returns every tenant's rows although the base table has a policy, because a view checks policies as its owner unless it is created with `security_invoker` (PostgreSQL 15+).","Assuming RLS hides everything; unique and foreign-key checks bypass row security, so \"this email is already registered\" on a globally unique column tells tenant A that a value exists in tenant B. The symptom is an enumeration finding in a pen test; scope unique constraints to `(tenant_id, …)`.","Logging full prompts to a shared log store without tenant tags and access control; the symptom is support engineers reading tenant data they are not cleared for, which is itself a reportable breach."],"when":"Every multi-tenant AI system needs at least two independent isolation layers for data and one for\nperformance from the first production tenant; the question is how far up the ladder each tier goes.\nPooled tenants: RLS or equivalent plus mandatory retrieval filters plus quotas. Mid tier: add per-tenant\nnamespaces and encryption keys. Enterprise or regulated: bridge or silo data, dedicated capacity, and\nper-tenant credentials end to end. Measure it: count queries executed without a tenant setting (should be\nzero), run the cross-tenant canary continuously, and track per-tenant latency so noisy neighbours are\nseen as a graph rather than a ticket. In security reviews and interviews, the expected depth is naming\neach layer, explaining what failure it catches that the layer above misses, and showing the test that\nproves it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets the tenant in the transaction, makes the retrieval client refuse calls without a tenant filter, keys every cache by tenant, and writes the negative access tests.","architect":"Defines the isolation ladder per tier, the roles and policies in the database, the key-management model, and signs off that two independent layers exist for every data path.","fde":"Walks a customer's security team through the controls with evidence (policies, role grants, canary results), and translates their requirements into the tier that actually meets them."},"check":{"q":"A security reviewer says, \"Your API filters by tenant in every query, and you have unit tests. Why\nis that not enough for our data?\" Give the answer a strong architect would give, including what you\nwould add and how you would prove it works.","a":"Application filtering has one point of failure: a developer forgetting it on one path, and in an\nLLM product that missed row ends up in a prompt. I would add a second, independent layer that holds\neven when the code is wrong: row-level security in PostgreSQL with a transaction-local tenant setting,\nwith the application role denied `BYPASSRLS` and the policy forced on the owner, so a missing filter\nreturns nothing rather than everything. Retrieval gets a mandatory tenant filter in the client signature\nor a namespace per tenant, and every cache key includes the tenant. For evidence, I would show the\npolicy definitions and role grants, a CI suite that calls every data function as tenant A against\ntenant B's IDs, and a continuously running cross-tenant canary that plants marker documents and alerts\nif any marker is ever retrieved or generated for another tenant."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `SET LOCAL app.tenant_id` preferred over a session-level `SET` when using row-level security?","options":["It is faster because it avoids writing to the catalog","It is scoped to the transaction, so a pooled connection cannot carry one tenant's setting into the next request","It encrypts the setting in memory","Session-level settings cannot be read by `current_setting`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transaction-mode poolers hand the same connection to different requests; a session setting would persist across them and make a later request run as the earlier tenant. Transaction scope removes that failure mode."},{"q":"What is the main reason to make the tenant filter a hard pre-filter in vector search rather than filtering the top-k results afterwards?","options":["Pre-filtering produces better-calibrated similarity scores","Post-filtering is not supported by any vector database","Post-filtering lets cross-tenant candidates cross the boundary and can return fewer than k results, inviting a wider search","Pre-filtering is required for cosine distance to work"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cross-tenant neighbours already fetched into memory and logs are a leak surface, and small tenants see depleted result sets after filtering, which pushes engineers to widen the query. A hard filter or a namespace prevents both."},{"q":"Which control best limits the blast radius if one tenant's connector token is leaked through an agent trace?","options":["Rotating the model-provider API key","Reducing the model's temperature","Per-tenant credentials fetched at run time and injected only into that tenant's tool adapter, never into prompts","Enabling row-level security on the conversations table"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A leaked token is bounded to one tenant only if credentials are scoped per tenant and kept out of the prompt and traces. Row-level security protects rows, not secrets, and the provider key is unrelated."}]},{"key":"system-design/c4-diagrams","sources":["Brown — The C4 model for visualising software architecture (c4model.com)","Official docs: Structurizr DSL"],"why":"Most architecture diagrams fail at the same thing: nobody can tell what level they are looking at. One\npicture shows a Kubernetes cluster, a \"Users\" stick figure, a PostgreSQL cylinder, an `AuthService`\nclass and a team name, connected by unlabelled lines that may mean \"calls\", \"deploys to\" or \"is owned\nby\". Every review then spends its first twenty minutes establishing what the boxes are. UML tried to fix\nthis with a rigorous notation that almost nobody learned, so teams fell back to free-form whiteboards\nand the ambiguity returned.\n\nC4 borrows the idea of a map. A country map, a city map and a street map use different symbols and a\ndifferent density of detail, and you know which one you are holding. C4 fixes four zoom levels and a\ntiny notation, so a reader knows that a box on a Container diagram is a deployable unit and a box on a\nComponent diagram is a part of one. Everything else, tooling, layout, colours, is left free.\n\nAI systems raise the stakes. The first question from security, legal and the customer's architects is\n\"where does our data go, and which parts talk to the model provider?\" A diagram that cannot answer that\nin one glance costs you a review cycle. C4's insistence on labelling technology and the direction and\nmeaning of each flow is exactly what that audience needs.","how":"### The four levels\n1. **System Context.** Your system is one box. A *software system* here is the thing one team builds\n   and owns; anything owned by someone else, including another team's platform, is drawn as external.\n   Around the box are the people who use it and the external systems it depends on: the identity provider, the CRM it reads tickets from, the hosted model\n   provider. No technology inside the box yet. This exists because the first thing any audience needs is\n   the system's place in the world, and it is the diagram you show executives and customers.\n2. **Container.** Zoom into the system box. A *container* here is a separately runnable or deployable\n   unit: the web app, the API, the ingestion worker, the database, the vector store, the queue, the model\n   server, the LLM gateway. The term predates Docker and is wider than it; a database is a container, a\n   Docker image is not necessarily one container. Each shows its technology (\"FastAPI\", \"PostgreSQL +\n   pgvector\", \"vLLM\"). This is the diagram engineers and operators live in.\n3. **Component.** Zoom into one container and show its major structural parts and responsibilities:\n   inside the API, an authentication middleware, a tenancy resolver, an orchestration component, a\n   retrieval client, a guardrail layer, a usage meter. Components map to modules or packages, not to\n   classes. Draw this only for containers whose internals matter to someone other than their authors.\n4. **Code.** Class or entity diagrams for one component. Generate them from the code if you need them\n   at all; hand-maintained code diagrams are the first to rot.\n\nThree supplementary diagrams reuse the same elements: a **System Landscape** for several systems, a\n**Dynamic** diagram that numbers the arrows to tell the story of one use case (a RAG request: resolve\ntenant, embed query, search, assemble prompt, call gateway, stream, meter), and a **Deployment** diagram\nthat places containers onto infrastructure (region, VPC, cluster, GPU node pool, the customer's network\nversus yours). The deployment view is the one forward-deployed engineers draw most, because it is where\ntrust boundaries and data residency become visible.\n\n### The notation rules that do the work\nEach rule exists to remove one specific ambiguity:\n- Every element carries **name, type, technology and a one-line description**, so a reader never has to\n  guess whether \"Gateway\" is an API gateway, an LLM gateway or a network device.\n- Every relationship is a **single-direction arrow labelled with intent and, where useful, protocol**\n  (\"sends completion requests to, HTTPS/JSON\"), so data-flow direction is never inferred.\n- Every diagram has a **title stating its level and scope**, and a **legend** if colours or shapes carry\n  meaning.\n- Keep a diagram to roughly twenty elements. Past that, the right move is a new diagram one level down,\n  not a smaller font.\n- Colour and shape are consistent across diagrams: people, your containers, external systems and data\n  stores each look the same everywhere.\n\n### Diagrams as code\nThe C4 authors' tooling, Structurizr, separates the **model** (elements and relationships, defined once)\nfrom **views** (which subset to draw at which level). This exists because consistency is the hard part:\nwhen the model is one source of truth, renaming a container updates every diagram, and a relationship\ncannot appear on the Container view but be missing from the Context view. C4-PlantUML and Mermaid's C4\nsyntax give the same notation in text form with less model discipline. Text diagrams live in the\nrepository, change in pull requests next to the code they describe, and can be regenerated in CI, which\nis the practical answer to diagram rot.\n\n### What to make visible in an AI system\nTreat the model provider as an external system on the Context diagram, because that arrow is a data\negress a reviewer must see. Show the gateway, the vector store and the evaluation pipeline as containers,\nbecause each is where a decision (routing, isolation, release gating) is enforced. On the Deployment\nview, draw the boundary between the customer's environment and yours and mark which flows carry\ncustomer documents or prompts. A Dynamic diagram of one request is usually worth more than a Component\ndiagram of everything.\n\nC4 has no first-class trust-boundary element, and it does not pretend to be a threat model. Draw\nboundaries with deployment nodes (the customer's VPC, your cloud account, the provider's region) or with\nnamed groups, and state in the legend what crossing one means. A security team running a STRIDE-style\nreview will still want a data-flow diagram; a good Deployment view gives them most of it.","example":"A Structurizr DSL workspace for a support assistant. One model feeds three views, so the Context\ndiagram for stakeholders, the Container diagram for engineers and the Dynamic view of one request cannot\ndisagree. The model provider is external, the vector-capable database is tagged so it renders as a\ncylinder, and every relationship states intent and protocol. The dynamic view numbers the steps of one\nreply in order, and Structurizr checks each step against the relationships in the model, so if someone\ndeletes the `api -> gateway` arrow the workspace fails to build instead of the request story silently\ngoing stale. Notice also\nwhat the Context view reveals once rendered: two arrows leave the system, to the CRM and to the model\nprovider, and the second is the data egress a privacy review will ask about first.\n\n```\nworkspace \"Support Copilot\" \"Drafts ticket replies grounded in the knowledge base\" {\n    model {\n        agent   = person \"Support Agent\" \"Answers customer tickets\"\n        crm     = softwareSystem \"CRM\" \"System of record for tickets and articles\" \"External\"\n        llm     = softwareSystem \"Model Provider\" \"Hosted LLM API\" \"External\"\n        copilot = softwareSystem \"Support Copilot\" \"Suggests grounded replies\" {\n            web     = container \"Web App\" \"Agent-facing UI\" \"React\"\n            api     = container \"API\" \"Auth, tenancy, orchestration, guardrails\" \"FastAPI\"\n            worker  = container \"Ingestion Worker\" \"Chunks and embeds articles\" \"Python\"\n            gateway = container \"LLM Gateway\" \"Routing, quotas, prompt cache\" \"Python\"\n            db      = container \"Database\" \"Tenants, chunks, embeddings, feedback\" \"PostgreSQL + pgvector\" \"Database\"\n        }\n        agent   -> web     \"Uses\"\n        web     -> api     \"Sends chat requests to\" \"HTTPS/JSON\"\n        api     -> db      \"Reads and writes\" \"SQL\"\n        api     -> gateway \"Requests completions from\" \"HTTPS\"\n        gateway -> llm     \"Sends prompts and receives completions\" \"HTTPS\"\n        worker  -> crm     \"Pulls articles from\" \"REST\"\n        worker  -> db      \"Writes chunks and embeddings to\" \"SQL\"\n    }\n    views {\n        systemContext copilot \"Context\"    { include * autolayout lr }\n        container     copilot \"Containers\" { include * autolayout lr }\n        dynamic copilot \"DraftReply\" \"One grounded reply\" {\n            agent -> web \"Opens a ticket and asks for a draft\"\n            web -> api \"Sends ticket text\"\n            api -> db \"Fetches top-8 chunks for this tenant\"\n            api -> gateway \"Requests a draft grounded in the chunks\"\n            gateway -> llm \"Forwards prompt; streams completion\"\n            autolayout lr\n        }\n        styles {\n            element \"External\" { background #999999 color #ffffff }\n            element \"Database\" { shape Cylinder }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","tradeoffs":["Fixed abstraction levels make diagrams instantly readable to anyone who knows C4, but they constrain you when a system does not decompose neatly; the fix is usually a Dynamic or Deployment view, not a bespoke notation.","Diagrams as code stay in sync with the repository and survive reviews, but auto-layout rarely looks as good as a hand-arranged picture; hand layout wins only for a diagram that will be shown far more often than it changes.","A single model with many views guarantees consistency, but it demands that someone own the model; without that owner, C4-PlantUML files per diagram are easier to keep alive.","C4 is deliberately lighter than UML or ArchiMate, which makes it learnable in an afternoon, but it says nothing about behaviour beyond numbered arrows or about formal semantics; for enterprise-wide capability mapping or protocol-level design, those heavier notations (or sequence diagrams) earn their cost.","Component diagrams explain a container's internals, but they rot fastest and are read least; draw them for the containers whose structure others depend on and skip the rest."],"pitfalls":["Mixing levels in one diagram; the symptom is a picture that shows \"Kubernetes\" and \"the retry helper class\" at the same time, and a review that spends its time asking what the boxes are.","Unlabelled or double-headed arrows; the symptom is a security reviewer asking \"which direction does customer data flow here?\" and nobody being sure.","Reading *container* as Docker container; the symptom is a diagram with a managed database and a hosted model provider missing because \"they are not containers\".","Leaving the model provider off the Context diagram because it is \"just an API\"; the symptom is a procurement or privacy review discovering the data egress late.","Diagrams in a slide deck disconnected from the code; the symptom is a Container view that still shows a service deleted two quarters ago.","Drawing everything; the symptom is a forty-box Container diagram nobody can read, instead of a Container view plus two Dynamic views of the requests that matter."],"when":"Draw a Context and a Container diagram for every system that more than one team or one organisation\nneeds to understand, and keep them in the repository as text. Add a Deployment view the moment the\nsystem crosses a trust boundary, which for an AI product is usually immediately (a hosted model provider,\na customer VPC). Add Dynamic views for the two or three request paths that reviewers and new engineers\nask about most, typically a chat request and the ingestion flow. Skip Component and Code diagrams unless\na specific audience needs them. A useful test: can a new engineer, a security reviewer and a customer\narchitect each find the diagram meant for them and answer their first question without you in the\nroom? In interviews, sketching a Context then a Container diagram is a clean way to structure any\nsystem-design answer, and naming technologies and flow directions as you go signals the discipline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps the Container and Dynamic diagrams in the repo as code, updates them in the same pull request that adds a queue or a model server, and uses them to onboard teammates.","architect":"Owns the model behind the views, decides which containers and boundaries exist, and uses the Context and Deployment diagrams as the shared picture in design and security reviews.","fde":"Produces the Deployment and Context views for each customer environment, marking where customer data crosses into the vendor's systems, which is often the document that unblocks procurement."},"check":{"q":"A customer's security team asks for \"an architecture diagram\" of your RAG product before approving\na pilot in their VPC. Which C4 views do you send, what must each make explicit, and why not just one\npicture?","a":"Three views, each for a different question. A System Context diagram showing their users, your\nsystem as one box, and every external system including the hosted model provider, because their first\nquestion is which third parties touch their data. A Container diagram naming each deployable with its\ntechnology, API, worker, gateway, database with vector extension, model server if self-hosted, with\narrows labelled by intent and protocol, so they can see where prompts and documents are stored and\nprocessed. And a Deployment diagram placing those containers inside their VPC versus your environment,\nwith the trust boundary drawn and the flows that cross it marked. One picture would have to mix the\nthree levels, which is exactly what makes reviewers unsure what each box is and which way data flows."},"quiz":[{"q":"In the C4 model, what does \"container\" mean?","options":["A Docker or OCI container image","A separately runnable or deployable unit such as an application, database, queue or model server","A bounded context from domain-driven design","A cloud account or Kubernetes namespace"],"answer":1,"explanation":"C4's container predates Docker and is broader: anything that runs or is deployed on its own, including managed databases and queues. Bounded contexts and namespaces are different concepts that may or may not align with containers."},{"q":"Why does the C4 model insist on labelling each relationship with its intent and direction?","options":["So that diagram tools can auto-layout more efficiently","Because UML requires it for sequence diagrams","So readers never have to infer which way data flows or what one element does to another","To make diagrams compatible with Structurizr's licensing model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An unlabelled line can mean calls, deploys to, reads from or owns, and a two-headed arrow hides who initiates. In security and data-flow reviews that ambiguity is precisely the question being asked."},{"q":"Which view best shows a customer where its data crosses from its environment into the vendor's?","options":["A Component diagram of the API","A Code diagram generated from the data model","A Deployment diagram placing containers on infrastructure with the trust boundary drawn","A System Landscape diagram of all the vendor's products"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only the deployment view maps containers onto environments, so it is the one where \"inside your VPC\" versus \"in our cloud\" and the flows between them become visible. Component and code views are below that level of concern."}]},{"key":"system-design/adrs","sources":["Nygard, 2011 — Documenting Architecture Decisions","Official docs: MADR (Markdown Any Decision Records)"],"why":"Every system is the sum of its decisions, and almost none of them are written down. Six months later a\nnew engineer finds that the vector store is PostgreSQL with pgvector rather than a dedicated database,\nthat the context window is capped at 32k tokens, and that every LLM call goes through a gateway. Two bad\nthings can happen. **Blind acceptance:** nobody dares change the choice because nobody knows what it was\nprotecting, so the system calcifies around constraints that may no longer exist. **Blind reversal:** the\nengineer \"fixes\" it, and the incident that caused the original decision happens again, because the\nreason lived in someone's head and they left.\n\nWiki pages do not solve this. They describe the current state, not the decision, and they are edited in\nplace, so the reasoning and the alternatives vanish. Design documents are closer but are proposals,\nlong, and written before the decision; they rarely record what was finally chosen or why the other\noptions lost. Commit messages record *what* changed, not what was weighed.\n\nAI systems make the problem acute because the landscape moves faster than the system. \"We chose model X\nbecause it scored best on our eval\" is true on the day and unknowable a year later unless the eval\nversion, the scores, the prices and the assumptions were captured. Without that, the team cannot tell\nwhether a decision is still right or merely old. The ADR is the cheapest artefact that makes decisions\ninspectable, reversible on purpose, and reviewable like code.","how":"### What deserves one\nThe test is architectural significance: would reversing this be expensive, or would someone later need\nto know why it was made? Choosing the vector store, the tenancy model, the gateway, the retrieval\nstrategy, the model tier for a workload, the evaluation gate for releases, the data-residency approach:\nyes. Choosing a logging library's formatter: no. A team producing an ADR a week is probably recording\ntasks; a team with three ADRs after a year is probably not recording decisions.\n\n### Anatomy\nNygard's original template has five parts, and each exists for a reason:\n- **Title and number.** `ADR-0007: Serve v1 through a hosted model API behind our gateway`. Numbers give\n  a stable reference; the title states the decision, not the topic.\n- **Status.** Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated, or Superseded by ADR-NNNN. Status is the only field that\n  changes after acceptance, *because* the record must stay an honest snapshot of what was decided.\n- **Context.** The forces at play, stated neutrally: the non-functional requirements, constraints, team\n  size, deadlines, costs, risks, and what is uncertain. This section is what lets a future reader judge\n  whether the decision still applies, so it must describe conditions, not advocate.\n- **Decision.** \"We will …\", in full sentences, including what is explicitly *not* being done.\n- **Consequences.** What becomes easier, what becomes harder, new risks, follow-up work. Every real\n  decision has negative consequences; a record without them was written to justify, not to decide.\n\nMADR (Markdown Any Decision Records) extends this with **decision drivers**, **considered options**\nwith pros and cons, a **decision outcome** with its justification, and a **confirmation** section\ndescribing how compliance will be checked. The extra structure is worth it when the decision had real\nalternatives, which for architecture is nearly always. A one-line **Y-statement** (\"In the context of\n…, facing …, we decided for … to achieve …, accepting …\") is a useful summary at the top.\n\n### Lifecycle and mechanics\nADRs live in the repository, typically `docs/adr/NNNN-title.md`, next to the code they govern, because\nthat is where engineers look and where pull-request review already happens. One is opened as\n*Proposed* in a pull request, argued in the review, and becomes *Accepted* when merged. After that it\nis never edited except for status; changing a decision means writing a new ADR that supersedes the old\none and linking both ways. This immutability is the feature: the history of why the system looks the\nway it does is the chain of records. Keep each to one or two pages and write it at decision time; an\nADR reconstructed months later is a story, not a record. Link ADRs from the C4 diagrams and README so\nthe \"why\" is one click from the \"what\". A longer design doc or RFC often comes first: it explores the\nspace and may run to ten pages; the ADR is the one-page outcome that links back to it. Tooling is\noptional, since a numbered Markdown file is enough; tools such as adr-tools or log4brains only script the\nnumbering, linking and index.\n\n### Who decides, and how a decision stays true\nAn ADR also records *decision rights*: the Deciders line says who could accept it. Some teams route every\nsignificant decision through an architect; others use an advice process, in which anyone may decide after\nconsulting the people affected and those with expertise, and the ADR is the evidence that the advice was\nsought. Either works if it is written down; what fails is an unstated rule that surfaces as a veto after\nmerge.\n\nAn accepted decision that nothing checks decays quietly: six months later someone imports the provider\nSDK directly \"just for this one feature\" and the gateway decision is void without anyone deciding so.\nMADR's **confirmation** section exists for this. Wherever possible, confirm with an automated check,\nwhat *Building Evolutionary Architectures* calls a fitness function: an import-lint rule, a CI test, a\npolicy-as-code rule, or a dashboard alert. A decision with an automated confirmation is enforced; one\nwithout is a suggestion.\n\n### What AI systems add to the template\n- **Evidence snapshot.** The evaluation set version and the scores, latency percentiles and cost per\n  request for each option at decision time, so the decision can be re-run, not just re-argued.\n- **Assumptions with expiry.** Provider pricing, rate limits, context limits, licence terms, and\n  expected traffic, each labelled with the date it was checked.\n- **Revisit triggers.** Concrete conditions under which the decision must be re-evaluated: a volume\n  threshold, an open-weight model reaching eval parity, a residency requirement, a price change. Without\n  a trigger, an ADR becomes a reason never to look again.\n- **Reversibility.** Whether this is a two-way door (swap a model behind the gateway) or a one-way door\n  (train on customer data, choose an embedding model that fixes the index).\n- **Compliance and data flow implications.** Where customer data goes under this decision, and which\n  approvals it needed.\n\n### How it fits with the rest of the design toolkit\nNon-functional requirements define what matters; trade-off analysis scores the options against them;\nthe ADR records the outcome and the reasoning; the C4 diagrams show the resulting structure. When any\none of these is missing, the others degrade: an ADR with no NFRs behind it is an opinion, and a diagram\nwith no ADRs behind it is a snapshot nobody can question.","example":"A MADR-style record for a decision almost every AI team makes early. The figures are illustrative\nscenario values, not benchmarks; the point is the structure: neutral context, real options, named\ndrivers, honest negative consequences, an evidence snapshot, an automated confirmation and explicit\nrevisit triggers.\n\n```markdown\n# ADR-0007: Serve v1 through a hosted model API behind our own gateway\n\n**Status:** Accepted (2026-03-12)  **Supersedes:** none  **Deciders:** platform lead, security, CTO\n\n**Context.** Six engineers, launch in ten weeks. Expected launch volume ~20M tokens/month, highly\nuncertain (illustrative). NFRs: p95 time-to-first-token <= 1.5 s, cost <= $0.04 per conversation,\nEU data residency, provider must be swappable without client changes.\n\n**Decision drivers.** Time to launch; engineering capacity; residency; cost at uncertain volume;\nability to switch models as quality and prices change.\n\n**Considered options.**\n1. Hosted model API (EU region) behind an internal gateway.\n2. Self-hosted open-weight model on two GPUs with vLLM.\n3. Hybrid: hosted for chat, self-hosted for embeddings.\n\n**Decision.** Option 1. All model calls go through the gateway, which owns routing, quotas, prompt\ncaching and per-tenant metering. We will not self-host any generation model in v1.\n\n**Consequences.**\n- Easier: launch on time; model swaps are a gateway config change; no GPU operations.\n- Harder: per-token cost grows linearly with usage; dependence on provider rate limits and uptime;\n  residency relies on the provider's EU endpoint and DPA.\n- Follow-up: contract tests against the gateway interface; monthly cost and quality review.\n\n**Confirmation.** CI import rule: only `gateway/` may import a provider SDK; provider keys exist only in\nthe gateway's secret scope.\n\n**Evidence at decision time.** Golden set v3 (312 cases): hosted model faithfulness 0.91 vs best\nopen-weight candidate 0.84; p95 TTFT 0.9 s vs 1.3 s on a single GPU under load test. Illustrative.\n\n**Revisit when:** sustained volume > 2B tokens/month for three months; an open-weight model reaches\n>= 0.90 on golden set v3 or later; the provider cannot meet a customer's residency requirement; or\nprovider price changes by more than 30%.\n```","tradeoffs":["Writing an ADR costs an hour or two at decision time, but it saves the repeated re-litigation of the same choice; the cost is not worth paying for decisions that are cheap to reverse and nobody will ask about.","Immutability keeps the history honest, but it produces chains of superseding records that a reader must follow; an index page with current status keeps this navigable.","A rich template (MADR) forces real alternatives and evidence into the open, but it discourages people from writing anything for medium-sized decisions; many teams use the full template for one-way doors and the short Nygard form otherwise.","Keeping ADRs in the repository puts them where engineers look and under review, but makes them invisible to non-engineering stakeholders unless they are also published or summarised."],"pitfalls":["Writing the ADR after the decision to justify it; the symptom is a record with one option, no negative consequences and a context section that reads like a sales pitch.","Editing an accepted ADR when the decision changes; the symptom is `git blame` showing the decision text rewritten with no superseding record and no explanation of what changed.","Recording decisions about trivia; the symptom is eighty ADRs in a year and no one able to find the five that matter.","Omitting the evidence and assumptions in AI decisions; the symptom is a team still on a model two generations old because \"there is an ADR\", with no way to tell whether its reasons still hold.","No revisit trigger; the symptom is the same, discovered only when a competitor's cost per conversation is a third of yours.","Accepting a decision with no confirmation mechanism; the symptom is code that contradicts an accepted ADR (a direct provider call, a second vector store) discovered months later during an incident.","Storing ADRs in a wiki away from the code; the symptom is that nobody updates status, and engineers learn about a decision from the code rather than the record."],"when":"Write an ADR whenever a choice shapes structure, cost, risk or data flow and would be expensive to\nreverse: tenancy model, vector store, gateway, retrieval strategy, model tier per workload, evaluation\ngates, residency approach, build versus buy. Write it as the decision is being made, in the pull request\nwhere it is argued, and link it from the diagram and the README. Measure the practice by whether new\nengineers cite ADR numbers in design discussions and whether superseding records exist for the decisions\nthat changed. In interviews, being able to describe a past decision in ADR shape, context, options,\nchoice, consequences, and what would have made you choose differently, is one of the clearest signals of\narchitectural maturity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Opens an ADR in the same pull request as a significant change, keeps the evidence section honest with real eval and latency numbers, and reads the existing chain before proposing a reversal.","architect":"Owns the ADR log, decides what is architecturally significant, enforces supersede-not-edit, and makes sure every ADR traces to the NFRs and trade-off analysis behind it.","fde":"Writes customer-specific ADRs for deviations from the reference architecture (residency, SSO, on-prem model serving) so the next engineer on the account inherits the reasoning, not just the config."},"check":{"q":"An ADR from fourteen months ago says \"use provider X's model Y because it scored best on our\nevaluation set\". A new engineer wants to switch to a cheaper model this sprint. How should the team\nhandle it, and what does the answer reveal about how the original ADR should have been written?","a":"Do not edit the old record and do not switch on a hunch. Read its context and evidence: which\neval set version, what scores, what cost and latency targets, which assumptions. Re-run the current\ngolden set against both models, measure cost per conversation and p95 latency under the same NFRs, and\ncheck whether any assumption (price, rate limits, residency) has changed. If the cheaper model meets the\ndrivers, write a new ADR that supersedes ADR-N, records the new evidence, and marks the old one\n*Superseded*. The exercise shows what the original should have contained: an evidence snapshot with the\neval version, dated assumptions, and a revisit trigger such as \"re-evaluate when a model within 10% of\nthe cost achieves parity\", which would have made this review routine rather than a debate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are accepted ADRs treated as immutable, with changes made by superseding records?","options":["Because version control cannot track edits to Markdown files reliably","So the record stays an honest snapshot of what was decided and why, and the history of changes is preserved as a chain","Because only architects are permitted to edit them","To keep the number of files in the repository stable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Editing in place destroys the context and reasoning of the original decision, which is exactly what a future reader needs to judge whether it still applies. Superseding keeps both the old and the new reasoning."},{"q":"Which element of an ADR is most often missing when the record was written to justify a decision after the fact?","options":["A number and title","The names of the deciders","Genuine alternative options and negative consequences of the chosen one","A link to the related C4 diagram"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A real decision weighs options and accepts costs. A record with a single option and \"no downsides\" is advocacy, and it gives a future team nothing to re-evaluate when conditions change."},{"q":"What should an ADR about choosing an LLM provider include that a classic database-choice ADR usually would not need?","options":["A sequence diagram of the request path","An evidence snapshot (eval set version, scores, cost, latency), dated assumptions and explicit revisit triggers","The names of all competing vendors in the market","A rollback script"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Model quality, pricing and limits change far faster than database characteristics, so the decision must carry the data to be re-run and the conditions under which it should be. Without them the ADR becomes a reason never to re-evaluate."}]},{"key":"system-design/nfrs","sources":["ISO/IEC 25010 — Systems and software quality models","Bass, Clements, Kazman, 2012 — Software Architecture in Practice (3rd ed.)"],"why":"Two systems can implement the same feature list and be completely different architectures. One serves\nten internal users from a single process; the other serves ten thousand tenants across regions with a\n99.9% availability target. Nothing in the functional requirements distinguishes them. What does is the\nset of quality attributes they must satisfy, which is why experienced architects say the NFRs *are* the\narchitecture.\n\nThe naive way to state them is with adjectives: \"the assistant must be fast, accurate, secure and\nscalable.\" Nobody can design to that, test it, or tell when it has been violated. Fast for whom, at what\nload, at which percentile? Accurate on which inputs, measured how, against what threshold? The cost of\nleaving these vague is paid late and in production: a pilot that works for five users streams its first\ntoken in eight seconds at five hundred; a customer's security review surfaces a residency requirement the\nchosen model provider cannot meet; the cost per conversation turns out to exceed the price per seat.\n\nAI systems add a category classic engineering never had to specify. Answer quality is probabilistic and\ncan only be stated statistically, over a defined evaluation set, with a threshold and a tolerance. Safety\nproperties (harmful output rate, prompt-injection resistance) are the same. And the three that matter\nmost, quality, latency and cost, pull against each other, so an NFR list without priorities cannot\nresolve the first real design conflict. Writing NFRs properly is how the trade-offs become explicit\nbefore the code does.","how":"### From adjective to scenario\nThe quality attribute scenario format from Bass, Clements and Kazman turns an adjective into something\ntestable. It has six parts: a **source** of the stimulus, the **stimulus** itself, the **environment** in\nwhich it arrives, the **artifact** it hits, the expected **response**, and the **response measure**. For\nexample: a logged-in support agent (source) submits a chat message (stimulus) during normal operation\nwith 50 concurrent sessions (environment) to the chat API (artifact); the first token is streamed back\n(response) within 1.5 seconds at the 95th percentile over any five-minute window (measure). Each part\nexists to close a loophole: the environment stops \"it was fast in the demo\", the artifact stops arguments\nabout where latency is counted, and the measure's percentile and window stop averages hiding tail pain.\n\n### The categories, and what AI adds\nISO/IEC 25010 organises product quality into characteristics: functional suitability, performance\nefficiency, compatibility, usability, reliability, security, maintainability and portability (a later\nrevision adds safety and renames some). The list is a checklist for discovery, not a template to fill in\nfull. For an AI system, add the attributes that classic software never needed to specify:\n\n- **Answer quality.** Faithfulness or groundedness, task success rate, citation correctness, measured on\n  a versioned golden set with a threshold and a minimum sample size.\n- **Safety.** Rate of harmful or policy-violating outputs, refusal correctness, resistance to a\n  prompt-injection test suite.\n- **Freshness.** Maximum lag between a source document changing and the index reflecting it.\n- **Consistency.** How much output may vary for the same input, and whether a run can be reproduced.\n- **Cost.** Cost per successful task or per conversation, including retries and tool calls.\n- **Data handling.** Residency, retention, no-training clauses, what may appear in logs and traces.\n- **Operability.** Time to roll back a prompt or model version, and to re-index after an embedding change.\n\n### Making each one measurable and owned\nEvery NFR gets five fields: the metric, the target, the measurement method, the condition (load, data,\ntime window) and the place it is enforced. That last field is the one most often missing, and it exists\nbecause an NFR with no enforcement point is a wish. Runtime attributes (latency, availability, freshness)\nbecome SLOs with alerts. Quality and safety attributes become evaluation gates in CI and release\npipelines. Security and residency attributes become review checklist items and automated tests. Cost\nattributes become a per-tenant, per-feature dashboard with budgets. Use percentiles, never averages, for\nanything latency-shaped, and state the window over which a percentile is computed.\n\nStatistical targets need one more field: the uncertainty of the measurement. A proportion measured on\n*n* cases has a standard error of about √(p(1−p)/n). At p = 0.90 and n = 300 that is ≈ 0.017, so the 95%\ninterval is roughly ±3.4 points, and a run scoring 0.91 cannot be told apart from one scoring 0.88. Write\nthe gate accordingly: either \"the lower bound of the interval is at least 0.90\", or \"no statistically\nsignificant regression against the current release on the same cases\". The paired form needs far fewer\ncases to detect a small drop, because each case is compared with itself.\n\n### Decomposing a target into budgets\nAn end-to-end NFR is not actionable for one team until it is split along the request path. Latency\nbudgets add: a 1.5 s p95 time-to-first-token might be allotted as 50 ms for auth and tenant resolution,\n100 ms for query embedding, 150 ms for vector search, 200 ms for guardrails and prompt assembly, leaving\nabout 1 s for the model's own first token (illustrative). Summing component p95s usually overestimates the\nend-to-end p95, because components rarely hit their tails on the same request, so budgets set this way\nare conservative; the end-to-end measurement is still the one that counts. Availability multiplies\ninstead: a chat path that depends serially on your stack at 99.95% and a provider at 99.9% delivers about\n0.9995 × 0.999 ≈ 99.85%, below a 99.9% target before your own bugs. That one multiplication is why the\navailability row in the example below forces a provider fallback rather than a better runbook. A 99.9%\nmonthly target is about 43 minutes of allowed unavailability in a 30-day month; stating the error budget\nin minutes makes the cost of each dependency concrete.\n\n### Prioritising, because they conflict\nNot every NFR drives structure. The **architecturally significant requirements** are the handful whose\nsatisfaction forces a design choice: a 1.5-second time-to-first-token forces streaming and a model-tier\ndecision; EU residency forces region pinning and provider selection; a cost ceiling forces routing and\ncaching. ATAM's utility tree ranks scenarios by business importance and architectural difficulty so the\nteam argues about the top five rather than the full list. Then the conflicts must be ordered explicitly:\nif quality and latency collide, which gives? That written priority is what trade-off analysis uses as a\ntie-breaker, and without it every design review reopens the question.\n\n### Traceability\nEach architecturally significant NFR should be traceable forward to the decision it drove (an ADR) and\nto the test, SLO or eval gate that verifies it, and backward to the stakeholder and business reason it\ncame from. Traceability exists because requirements change: when the cost ceiling moves, you need to\nknow which decisions and which tests depended on the old number.\n\n### Where they come from\nFinance and product supply cost and availability targets; security and legal supply residency,\nretention and access requirements; users supply latency tolerance (and it differs: an agent drafting\nreplies tolerates less than a batch summariser); operations supply observability and rollback needs;\nregulation supplies audit and explainability demands. A baseline measurement of the current process\n(how long a human takes, how often they are wrong) is often the most defensible source for a quality\ntarget.","example":"A worked NFR set for a multi-tenant support assistant that drafts replies for human agents. The figures\nare illustrative targets chosen for the scenario, not benchmarks. The point is the shape: each row is\nmeasurable, has an enforcement point, and visibly drives an architectural decision; the last column is\nwhere the trade-off analysis and ADRs begin.\n\n| Attribute | Scenario and measure (illustrative) | Enforced by | Architectural consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Latency | First token within 1.5 s at p95, 50 concurrent sessions, 5-min window | SLO + load test in staging | Streaming responses; model tier chosen for TTFT; gateway timeout budget |\n| Answer quality | Faithfulness ≥ 0.90 and citation correctness ≥ 0.95 on golden set v3 (≥ 300 cases) | Eval gate in CI; blocks release | RAG with mandatory citations; reranking; no release without eval run |\n| Safety | Harmful-output rate ≤ 0.5% on red-team suite; ≥ 99% refusal of injection probes | Eval gate + quarterly red team | Input/output guardrails as a separate component; tool allow-lists |\n| Availability | 99.9% monthly for the chat API; degraded mode allowed | SLO, error budget policy | Provider fallback via gateway; graceful degradation to retrieval-only |\n| Cost | ≤ $0.04 per conversation averaged weekly, per tenant | Meter + budget alert | Model routing by difficulty; prompt caching; context trimming |\n| Residency | EU tenants' prompts, documents and logs never leave EU regions | Architecture review + automated egress test | Region-pinned deployment; EU provider endpoint; log pipeline per region |\n| Freshness | Knowledge-base edits searchable within 15 minutes at p95 | SLO on index lag | Incremental ingestion with change feed, not nightly rebuild |\n| Operability | Prompt or model rollback in under 10 minutes without a deploy | Runbook drill | Versioned prompt registry; gateway config as the switch |\n\nRead the table against the conflicts. The quality row wants a large model and long context; the latency\nand cost rows want a small one and short prompts. The written priority for this product was quality,\nthen latency, then cost, which is why the consequence column resolves to routing (large model only for\nhard cases) rather than a single small model. Change the priority and the architecture changes with it.","tradeoffs":["Precise, tight NFRs make design and testing unambiguous, but each tightened target costs real money (more capacity, bigger models, more regions); the right move is to negotiate the number with its cost attached rather than accept a round figure.","Percentile-based latency targets protect the tail, but p99 on a low-traffic endpoint is noisy; use p95 or longer windows until volume justifies p99.","A long NFR list is thorough, but only the architecturally significant few deserve scenario-level rigour; spreading effort evenly produces a document nobody reads.","Treating NFRs as hard contracts (SLAs) gives customers certainty, but it removes the room to trade; keep internal SLOs tighter than external SLAs so the trade-off space stays yours.","Quality thresholds set from the start make release gates possible, but a threshold set before a baseline exists is arbitrary; measure the human baseline first, then set the bar relative to it."],"pitfalls":["Specifying latency as an average; the symptom is a green dashboard while a fifth of users wait ten seconds for a first token.","A quality NFR with no named evaluation set or sample size; the symptom is \"accuracy 92%\" that cannot be reproduced and silently changes when someone edits the test cases.","Gating releases on a point estimate from a small eval set; the symptom is a gate that flips between pass and fail on reruns of the same build, and a team that learns to rerun until green.","A latency target with no load condition; the symptom is a staging pass followed by a launch-day collapse at ten times the concurrency.","Copying \"99.99% availability\" from a template; the symptom is a multi-region design and on-call rotation for an internal tool whose users go home at six.","Forgetting cost as an NFR; the symptom is a successful pilot whose unit economics make the product unprofitable at scale, discovered from the invoice.","NFRs without an owner or an enforcement point; the symptom is a requirements document that nobody has opened since the kickoff."],"when":"Write NFRs at the start of any system that will have real users, and revisit them at every scope change,\nbecause they are the inputs to every architecture decision that follows. Spend the scenario-level rigour\non the five to eight that are architecturally significant and keep the rest as a checklist. Attach an\nenforcement mechanism to each: an SLO, an eval gate, a security test, a budget alert. In an engagement,\ngetting the customer to state and prioritise NFRs early is how you avoid discovering residency or cost\nconstraints in week ten. In interviews, when asked to design a system, state the NFRs you are assuming\nbefore drawing anything, with numbers and percentiles, and name which one is driving each structural\nchoice; that is the difference between a feature sketch and an architecture.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns each NFR into a concrete check, a load test, an eval gate, an SLO alert, and knows the numeric target behind the component being built.","architect":"Owns the elicitation, prioritisation and traceability of NFRs, and uses them as the scoring dimensions for every trade-off and the justification for every ADR.","fde":"Extracts the customer's real constraints (residency, latency tolerance, budget, audit) early, writes them as measurable NFRs, and uses them to set acceptance criteria for the pilot."},"check":{"q":"A product manager says the new assistant must be \"fast, accurate and cheap\". Turn that into\nNFRs, explain why they conflict, and say what you would ask the business to decide.","a":"I would write each as a measurable scenario with an enforcement point. Fast: first token within\n1.5 seconds at p95 under the expected peak concurrency, enforced as an SLO and a staging load test.\nAccurate: faithfulness and task success above a threshold on a versioned golden set of at least a few\nhundred representative cases, enforced as a release gate, with the threshold set relative to the measured\nhuman baseline. Cheap: a cost per successful conversation ceiling, metered per tenant with alerts. They\nconflict because quality pushes toward larger models and longer context, which raise latency and cost,\nwhile the cost ceiling pushes toward smaller models and shorter prompts, which lower quality. I would ask\nthe business to rank the three explicitly, so the design can resolve the conflict, typically by routing\nonly hard cases to the expensive model, and to attach a cost to any tightening of the targets."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a well-formed non-functional requirement?","options":["The system must be highly available","The chat API returns its first token within 1.5 s at p95 under 50 concurrent sessions, measured over any 5-minute window","The model should be as accurate as possible on customer questions","Latency must average under one second"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A testable NFR names the artifact, the condition, the percentile and the window. An average hides tail latency, and \"highly available\" or \"as accurate as possible\" cannot be verified or designed to."},{"q":"Why should an AI quality requirement name a versioned evaluation set and a minimum sample size?","options":["Because model providers require it for support contracts","To make the requirement shorter and easier to read","Because a statistical threshold is meaningless without the population it is measured on, and the gate must be reproducible as cases change","Because regulators mandate exactly 300 test cases"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quality is a probabilistic property; \"92% accurate\" means nothing without the set, its version and enough cases for the estimate to be stable. Versioning lets a release gate be re-run and compared over time."},{"q":"What makes a requirement \"architecturally significant\"?","options":["It appears in the customer contract","It concerns security rather than performance","Satisfying it forces a structural choice, such as streaming, region pinning or model routing","It has the largest numeric target"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Architectural significance is about consequences for structure, not about the source or the domain of the requirement. Many contractual or security requirements are satisfied by configuration and do not shape the architecture."}]},{"key":"system-design/trade-off-analysis","sources":["Richards, Ford, 2020 — Fundamentals of Software Architecture","Ford, Richards, Sadalage, Dehghani, 2021 — Software Architecture: The Hard Parts","Kazman, Klein, Clements, 2000 — ATAM: Method for Architecture Evaluation"],"why":"Richards and Ford's first law of software architecture is that everything is a trade-off, and their\ncorollary is that if you think you have found a choice without one, you have not yet found the\ntrade-off. The practice exists because teams keep making architectural decisions in ways that hide the\ncost until it is paid.\n\nThe common failure modes are recognisable. The **pros-and-cons list** has no weights, no evidence and no\nconditions, so it can justify anything. The **weighted scoring matrix** looks rigorous but is usually\nbuilt backwards: the favourite is chosen first and the weights are adjusted until it wins, with\nsingle-digit precision pretending to be measurement. **Best-practice** and **vendor-benchmark**\ndecisions import someone else's context, which is the one thing a trade-off is sensitive to. And\n**analysis paralysis** is the opposite failure, where the absence of a method makes every decision a\ndebate that ends with the loudest voice.\n\nAI systems make this worse in a specific way. The options change monthly, published benchmarks do not\ntransfer to your data, and the honest answer to most questions, hosted or self-hosted, large model or\nsmall, long context or retrieval, fine-tune or prompt, is \"it depends on volume, quality threshold and\nhow often you will need to switch\". A good analysis does not pretend otherwise; it tells you exactly what\nit depends on, at what value the answer changes, and how expensive it is to be wrong. That is a\ndifferent output from \"we picked X\", and it is the one that survives contact with next quarter.","how":"### 1. Frame the decision\nState the decision in one sentence, its scope, and who decides by when. Then separate **hard\nconstraints** from **dimensions**. Hard constraints (EU residency, a licence that forbids commercial\nuse, a budget ceiling, a launch date) are filters: an option that violates one is removed before\nscoring. Treating a constraint as a weighted dimension is how a disqualified option \"wins on points\".\nThe constraints come from the NFRs, which is why trade-off analysis without written NFRs degrades into\nopinion.\n\n### 2. Enumerate real options\nAt least three, including \"do nothing or defer\" and usually a hybrid. A decision with one option is a\nratification, and a decision between two is often a false dichotomy; the hybrid (hosted model for\ngeneration, self-hosted for embeddings; small model with escalation to a large one) is frequently the\nanswer and is only found if it is on the list.\n\n### 3. Choose the dimensions from the NFR priorities\nFour to seven dimensions, drawn from the architecturally significant requirements rather than a generic\ntemplate: quality on your golden set, p95 latency, cost per successful task, operational burden,\nvendor or lock-in risk, time to deliver, reversibility. Fewer dimensions with real evidence beat a\ntwelve-column matrix of guesses. The priority order among them must be the one the business already\nagreed in the NFRs, so that the tie-breaker is not invented during the analysis.\n\n### 4. Fill each cell with evidence, and label its grade\nEvery cell is one of: **measured** (your workload, your eval set), **prototyped** (a spike on\nrepresentative data), **estimated** (back-of-envelope from known prices and throughput), or\n**asserted** (someone's belief). Write the grade next to the number. The two cells with the highest\nweight and the lowest grade are where to spend a prototyping day, because they decide the outcome and\nare the most likely to be wrong. Carry ranges, not points: \"cost per 1k conversations $30–55 depending on\ncache hit rate\" is more useful than \"$42\".\n\n### 5. Find the sensitivity points and the flip conditions\nThis is the step that distinguishes analysis from scoring. For each important input, ask at what value\nthe ranking changes: monthly volume, GPU utilisation, provider price, the quality threshold, team size.\nATAM calls these sensitivity points, and a parameter that moves two quality attributes in opposite\ndirections is a trade-off point. If a flip condition sits close to your expected operating point, the\ndecision is fragile, and the right output is not a winner but a design that makes switching cheap (an\nabstraction such as a gateway) plus a trigger to re-run the analysis.\n\n### 6. Weigh reversibility and the cost of being wrong\nA two-way door (swap the model behind a gateway, change a chunking strategy) can be decided quickly on\nthin evidence because the cost of error is small. A one-way door (train on customer data, commit to an\nembedding model that fixes a billion-vector index, sign a multi-year commitment) deserves prototypes,\nranges and a pre-mortem. Under uncertainty the reversible option carries an option value that a static\nmatrix does not show, so it should get explicit credit.\n\n### 7. Decide, record, schedule the revisit\nWrite the outcome as an ADR: the constraints, the options, the matrix with evidence grades, the flip\nconditions and the revisit triggers. The analysis is reusable only if the inputs were recorded; \"we\nchose hosted\" is unreproducible, \"we chose hosted because break-even was 18B tokens a month at 50%\nutilisation and we expect 2B\" can be re-run in five minutes next year.\n\n### Techniques that fit inside the steps\nBreak-even arithmetic for cost cells; scenario walkthroughs (run the top NFR scenarios through each\noption on a whiteboard) for availability and operability; a utility tree to agree which dimensions\nmatter; a pre-mortem (\"it is a year later and this choice failed, why?\") to surface risks the matrix\nmissed; and the halving test (\"would we still choose this if volume halved or doubled?\") as a cheap\nsensitivity check.\n\nWhen the forecast itself is the uncertainty, score options per scenario rather than at one point:\ncompute each option's cost at the low, expected and high ends of the range, and its **regret** in each\n(its cost minus the best option's cost in that scenario). Choosing the option whose worst-case regret is\nsmallest is a defensible rule when nobody can honestly narrow the range, and the table makes visible\nwhich scenario each option is betting on.","example":"The cost dimension of a hosted-versus-self-hosted decision, done as a break-even with an explicit flip\ncondition. Every figure is illustrative and must be replaced with your own quotes and a load-tested\nthroughput number; the script is the shape of the reasoning, not a benchmark. Note what it shows: at\nthese inputs self-hosting only wins above roughly 18 billion tokens a month, and a drop in utilisation\nfrom 50% to 40% makes it never win, because idle GPUs still bill. That utilisation sensitivity is the\nfinding, and it belongs in the ADR alongside the quality and risk dimensions this cell says nothing about.\n\n```python\n# Illustrative figures only. Replace with your provider quote and your measured throughput.\nHOSTED_PER_M  = 2.00         # $ per 1M tokens, blended input/output\nGPU_HOUR      = 2.50         # $ per GPU-hour, on-demand\nTOK_PER_GPU_H = 3_000_000    # tokens one GPU serves per hour on YOUR workload (load-tested)\nOPS_PER_MONTH = 6_000        # $ people + tooling to run serving, paid regardless of volume\n\ndef monthly(tokens: float, util: float) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    hosted = tokens / 1e6 * HOSTED_PER_M\n    self_hosted = tokens / (TOK_PER_GPU_H * util) * GPU_HOUR + OPS_PER_MONTH\n    return hosted, self_hosted\n\ndef break_even_tokens(util: float) -> float:\n    # hosted == self_hosted  =>  tokens * (price_per_token - gpu_cost_per_token) == fixed ops cost\n    gap = HOSTED_PER_M / 1e6 - GPU_HOUR / (TOK_PER_GPU_H * util)\n    return OPS_PER_MONTH / gap if gap > 0 else float(\"inf\")\n\nfor util in (0.5, 0.4):\n    be = break_even_tokens(util)\n    verdict = f\"{be / 1e9:.1f}B tokens/month\" if be != float(\"inf\") else \"never (GPU cost/token exceeds hosted)\"\n    print(f\"utilisation {util:.0%}: self-hosting pays off above {verdict}\")\n    for t in (5e8, 5e9, 5e10):\n        h, s = monthly(t, util)\n        print(f\"   {t / 1e9:>5.1f}B tokens   hosted ${h:>9,.0f}   self-hosted ${s:>9,.0f}\")\n\n# utilisation 50%: self-hosting pays off above 18.0B tokens/month\n#      0.5B tokens   hosted $    1,000   self-hosted $    6,833\n#      5.0B tokens   hosted $   10,000   self-hosted $   14,333\n#     50.0B tokens   hosted $  100,000   self-hosted $   89,333\n# utilisation 40%: self-hosting pays off above never (GPU cost/token exceeds hosted)\n#      0.5B tokens   hosted $    1,000   self-hosted $    7,042\n#      5.0B tokens   hosted $   10,000   self-hosted $   16,417\n#     50.0B tokens   hosted $  100,000   self-hosted $  110,167\n```\n\nOne input deserves suspicion: utilisation is not a free parameter. It is served tokens divided by the\ncapacity you pay for, and two things cap it. A fleet sized for peak traffic idles off-peak, so with a\npeak-to-average ratio of 2 and no autoscaling, utilisation cannot exceed 50%. And a minimum fleet, say two\nGPUs for redundancy running 24/7 (~1,460 GPU-hours, ~$3,650 a month here), is billed at any volume: at\n0.5B tokens it would run about 11% utilised, so the real self-hosted cost is ~$9,650, not $6,833. The floor\ndoes not move the 18B break-even, where the fleet is already ~16 GPUs, but it shows why the utilisation\ncell should be derived from a traffic profile rather than typed in.","tradeoffs":["A full matrix with evidence grades and sensitivity analysis produces decisions that survive review, but it costs days; for two-way doors a one-paragraph analysis with a named flip condition is the right amount of rigour.","Prototyping the uncertain cells replaces guesses with measurements, but a spike on unrepresentative data is worse than an honest estimate; the prototype must use your golden set and your traffic shape.","Explicit weights make priorities arguable in the open, but they invite tuning toward a favourite; the defence is to fix the priority order in the NFRs before any option is scored.","Optimising for reversibility keeps options open, but every abstraction that enables switching costs something and can hide capabilities of the thing behind it; it pays off when the flip condition is near, not when one option clearly dominates.","Sensitivity analysis tells you what the decision depends on, but it needs real inputs (volume forecasts, utilisation, prices); with none available, say so and set a short revisit date instead of inventing them."],"pitfalls":["Weights adjusted after the first scoring pass; the symptom is a matrix whose history shows the favourite losing, then the weights changing, then the favourite winning.","Dimensions copied from a generic template rather than from the NFRs; the symptom is a column such as \"community size\" outweighing the quality threshold the product will be judged on.","Point estimates with false precision; the symptom is a decision resting on a 7.4 versus 7.1 total that no one can reproduce.","Using the provider's or a paper's benchmark as the quality cell; the symptom is a model that wins on the leaderboard and loses on your golden set after the contract is signed.","Forgetting the fixed operational cost of running things yourself; the symptom is a self-hosting plan whose per-token cost looked lower and whose monthly bill is higher.","Typing in a utilisation figure instead of deriving it from the traffic profile; the symptom is a GPU bill well above plan because the fleet is sized for peak and idles overnight and at weekends.","Treating a hard constraint as a weighted dimension; the symptom is a vendor that \"scored best\" and cannot satisfy the customer's residency requirement.","Never recording the inputs; the symptom is the same debate, from scratch, every time a new model or a new price list appears."],"when":"Run the full method for one-way doors and for any choice whose flip condition is plausibly near your\noperating point: hosted versus self-hosted, embedding model and vector store, tenancy model, fine-tune\nversus retrieval, build versus buy on a core capability. Run the short form, constraints, three options,\none flip condition, a reversibility note, for everything else, and resist the urge to run anything for\ndecisions that are cheap to undo. What to measure: your own quality numbers on your own golden set, your\nown throughput under your own load, and your own volume forecast with a range. In interviews, the\nstrongest answers to \"would you use X or Y\" name the dimensions, state the assumption that decides it,\ngive the value at which the answer flips, and say how they would keep the choice reversible until the\ndata arrives.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the measured cells, latency under load, eval scores, token counts, cache hit rates, and builds the spike that turns the riskiest estimate into a measurement.","architect":"Owns the framing, the constraint-versus-dimension split, the priority order inherited from the NFRs, the sensitivity analysis and the written ADR with revisit triggers.","fde":"Runs the analysis with the customer's real volumes, prices and constraints, and shows them the flip condition so the recommendation is understood as conditional rather than as vendor preference."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks whether they should self-host an open-weight model or keep using a hosted API,\nand wants a yes or no. How do you structure the answer so it is both decisive and honest?","a":"I would first apply the hard constraints: residency, licence, security review, and whether they\nhave anyone to operate GPUs. If self-hosting survives those, I would score the live options, hosted,\nself-hosted and a hybrid, on the dimensions their NFRs prioritise: quality on their golden set, p95\nlatency, cost per successful task, operational burden and reversibility, labelling each cell as measured,\nprototyped or estimated. The decisive part is the flip condition: with their quotes and a load-tested\nthroughput, self-hosting breaks even above some monthly token volume at a given utilisation, and I would\ncompare that to their forecast range. If the forecast is well below it, the answer is hosted, behind a\ngateway so the switch stays cheap, with a revisit trigger at a stated volume or when an open-weight model\nreaches eval parity. If the forecast straddles the flip point, the recommendation is the hybrid and a\nmeasured pilot rather than a bet."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should hard constraints be applied as filters before scoring, rather than as heavily weighted dimensions?","options":["Because scoring matrices cannot represent more than five dimensions","Because an option that violates a constraint can still win on total points, producing a choice that cannot actually be deployed","Because constraints always come from legal rather than engineering","Because filtering is faster to compute"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A residency or licence violation is disqualifying regardless of how well the option scores elsewhere; giving it a weight lets strong scores on other columns outvote a requirement that has no compensation."},{"q":"What is the most valuable output of a sensitivity analysis in an architecture decision?","options":["A more precise total score for the winning option","A list of all the stakeholders consulted","The input values at which the ranking of options changes, compared with the expected operating point","A ranking of dimensions by how hard they are to measure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Knowing where the answer flips tells you whether the decision is robust or fragile and what to monitor as a revisit trigger. A more precise score does not reveal how much the result depends on uncertain inputs."},{"q":"In the break-even example, why can lowering GPU utilisation from 50% to 40% make self-hosting never pay off?","options":["Because the hosted provider lowers its price at lower volumes","Because lower utilisation increases the fixed operational cost","Because idle GPU time still bills, so the effective cost per served token rises above the hosted per-token price","Because utilisation below 50% triggers autoscaling penalties"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Self-hosted cost per token is GPU cost per hour divided by tokens actually served per hour; serving fewer tokens on the same billed hardware raises it. Once it exceeds the hosted price per token, no volume recovers the fixed costs."}]}]});
